window.LMS_DATA = {"meta":{"generated_from":"AI Roles Skills Taxonomy, Oct 20 2026, Nugen IT Services","built_at":"2026-10-20 15:52 UTC","build_id":"2026-10-20_1552_UTC","topic_count":584,"ok_count":584},"tiers":{"1":{"title":"Tier 1 — Shared core (AI Engineer base)","blurb":"Everything in this tier is assumed by Tiers 2 and 3. It is the AI Engineer baseline."},"2":{"title":"Tier 2 — AI Architect (adds)","blurb":"The Architect owns the decisions and boundaries; Tier 1 is implemented by engineers under them."},"3":{"title":"Tier 3 — Forward Deployed Engineer (adds)","blurb":"The FDE takes Tier 1 into a specific customer's systems, data, security posture and people."}},"role_paths":{"all":{"title":"Everything","desc":"All three tiers, no trimming.","rules":{}},"llm-app":{"title":"LLM-app AI Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 minus ML-fundamentals depth; distributed training and feature stores dropped.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","tier3":"skip","ml-fundamentals":"background","caveat":"skip"}},"ml-heavy":{"title":"ML-heavy AI Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 in full.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","tier3":"skip"}},"architect":{"title":"AI Architect","desc":"Tier 1 breadth + Tier 2.","rules":{"tier3":"skip"}},"fde":{"title":"Forward Deployed Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 + Tier 3, with Tier 2 Reliability and Security as background reading.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","reliability":"background","security-governance-compliance":"background"}}},"modules":[{"id":"ml-fundamentals","tier":1,"order":0,"title":"ML fundamentals","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the machine learning that sits underneath every model an AI engineer touches, in\nthree connected parts. The first part covers problem framing. It looks at where the training signal\ncomes from (supervised, unsupervised or self-supervised), at the three main problem shapes\n(classification, regression and clustering), and at the loss function, which is the differentiable\nstand-in for the thing you actually want. The second part covers how a loss becomes a trained model.\nBackpropagation produces the gradient. Gradient descent and the optimizers turn it into updates.\nLearning-rate schedules, warmup, gradient clipping, batch size, gradient accumulation and mixed\nprecision decide whether those updates are stable, fast and affordable. Normalization, residual\nconnections and dropout are the architectural pieces that make deep networks trainable and keep them\nfrom memorizing. The third part covers evaluation: train-test leakage, class imbalance, calibration,\nprecision, recall and F1, ROC-AUC and the confusion matrix that all of those are derived from. After\nthe module you can read a fine-tuning config and say what every hyperparameter does and how they\ndepend on each other. You can diagnose a loss curve that spikes, plateaus or never moves. You can set\nup an evaluation that cannot flatter the model, and you can choose a decision threshold from what each\nkind of error costs. It matters most for AI Engineers, who train, fine-tune and evaluate models\ndirectly, and it gives Architects and FDEs the vocabulary to question a training budget or a headline\nmetric.","why":{"engineer":"You will fine-tune models with AdamW, warmup, cosine decay, clipping, bf16 and gradient accumulation, and debug NaNs, loss spikes and runs that never learn. You will also build routers, guardrails and judges whose thresholds, calibration and confusion matrices decide whether they are safe to ship.","architect":"Training memory, step count and GPU hours follow from batch size, sequence length, precision and the roughly 16 bytes per parameter of optimizer state. Evaluation design (group and time splits, prevalence-aware metrics, calibrated scores feeding cascades) decides whether offline numbers will hold in production, so both belong in the system design.","fde":"Customers arrive with a 0.99 AUC model that fails in production, a fraud or abuse detector tuned for accuracy on 1% positives, or a request to fine-tune on two thousand tickets. You need to find the leak, reset the threshold to their costs and size the fine-tune on their own data, quickly."},"mental_model":"Hold one loop and one gap. The loop is training. A batch goes through the network forward, a loss turns\nthe output into one number, backpropagation sends that number's sensitivity back to every parameter,\nand an optimizer takes a step whose size is set by the learning rate schedule. Every training topic\ncontrols one part of that loop. Loss functions decide what the gradient says. Batch size and\naccumulation decide how noisy the gradient is. Warmup, clipping and mixed precision stop a single bad\nstep from wrecking the run. Normalization and residual connections keep the signal and the gradient at\na usable scale through depth. Dropout and weight decay make memorizing harder. The gap sits between\noffline evaluation and production. A model outputs scores, a threshold turns scores into decisions, and\nthe confusion matrix counts what those decisions got right and wrong. Every evaluation topic asks\nwhether that count will still hold on future data. Leakage makes the gap look smaller than it is.\nImbalance and accuracy hide which errors are being made. ROC-AUC measures ranking, calibration measures\nwhether the probabilities can be trusted, and precision and recall measure the errors at the threshold\nyou ship. When something goes wrong, first decide whether the problem is in the loop (the model did\nnot learn) or in the gap (the model learned the wrong thing, or the measurement was wrong).","order":["supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","classification","regression","clustering","loss-functions","gradient-descent","backpropagation","optimizers","learning-rate-schedulers","warmup","gradient-clipping","batch-size","gradient-accumulation","mixed-precision","normalization","residual-connections","dropout-regularization","train-test-leakage","confusion-matrix","precision-recall-f1","roc-auc","class-imbalance","calibration"],"order_reasons":{"supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning":"starts with where the training signal comes from, which frames every later choice about labels, pre-training and fine-tuning.","classification":"the most common problem shape, and it introduces logits, softmax and the separation of scoring from deciding.","regression":"the quantity-shaped counterpart, showing that picking a loss means deciding what noise you expect and what you are estimating.","clustering":"the unsupervised shape for data with no labels yet, done on embeddings, before any loss is trained.","loss-functions":"turns each problem shape into a differentiable target and explains why cross-entropy and contrastive losses dominate.","gradient-descent":"the update rule that minimizes the loss, and where the learning rate and the noise of mini-batches first appear.","backpropagation":"how the gradient is computed in one backward pass, and why training memory grows with activations.","optimizers":"SGD, Adam and AdamW turn raw gradients into per-parameter updates and add optimizer-state memory.","learning-rate-schedulers":"makes the learning rate change over time, so it has to be planned in optimizer steps.","warmup":"the start of every schedule, needed because Adam's statistics and the early gradients cannot be trusted.","gradient-clipping":"limits how big a single update can be, building on backpropagation's exploding products and Adam's moment estimates.","batch-size":"couples gradient noise, hardware use, learning rate and step count into one decision.","gradient-accumulation":"reaches the effective batch the recipe asks for when memory only holds a small micro-batch.","mixed-precision":"trades number format for speed and activation memory, which matters for clipping and accumulation order.","normalization":"keeps activations at a stable scale through depth, and where it sits (Pre-LN or Post-LN) explains the need for warmup.","residual-connections":"the identity path that keeps gradients alive in deep stacks, best read alongside normalization placement.","dropout-regularization":"closes the training part by trading training fit for generalization, framed by the gap between training and validation loss.","train-test-leakage":"opens evaluation with the rule that a held-out set has to simulate the future, or every metric after it lies.","confusion-matrix":"the raw table of counts that every classification metric after it is derived from.","precision-recall-f1":"the detection metrics read from the table, and how the threshold trades one against the other.","roc-auc":"scores the ranking across every threshold, which only makes sense once metrics at a single threshold are clear.","class-imbalance":"uses the table, precision and recall, and AUC to show how defaults fail on rare positives and how to fix the threshold first.","calibration":"ends the module by turning scores into trustworthy probabilities for cost-based thresholds, abstention and cascades, repairing what imbalance fixes distort."},"capstone":{"brief":"A support team wants an \"escalate to a senior agent\" classifier for incoming tickets. About 4% of\ntickets need escalating. A missed escalation costs roughly twenty times as much as an unnecessary one,\nand tickets the model is unsure about should go to a human review queue. Use a public support or\ncomplaint dataset, or a synthetic one with realistic customer IDs and timestamps. Start by embedding and\nclustering the tickets to understand what they contain, and use the clusters to draw a stratified sample\nfor labelling or to check the existing labels. Train a TF-IDF logistic regression baseline. Then\nfine-tune a small pre-trained encoder with a training loop you write yourself. Use AdamW, warmup\nfollowed by cosine decay, global-norm gradient clipping, bf16 autocast and gradient accumulation to\nreach a target effective batch, and log loss, learning rate, gradient norm before clipping, and the\nfraction of steps that were clipped. Run one ablation (no warmup, no clipping, or a learning rate ten\ntimes higher) and explain how the curves change. Split the data by customer and by time, and measure\nhow much a naive random split inflates the scores. Evaluate with a confusion matrix, precision–recall\nand ROC curves, and a reliability diagram. Compare class weighting with plain threshold tuning. Apply\ntemperature scaling, then derive the escalation threshold and the review band from the cost ratio, not\nfrom 0.5.","deliverables":["A reproducible training script or notebook containing the baseline and the fine-tuned encoder. Its config names peak learning rate, warmup steps, schedule, clip norm, precision, micro-batch, accumulation steps and effective batch. Logged curves show loss, learning rate, gradient norm before clipping and the fraction of clipped steps, and there is a one-paragraph explanation of the ablation run.","A leakage audit giving metrics under a random split, a customer-group split and a time split, with exact and near-duplicate checks across splits and a list of every fitted preprocessing step and where it is fitted.","An evaluation report with the confusion matrix at the shipped threshold, the precision–recall curve with average precision, ROC-AUC with a bootstrap confidence interval, and per-cluster recall, for both the baseline and the fine-tuned model, with and without class weighting.","A calibration and decision note: a reliability diagram, ECE and Brier score before and after temperature scaling on a split the calibrator never saw, the threshold derived from the 20:1 cost ratio, the review band, and the expected escalations, misses and review-queue volume per thousand tickets at production prevalence.","A short cluster summary that names the main ticket groups found by clustering, gives their sizes, and states which labelling or evaluation-sampling decision each one changed."],"rubric":["The effective batch equals micro-batch × accumulation steps × devices and is stated explicitly. Warmup and total steps are counted in optimizer steps. Printed learning rates at step 0, end of warmup, midpoint and end match the plan, and the ablation's change in its curves is explained by the right mechanism (early Adam variance, exploding gradient norm, noise floor).","The reported headline metric comes from the group or time split, not the random split, the gap between the two is quantified, and no scaler, vectorizer, calibrator or threshold was fitted on data that the final evaluation scores.","Accuracy is not used as the headline number. The confusion matrix, precision and recall at the shipped threshold, and average precision are reported together with prevalence and support, and precision measured on any enriched or rebalanced set is converted to production prevalence.","The threshold is derived from the cost ratio using calibrated probabilities (close to C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN), about 0.05). Calibration error is lower after temperature scaling while ROC-AUC stays the same, and the note shows the expected cost at the chosen threshold compared with the cost at 0.5.","The class-weighting comparison reports its effect on average precision and on calibration separately, and the recommendation states whether weighting is worth keeping once threshold tuning and recalibration are in place."]}},"topics":[{"id":"supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","key":"ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Supervised vs unsupervised vs self-supervised learning","taxonomy_name":"supervised vs unsupervised vs self-supervised learning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/clustering","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-labeling","rag-retrieval/embedding-models"],"tldr":"The three paradigms differ in one thing: where the training signal comes from. Supervised learning takes\neach label from outside the input, usually a paid human. Unsupervised learning has inputs only and\noptimises a property of the data, such as how tightly points group. Self-supervised learning\nmanufactures labels from the input (the next token, the masked word, the matching caption), so the\nsignal is free and unlimited. Modern LLMs exist because self-supervision turned the internet into\ntraining labels, with human supervision added at the end.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"classification","key":"ml-fundamentals/classification","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Classification","taxonomy_name":"classification","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","model-internals/softmax"],"tldr":"Classification predicts which of a fixed set of categories an input belongs to. A classifier produces a\nscore per class (logits), converts scores to probabilities (sigmoid or softmax), and a separate decision\nrule, a threshold or argmax, turns probabilities into a label. Keeping scoring and deciding apart matters\nbecause the threshold encodes the business cost of each kind of mistake. Every token an LLM emits is a\nclassification over its vocabulary, so this is the most common problem shape in AI engineering.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"regression","key":"ml-fundamentals/regression","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Regression","taxonomy_name":"regression","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/overfitting","python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization"],"tldr":"Regression predicts a continuous quantity: latency in milliseconds, cost in dollars, a quality score.\nThe model outputs a number and the loss measures its distance from the truth; choosing squared versus\nabsolute error is choosing whether you estimate the conditional mean or the median, and how much a few\nextreme points steer the fit. In AI systems regression appears as reward models, latency and cost\npredictors, judge scores and value heads, and it fails in the same ways least squares always has:\nextrapolation, leverage points and leakage.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"clustering","key":"ml-fundamentals/clustering","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Clustering","taxonomy_name":"clustering","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","ml-fundamentals/classification","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","training-adaptation/data-curation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling"],"tldr":"Clustering groups items by similarity without labels. It answers \"what kinds of things are in this\ndata?\" when nobody has defined the kinds yet. In AI engineering it mostly runs on embeddings: discovering\nintents in chat logs, deduplicating training data, choosing diverse evaluation samples, finding failure\nmodes. The algorithm matters less than two choices made before it runs, the distance metric and the\nquestion you want answered; k-means, hierarchical and density-based methods differ mainly in what cluster\nshapes they assume.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"loss-functions","key":"ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Loss functions (cross-entropy, contrastive)","taxonomy_name":"loss functions (cross-entropy, contrastive)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/perplexity","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","multimodal/clip"],"tldr":"A loss function is the differentiable stand-in for what you actually want; its gradient is the only\nsignal the model ever receives. Cross-entropy, −log p(correct answer), is the loss for anything that\npredicts a probability distribution, including every token an LLM emits, because it is maximum\nlikelihood and its gradient through softmax is simply prediction minus target. Contrastive losses teach\nembeddings by asking \"which candidate is the match\", and the modern form, InfoNCE, is cross-entropy over\nsimilarity scores.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-descent","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient descent","taxonomy_name":"gradient descent","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/warmup","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/overfitting"],"tldr":"Gradient descent minimises a loss by repeatedly moving the parameters a small step against the\ngradient: θ ← θ − η∇L(θ). It is the only practical way to fit models with millions to billions of\nparameters, because the gradient costs a small constant multiple of a forward pass regardless of\nparameter count.\nStochastic (mini-batch) gradient descent uses a noisy estimate from a few examples, trading precision per\nstep for many more steps per second, and that noise turns out to help rather than hurt.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpropagation","key":"ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Backpropagation","taxonomy_name":"backpropagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/normalization","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","python-for-ai/pytorch"],"tldr":"Backpropagation computes the gradient of a scalar loss with respect to every parameter in one backward\nsweep, by applying the chain rule from the loss back through the recorded computation graph. It costs a\nsmall constant multiple of the forward pass no matter how many parameters there are, which is the only\nreason training billion-parameter models is possible. The price is memory: the backward pass needs the\nforward activations, so training memory scales with batch × sequence length × depth, not just with\nparameter count.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"optimizers","key":"ml-fundamentals/optimizers","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Optimizers (Adam, AdamW)","taxonomy_name":"optimizers (Adam, AdamW)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/peft"],"tldr":"An optimizer turns gradients into parameter updates. Plain SGD applies one learning rate to every\nparameter, which fails when gradient scales differ by orders of magnitude across a network. Adam keeps a\nrunning mean (momentum) and a running mean square of each parameter's gradient and divides one by the\nroot of the other, so every parameter moves by roughly the learning rate whatever its gradient scale.\nAdamW applies weight decay directly to the weights instead of through the gradient, and is the default\nfor transformers.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"learning-rate-schedulers","key":"ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Learning-rate schedulers","taxonomy_name":"learning-rate schedulers","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/checkpointing"],"tldr":"A fixed learning rate is a compromise: large enough to make progress early, it is too noisy to settle\ninto a good minimum late. A scheduler changes the learning rate over the run, ramping up briefly, holding\nor slowly decaying, then annealing down, so the optimizer takes big steps while the loss surface is\nforgiving and small ones when it is not. The schedule is defined over total optimizer steps, so it must be\nplanned together with batch size and training length.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"warmup","key":"ml-fundamentals/warmup","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Warmup","taxonomy_name":"warmup","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","training-adaptation/learning-rate"],"tldr":"Warmup ramps the learning rate from near zero to its peak over the first few hundred or thousand\noptimizer steps. It exists because the first steps are the most dangerous: weights are random, gradients\nare large and poorly conditioned, and Adam's running variance estimates are built from almost no data,\nso a full-size step can throw the model into a region it never recovers from. A short ramp costs almost\nnothing and removes most early-training divergence.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-clipping","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient clipping","taxonomy_name":"gradient clipping","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Gradient clipping caps the size of the update computed from one batch: if the global L2 norm of all\ngradients exceeds a threshold, every gradient is scaled down by the same factor so the norm equals the\nthreshold. The direction is preserved; only the step length is bounded. It exists because a single rare\nbatch can produce a gradient tens of times larger than usual, and one such step can undo thousands of\ngood ones or corrupt the optimizer's running statistics for the rest of the run.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"batch-size","key":"ml-fundamentals/batch-size","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Batch size","taxonomy_name":"batch size","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Batch size is how many examples (for LLMs, how many tokens) contribute to one gradient estimate. A\nlarger batch gives a less noisy gradient and better hardware utilization, so each step is worth more and\ncosts less per example, but only up to a point: past the *critical batch size* set by gradient noise,\nextra examples per step stop reducing the number of steps needed and simply burn data. Batch size,\nlearning rate and step count are therefore one coupled decision, not three.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-accumulation","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient accumulation","taxonomy_name":"gradient accumulation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Gradient accumulation runs several small micro-batches and lets their gradients add up before one\noptimizer step. Because the gradient of a mean loss is linear in the examples, the sum equals the\ngradient of the larger batch, so the optimizer sees the batch the recipe asked for while GPU memory only\never holds one micro-batch of activations. It costs no memory and changes nothing in the optimization,\nbut it buys no speed: every micro-batch still runs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mixed-precision","key":"ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Mixed precision","taxonomy_name":"mixed precision","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","training-adaptation/quantization","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Mixed precision runs the expensive parts of training (the matrix multiplications) in a 16-bit float while\nkeeping an fp32 copy of the weights for the update. Tensor cores are many times faster at 16 bits,\nactivations take half the memory and bandwidth, and the fp32 master copy stops tiny updates from being\nrounded away. fp16 has range problems that need loss scaling; bf16 has fp32's range and skips that\nmachinery at a precision cost, so it is the default where hardware allows.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"normalization","key":"ml-fundamentals/normalization","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Normalization (LayerNorm, RMSNorm)","taxonomy_name":"normalization (LayerNorm, RMSNorm)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/self-attention"],"tldr":"Normalization layers rescale each token's activation vector to a fixed scale before it enters a sublayer,\nso what attention and the MLP see does not depend on what every earlier layer happened to do. LayerNorm\nsubtracts the mean and divides by the standard deviation over the feature dimension; RMSNorm skips the\nmean and divides by the root mean square, which is cheaper and works as well. Where the norm sits\nrelative to the residual (Pre-LN versus Post-LN) decides how stable training is.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"residual-connections","key":"ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Residual connections","taxonomy_name":"residual connections","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/warmup","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/self-attention","training-adaptation/peft"],"tldr":"A residual connection adds a layer's input to its output, `y = x + F(x)`, so each layer learns a\ncorrection to the signal rather than a replacement for it. It exists because plain deep stacks get harder\nto optimize as depth grows: the identity path gives gradients a route that products of layer Jacobians\ncannot shrink, and makes \"do nothing\" the starting point for every layer. In a transformer this shared\npath is the residual stream that every attention head and MLP reads and writes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dropout-regularization","key":"ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Dropout / regularization","taxonomy_name":"dropout / regularization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split"],"tldr":"Regularization deliberately makes a model fit its training data *worse* so it generalizes *better*:\na penalty on weight size, noise injected during training, or stopping early. Dropout is the noise\nversion: during training each activation is zeroed with probability *p* and the survivors are scaled\nby 1/(1−p), so no unit can rely on another being present; at inference nothing is dropped. Whether\nyou want it depends on the data regime: small fine-tuning sets, yes; single-epoch pretraining on\ntrillions of tokens, usually no.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"train-test-leakage","key":"ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Train/test leakage","taxonomy_name":"train/test leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/overfitting","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/golden-dataset","data-architecture/feature-stores","data-architecture/data-lineage","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes"],"tldr":"Leakage is any path by which information that will not be available at prediction time, or that\nbelongs to the evaluation set, reaches the model or the metric. It makes offline numbers lie in the\noptimistic direction, which is the worst direction: you ship something that collapses in production.\nIt rarely looks like a bug. It looks like a feature that is \"surprisingly predictive\", a scaler fitted\nbefore the split, a random split of time-ordered data, or a golden set the prompt was tuned against.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"class-imbalance","key":"ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Class imbalance","taxonomy_name":"class imbalance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"When one class is rare, the default recipe of accuracy as the metric, an unweighted loss and a 0.5\nthreshold quietly optimises for \"say the common class\". Imbalance is less a data problem than a cost\nproblem that the defaults get wrong. Fix the evaluation first (confusion matrix, precision–recall,\nstratified splits), then the decision threshold, and only then reach for class weights, resampling or\nfocal loss, remembering that each of those distorts the probabilities you may need later.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"calibration","key":"ml-fundamentals/calibration","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Calibration","taxonomy_name":"calibration","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","system-design/model-routing-cascading","agents/human-in-the-loop"],"tldr":"A model is calibrated when its stated confidence matches its hit rate: of everything it calls \"90%\",\nabout 90% should be right. Ranking metrics like AUC do not need this; decisions do: cost-based\nthresholds, abstaining, escalating to a human or a bigger model, combining scores. Modern neural\nnetworks are usually overconfident, so you measure calibration on held-out data (reliability diagram,\nECE, Brier score) and fix it after training, most often with temperature scaling.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"precision-recall-f1","key":"ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Precision / recall / F1","taxonomy_name":"precision / recall / F1","kind":"metric","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/hallucination-evaluation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails"],"tldr":"Precision asks \"of what I flagged, how much was right\"; recall asks \"of what was there, how much did I\nfind\". They pull against each other through the decision threshold, so a scorer is a curve of\n(precision, recall) pairs, not a point. F1 is the harmonic mean at one chosen point: a single number\nthat is only meaningful once you have decided the two error types cost about the same, which in\nproduction they rarely do.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"roc-auc","key":"ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"ROC-AUC","taxonomy_name":"ROC-AUC","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/model-evaluation","evals/llm-as-a-judge","rag-retrieval/reranking","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"ROC-AUC measures how well a scorer *ranks* positives above negatives across every threshold at once:\nit equals the probability that a randomly chosen positive outscores a randomly chosen negative. It is\nthreshold-free and unaffected by class prevalence, which makes it the right number for comparing\nscorers and the wrong number for judging a deployed classifier on rare positives, where it hides the\nfalse-alarm volume and says nothing about whether the scores are calibrated probabilities.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"confusion-matrix","key":"ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Confusion matrix","taxonomy_name":"confusion matrix","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/error-analysis","evals/llm-as-a-judge","safety-security/content-moderation"],"tldr":"A confusion matrix is the raw count of what the classifier said against what was true, one cell per\n(actual, predicted) pair. Every single-number classification metric is a ratio of its cells, so any one\nnumber throws away what the table shows: which class is mistaken for which, and in which direction.\nRead the table first and choose the metric second, because the right metric depends on which cell's\nerrors are expensive, and that is a product decision.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"model-internals","tier":1,"order":1,"title":"Model internals","intro":{"overview":"This module opens up the large language model and follows a token all the way through it. It starts\nwith the transformer block and the attention mechanism at its centre: self-attention as the only\noperation that moves information between positions, multi-head attention as several lookups in\nparallel, and grouped-query attention and FlashAttention as the two fixes for attention's memory cost.\nPositional encoding, tokenization and embeddings explain how text becomes ordered vectors in the first\nplace, and llm-architecture explains why one causal decoder stack won over encoder–decoder designs.\nThe serving half follows from the same shapes: the context window and the KV cache set how much a\nrequest can hold and how many requests a GPU can serve, logits and softmax are the raw output and its\nnormalisation, sampling strategies and speculative decoding turn that output into text quickly and\npredictably. The final group covers the model as a whole: mixture-of-experts and small language models\nas two answers to the cost of parameters, scaling laws as the rule that links size, data and compute,\nperplexity as the native quality metric, hallucination as a consequence of the training objective, and\nreasoning models as the move from bigger weights to more inference compute. Afterwards you can read a\nmodel config and derive parameters, FLOPs per token and KV-cache bytes; explain latency, cost and odd\nbehaviours from first principles; choose decoding settings from a task's error model; and pick or\nreject a model for a deployment with numbers instead of benchmark tables. It matters most for AI\nEngineers, and it is the shared vocabulary Architects and FDEs need to defend model and hardware choices.","why":{"engineer":"You will tune temperature and top-p, budget prompts with the real tokenizer, read logprobs, enable prefix caching and speculative decoding, and debug truncation, NaNs and repetition loops, and each of these is only predictable once you know the logits-to-sampler path and what the KV cache holds.","architect":"Concurrency per GPU, cost per request and the dense-versus-MoE-versus-SLM decision all follow from head counts, KV-cache bytes per token, active versus total parameters and the 2N-FLOPs-per-token rule, so capacity plans and model choices are arithmetic you can check rather than vendor claims.","fde":"Customers ask why their language costs three times more tokens, why the model invents facts, whether their older GPUs can run long context, and whether a small fine-tuned model can replace the API; you need first-principles answers and a quick measurement on their own data."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: text goes through a pipeline of tensors with fixed shapes, and every cost and\nbehaviour can be traced to one stage of it. The tokenizer turns characters into ids, which sets the\ntoken bill. Embeddings turn ids into vectors, positional encoding stamps order onto them, and a stack of\nidentical blocks alternates attention (positions exchange information, cost n² in length, cache grows\nwith length times KV heads) with a feed-forward network (each position transformed alone, cost tracks\nparameters). The last hidden state becomes logits, softmax turns them into a distribution, and a\nsampler picks one token, which is appended and fed back. Prefill is that pipeline run once over the\nprompt and limited by compute; decode is the loop, limited by memory bandwidth. When something is slow,\nexpensive or wrong, ask which stage and which shape is responsible: token count, sequence length, KV\nheads, active parameters, the logit vector or the sampler. Hallucination and reasoning sit at the end\nof the loop: the objective rewards plausible continuations, and more loop iterations buy more compute.","order":["tokenization","embeddings","attention-mechanism","softmax","self-attention","multi-head-attention","positional-encoding","transformer","llm-architecture","logits","sampling-strategies","context-window","kv-cache","grouped-query-attention","flashattention","speculative-decoding","perplexity","scaling-laws","mixture-of-experts","small-language-models","hallucination","reasoning-models-test-time-compute"],"order_reasons":{"tokenization":"text must become token ids before anything else happens, and tokens are the unit of cost and context used everywhere after.","embeddings":"turns token ids into the vectors the network computes on, and separates the LLM's embedding table from embedding models.","attention-mechanism":"introduces the query, key and value lookup that every later attention topic refines.","softmax":"the normalisation inside every attention head and at the output, needed before scaling, masking and sampling make sense.","self-attention":"applies attention within one sequence, adds masking and the O(n²) cost that drives the serving topics.","multi-head-attention":"splits the width into parallel heads and introduces the head counts read from every model config.","positional-encoding":"supplies the order signal self-attention lacks and explains RoPE and context extension.","transformer":"assembles attention, the feed-forward network, residuals and normalisation into the block and derives 2N FLOPs per token.","llm-architecture":"compares encoder, decoder and encoder–decoder stacks and explains why causal decoders dominate chat models.","logits":"the raw per-vocabulary scores the stack outputs, where logit bias, constrained decoding and logprobs act.","sampling-strategies":"turns the softmaxed logits into tokens with greedy, beam, temperature, top-k, top-p and min-p.","context-window":"the budget every request shares, constrained by attention cost, cache size and trained positions.","kv-cache":"makes decoding incremental and turns sequence length into GPU memory, the limit on concurrency.","grouped-query-attention":"shrinks the KV cache by sharing key/value heads, which only lands after the cache formula.","flashattention":"removes the n² memory traffic with tiling and online softmax, building on self-attention and softmax.","speculative-decoding":"uses idle compute during bandwidth-bound decode, relying on logits, sampling and the cache.","perplexity":"the native fit metric computed from logits, needed before comparing models, quantisation and scale.","scaling-laws":"links parameters, tokens and compute to loss, and frames over-training and inference cost.","mixture-of-experts":"separates active from total parameters, building on the FFN and on scaling arguments.","small-language-models":"applies scaling and distillation results to narrow, cheap and private deployments.","hallucination":"explains fabrication from the objective, decoding and incentives, using logits, sampling and calibration.","reasoning-models-test-time-compute":"closes the module by spending more decode tokens instead of more parameters, tying cost back to the loop."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer wants to move a multilingual support-ticket triage and reply-drafting workload off a\nfrontier API onto self-hosted open weights, on a node with two 48 GB GPUs, and asks you to recommend a\nmodel and serving configuration with evidence. Shortlist three open models: one dense mid-size model,\none mixture-of-experts model and one small language model of 8B parameters or fewer. From each model's\nconfig alone, derive parameter count, active parameters, FLOPs per token and KV-cache bytes per token,\nthen compute the maximum concurrent requests at the customer's real prompt and output lengths. Measure\ntokens per character for each tokenizer on a sample of the customer's tickets in at least three\nlanguages. Serve at least two candidates with an inference engine such as vLLM or llama.cpp and measure\nthroughput and time per output token at several concurrency levels, with and without prefix caching\nand speculative decoding. Compare a quantised build against the original with perplexity on held-out\ntickets. Run a small grid of sampling settings for the classification step and the drafting step, and\nmeasure hallucination on drafts as unsupported claims against the ticket and knowledge-base article.\nFinally, test whether a reasoning mode or best-of-n voting improves the hardest triage cases enough to\njustify its token cost, and route only those cases to it.","deliverables":["A config-derived capacity sheet (script or notebook) that computes parameters, active parameters, 2N FLOPs per token, KV-cache bytes per token and maximum concurrency at the target context for each candidate, checked against the engine's reported memory.","A tokenizer cost table giving tokens per character and estimated monthly token volume per language for each candidate, measured on the customer's own ticket sample with each model's real tokenizer.","A benchmark report of throughput, p50 and p95 time per output token and KV-cache utilisation across concurrency levels, with prefix caching and speculative decoding toggled, including the speculative acceptance rate.","An evaluation set of 50+ labelled tickets with triage labels and reference facts, plus results for the sampling grid, the quantised-versus-original perplexity check, the unsupported-claim rate on drafts and the accuracy-versus-tokens curve for the reasoning or best-of-n escalation.","A two-page recommendation naming the model, quantisation, decoding settings per step, context limit and routing rule, with cost per resolved ticket compared to the current API."],"rubric":["Every capacity number traces to named config fields (`hidden_size`, `num_hidden_layers`, `num_key_value_heads`, `head_dim`, expert counts) through a written formula, and predicted KV-cache memory agrees with the engine's measurement within 15 percent.","Benchmarks are run at production-like concurrency and prompt lengths, and the report explains each result with the right mechanism (bandwidth-bound decode, cache pressure, preemptions, low draft acceptance) rather than only listing numbers.","Sampling settings differ between triage and drafting and are justified by measured accuracy and format-failure rate from the grid, not copied defaults.","Hallucination is reported as a claim-level unsupported rate on the labelled set with at least five traced examples, and the recommendation includes a concrete mitigation such as grounding, abstention or verification, with its measured effect.","The escalation rule is backed by an accuracy-versus-tokens curve showing the chosen budget sits at the knee, and the recommendation states which tickets are routed to it and what that adds to cost per ticket."]}},"topics":[{"id":"transformer","key":"model-internals/transformer","module":"model-internals","name":"Transformer","taxonomy_name":"transformer","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/llm-architecture","model-internals/embeddings","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/normalization"],"tldr":"A transformer is a stack of identical blocks. Each block first lets every position pull information from\nother positions through attention, then transforms each position on its own with a feed-forward network,\nwith residual connections and normalization holding the stack together. It replaced recurrence because\nall positions of a sequence are processed in parallel as one matrix multiplication and any two positions\nare a single attention step apart, which made training on web-scale text affordable. Nearly every\ncurrent LLM is a decoder-only variant of this design.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"attention-mechanism","key":"model-internals/attention-mechanism","module":"model-internals","name":"Attention mechanism","taxonomy_name":"attention mechanism","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search"],"tldr":"Attention is a differentiable lookup. A query is compared against a set of keys, the similarity scores\nare turned into weights that sum to one with a softmax, and the output is the weighted sum of the\nmatching values. It exists because squeezing a variable-length input into one fixed-size vector throws\ninformation away; attention lets each step of a computation reach back and pull exactly the parts of\nthe input it needs, with the notion of \"matching\" learned end to end.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"self-attention","key":"model-internals/self-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Self-attention","taxonomy_name":"self-attention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode"],"tldr":"Self-attention is attention in which queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence: every\ntoken asks every other token it is allowed to see for information and builds its new representation\nfrom the answers. It is the only operation in a transformer that moves information between positions,\nit runs as one matrix multiplication over all positions at once, and it costs O(n²) in sequence length,\nwhich is where context limits, the KV cache and FlashAttention all come from.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-head-attention","key":"model-internals/multi-head-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Multi-head attention","taxonomy_name":"multi-head attention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/transformer","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference"],"tldr":"Multi-head attention runs several attention operations in parallel, each in its own lower-dimensional\nprojection of the input, and concatenates the results. One softmax can express only one weighting over\nthe sequence per position; with *h* heads a position can look in *h* places for *h* different reasons at\nonce. Because the model width is split across heads (d_k = d_model / h), the parameter count and FLOPs\nmatch a single wide head, while the attention-probability memory and the natural unit for sharding both\nscale with *h*.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"grouped-query-attention","key":"model-internals/grouped-query-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Grouped-query attention (GQA)","taxonomy_name":"grouped-query attention (GQA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/llm-architecture","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/tpot","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Grouped-query attention lets several query heads share one key/value head, a middle point between full\nmulti-head attention (one KV head per query head) and multi-query attention (one for all). It exists\nbecause decoding is bound by reading the KV cache from GPU memory, and that cache scales with the number\nof KV heads: going from 32 to 8 shrinks it fourfold at a quality cost small enough that Llama 2 70B,\nLlama 3 and Mistral all adopted it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"flashattention","key":"model-internals/flashattention","module":"model-internals","name":"FlashAttention","taxonomy_name":"FlashAttention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","python-for-ai/pytorch"],"tldr":"FlashAttention computes exactly the same softmax attention as the textbook formula but never writes the\nn×n score matrix to GPU memory. It streams blocks of keys and values through the fast on-chip SRAM,\nkeeps a running softmax per query row, and stores only O(n) extra state. Attention was slow not because\nof its FLOPs but because of reading and writing that n² matrix in high-bandwidth memory, so an IO-aware\nkernel gives large wall-clock speedups and makes long contexts fit in memory at all.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"positional-encoding","key":"model-internals/positional-encoding","module":"model-internals","name":"Positional encoding (RoPE)","taxonomy_name":"positional encoding (RoPE)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/context-window","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/transformer","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Self-attention treats its input as a set, so a transformer needs an explicit signal for token order.\nEarly models added a sinusoid or learned vector per absolute position to the embeddings. RoPE instead\nrotates each query and key by an angle proportional to its position, so their dot product depends only\non their distance apart. That relative property, zero parameters and clean interaction with the KV cache\nmade RoPE the default in open LLMs, and context extension is mostly about stretching those angles.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tokenization","key":"model-internals/tokenization","module":"model-internals","name":"Tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece)","taxonomy_name":"tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/embeddings","model-internals/context-window","model-internals/logits","python-for-ai/tiktoken","observability/token-cost-tracking","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies"],"tldr":"A tokenizer turns text into the integer ids a model consumes. Byte-pair encoding, the dominant method,\nbuilds a vocabulary of frequent subword chunks by repeatedly merging the most common adjacent pair, so\ncommon words become one token and rare words split into pieces without ever being out of vocabulary.\nSentencePiece is a toolkit that trains BPE or unigram models on raw text with whitespace as an ordinary\nsymbol, making it language-independent. Tokens are the unit of cost, context and many odd model\nbehaviours.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"embeddings","key":"model-internals/embeddings","module":"model-internals","name":"Embeddings","taxonomy_name":"embeddings","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/logits","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"An embedding is a learned dense vector that stands in for a discrete thing (a token, a sentence) so a\nnetwork can do arithmetic on it. Inside an LLM, the embedding table turns token ids into the vectors\nthe transformer consumes, and a matrix of the same shape (sometimes the very same one) turns hidden\nstates back into logits. Outside the LLM, an\nembedding *model* compresses whole passages into vectors whose distance tracks meaning, which is what\nmakes semantic search possible. Same word, two different training objectives.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"llm-architecture","key":"model-internals/llm-architecture","module":"model-internals","name":"LLM architecture (encoder–decoder vs decoder-only)","taxonomy_name":"LLM architecture (encoder–decoder vs decoder-only)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/transformer","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/context-window","training-adaptation/pre-training","rag-retrieval/reranking","multimodal/speech-to-text"],"tldr":"The original transformer had two stacks: an encoder that reads the input bidirectionally and a decoder\nthat writes the output token by token while cross-attending to the encoder. Modern chat LLMs keep only\nthe decoder and treat prompt and answer as one causal stream, because one stack with one next-token\nobjective scales on raw text, handles multi-turn chat by appending, and reuses its KV cache.\nEncoder–decoder still wins for fixed, read-heavy inputs with short outputs, such as translation and\nspeech recognition.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-window","key":"model-internals/context-window","module":"model-internals","name":"Context window","taxonomy_name":"context window","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/self-attention","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle"],"tldr":"The context window is the maximum number of tokens a model can attend over in one request: system\nprompt, conversation history, retrieved documents, tool results and the answer all share it. It is\nfinite because attention cost grows with length, the KV cache grows with length, and positional\nencodings are only trained up to some length. The model has no memory beyond it, so everything the\nmodel \"knows\" about this conversation has to fit, and managing that budget is a core engineering job.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache","key":"model-internals/kv-cache","module":"model-internals","name":"KV cache","taxonomy_name":"KV cache","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/prefix-caching"],"tldr":"A decoder-only transformer generates one token at a time, and every new token must attend to every\nearlier token. The KV cache stores the key and value vectors already computed for earlier positions so\neach step only computes attention for the new token instead of recomputing the whole prefix. Each\nposition is projected through the network once instead of once per later step, at the cost of GPU memory\nthat grows with sequence length, which is why memory, not compute, usually limits how many requests a\nserver can run.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"logits","key":"model-internals/logits","module":"model-internals","name":"Logits","taxonomy_name":"logits","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/softmax","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/embeddings","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/calibration","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"Logits are the raw, unnormalized scores a language model produces for every vocabulary entry at a\nposition, one real number per token, before softmax turns them into probabilities. They are the model's\nlast honest output: everything you can do to steer generation without retraining (temperature, logit\nbias, banning tokens, grammar-constrained decoding) is an edit to this vector, and everything you can\nmeasure about confidence (logprobs, perplexity) is read from it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"softmax","key":"model-internals/softmax","module":"model-internals","name":"Softmax","taxonomy_name":"softmax","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/logits","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/flashattention","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/classification"],"tldr":"Softmax turns a vector of arbitrary real scores into a probability distribution:\n`p_i = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)`. It is differentiable, order-preserving, invariant to shifting\nall scores, and its sharpness is controlled by the temperature *T*. An LLM uses it twice: inside every\nattention head to weight positions, and at the output to turn logits into next-token probabilities.\nIts properties, including \"never exactly zero\" and \"must sum to one\", explain sampling behaviour,\nattention sinks and several numerical bugs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sampling-strategies","key":"model-internals/sampling-strategies","module":"model-internals","name":"Sampling strategies (temperature, top-k / top-p, greedy vs beam)","taxonomy_name":"sampling strategies (temperature, top-k / top-p, greedy vs beam)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/logits","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/perplexity","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"A language model emits a probability distribution over the whole vocabulary at each step; a sampling\nstrategy decides which token to take. Greedy always takes the most likely token and tends to loop; beam\nsearch keeps several candidate sequences and maximizes total likelihood, which suits translation but\nproduces bland, repetitive open-ended text. Sampling draws from the distribution after reshaping it with\ntemperature and truncating the tail with top-k or top-p, trading determinism for diversity. The right\nsetting depends on whether the task has one correct answer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"speculative-decoding","key":"model-internals/speculative-decoding","module":"model-internals","name":"Speculative decoding","taxonomy_name":"speculative decoding","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/logits","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/inference-optimization","serving-inference/tpot","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Decoding is memory-bandwidth bound: each step reads all the weights to produce one token while the\nGPU's arithmetic units sit mostly idle. Speculative decoding uses that idle compute: a cheap draft model\nguesses several tokens ahead and the large model verifies all of them in one forward pass. A\nrejection-sampling rule keeps the output distribution exactly the large model's, so it is a pure\nlatency optimization whose gain depends on how often the draft is right.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mixture-of-experts","key":"model-internals/mixture-of-experts","module":"model-internals","name":"Mixture of experts (MoE)","taxonomy_name":"mixture of experts (MoE)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/transformer","model-internals/llm-architecture","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"A mixture-of-experts transformer replaces each layer's dense feed-forward block with several parallel\n\"expert\" FFNs and a small router that sends each token to one or two of them. Compute per token tracks the\nactive parameters, while quality lands well above a dense model of that active size, though usually below\na dense model of the full size. The price: every expert must sit in memory, routing must be kept balanced,\nand serving economics shift with batch size.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"small-language-models","key":"model-internals/small-language-models","module":"model-internals","name":"Small language models (SLMs)","taxonomy_name":"small language models (SLMs)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/kv-cache","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","system-design/model-routing-cascading","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/quantization","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment"],"tldr":"\"Small language model\" has no fixed boundary; in practice it means a model of roughly 0.5–10B parameters\nthat runs on one consumer GPU, a CPU or a phone. SLMs matter because most production tasks are narrow,\ncost and latency scale with parameter count, and modern recipes (training on far more tokens than\ncompute-optimal, distillation from large teachers, curated data) make small models far stronger than their\nsize once implied. They trade breadth of knowledge and multi-step reasoning for speed, cost, privacy and\ncheap fine-tuning.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"scaling-laws","key":"model-internals/scaling-laws","module":"model-internals","name":"Scaling laws","taxonomy_name":"scaling laws","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","model-internals/perplexity","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/data-curation","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"Scaling laws are empirical power-law fits that predict a language model's pre-training loss from its\nparameter count N, training tokens D and compute C. They exist because frontier runs cost too much to try\nblindly: fitting the curve on small runs tells you how to split a budget between model size and data.\nKaplan showed loss falls smoothly along each axis; Chinchilla corrected the split to about 20 tokens per\nparameter; practice has since moved to over-training smaller models because serving cost scales with N.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"perplexity","key":"model-internals/perplexity","module":"model-internals","name":"Perplexity","taxonomy_name":"perplexity","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/logits","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/scaling-laws","evals/model-evaluation","evals/dataset-contamination","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation"],"tldr":"Perplexity is the exponential of a language model's average per-token cross-entropy on a text:\n`exp(mean of −log p(token))`. It reads as the effective number of equally likely choices the model faces at\neach token, so lower is better. It measures the pre-training objective directly and is interpretable, which\nmakes it the standard tool for tracking training, comparing checkpoints, checking quantisation and filtering\ndata. It is tokenizer-dependent and says nothing about instruction-following or factual correctness.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hallucination","key":"model-internals/hallucination","module":"model-internals","name":"Hallucination","taxonomy_name":"hallucination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/small-language-models","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Hallucination is fluent, confident output that is not supported by the model's input or by reality. It is\na consequence of how language models work, not a bug awaiting a patch: they are trained to produce the\nmost probable continuation, not to know what they know, and sampling, long-tail knowledge gaps and rewards\nfor confident answers all push toward plausible fabrication. Manage it like any error rate: measure it,\nground the model in evidence, verify claims, and make abstention a valid output.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"reasoning-models-test-time-compute","key":"model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","module":"model-internals","name":"Reasoning models / test-time compute","taxonomy_name":"reasoning models / test-time compute","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["prompting/chain-of-thought","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/kv-cache","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","agents/reflection-self-critique","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"Test-time compute means spending more inference computation on a query to get a better answer: a longer\nchain of thought, several sampled attempts with a vote or verifier, or search over partial solutions.\nReasoning models are LLMs trained, mostly with reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks, to do this\ninternally in a long thinking trace before answering. A transformer does fixed computation per token, so\nhard problems need more tokens, and extra inference compute often buys more accuracy per dollar than a\nbigger model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"training-adaptation","tier":1,"order":2,"title":"Training & adaptation","intro":{"overview":"This module follows a model from random weights to a deployed, adapted artifact, and teaches you when\neach stage is worth paying for. It opens with the lifecycle: pre-training produces a base model that\nknows language but does not follow instructions, and post-training (supervised fine-tuning, instruction\ntuning, then preference and reward optimisation with RLHF, DPO and GRPO) installs the behaviour users\nactually see. Before any of that, the fine-tune-vs-RAG-vs-prompt decision asks whether your gap is\nknowledge, behaviour or economics, because only the last two are good reasons to touch weights. The data\ngroup (curation, labelling, synthetic generation and a split that follows the real unit of\ngeneralisation) is where most fine-tunes are won or lost. The training-dynamics group (learning rate,\nepochs, overfitting, catastrophic forgetting, checkpointing) explains how a small dataset quietly turns\ninto memorisation or a regression on general skills. The efficiency group (PEFT and QLoRA, gradient\ncheckpointing, tooling such as TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth, and distributed training) makes a run fit the\nhardware you have. The compression group (distillation, quantization, pruning, model merging) turns a\ngood model into one that is cheap to serve. Afterwards you can decide with evals whether to train at all,\nbuild and audit a fine-tuning dataset, run a LoRA SFT and a DPO pass on one GPU, catch overfitting and\nforgetting before release, and ship a quantised or merged model whose quality loss you have measured. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers who own a fine-tune end to end, and gives Architects and FDEs the numbers\nto approve or turn down one.","why":{"engineer":"You will format chat data through the right template, mask the loss to assistant turns, pick a learning rate and epoch count for a few thousand rows, attach LoRA to the right projections, read diverging train and validation curves, and resume a crashed run from the last good checkpoint without losing optimizer state.","architect":"Whether to fine-tune, retrieve or prompt sets cost per request, freshness, tenant isolation and the redo bill on every base-model upgrade, and memory per parameter, adapter-per-tenant serving, quantised weight bytes and distillation targets decide hardware, latency and the platform you commit to.","fde":"Customers arrive saying \"fine-tune it on our documents\"; you need to show with a quick eval that RAG fixes their knowledge gap, scope a small fine-tune for the format or cost problem that remains, audit their labels for agreement, and keep their private data inside their environment."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: training is a single dial, the gradient, applied to one shared set of weights, and\nevery lesson is about what you point it at, how hard you turn it, and what else moves. The data decides\nthe target: whatever is in the batch, including duplicates, label noise, leaked test items and\nsynthetic artefacts, is what the model becomes. The objective decides the kind of signal: next-token\nimitation for pre-training and SFT, comparisons for RLHF and DPO, verifiable scores for GRPO, a\nteacher's distribution for distillation. The learning rate, epochs and trainable capacity decide how far\nthe weights travel from the base, and distance is the hazard: too far on a small set means overfitting\nyour rows and forgetting everything else. The held-out splits are the only instruments that tell you\nwhere you landed. Memory and compute are the budget: PEFT, gradient checkpointing, sharding and\nquantisation are ways to afford the turn. Compression and merging act after training, trading measured\nquality for serving cost. Before any of it, ask whether the weights are the object that needs changing.","order":["pre-training","post-training","fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","data-curation","data-labeling","synthetic-data-generation","train-val-test-split","supervised-fine-tuning","instruction-tuning","learning-rate","epochs","overfitting","catastrophic-forgetting","peft","gradient-checkpointing","checkpointing","tooling","rlhf","dpo","grpo","knowledge-distillation","quantization","pruning","model-merging","distributed-training"],"order_reasons":{"pre-training":"establishes what a base model is, the next-token objective and the compute scale every later stage adapts.","post-training":"maps the SFT, preference and RL stages and why they run in that order, giving the module its backbone.","fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision":"decides whether training is the right lever at all before you spend anything on data or GPUs.","data-curation":"selection, deduplication and decontamination set the ceiling no hyperparameter can raise.","data-labeling":"turns raw inputs into targets with measured agreement, since the model learns the labelling function.","synthetic-data-generation":"fills gaps in real data and shows why verification filters decide its value.","train-val-test-split":"cuts by the unit the model must generalise across, which every later evaluation depends on.","supervised-fine-tuning":"the first and most predictable training step, with response-only loss on demonstrations.","instruction-tuning":"extends SFT to a diverse task mixture for zero-shot instruction following.","learning-rate":"the most sensitive knob in every run, including warmup, decay and fine-tuning ranges.","epochs":"how many passes a small set can bear, and why fine-tuning counts epochs while pre-training counts tokens.","overfitting":"reads diverging train and validation loss and applies early stopping and capacity limits.","catastrophic-forgetting":"the regression your task eval cannot see, and the defences of data mixing and general suites.","peft":"LoRA and QLoRA make fine-tuning fit one GPU and yield swappable adapters, limiting forgetting too.","gradient-checkpointing":"trades recomputation for activation memory so long-context fine-tunes fit.","checkpointing":"saves full training state for resume, rollback and best-checkpoint selection, distinct from the previous topic.","tooling":"TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth package the loop you now understand, with the details that are easy to get wrong.","rlhf":"reward models and PPO turn comparisons into a training signal, and introduce reward hacking and the KL anchor.","dpo":"removes the reward model and RL loop from RLHF, building directly on its objective.","grpo":"replaces PPO's critic with group-relative baselines for verifiable rewards, the reasoning-model recipe.","knowledge-distillation":"trains a small student on a teacher's outputs, the bridge from quality to serving cost.","quantization":"cuts weight bytes for bandwidth-bound decode, measured against the unquantised model.","pruning":"removes structure the model barely uses and repairs it, often with distillation.","model-merging":"combines fine-tunes of one base by weight arithmetic, needing PEFT and forgetting intuition first.","distributed-training":"closes with sharding and parallelism for runs that outgrow one GPU, vocabulary for most roles."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer's support team uses a frontier API to turn incoming tickets into a structured JSON triage\nrecord plus a short reply draft. It costs too much per ticket, the JSON breaks a few percent of the time,\nand they ask whether a fine-tuned open model can replace it. Start by building a held-out evaluation set\nsplit by customer account, then run the decision honestly: measure a prompted baseline and a RAG baseline\nover their help-centre articles on the same set, and name which gap remains (knowledge, format or cost).\nCurate a training set from their historical tickets: deduplicate, strip personal data, decontaminate\nagainst the eval set, double-label a sample to measure agreement, and add verified synthetic examples for\nrare categories. Train a QLoRA SFT adapter on a 3B to 8B open model with TRL, Axolotl or Unsloth,\nsweeping learning rate and epochs and keeping the best checkpoint on validation. Collect preference pairs\non reply drafts and run a DPO pass. Gate the result on a general regression suite to catch forgetting,\nthen quantise the merged model to 4-bit and confirm quality on the test set before recommending it.","deliverables":["A decision memo comparing prompt-only, RAG and fine-tuned variants on the same held-out set, stating which gap each fixes and the cost per 1,000 tickets of each option.","A versioned dataset with a datasheet recording sources, deduplication and decontamination counts, personal-data scrubbing, synthetic share and its verification filter, inter-annotator agreement on the double-labelled sample, and the account-level split.","A reproducible training config (Axolotl YAML or a TRL script) for the SFT and DPO runs, with loss curves, the learning-rate and epoch sweep, and the selected checkpoint saved with full optimizer state.","An evaluation report covering JSON validity, field-level triage accuracy, a judged reply-quality score, a general-capability regression suite run on base and fine-tuned models, and results before and after 4-bit quantisation.","A deployable artifact: the LoRA adapter and the merged, quantised weights, with a note on the serving setup and peak training and inference memory."],"rubric":["The split is by customer account, a check shows no near-duplicate between training and test rows, and the test set is scored once, after every hyperparameter and checkpoint choice was made on validation.","The fine-tune is justified only by a gap the prompt and RAG baselines leave open, and the memo shows that knowledge questions are still served by retrieval rather than baked into the weights.","Loss curves show where validation loss bottomed out, and the chosen checkpoint and epoch count are taken from that point rather than the last step.","The general regression suite shows the fine-tuned model within a stated tolerance of the base, or the report names the mitigation (data mixing, lower rank, fewer steps) and its measured effect.","DPO's effect is reported as a win rate on reply drafts against the SFT-only model, with the reference model and beta stated, and any increase in length or refusals is called out.","The quantised model's JSON validity and triage accuracy are reported next to the bf16 model's, and the recommendation states the accepted quality loss against the latency, memory and cost gained."]}},"topics":[{"id":"pre-training","key":"training-adaptation/pre-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Pre-training","taxonomy_name":"pre-training","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/distributed-training","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/perplexity","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"Pre-training turns a randomly initialised transformer into a model that knows language, facts and\nreasoning patterns by making it predict the next token over trillions of tokens of text. It is\nself-supervised because the text provides its own labels, which is why it can use the whole internet\ninstead of a hand-labelled set. It consumes almost all of the compute and produces a *base model* that\ncompletes text but does not yet follow instructions; every later stage adapts that base.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"post-training","key":"training-adaptation/post-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Post-training","taxonomy_name":"post-training","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"Post-training is everything done to a pre-trained base model to make it useful and safe: supervised\nfine-tuning on demonstrations, preference optimisation (RLHF, DPO) on comparisons, and increasingly\nreinforcement learning on verifiable rewards. It uses a tiny fraction of pre-training's compute but\ndecides almost everything a user experiences: whether the model answers, how it formats, what it\nrefuses, how it reasons and how it calls tools. The stages are ordered because each one needs the\nbehaviour the previous one installed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"supervised-fine-tuning","key":"training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)","taxonomy_name":"supervised fine-tuning (SFT)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision"],"tldr":"SFT continues training a pre-trained model on prompt-response demonstrations with the same next-token\nloss as pre-training, but computed only on the response tokens. It is the cheapest and most predictable\nway to install a format, a persona or a domain style, because the model is told exactly what to say.\nIts limit is that it can only imitate: it cannot express \"this answer is better than that one\", which\nis why preference methods follow it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"instruction-tuning","key":"training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Instruction tuning","taxonomy_name":"instruction tuning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/data-curation","prompting/zero-shot","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought"],"tldr":"Instruction tuning is SFT with a particular data design: many different tasks, each written as a\nnatural-language instruction with its answer, so the model learns the general skill \"read an\ninstruction, do what it says\" rather than any one task. The payoff is zero-shot generalisation to\ninstructions it never saw. Task diversity, not examples per task, is what drives it, which is why\nbuilding the mixture is most of the work.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rlhf","key":"training-adaptation/rlhf","module":"training-adaptation","name":"RLHF","taxonomy_name":"RLHF","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/data-labeling","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/llm-as-a-judge"],"tldr":"RLHF turns human judgements of \"which answer is better\" into a trained reward model, then uses\nreinforcement learning (usually PPO) to push the language model toward responses that score highly,\nwhile a KL penalty keeps it close to the supervised starting point. It exists because quality is easier\nto compare than to write down, and because a learned reward can be optimised far beyond the examples\npeople could author. Its central hazard is that the policy learns to exploit the reward model's errors.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dpo","key":"training-adaptation/dpo","module":"training-adaptation","name":"DPO","taxonomy_name":"DPO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/overfitting","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/llm-as-a-judge","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"DPO trains a model on preference pairs with a plain classification-style loss, no reward model and no\nRL loop. It works because the KL-constrained objective RLHF optimises has a closed-form solution in\nwhich the reward is a function of the policy itself, so the reward model can be substituted out of the\nBradley–Terry loss. You get most of RLHF's effect for the cost of an SFT run, but offline: the pairs\ndescribe some earlier model's outputs, not the policy's current ones.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"grpo","key":"training-adaptation/grpo","module":"training-adaptation","name":"GRPO","taxonomy_name":"GRPO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","prompting/chain-of-thought","evals/pass-k","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria"],"tldr":"GRPO is a policy-gradient method that drops PPO's value model: for each prompt it samples a group of\nresponses, scores them, and uses the group's mean and spread as the baseline, so each advantage is\n\"how much better than its siblings\". It keeps PPO's clipped update and a KL term to a\nreference. It exists because a critic the size of the policy is costly and unstable for sequence-level\nrewards, and it is the usual choice for training open reasoning models with verifiable rewards.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"peft","key":"training-adaptation/peft","module":"training-adaptation","name":"PEFT (LoRA, QLoRA)","taxonomy_name":"PEFT (LoRA, QLoRA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/tooling","python-for-ai/hugging-face","serving-inference/engines","ml-fundamentals/optimizers"],"tldr":"Full fine-tuning updates every weight, so a 7B model needs on the order of 100 GB of GPU memory for\nweights, gradients and optimizer state. PEFT freezes the base model and trains a small set of extra\nparameters instead. LoRA learns each weight update as the product of two thin matrices; QLoRA also stores\nthe frozen base in 4-bit so the job fits one consumer GPU. You keep most of full fine-tuning's quality and\nget adapters you can swap at serving time.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-distillation","key":"training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Knowledge distillation","taxonomy_name":"knowledge distillation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/pruning","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/logits","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"A large model knows more than its top-1 answer shows: the full next-token distribution encodes which\nalternatives are plausible and which are absurd. Knowledge distillation trains a smaller student to match a\nlarger teacher's outputs, either its soft probability distributions or its generated text, instead of only\nthe one-hot labels in the data. Each example then carries far more signal, which is why a distilled model\nusually beats the same architecture trained on the labels alone.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"quantization","key":"training-adaptation/quantization","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Quantization (INT8/INT4, GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF)","taxonomy_name":"quantization (INT8/INT4, GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/pruning","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/engines","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision"],"tldr":"Quantization stores weights, and sometimes activations, in fewer bits than the 16-bit training format:\nINT8, INT4 or small floats. It pays off because LLM decoding is limited by bytes read per token, not by\narithmetic, so fewer bytes per weight means a model that fits cheaper hardware and decodes faster. GPTQ\nand AWQ are calibration-based recipes for choosing 4-bit values that minimise each layer's output error;\nGGUF is the llama.cpp file format for shipping such models to CPUs and laptops.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pruning","key":"training-adaptation/pruning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Pruning","taxonomy_name":"pruning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","serving-inference/inference-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Pruning removes parameters a trained network barely uses, individual weights, attention heads, MLP\nneurons or whole layers, and then repairs the damage with a short fine-tune or distillation from the\noriginal. It exists because large models are heavily over-parameterised, so much of the compute spent at\ninference moves the output very little. The catch is that only structured removal (whole rows, heads,\nlayers, or the hardware-supported 2:4 pattern) turns into real speed and memory wins on GPUs; scattered\nzeros mostly do not.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-merging","key":"training-adaptation/model-merging","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Model merging","taxonomy_name":"model merging","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/tooling","evals/regression-tests","system-design/model-portfolio","llmops-platform/model-registry","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Model merging combines several fine-tuned checkpoints of one base model into a single set of weights by\narithmetic on the parameters: averaging, adding scaled task vectors, or trimming and sign-voting the\ndeltas. No training is involved. It works because fine-tunes that start from the same initialisation\nstay in a connected low-loss region, so their deltas behave roughly additively. You get one model carrying\nseveral skills at the serving cost of one, paying with task interference that no gradient step corrects.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-curation","key":"training-adaptation/data-curation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Data curation","taxonomy_name":"data curation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-labeling","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","evals/dataset-contamination","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","safety-security/pii-redaction"],"tldr":"Data curation is the deliberate selection, cleaning, deduplication, decontamination and mixing of what a\nmodel trains on. It exists because gradient descent learns whatever is in the batch, boilerplate,\nduplicates, leaked test sets and labelling noise included, and no architecture or hyperparameter choice\nrecovers what bad data teaches. For fine-tuning, a few thousand carefully chosen examples routinely beat a\nhundred thousand scraped ones; for pretraining, filtering and deduplication are worth more than extra\ntokens.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"synthetic-data-generation","key":"training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Synthetic data generation","taxonomy_name":"synthetic data generation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Synthetic data generation uses a model, a program or a simulator to manufacture training or evaluation\nexamples instead of collecting them from people. It exists because the examples you need, rare failure\ncases, verified reasoning traces, formats the public web lacks, are expensive or impossible to gather at\nscale. Its value is decided by verification: generated examples are only as good as the filter that\nchooses which survive, and training repeatedly on unfiltered model output collapses the distribution.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-labeling","key":"training-adaptation/data-labeling","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Data labeling","taxonomy_name":"data labeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/rubrics","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"A fine-tuned model learns the labeling function, including every inconsistency and shortcut the labelers\nhad. Data labeling turns raw inputs into targets (classes, spans, demonstrations, preferences) in a way\nwhose quality you can measure, chiefly by having several people label the same items and computing\nagreement. At fine-tuning scale a few thousand examples carry the whole signal, so each bad label is a\nmeasurable share of what the model becomes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"train-val-test-split","key":"training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Train / validation / test split","taxonomy_name":"train/val/test split","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/data-labeling","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","llmops-platform/drift"],"tldr":"Weights are fit on the training set. Every decision made while looking at a score (learning rate, epochs,\nwhich checkpoint to keep) is fit on the validation set. The test set is read once, at the end, to estimate\nwhat the chosen model does on data nobody tuned against. Three sets exist because a score you optimised\nagainst is no longer an unbiased estimate, and the cut must follow the unit the model must generalise\nacross (conversation, user, document, time), not rows.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"epochs","key":"training-adaptation/epochs","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Epochs","taxonomy_name":"epochs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/pre-training","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers"],"tldr":"An epoch is one full pass of the optimiser over the training set. It exists because minibatch training\nconsumes data piecewise, and \"every example seen once\" is the natural unit for reshuffling, evaluating\nand stopping. More epochs means more optimizer steps over the same examples: necessary when the set is\nsmall, harmful once the model starts memorising. For LLMs the unit flips with scale: pretraining counts\ntokens and rarely completes one pass, while fine-tuning counts epochs and usually needs only a few.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"learning-rate","key":"training-adaptation/learning-rate","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Learning rate","taxonomy_name":"learning rate","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting"],"tldr":"The learning rate is the optimiser's step size: how far parameters move per update along the gradient\n(SGD) or the per-coordinate-normalised gradient (Adam). It is the most sensitive hyperparameter because a\ngradient gives direction, not a safe distance: too large and the loss oscillates or diverges, too small\nand the run crawls and ends worse. In LLM work, peak, warmup and decay are chosen together; fine-tuning\nuses rates far below pretraining to stay near the pretrained solution.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"overfitting","key":"training-adaptation/overfitting","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Overfitting","taxonomy_name":"overfitting","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/epochs","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","evals/dataset-contamination"],"tldr":"Overfitting is a model fitting the particulars of its training examples (label noise, phrasing) instead\nof the pattern that transfers to new data. The signature is training loss that keeps falling while\nheld-out loss stalls and then rises. It happens because a network with far more parameters than examples\ncan memorise anything; the remedies are more or cleaner data, fewer steps, less trainable capacity,\nregularisation and early stopping. Fine-tuning a small set for many epochs is the commonest way engineers\noverfit an LLM.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"catastrophic-forgetting","key":"training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Catastrophic forgetting","taxonomy_name":"catastrophic forgetting","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/model-merging","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/data-curation","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Fine-tuning computes gradients of the new loss only; nothing stops them overwriting the weights that\nencoded older capabilities, so the model forgets what it is not being trained on. For an LLM, a fine-tune\non your task can silently lose general instruction following, reasoning or safety behaviour, because your\nevaluation measures only your task. Defences: mix in general data, keep the update small, isolate it in\nadapters, pull back toward the original weights, and gate every fine-tune on a general regression suite.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"checkpointing","key":"training-adaptation/checkpointing","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"checkpointing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/tooling","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/rollback","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing"],"tldr":"Checkpointing saves the complete training state (weights, optimizer moments, scheduler, RNG and data\nposition) to durable storage at intervals, so a run can resume after a failure, be rolled back, and be\nselected from: the best checkpoint on validation is early stopping made concrete. Long runs fail, at\ncluster scale they fail often, and a full training checkpoint of a 7B model is on the order of 100 GB,\nso frequency, format, sharding and retention are engineering decisions. It is unrelated to gradient\ncheckpointing, which recomputes activations to save memory.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-checkpointing","key":"training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Gradient checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"gradient checkpointing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/tooling","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","model-internals/flashattention"],"tldr":"Despite the name, gradient checkpointing has nothing to do with saving a model to disk; it is activation\nrecomputation. Backprop needs every layer's forward activations, and at long sequence lengths those\noutweigh the weights. Checkpointing keeps only each block's input, discards the rest, and recomputes a\nblock's internals during backward. You pay roughly one extra forward pass of compute to shrink activation\nmemory from \"all layers\" to \"all block inputs plus one block\", which is what lets long-context fine-tunes\nfit on one GPU.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","key":"training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Fine-tune vs RAG vs prompt decision","taxonomy_name":"fine-tune vs RAG vs prompt decision","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-caching","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"The three levers change different objects. A prompt changes the input of one request and costs nothing\nto try. RAG puts knowledge the model does not have into the input at request time, with freshness,\naccess control and citations. Fine-tuning changes the weights, which is the right tool for behaviour,\nformat, skill and per-request cost, and a poor tool for injecting facts. Decide by naming the gap\n(knowledge, behaviour or economics) and climb prompt → RAG → fine-tune only when evals show the cheaper\nrung is not enough.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tooling","key":"training-adaptation/tooling","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Tooling (Unsloth, Axolotl, TRL)","taxonomy_name":"tooling (Unsloth, Axolotl, TRL)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/quantization","python-for-ai/hugging-face"],"tldr":"Fine-tuning an LLM is the same loop almost every time: load the model, maybe quantise it, attach LoRA,\nformat chat data, train with a known loss, save the adapter. TRL (Hugging Face) provides those loops as\ntrainers (SFT, DPO, GRPO, PPO, reward). Axolotl wraps them in a YAML file so a run is reproducible and\nmulti-GPU is a config flag. Unsloth rewrites the kernels so the same LoRA/QLoRA run is faster and lighter\non one GPU. Pick by how much control, reproducibility or raw speed you need.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-training","key":"training-adaptation/distributed-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Distributed training (data / tensor / pipeline parallelism, FSDP, DeepSpeed)","taxonomy_name":"distributed training (data / tensor / pipeline parallelism, FSDP, DeepSpeed)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/tooling","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Caveat: this topic matters only for training-heavy roles; for LLM-application roles, learn the vocabulary\nand skip the practice. One GPU cannot hold a large model's training state (about 16 bytes per parameter\nwith Adam in mixed precision) or finish pretraining in calendar time. Data parallelism splits the batch,\ntensor parallelism splits each matrix, pipeline parallelism splits the layers, and FSDP or DeepSpeed ZeRO\nshard the replicated state so data parallelism stops wasting memory. The interconnect decides which\ncombination is affordable.","caveat":"only if the JD is training-heavy; drop for LLM-app roles","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"prompting","tier":1,"order":3,"title":"Prompting","intro":{"overview":"This module treats the prompt as what it really is: the full specification of a task, written in the only\ninput a language model has, and handled with the same discipline as code. It opens with prompt engineering\nas a measured loop (specify, run against a fixed eval set, change one variable, measure again) and then\nwalks through the techniques in the order you reach for them. Zero-shot is the baseline. Few-shot is for\nthe cases where showing the shape works better than describing it. Chain-of-thought buys sequential\ncomputation for multi-step problems. The system prompt carries the operator's standing policy. Next come\nthe production mechanics around the words. Templates separate fixed instructions from per-request\nvariables. Structured outputs turn free text into a schema-valid object. Function calling and the ReAct\nloop let the model ask your code to look things up and act. Context engineering decides what fills a\nfinite, billed window, and prompt caching makes a stable prefix nearly free. Versioning makes every\nresponse traceable to the exact prompt that produced it. The module ends with prompt injection and\njailbreaks, which show why the prompt can never be a security boundary. Afterwards you can take a vague\nprompt to a versioned, tested, cache-friendly request builder with schema-checked output and tool use,\nexplain why each part works in terms of how the model was trained and how it runs, and say when prompting\nshould give way to retrieval, tools or fine-tuning. It matters most for AI Engineers, who write and ship\nthese prompts every day, and for FDEs, who tune them against a customer's real data.","why":{"engineer":"You write the request builder, the templates, the tool schemas and the agent loop, so you need to set a zero-shot baseline, add examples or reasoning only when the eval shows a gain, put static text first so caching works, get zero parse failures from constrained decoding, and record a prompt version on every trace.","architect":"You decide what lives in the prompt and what has to live in code: policy with money or legal consequences, tool permissions and injection containment belong outside the model, while context budgets, compaction strategy and the prompt registry with its eval gate and rollback path are platform choices that set both cost and auditability.","fde":"On a customer engagement you turn their vague requirement into a precise spec, choose few-shot examples from their own tickets or documents, threat-model their data sources for injection, red-team the bot against their business policies, and hand over a versioned prompt plus an eval set they can rerun after every model upgrade."},"mental_model":"Picture each model call as one assembled document that the model reads from top to bottom and then\ncontinues, with no hidden channel and no memory beyond what is on the page. Every topic in this module is\na decision about that page. From the top: a byte-stable system section holding policy, tool definitions and\nexamples, which is the cacheable prefix. Below it, managed context: retrieved material, compacted history\nand tool results, kept within a token budget and placed where attention reaches it. At the bottom, the\nper-request variables, rendered through a template. Then comes the generation itself, which you shape with\nreasoning space before the answer, a schema that constrains which tokens can appear, or a structured tool\ncall that hands control back to your code. Everything on the page is just tokens. That one fact explains\nwhy precise specification works, why caching depends on exact prefixes and why injected text can steer\nthe model. It is also why anything that must hold for certain has to be enforced in the code around the\npage, never in the words on it. Versioning is the label on the page that lets you reproduce it later.","order":["prompt-engineering","zero-shot","few-shot","chain-of-thought","system-prompt","prompt-templates","structured-outputs-json-mode","function-tool-calling","react","context-engineering","prompt-caching","prompt-versioning","prompt-injection","jailbreaks"],"order_reasons":{"prompt-engineering":"orients the module: the prompt as a specification and the measure-change-measure loop every later technique is judged by.","zero-shot":"sets the baseline: a precise instruction with no examples, and how to read its failures.","few-shot":"the first lever when a precise zero-shot prompt plateaus, along with the order, label and copy biases examples bring.","chain-of-thought":"adds reasoning tokens for multi-step tasks, building on the baseline-versus-variant A/B habit from the previous two.","system-prompt":"moves from single prompts to deployed products: the operator's channel, why the model honours it and why it guarantees nothing.","prompt-templates":"turns system and user text into a typed, rendered, testable artefact with the static-first layout.","structured-outputs-json-mode":"makes output machine-consumable with constrained decoding, which you need before code can consume tool arguments.","function-tool-calling":"uses schemas to let the model request actions your code executes, the boundary agents are built on.","react":"wraps tool calls in a reason-act-observe loop and shows what the harness owns: budget, termination and safety checks.","context-engineering":"manages the window that loops and retrieval fill: budgets, ordering, pruning and compaction.","prompt-caching":"rewards the stable prefixes that templates and context layout produce, with the economics of writes versus reads.","prompt-versioning":"makes the effective prompt identifiable, evaluated before promotion and revertible, now that it has many moving parts.","prompt-injection":"the attack that follows from \"everything is tokens\", and why containment lives in architecture, not wording.","jailbreaks":"finishes with attacks on safety training and operator policy, measured with attack suites rather than asserted."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a support-triage assistant for a fictional SaaS product, then deliver it as if a customer team will\ntake it over. Given an inbound ticket, it classifies the ticket into a fixed label set, extracts structured\nfields (product area, severity, account id, requested action) into a schema-constrained object, and drafts\na reply. To draft the reply it runs a short ReAct loop over three mock tools: knowledge-base search, account\nlookup, and a refund-request tool that only creates a pending request a human must approve. Start\nzero-shot, measure on a labelled golden set of at least 60 tickets, and add few-shot examples or a\nreasoning field only where the eval shows a gain. The system prompt and tool definitions live in versioned\ntemplate files rendered static-first, so prompt caching hits from the second call. A context budget prunes\nold tool results in longer loops. Every call logs the prompt version hash and token usage. Finally, attack\nyour own build. Seed tickets and knowledge-base articles with indirect injection attempts, and run a\njailbreak suite against two business policies: never promise a refund, and never reveal the system prompt.\nShow that the refund rule is enforced in code, not only in the prompt.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the request builder, versioned prompt templates with metadata headers, JSON schemas for the extraction output and tool definitions, the ReAct loop with step and token budgets, and the mock tools.","A golden set of 60+ labelled tickets plus an eval script that reports label accuracy, field-level extraction accuracy, parse failure rate, tokens, latency and cache hit ratio for each prompt version.","An experiment log comparing zero-shot, few-shot and chain-of-thought variants on the same set, with the decision each comparison led to.","An attack suite of injected tickets and documents and at least three jailbreak families per policy, with attack success rate and benign pass rate reported per prompt version.","A one-page handover note covering the prompt versions shipped, how to roll back, and which rules are enforced in code versus in the prompt."],"rubric":["Parse failure rate on the golden set is zero, and the eval script prints a per-label confusion matrix plus a per-version comparison table that the experiment log's decisions refer to.","A second identical request reports cache reads in the usage fields, an integration test asserts this, and no per-request field is rendered above the static instructions.","Every logged call carries a prompt version hash, and rolling back to the previous version is a single command that the eval then confirms.","The ReAct loop ends every run with an answer, a budget stop or a handled error, never by looping until the context overflows, and tool-call arguments that fail validation are rejected before execution.","No injected ticket or jailbreak creates an executed refund. The refund tool only ever produces pending requests, which a test checks even when the model is told to approve them."]}},"topics":[{"id":"prompt-engineering","key":"prompting/prompt-engineering","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt engineering","taxonomy_name":"prompt engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/few-shot","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/context-engineering","evals/eval-driven-development","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering"],"tldr":"A language model is a conditional distribution over next tokens; everything it does is determined by\nthe tokens in front of it. Prompt engineering is the practice of specifying a task through that input\n(instructions, context, examples, output format) and iterating against a measured eval set, because an\nunder-specified prompt makes the model average over every plausible interpretation. It is the cheapest\nlever you own: seconds per iteration, no training data, no redeploy. This lesson orients the module;\neach technique has its own deep lesson.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"system-prompt","key":"prompting/system-prompt","module":"prompting","name":"System prompt","taxonomy_name":"system prompt","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/context-engineering","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","serving-inference/prefix-caching","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"A deployed chat has two authors: the operator who built the product and the user typing into it. The\nsystem prompt is the operator's channel: text in a dedicated role at the start of the context, which\npost-training taught the model to treat as standing policy over what users say. It is a learned prior,\nnot enforcement, so it shapes behaviour reliably but guarantees nothing; and as a stable prefix it is the\npart of the prompt that caching makes nearly free.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"zero-shot","key":"prompting/zero-shot","module":"prompting","name":"Zero-shot prompting","taxonomy_name":"zero-shot","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["prompting/few-shot","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Zero-shot prompting describes the task in words and includes no demonstrations. It works because\ninstruction tuning trained the model on hundreds to thousands of tasks phrased as instructions, so a clearly\ndescribed new task is in distribution even though the model never saw an example of it. It is the\ndefault starting point: the shortest prompt, no example-induced bias, easiest to maintain. It fails where\nthe specification cannot be written down precisely, which is exactly where examples earn their tokens.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"few-shot","key":"prompting/few-shot","module":"prompting","name":"Few-shot prompting","taxonomy_name":"few-shot","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/zero-shot","prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Few-shot prompting puts input → output demonstrations in the prompt. The model performs in-context\nlearning: conditioning on the examples shifts its output distribution toward their format, label space\nand style without any weight update. It is how you specify what is easier to show than to say. The price\nis tokens on every call and a set of biases the examples bring with them: order, label frequency and\ncopied surface detail.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"chain-of-thought","key":"prompting/chain-of-thought","module":"prompting","name":"Chain-of-thought prompting","taxonomy_name":"chain-of-thought","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/zero-shot","prompting/few-shot","prompting/react","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/pass-k","agents/reflection-self-critique"],"tldr":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) has the model write intermediate reasoning before its final answer. It works\nbecause a transformer spends a fixed amount of computation per generated token, so a problem that needs\nmany dependent steps cannot be solved inside one token; writing the steps out buys more sequential\ncomputation and lets the sequence carry state. Gains are large on arithmetic, logic and multi-step\nplanning, near zero on lookups, and the written chain is not guaranteed to be the real cause of the\nanswer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"react","key":"prompting/react","module":"prompting","name":"ReAct (reason + act)","taxonomy_name":"ReAct","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-injection","agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/planning","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/loop-detection"],"tldr":"ReAct interleaves reasoning and acting in one loop: the model writes a thought, picks a tool call, the\nharness runs it and appends the observation, and the model reasons again over fresh evidence. It exists\nbecause chain-of-thought alone cannot look anything up, so its errors compound, while action-only agents\npick tools without a stated reason and cannot revise a plan. Native tool calling is ReAct with the\nparsing moved into the API; the loop, budget and safety checks remain yours.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-templates","key":"prompting/prompt-templates","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt templates","taxonomy_name":"prompt templates","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","python-for-ai/pydantic","llmops-platform/prompt-registry"],"tldr":"A prompt template separates the fixed instructions from the per-request variables and renders them into\nthe message structure the API expects. It exists because a prompt is code that runs on data: string\nconcatenation mixes the two, breaks on stray braces and delimiters, cannot be unit-tested or versioned,\nand silently destroys cache hits by putting variable text before stable text. There are two template\nlayers, yours and the model's chat template, and you need to understand both.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-versioning","key":"prompting/prompt-versioning","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt versioning","taxonomy_name":"prompt versioning","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-caching","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/rollback","evals/regression-tests","evals/golden-dataset","observability/tracing"],"tldr":"A prompt is behaviour-defining code that happens to be prose, so it needs what code already has:\nimmutable versions, an identifier recorded on every call, review, an eval before promotion and a\none-step rollback. Without that, a quality regression cannot be tied to a change, a user complaint\ncannot be reproduced, and \"the prompt in production\" is whatever someone last pasted from a playground.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-caching","key":"prompting/prompt-caching","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt caching","taxonomy_name":"prompt caching","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/context-engineering","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/ttft","observability/token-cost-tracking"],"tldr":"Every request re-runs prefill over the whole prompt, yet in most applications most of the prompt\n(system instructions, tool definitions, few-shot examples, a long reference document) is identical from\none request to the next. Prompt caching stores the model's internal state for a prefix and reuses it\nwhen a later request starts with the same bytes, cutting input cost and time-to-first-token. It only\nworks on exact prefixes, so the layout of your prompt decides whether you get the discount.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-outputs-json-mode","key":"prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","module":"prompting","name":"Structured outputs / JSON mode","taxonomy_name":"structured outputs / JSON mode","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/typed-tool-contracts","safety-security/output-validation","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Downstream code needs a parseable object with known keys and types, but a language model emits free\ntext. JSON mode guarantees syntactically valid JSON; structured outputs go further and guarantee\nconformance to a schema you supply, by constraining which tokens the model may emit at each step. This\nremoves a class of parse failures, not a class of wrong answers: schema-valid output can still be wrong,\nso semantic validation and evals remain your job.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"function-tool-calling","key":"prompting/function-tool-calling","module":"prompting","name":"Function / tool calling","taxonomy_name":"function / tool calling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/react","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol"],"tldr":"A language model can only emit tokens: it cannot look anything up, change any state or do reliable\narithmetic. Function calling (tool calling) is a trained protocol in which you describe functions with a\nname, description and JSON schema, the model emits a structured request to call one with arguments, your\ncode executes it and returns the result, and the model continues. The model never runs anything. The\n\"call\" is a message; execution, permissions and error handling are yours.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-engineering","key":"prompting/context-engineering","module":"prompting","name":"Context engineering","taxonomy_name":"context engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/few-shot","model-internals/context-window","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agents/agent-memory"],"tldr":"The context window is the model's entire working memory for one call: finite, billed per token and\nattended to unevenly. Context engineering is deciding, for every call, what goes in, in what order, in\nwhat form and under what budget: instructions, tool definitions, retrieved documents, memory, history and\ntool results. Prompt engineering writes the instructions; context engineering manages everything around\nthem, and in multi-step systems that is where quality and cost are decided.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection","key":"prompting/prompt-injection","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt injection","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/jailbreaks","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/context-engineering","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Prompt injection is text in the model's context, placed there by someone other than the operator, that\nthe model treats as instructions. It works because a transformer has no privileged channel: system\nprompt, user message, retrieved page and tool result are all tokens in one sequence, and\ninstruction-following training makes the model obey instruction-shaped text wherever it appears. No\nprompt wording fixes this; containment means limiting what the model can do and see, which the\nsecurity modules cover in depth.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"jailbreaks","key":"prompting/jailbreaks","module":"prompting","name":"Jailbreaks","taxonomy_name":"jailbreaks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/system-prompt","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/llama-guard","evals/red-teaming","evals/adversarial-evaluation","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling"],"tldr":"A jailbreak is an input crafted to make a model do what its safety training or its operator's policy\nforbids. It works because refusal is a learned behaviour competing with helpfulness and instruction\nfollowing, and because training never covers every framing. For an application builder the practical\nthreat is usually policy-level, getting your bot to leave its lane, leak its prompt or promise a refund,\nand the defence is the same as for injection: do not make the prompt the boundary.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"rag-retrieval","tier":1,"order":4,"title":"RAG & retrieval","intro":{"overview":"This module is about getting the right evidence in front of a model, and only that evidence, so its\nanswers come from your documents instead of its memory. It follows one document through the whole\nsystem. Parsing turns PDFs, scans and tables into clean ordered text. Chunking, overlap and granularity\ndecide what counts as a retrievable unit. Embedding models, similarity metrics, normalization, ANN\nindexes and vector databases make dense search fast. An idempotent embedding pipeline keeps the index\nin step with its sources. Inverted indexes and BM25 provide the lexical half, and dense-versus-sparse\nanalysis, hybrid fusion and cross-encoder reranking combine the two arms into a staged pipeline tuned on\nrecall and precision. Metadata and ACL filters make certain results impossible to return. Parent-document\nand contextual retrieval repair chunks that lost their context. Query rewriting, HyDE, decomposition and\nmulti-hop loops repair queries that were never going to match. On the generation side the module covers\nlost-in-the-middle position effects, grounding with citations, and measuring whether each citation\nactually supports its claim. It closes with production concerns: freshness, deletion and index\nconsistency, staged failure diagnosis, the long-context-versus-RAG decision, knowledge graphs, GraphRAG\nand agentic RAG. Afterwards you can design, build, measure and debug a production retrieval system,\nand defend each stage with a number. It matters most for AI Engineers, who spend most of their RAG\ntime in these stages, and for FDEs, who have to make retrieval work on a customer's messy, permissioned\ncorpus.","why":{"engineer":"You build the ingestion pipeline with content-hashed chunk ids and query-time parity, run BM25 and dense arms fused with RRF in front of a reranker, and, when an answer is wrong, trace the gold chunk stage by stage to find where it was lost instead of swapping the embedding model on a hunch.","architect":"You choose the vector store from vector count, dimension, filter selectivity and operational capacity, place ACL enforcement inside the search rather than in the prompt, set freshness and deletion SLOs per content class, and decide from arithmetic and evals when long context, a knowledge graph, GraphRAG or an agent loop earns its cost over plain hybrid retrieval.","fde":"You open the customer's parsed text before tuning anything, map their SharePoint or Confluence permissions into per-chunk principals, build a labelled query set from their own users' questions, and show recall@k and citation correctness on their documents so sign-off rests on measurements rather than a polished demo."},"mental_model":"Picture retrieval as a funnel with a leak test at every stage. The whole corpus goes in at the top. The\nparser decides what text exists. The chunker decides what a hit means. The index (dense vectors and an\ninverted index side by side) decides what can be found. Filters remove what this user must never see.\nA wide first stage keeps a few dozen candidates for recall, a reranker keeps a few for precision, and\nthe prompt builder decides where they sit and how they are cited. Each stage can only lose evidence; no\nlater stage can recover what an earlier one dropped. The techniques in this module either widen a\nstage that loses too much (hybrid, rewriting, HyDE, decomposition, multi-hop, contextual chunks) or\nnarrow one that lets too much through (reranking, filtering, a smaller k). So you diagnose by finding\nthe first stage where the gold evidence disappears, fix that stage, and measure it with its own metric.\nAround the funnel runs a sync loop that keeps the index a faithful, current, permission-correct copy of\nits sources.","order":["document-parsing","chunking-strategies","chunk-overlap","retrieval-granularity","embedding-models","similarity-search","embedding-dimension-and-normalization","ann-indexes","vector-databases","embedding-pipelines","inverted-indexes","bm25-internals","dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","hybrid-search","recall-vs-precision-trade-off","reranking","metadata-filtering","acl-aware-retrieval","parent-document-retrieval","contextual-retrieval","query-rewriting","hyde","query-decomposition","multi-hop-retrieval","lost-in-the-middle","citations-grounding","citation-correctness","freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","retrieval-failure-diagnosis","long-context-vs-rag","knowledge-graphs","graphrag","agentic-rag"],"order_reasons":{"document-parsing":"every later stage sees only what the parser produced, so learn to inspect parsed text first.","chunking-strategies":"defines the retrievable unit and why whole documents embed as blurry averages.","chunk-overlap":"a cheap patch for boundary damage, best understood right after the splitters it patches.","retrieval-granularity":"separates the unit you match on from the unit you return, framing later small-to-big work.","embedding-models":"explains bi-encoders, prefixes and versioning, the basis of dense retrieval.","similarity-search":"shows how dot product, cosine and Euclidean relate and why the trained metric must be used.","embedding-dimension-and-normalization":"adds the storage arithmetic, normalization, truncation and quantization behind index sizing.","ann-indexes":"explains HNSW and IVF-PQ and the recall–latency knobs once exact search stops fitting.","vector-databases":"maps pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Pinecone and Chroma onto the operations you now understand.","embedding-pipelines":"turns one-off indexing into an idempotent, incremental, versioned job.","inverted-indexes":"introduces posting lists, the data structure underneath lexical search.","bm25-internals":"explains IDF, saturation and length normalization, the lexical baseline every retriever must beat.","dense-vs-sparse-retrieval":"compares the two families' structural failure modes to motivate combining them.","hybrid-search":"fuses the BM25 and dense arms by rank and verifies the gain with recall@k.","recall-vs-precision-trade-off":"frames retrieval as a wide recall stage followed by a narrow precision stage.","reranking":"supplies the cross-encoder precision stage that the trade-off calls for.","metadata-filtering":"places hard predicates inside the search and explains why post-filtering returns too few results.","acl-aware-retrieval":"applies filtering to per-user permissions, the security requirement of every enterprise deployment.","parent-document-retrieval":"implements small-to-big retrieval so matching and answering use different units.","contextual-retrieval":"repairs chunks that lost their subject by enriching them at ingestion.","query-rewriting":"repairs the query side: follow-ups, vocabulary mismatch and pasted noise.","hyde":"bridges the question-versus-passage gap in embedding space with a hypothetical answer.","query-decomposition":"splits compound questions so each facet gets its own retrieval.","multi-hop-retrieval":"chains dependent sub-questions through bridging facts under a hop limit.","lost-in-the-middle":"shows why more context can lower accuracy and how to order evidence in the prompt.","citations-grounding":"constrains the answer to the sources with stable ids, per-claim citations and abstention.","citation-correctness":"measures whether each citation actually supports its claim, the check on the previous lesson.","freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency":"keeps the index current, deletion-safe and version-consistent in production.","retrieval-failure-diagnosis":"ties the funnel together as a staged trace that finds where evidence was lost.","long-context-vs-rag":"decides with cost and accuracy arithmetic when retrieval is needed at all.","knowledge-graphs":"adds typed entities and relationships for questions that are joins and aggregations.","graphrag":"builds community summaries over an extracted graph for corpus-wide and thematic questions.","agentic-rag":"puts retrieval tools in a bounded loop, earned only when the eval set shows the simpler pipelines fall short."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a permission-aware question-answering assistant over a realistic internal corpus of 500 to 2,000\ndocuments: a mix of born-digital PDFs, at least a few scanned pages, table-heavy spreadsheets or reports,\nand wiki pages, each assigned to one of three or four access groups. Start by parsing a sample of every\ndocument type and fixing what breaks. Then build an idempotent ingestion pipeline with structure-aware\nchunking, content-hashed chunk ids, contextual prefixes, and per-chunk metadata holding source, version,\ndate and allowed principals. Index each chunk in both a vector store and a BM25 index, fuse the two arms\nwith reciprocal rank fusion, rerank the top 30 to 50 with a cross-encoder, and return parent sections to\na generator that must cite a source id for every claim or abstain. Add a query rewriter for\nconversational follow-ups and decomposition for compare-style questions. Build a labelled golden set of\nat least 100 questions, sliced into lookup, identifier, paraphrase, compound, multi-hop and unanswerable\ntypes. Measure each stage, then make the pipeline survive change: edit, revoke and delete documents and\nprove that retrieval reflects each change within a stated lag. Finish with a written analysis of when\nlong context or GraphRAG would beat what you built on this corpus.","deliverables":["A parsing report with sampled pages per document type, the observed failures, the fixes applied, and OCR character error rate and table cell accuracy on a hand-checked subset.","The ingestion pipeline, runnable from one command, whose second run on an unchanged corpus re-embeds nothing, plus a CI golden-vector test proving the index-time and query-time embed paths match.","The retrieval service with BM25, dense, RRF fusion, reranking, metadata and ACL filters, parent expansion, query rewriting and decomposition, each stage switchable by configuration.","The labelled golden set and an evaluation report giving recall@50, recall@5 after reranking, MRR, citation recall and precision, and abstention rate, per slice and per ablation (BM25 only, dense only, hybrid, plus reranker, plus contextual prefixes).","A freshness and deletion test suite that edits, revokes and deletes documents and reports staleness lag, deleted-document leak probes and orphan counts from a reconciliation job.","A failure-diagnosis sheet of at least 30 wrong answers, each labelled with the first stage where the gold evidence was lost, with counts per stage and the fix chosen for the largest bucket.","A two-page design memo on long context versus RAG and on whether GraphRAG or an agent loop is warranted for this corpus, with cost-per-query and accuracy arithmetic."],"rubric":["A red-team query run as a low-privilege test user never returns a chunk from a restricted document, and the same holds within the stated lag after a permission is revoked at the source.","The ablation table shows the measured contribution of each stage, and any stage that does not improve its target metric on the golden set is either removed or justified in writing.","Recall at candidate depth, recall after reranking, and gold-in-context rate are reported separately, so a generation failure is never misread as a retrieval failure.","Every cited source id in a sampled set of answers resolves to a passage that supports its claim, as checked by an entailment model or calibrated judge whose agreement with human labels is reported.","Deleting a source document removes every derived chunk from both indexes, which is verified by a probe query and a reconciliation run reporting zero orphans.","The diagnosis sheet's largest failure bucket is the one the final change targeted, and the before-and-after numbers for that slice are shown."]}},"topics":[{"id":"chunking-strategies","key":"rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Chunking strategies (fixed, recursive, semantic)","taxonomy_name":"chunking strategies (fixed, recursive, semantic)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","model-internals/tokenization","python-for-ai/tiktoken"],"tldr":"An embedding model turns a span of text into one vector, so a whole document becomes a blurry average\nin which the specific passage a question needs is drowned out, and text past the\nencoder's input limit is lost. Chunking cuts documents into retrievable units first. Fixed-size is\npredictable but blind to structure; recursive splitting tries natural boundaries (paragraph, line,\nsentence) before falling back to hard cuts; semantic splitting places boundaries where adjacent\nsentences stop being about the same thing. The strategy defines what \"a hit\" means downstream.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"chunk-overlap","key":"rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Chunk overlap","taxonomy_name":"chunk overlap","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","python-for-ai/tiktoken"],"tldr":"Overlap makes consecutive chunks share their last and first few tokens, so a sentence or reference\nthat straddles a boundary appears whole in at least one chunk instead of being half in each. It is a\ncheap hedge against boundary damage, paid for with a larger index and near-duplicate results that must\nbe deduplicated at query time. It does not replace good boundaries; structure-aware splitting and\nparent retrieval fix the root cause that overlap only patches.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-granularity","key":"rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Retrieval granularity","taxonomy_name":"retrieval granularity","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Granularity is the size of the unit you retrieve: sentence, proposition, passage, section or whole\ndocument. Small units make sharp vectors and precise matches but starve the generator of context;\nlarge units carry context but blur the vector and crowd the prompt. The resolving move is to separate\nthe unit you *match on* from the unit you *return*: index small, return the enclosing parent. The\nright pair depends on the question type and the context budget, and should be measured, not assumed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-models","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-models","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding models","taxonomy_name":"embedding models","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/reranking","python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"An embedding model is an encoder trained so that texts with similar meaning land close together in a\nfixed-size vector space. It exists because keyword search cannot see that \"money back\" and \"refund\"\nmean the same thing, and because scoring every document against the query with a full cross-attention\nmodel is too slow. A bi-encoder embeds documents once, ahead of time, so retrieval becomes a\nnearest-neighbour lookup. The price is that the model must be chosen, prefixed and versioned\nconsistently, or the index is silently wrong.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-dimension-and-normalization","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding dimension & normalization","taxonomy_name":"embedding dimension & normalization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","ml-fundamentals/normalization","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"The dimension of an embedding is how many floats each vector holds, and it sets the storage, memory and\nper-comparison cost of the whole index linearly. L2-normalizing vectors to unit length makes cosine\nsimilarity equal to the dot product and makes Euclidean ranking identical to both, so the metric choice\nstops mattering and scores stop rewarding long, verbose chunks. Matryoshka-trained models let you\ntruncate dimensions after the fact; quantization shrinks the bytes per dimension. Both trade a little\nrecall for a lot of memory.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-pipelines","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding pipelines","taxonomy_name":"embedding pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers"],"tldr":"An embedding pipeline is the job that turns source documents into indexed vectors and keeps them that\nway: parse, chunk, enrich, embed, upsert, and later update and delete. It exists because the \"loop over\nfiles and call the API\" script fails the moment a document changes, a batch half-fails, or the model is\nupgraded. The design goals are idempotency (same input, same chunk id, no duplicates), incremental\nupdates, and strict parity between how text is embedded at index time and at query time.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-databases","key":"rag-retrieval/vector-databases","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Vector databases (pgvector, Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma)","taxonomy_name":"vector databases (pgvector, Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","system-design/build-vs-buy"],"tldr":"A vector database stores embeddings with their metadata and answers \"the k nearest vectors to this one,\nsubject to these filters\" fast enough for a request path. It exists because brute-force scanning\nmillions of vectors is too slow and a bare ANN library (FAISS, hnswlib) has no persistence, updates,\nfiltering or multi-tenancy. pgvector adds this inside Postgres; Qdrant, Weaviate and Milvus are dedicated\nopen-source engines; Pinecone is fully managed; Chroma is the embedded prototyping option. The choice is\nmostly about operations and scale, not retrieval quality.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"similarity-search","key":"rag-retrieval/similarity-search","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Similarity search (cosine, dot product)","taxonomy_name":"similarity search (cosine, dot product)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"Dense retrieval reduces to one operation: score a query vector against every stored vector and keep the\ntop k. Dot product and cosine are the two scores that matter; cosine is the dot product of unit-length\nvectors, and squared Euclidean distance is the same quantity rearranged. The right choice is whichever\nthe embedding model was trained against, because training shaped the geometry. Pick the wrong one and\nrankings degrade silently, with no error to catch.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ann-indexes","key":"rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"ANN indexes (HNSW, IVF)","taxonomy_name":"ANN indexes (HNSW, IVF)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":19,"related":["rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Exact nearest-neighbor search reads every vector per query, which stops fitting a latency budget\nsomewhere in the millions. Approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes give up a few percent of recall for\nsublinear search. HNSW builds a layered proximity graph and walks it greedily; IVF clusters the space\nwith k-means and scans only the closest clusters, often with product quantization to shrink the vectors.\nThe knobs (ef, nprobe) move you along a recall–latency curve you must measure, not assume.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Dense vs sparse retrieval","taxonomy_name":"dense vs sparse retrieval","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"Sparse retrieval represents text as a vocabulary-sized vector that is almost all zeros (which words\nappear, weighted by BM25) and finds matches through an inverted index. Dense retrieval represents text as\na few hundred learned floats and finds matches by vector similarity. Sparse is exact about terms and blind\nto meaning; dense is fluent about meaning and unreliable about exact terms. Neither dominates, which is\nwhy production systems usually run both and fuse the results.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"bm25-internals","key":"rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"BM25 internals","taxonomy_name":"BM25 internals","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","model-internals/tokenization","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"BM25 scores a document for a query by summing, over query terms, a rarity weight (IDF) times a\nterm-frequency term that saturates and is normalized by document length. Each piece fixes a specific\nfailure of raw term counting: IDF stops common words dominating, saturation stops a term repeated 50\ntimes beating one repeated 5 times by 10×, and length normalization stops long documents winning by\nhaving more words. Two parameters, k1 and b, set how strong those corrections are.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"inverted-indexes","key":"rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Inverted indexes","taxonomy_name":"inverted indexes","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","backend-fundamentals/database-design"],"tldr":"An inverted index flips the document→words mapping into words→documents: for every term, a sorted\nposting list of the documents that contain it, with counts and positions. A query then touches only the\nlists for its terms instead of every document, which is what makes lexical search over hundreds of\nmillions of documents answerable in milliseconds on CPUs (sharded across machines at the high end). The design choices (sorted ids, compression,\nimmutable segments, skip pointers) all exist to make those list scans fast and updates safe.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hybrid-search","key":"rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Hybrid search (BM25 + dense)","taxonomy_name":"hybrid search (BM25 + dense)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Hybrid search runs a lexical retriever (BM25) and a dense retriever (embeddings) on the same query and\nmerges their results, because the two fail on different queries: BM25 misses paraphrase, dense misses\nexact identifiers and out-of-domain jargon. The hard part is the merge: BM25 and cosine scores live on\nunrelated scales, so you fuse by rank (reciprocal rank fusion) or normalize per query before a weighted\nsum, and verify the gain with recall@k on labeled queries.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"reranking","key":"rag-retrieval/reranking","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Reranking (cross-encoder)","taxonomy_name":"reranking (cross-encoder)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","model-internals/self-attention","evals/retrieval-evaluation","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"A bi-encoder must compress each passage into one vector before it ever sees the query, so it cannot\nmodel how specific query words interact with specific passage words. A cross-encoder reads query and\npassage together in a single transformer pass and scores the pair directly, which is far more accurate\nand far too slow to run over a corpus. Reranking resolves that: a cheap retriever pulls a few dozen\ncandidates, the cross-encoder reorders them, and only the top few reach the prompt.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"recall-vs-precision-trade-off","key":"rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Recall vs precision trade-off","taxonomy_name":"recall vs precision trade-off","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Recall is the share of the answer-bearing passages you actually retrieved; precision is the share of what\nyou retrieved that is answer-bearing. Widening top-k raises recall and lowers precision at once, and in\nRAG both failures hurt: a missing passage means the model cannot answer, a noisy context means it may\nanswer from the wrong passage. The standard resolution is to stage retrieval: cheap and wide for recall\nfirst, expensive and narrow for precision second, each measured with its own metric.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"query-rewriting","key":"rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Query rewriting","taxonomy_name":"query rewriting","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/hyde","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","prompting/context-engineering","prompting/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"The text a user types is written for a human, not for an index: it is short, refers to earlier turns,\nuses different words from the documents, and often buries one useful identifier in a wall of paste.\nQuery rewriting inserts an LLM step between the user and the retriever that turns the raw input into\none or more retrieval-friendly queries, because fixing the key at query time is far cheaper than\nre-indexing the corpus to tolerate every way a human might ask.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"query-decomposition","key":"rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Query decomposition","taxonomy_name":"query decomposition","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","agents/task-decomposition","agents/parallel-tool-calls","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"A compound question (\"compare the refund windows of the Standard and Enterprise plans for EU customers\")\nembeds as one blurry point and its top-k is dominated by whichever half the index matches best, so the\nother half never reaches the prompt. Query decomposition has an LLM split the question into focused,\nstandalone sub-questions, retrieves for each (in parallel when they are independent), and answers from\nthe merged evidence or from per-sub-question answers. Independent parts fan out; dependent parts chain,\nwhich is multi-hop retrieval.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-hop-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Multi-hop retrieval","taxonomy_name":"multi-hop retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","rag-retrieval/graphrag","rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","rag-retrieval/reranking","prompting/react","agents/agent-loop","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Some questions cannot be answered from any single passage because the second fact is only findable once\nyou know the first: \"who manages the author of the report for incident 4812?\" needs the author's name\nbefore \"manager of Alice\" is even a query. One-shot retrieval finds the first hop at best, since the\nterms that would retrieve the second do not exist yet. Multi-hop retrieval interleaves retrieving and\nreading: retrieve, extract the bridging fact, form the next query, repeat under a hop limit, then answer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hyde","key":"rag-retrieval/hyde","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)","taxonomy_name":"HyDE","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/reranking","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"HyDE asks an LLM to write a plausible answer passage first, embeds that hypothetical instead of (or\nalongside) the question, and retrieves the real passages nearest to it. It exists because a short\nquestion and a long answer are different kinds of text in different regions of embedding space; a\nhypothetical answer lands in the answer region even when its facts are wrong, because the encoder keeps\ntopic and vocabulary and washes out detail. It costs one generation per query.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"parent-document-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Parent-document retrieval","taxonomy_name":"parent-document retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"The best unit for matching a query is small, because a sentence or short paragraph embeds as a sharp\nkey; the best unit for answering is larger, because the surrounding section carries the definitions,\ntable headers and caveats the passage depends on. Parent-document retrieval separates the two: index\nsmall child chunks, each linked to a larger parent, search on the children, and hand the generator the\nparents. You get the recall of small chunks and the context of big ones, paying in context tokens.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"contextual-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Contextual retrieval","taxonomy_name":"contextual retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","prompting/prompt-caching","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Chunking destroys context: \"revenue grew 3% over the previous quarter\" no longer says which company or\nwhich quarter, so neither its embedding nor its BM25 terms can match a query that names them. Contextual\nretrieval fixes the index rather than the query: at ingestion an LLM reads the whole document plus each\nchunk and writes a short context that is prepended to the chunk before embedding and before BM25\nindexing. It costs one LLM call per chunk, made affordable by prompt caching, and adds no query-time\nlatency.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"metadata-filtering","key":"rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Metadata filtering","taxonomy_name":"metadata filtering","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture"],"tldr":"An embedding encodes what a chunk is about, not which tenant owns it, when it was written or which\nproduct version it describes. Metadata filtering stores those structured attributes next to each vector\nand applies them as hard predicates *inside* the search, because applying them afterwards to an\napproximate top-k returns too few results, or none, as soon as the predicate is selective. The\nengineering question is where the predicate runs: before the ANN walk, during it, or after it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"acl-aware-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"ACL-aware retrieval","taxonomy_name":"ACL-aware retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","system-design/tenant-isolation","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"An enterprise index flattens documents with very different permissions into one searchable bag, yet\neach answer must draw only on documents the asking user could open themselves. ACL-aware retrieval turns\nthe authenticated identity into a hard predicate over each chunk's allowed principals inside the search,\nbecause the model cannot be trusted to withhold what it has already read and filtering after generation\nis too late. The hard parts are capturing permissions at ingestion, expanding groups at query time, and\nkeeping both fresh.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"lost-in-the-middle","key":"rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Lost-in-the-middle","taxonomy_name":"lost-in-the-middle","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["model-internals/context-window","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/positional-encoding","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","prompting/context-engineering","agents/context-compaction-summarization"],"tldr":"Language models do not read a long prompt uniformly: information at the beginning and the end is used\nfar more reliably than information in the middle, the U-shaped curve measured by the lost-in-the-middle\nexperiments. For RAG, retrieving more chunks can lower answer quality even when the right chunk is\npresent, since every extra chunk is a distractor that pushes the evidence toward the middle. So retrieve\nfewer and better, place the strongest evidence at the edges, and put the question last.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"citations-grounding","key":"rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Citations / grounding","taxonomy_name":"citations / grounding","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/reranking","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","model-internals/hallucination","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Grounding means the answer is derived from, and only from, the retrieved sources; citations are the\nmachine-checkable pointers from each claim back to the source span that supports it. You need both\nbecause retrieval alone does not stop a model from mixing in memorised or invented facts, and without\npointers neither a user nor an evaluator can tell which sentences are backed. Give sources stable IDs,\nrequire a citation per claim, allow abstention, and verify citations in code.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"citation-correctness","key":"rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Citation correctness","taxonomy_name":"citation correctness","kind":"metric","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Citation correctness asks whether each citation actually supports the claim it is attached to, not merely\nwhether citations are present. It splits into citation recall (is every claim fully supported by what it\ncites) and citation precision (is every citation necessary and relevant). It matters because a cited but\nunsupported sentence is worse than an uncited one: it borrows the source's authority. Measure it with an\nentailment model or calibrated judge against labelled samples.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"graphrag","key":"rag-retrieval/graphrag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"GraphRAG","taxonomy_name":"GraphRAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","agents/context-compaction-summarization","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"GraphRAG uses an LLM at index time to extract entities and relationships from every chunk, builds a graph,\nclusters it into a hierarchy of communities, and pre-writes a summary of each community. Queries then\nretrieve over entities and summaries instead of raw chunks. It exists because chunk-level vector search\ncannot answer corpus-wide questions (\"what are the main themes?\") or join facts spread across documents;\nthe price is a large, LLM-heavy indexing cost that must be repaid by those query types.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-graphs","key":"rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Knowledge graphs","taxonomy_name":"knowledge graphs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/graphrag","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","scoping/domain-modeling","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas"],"tldr":"A knowledge graph stores facts as typed entities connected by typed relationships, with a schema that\nsays which connections are valid. In RAG it complements vector search: embeddings find text that sounds\nlike the question, while a graph answers questions that are joins (\"who reports to the owner of system\nX?\"), aggregations and exact constraints. The hard parts are not the database but building it: extraction,\nentity resolution, schema design and keeping it in sync with sources.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","key":"rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Freshness, document deletion and index consistency","taxonomy_name":"freshness / document deletion / index consistency","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"A retrieval index is a derived copy of the source documents, and it starts going stale the moment it is\nbuilt. Freshness is how long a source change takes to become visible in retrieval; deletion is removing\nevery derived artifact of a document when the source goes away; consistency is never serving a mix of\nold and new versions of the same document. All three reduce to one discipline: stable document identity,\nversioned chunk ids, write-new-then-delete-old, and a reconciliation job for whatever the incremental\npath missed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-failure-diagnosis","key":"rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Retrieval failure diagnosis","taxonomy_name":"retrieval failure diagnosis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","observability/tracing","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search"],"tldr":"When a RAG system gives a wrong answer, the evidence was lost at one specific stage: never ingested,\nparsed or chunked badly, missing from the index, filtered out, ranked too low, dropped from the\ncontext, or ignored by the model. Diagnosis means tracing the gold evidence through each stage and\nfinding the *first* one where it disappears, because each stage has a different fix and tuning the wrong\none changes nothing.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"long-context-vs-rag","key":"rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Long-context vs RAG","taxonomy_name":"long-context vs RAG","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/context-window","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","system-design/rag-architecture","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval"],"tldr":"With context windows of hundreds of thousands of tokens, you can sometimes skip retrieval and put the\nwhole corpus in the prompt. That wins when the corpus is small, static and reused, or when the task needs\nwhole-document reasoning. RAG wins when the corpus is large, changing, permissioned or high-volume, because\nyou pay per input token, prefill latency grows with length, and models use long contexts less reliably than\nthe advertised window suggests.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"agentic-rag","key":"rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Agentic RAG","taxonomy_name":"agentic RAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","prompting/react","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"Agentic RAG turns retrieval from a fixed step before generation into tools the model calls in a loop: it\ndecides whether to search, what to search for, which source to use, whether the results suffice, and when\nto stop. It exists because multi-hop, ambiguous and multi-source questions cannot be answered by one query\nagainst one index, and it costs more latency, tokens and predictability than a fixed pipeline.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-parsing","key":"rag-retrieval/document-parsing","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Document parsing (OCR, layout, tables)","taxonomy_name":"document parsing (OCR, layout, tables)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"Parsing turns PDFs, scans and office files into clean, ordered text with structure before anything is\nchunked or embedded. It needs OCR for pages that are images, layout analysis to recover reading order and\ndrop headers and footers, and table extraction that keeps each cell tied to its row and column headers.\nIt matters because every later stage only sees what the parser produced: retrieval cannot find text that\nwas garbled, reordered or flattened.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"agents","tier":1,"order":5,"title":"Agents","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building LLM agents that do real work against real systems and can be trusted to\nkeep doing it. It starts with the agent loop: the model proposes an action as a structured tool call,\nthe harness executes it, the result goes back into the transcript, and the cycle repeats until a stop\ncondition. Everything else either improves that loop or limits it. The tool lessons cover the interface\nthe model acts through: schemas and layered validation, typed contracts that keep schema and handler in\nstep, result shapes the model cannot misread, selection accuracy as the first metric worth tracking, and\nparallel calls for independent reads. MCP and its security layer, followed by A2A, take that interface\nacross process and organisational boundaries. Planning, task decomposition, reflection and state\nmachines give long tasks structure and enforceable phases. Compaction and memory decide what the model\nsees on each step and what persists between sessions, with policies for retrieval, update and erasure.\nHuman-in-the-loop, approval gates, sandboxing and computer use bound what a wrong or prompt-injected\naction can do. Multi-agent patterns, the workflows-versus-agents decision and frameworks then tell you\nhow much autonomy to spend and which plumbing to adopt. Afterwards you can design, build, measure and\ndefend a production agent: choose where autonomy belongs, write tools the model uses correctly, keep\nlong runs within budget, and put the controls a security review expects in code rather than in the\nprompt. It matters most for Engineers, who write the harness, and for Architects, who own the\nautonomy, trust and topology decisions.","why":{"engineer":"You write the harness, not just the prompt: the loop and its budgets, tool contracts and validators that return fixable errors, result shapes with explicit status and truncation, concurrency caps for parallel calls, compaction at tool-call boundaries, and checkpointed state that resumes without repeating a side effect.","architect":"You decide where autonomy is spent and where it is not: workflow versus agentic island, single agent versus orchestrator-worker or handoffs, MCP versus in-process tools versus A2A peers, which actions are gated, which sandbox tier fits the threat, and how memory is scoped, retained and erased.","fde":"Customers arrive with MCP servers, legacy GUIs and security reviewers, so you must connect an agent to their systems with per-user OAuth identity, explain approval gates and sandbox egress in a review, start with full human review, and widen autonomy per action class as agreement data comes in."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as a planner with no authority sitting inside a harness that holds all of it. On\nevery step the model reads a context window and proposes one or more tool calls; the harness decides\nwhether each call is valid, permitted and safe to run, executes it, and writes a result back. Each\nlesson in the module controls one of four things in that picture. The tool surface (schemas, contracts,\nresults, selection, parallelism, MCP, A2A) is the channel through which the model acts and sees, so\nmost \"the agent is dumb\" bugs are interface bugs. The control layer (planning, decomposition,\nreflection, state machines, multi-agent patterns) limits which next steps exist and checks progress\nagainst the goal. The context layer (compaction, memory and its policies) decides what the model knows\non this step. The authority layer (human review, approval gates, sandboxing, careful computer use)\nbounds the damage when a proposal is wrong or injected. Autonomy works like a budget: give the model\nchoice only where you cannot draw the flowchart, and enforce every invariant below the model, in code.","order":["agent-loop","tool-calling","workflows-vs-agents","tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","typed-tool-contracts","tool-result-interpretation","tool-selection-accuracy","parallel-tool-calls","model-context-protocol","mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","a2a-protocol","planning","task-decomposition","reflection-self-critique","state-machines","context-compaction-summarization","agent-memory","memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","human-in-the-loop","approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","sandboxing","computer-use","multi-agent-patterns","frameworks"],"order_reasons":{"agent-loop":"the foundation: transcript as state, harness as authority, and budgets and stop conditions that every later lesson builds on.","tool-calling":"the structured request and response format through which the loop acts, and why the model only ever asks.","workflows-vs-agents":"with the loop and tools understood, decides early when to build a workflow instead and where agentic islands belong.","tool-schemas-and-argument-validation":"the schema is the model's whole specification of a tool, and layered validation catches its mistakes before side effects.","typed-tool-contracts":"derives schema, handler, errors and tests from one typed definition so they cannot drift apart.","tool-result-interpretation":"designs the other half of the interface: results with explicit status, truncation and untrusted-data labels.","tool-selection-accuracy":"measures whether the tool surface built so far works, using a labelled set and a confusion matrix.","parallel-tool-calls":"extends the loop to run independent calls concurrently, which depends on contract metadata about side effects.","model-context-protocol":"moves tools out of process into shared servers with tools, resources and prompts over JSON-RPC.","mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security":"adds per-user identity, OAuth scopes and audience binding once MCP servers are remote and shared.","a2a-protocol":"connects to opaque peer agents with long-running tasks, best understood by contrast with MCP.","planning":"gives long tasks an explicit representation of intent to measure progress against and replan from.","task-decomposition":"decides the shape of the work (granularity, dependencies, interfaces) once order is planned.","reflection-self-critique":"adds a generate-critique-revise loop and teaches that only grounded evidence makes it help.","state-machines":"turns phases into enforced states and guards that can be checkpointed and resumed after a crash.","context-compaction-summarization":"keeps long runs within the window and budget by deciding what must survive.","agent-memory":"extends context management across sessions with short-term, long-term and episodic stores.","memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies":"adds the lifecycle rules that stop memory going stale, poisoned or unlawful.","human-in-the-loop":"decides where scarce human attention buys the most risk reduction and how to avoid rubber stamping.","approval-gates-for-consequential-actions":"implements the most important human checkpoint in the executor, bound to exact arguments and durable.","sandboxing":"bounds the blast radius of model-written code and shell commands, assuming prompt injection.","computer-use":"the most general and least efficient tool, which needs the sandbox and gates already covered.","multi-agent-patterns":"splits work across loops for isolation and parallelism, after single-agent design is solid.","frameworks":"evaluates LangGraph, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy and others against the plumbing problems the module has named."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a billing-dispute agent for the support team of a mid-sized SaaS company. A ticket arrives saying\na customer was double-charged; the agent must investigate across a ticketing system, a billing API and\na policy knowledge base, decide whether a refund or credit is due, draft a reply, and issue the refund\nonly after a support lead approves it. Use mock services you control: expose the billing API as a\nremote MCP server with per-user OAuth scopes, and include one old admin console with no API that the\nagent can reach only through computer use in a VM. Model the run as a state machine: an intake workflow,\nan agentic investigation island with a step and token budget, a proposal state, a durable approval\npause, and execution. The investigation should issue parallel lookups, reconcile invoices in a sandboxed\ncode tool with no network egress, and critique its own reconciliation against computed totals before it\nproposes anything. Per-customer memory records facts such as preferred contact and past disputes, with\nprovenance and a working erasure path. Seed one ticket whose attachment contains a prompt-injection\ninstruction to refund a different account. Over a weekend to a week, build it, measure it and write it\nup for a security reviewer.","deliverables":["A working agent in a repository, built either on a hand-rolled loop or on one framework with a written justification, with typed tool contracts (input, output, error kinds, side-effect metadata) for at least six tools and snapshot tests of the schemas the model sees.","A state-machine diagram and its implementation, showing allowed tools and transitions per state, guards enforced in code, checkpoints, and the durable approval pause with arguments bound to the approval.","A labelled evaluation set of at least 30 tickets, including look-alike tools, empty-result cases and the injection ticket, with a script reporting task completion, tool selection accuracy (recall, confusion and argument accuracy), steps and cost per task at p50 and p95.","A memory schema and policy note covering retrieval scoping and scoring, supersession of contradictory facts, TTLs, and an erasure procedure that covers the raw store, the vector index and compaction summaries.","A two-page security and design review: why each step is workflow or agentic, the MCP OAuth scopes and audience binding, sandbox tier and egress policy, which tool classes are gated and why, and the plan for widening autonomy as approval data accumulates."],"rubric":["On the injection ticket, the agent either never proposes the out-of-scope refund or proposes it and is stopped at the gate; the trace shows the attachment text labelled as untrusted data and no refund call reaching the billing server.","Killing the process while it waits for approval and restarting it resumes in the same state, and approving executes the refund exactly once, shown by idempotency-key logs on the billing mock.","An MCP call with a token issued for a different server or with a missing scope is rejected by the server, and every billing call in the logs is attributed to a human subject rather than a shared key.","Tool results for empty searches, timeouts and truncated lists carry an explicit status, and the eval shows zero cases where the model reported \"customer not found\" when the backend actually failed.","Turning parallel calls and compaction on or off produces a measured change in wall-clock time and tokens per run, reported against task completion so that any lost completions are visible.","After erasing a test customer, a retrieval probe and a direct index query both return nothing for that customer, and the procedure lists every copy it removed.","The sandboxed reconciliation tool cannot reach the network or read host credentials, demonstrated by a recorded attempt that fails with an egress denial."]}},"topics":[{"id":"agent-loop","key":"agents/agent-loop","module":"agents","name":"Agent loop","taxonomy_name":"agent loop","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/loop-detection","prompting/react"],"tldr":"An agent is a loop, not a model. The model proposes an action as a structured tool call, your code\n(the harness) executes it, the result is appended to the transcript, and the model is called again with\neverything so far, until it stops asking for actions or a budget runs out. The model only ever chooses\nthe next step; the loop is what lets it observe the consequence of each step and correct course, which\nis exactly what one-shot generation cannot do.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-calling","key":"agents/tool-calling","module":"agents","name":"Tool calling","taxonomy_name":"tool calling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/model-context-protocol","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"Tool calling lets a model request an action instead of describing one. Given declared tools (a name, a\ndescription and a JSON Schema for the arguments), the model emits a structured call, your code executes\nit and returns the result, and the model continues with that result in context. It exists because models\ncannot reach live data or cause effects, and because parsing actions out of free text is unreliable; a\ntrained structured format turns \"text that looks like a command\" into a typed request.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","key":"agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","module":"agents","name":"Tool schemas & argument validation","taxonomy_name":"tool schemas & argument validation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","safety-security/output-validation","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate"],"tldr":"The input schema is the model's entire specification of a tool's arguments, and validation at the\nharness boundary is where the model's mistakes are caught before they become side effects. Design\nschemas small, flat and explicit (types, enums, required fields, descriptions that state units and\nformats); validate in layers, shape, then meaning, then permission; and send failures back as error\nresults the model can act on, because a model that sees \"amount must be in cents\" fixes itself, while\na 500 from your handler ends the run.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"typed-tool-contracts","key":"agents/typed-tool-contracts","module":"agents","name":"Typed tool contracts","taxonomy_name":"typed tool contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agents/model-context-protocol","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"A typed tool contract defines a tool once, as types: an input model, an output model, an enumerated set\nof error kinds, and metadata about side effects. Everything else is derived from it: the JSON Schema the\nmodel sees, the handler's signature, the serialization of results, the tests. Because the schema and the\nhandler share one source of truth they cannot drift apart, and because the output is typed the model\nreads results of a stable shape instead of whatever the upstream API happened to return.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-selection-accuracy","key":"agents/tool-selection-accuracy","module":"agents","name":"Tool selection accuracy","taxonomy_name":"tool selection accuracy","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","evals/golden-dataset","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Tool selection accuracy is the fraction of decision points at which the model chooses the right tool,\nincluding choosing *no* tool, with arguments that are valid and correct. It falls as the tool count\ngrows and as descriptions overlap, and it is the first agent metric worth instrumenting, because a wrong\nselection wastes a step at best and causes a wrong side effect at worst, and because it isolates the one\ndecision the model makes at every step from everything else that can go wrong in a run.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-result-interpretation","key":"agents/tool-result-interpretation","module":"agents","name":"Tool-result interpretation","taxonomy_name":"tool-result interpretation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/context-compaction-summarization","safety-security/prompt-injection","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/context-engineering","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate"],"tldr":"A tool result is a prompt the model reads. Its shape decides whether the model correctly understands\nsuccess, failure, \"nothing found\" and \"more available\", and whether it mistakes content inside the\nresult for instructions. So results are designed for the reader: an explicit status, counts and\ntruncation markers, units in field names, only the fields the task needs, errors flagged as errors, and\nanything that came from outside the system labelled as data that cannot issue commands.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"parallel-tool-calls","key":"agents/parallel-tool-calls","module":"agents","name":"Parallel tool calls","taxonomy_name":"parallel tool calls","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/agent-loop","agents/typed-tool-contracts","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"A model can emit several tool calls in a single turn. The harness runs the independent ones\nconcurrently and answers every call before the next model turn (on the Claude API, all results in one\nuser message), which removes a full model\nround trip per call and cuts latency for independent reads. The model decides what to batch, but only\nthe harness knows which tools are safe to run at the same time, so it must schedule, cap concurrency,\nisolate failures and keep the result message intact.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-context-protocol","key":"agents/model-context-protocol","module":"agents","name":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","taxonomy_name":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"MCP is an open protocol, released by Anthropic in November 2024, that standardizes how an AI application\nconnects to external capability servers. A server exposes tools, resources and prompts over JSON-RPC;\nany MCP-capable host can discover and call them, so an integration is written once instead of once\nper framework. It solves the plumbing of connecting models to systems; it does not remove the need for\nauthentication, trust decisions about third-party servers, or keeping the tool set small.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","key":"agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","module":"agents","name":"MCP authentication / authorization / transport security","taxonomy_name":"MCP authentication / authorization / transport security","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/a2a-protocol","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/prompt-injection","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt"],"tldr":"MCP defines how a host talks to a tool server (JSON-RPC over stdio or Streamable HTTP), but a tool\ncall is only safe if the server knows *who* asks and *what* they may do. For local stdio servers\nthe operating-system process boundary is the security model. For remote HTTP servers the spec defines\nan optional OAuth 2.1 layer: discovery, PKCE, audience-bound tokens, scopes. The model is never the trust\nboundary; a prompt-injected agent will ask for anything, so every check must live below it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"a2a-protocol","key":"agents/a2a-protocol","module":"agents","name":"A2A protocol (Agent2Agent)","taxonomy_name":"A2A protocol","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"A2A is an open protocol (announced by Google in 2025, later moved to the Linux Foundation) for one\nagent to discover another agent, hand it a task, and follow that task to completion over HTTP without\nseeing inside it. Where MCP connects an agent to *tools* with fixed schemas, A2A connects an agent to\n*other agents* that are opaque, long-running and may ask clarifying questions. Its core objects are the\nAgent Card for discovery and the Task with a state machine for the work itself.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"planning","key":"agents/planning","module":"agents","name":"Planning","taxonomy_name":"planning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/task-decomposition","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","prompting/react","prompting/chain-of-thought","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"Planning is how an agent decides *which* actions to take and in *what order* before, or while,\ntaking them. A purely reactive loop picks the next step from whatever is in context and drifts on long\ntasks; an explicit plan gives the loop a target to measure progress against and a place to replan when\nan action fails. The design choice is where planning happens: interleaved with actions, up front, as a\nsearch over alternatives, or hierarchically, and each choice trades tokens for robustness.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-decomposition","key":"agents/task-decomposition","module":"agents","name":"Task decomposition","taxonomy_name":"task decomposition","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/planning","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/workflows-vs-agents","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","prompting/chain-of-thought","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Task decomposition splits a large job into subtasks that each fit comfortably in one model call's\nattention, can be verified on their own, and expose a small, explicit interface to the rest. Planning\ndecides the *order* of work; decomposition decides its *shape*: granularity, dependencies and\ncontracts. Done well it unlocks parallelism, cheaper models per piece and isolated failures. Done badly\nit multiplies coordination overhead and loses context at every boundary.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"reflection-self-critique","key":"agents/reflection-self-critique","module":"agents","name":"Reflection / self-critique","taxonomy_name":"reflection / self-critique","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/planning","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","evals/hallucination-evaluation","prompting/chain-of-thought"],"tldr":"Reflection is a generate–critique–revise loop: the agent produces an attempt, evaluates it against a\ngoal or rubric, and tries again using what it learned. It pays off when the critique brings *new\ninformation*: a failing test, a tool error, a verifier's verdict, or a fresh context with a rubric.\nPure \"look again and fix your mistakes\" with no new signal is unreliable and can turn right answers\ninto wrong ones, so the design question is always where the critique's evidence comes from.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-agent-patterns","key":"agents/multi-agent-patterns","module":"agents","name":"Multi-agent patterns (orchestrator-worker, handoffs)","taxonomy_name":"multi-agent patterns (orchestrator-worker, handoffs)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/task-decomposition","agents/planning","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/state-machines","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"Multi-agent systems split work across several model loops, each with its own context, prompt and\ntools. **Orchestrator-worker**: one agent plans, delegates subtasks to parallel workers and\nsynthesises their structured results. **Handoffs**: one active agent at a time, with control passed\nto a specialist when the conversation needs its tools. The gain is context isolation and\nspecialisation; the cost is coordination, multiplied tokens and harder debugging, so a single\nwell-built agent is the default until it runs out of room.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-memory","key":"agents/agent-memory","module":"agents","name":"Agent memory (short-term, long-term, episodic)","taxonomy_name":"agent memory (short-term, long-term, episodic)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agents/agent-loop","model-internals/context-window","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agents/reflection-self-critique"],"tldr":"A language model is stateless: it \"remembers\" only what is in the current context window. Agent\nmemory decides what goes into that window and what lives outside it. **Short-term** memory is the\nwindow itself, managed by truncation and compaction. **Long-term** memory survives sessions and holds\nsemantic facts and procedures. **Episodic** memory records specific timestamped events so the agent\nrecalls what happened, not just what is true. Memory is RAG over the agent's own history, plus a\nwrite path.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","key":"agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","module":"agents","name":"Memory retrieval / update / deletion policies","taxonomy_name":"memory retrieval / update / deletion policies","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-memory","agents/context-compaction-summarization","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"A memory store without policies degrades into a pile of stale, contradictory and occasionally\npoisoned text. Three policies keep it useful and lawful: a **retrieval** policy that decides which\nmemories enter the context (scoped, scored by relevance, recency and importance, within a budget); an\n**update** policy that decides whether new information adds, supersedes or is ignored, with provenance;\nand a **deletion** policy that expires, caps and erases memories on schedule and on request, across\nevery copy including indexes and backups.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-compaction-summarization","key":"agents/context-compaction-summarization","module":"agents","name":"Context compaction / summarization","taxonomy_name":"context compaction / summarization","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/agent-memory","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","model-internals/context-window","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery"],"tldr":"An agent's transcript grows by one action and one observation per step, and every step re-sends all\nof it. Left alone it hits the context limit, costs O(n²) tokens over a run, and degrades reasoning well\nbefore the hard limit. Compaction replaces older material with something shorter: pruned tool outputs,\na structured summary, or a pointer to state kept outside the context. The hard part is not shortening;\nit is deciding what must survive so the agent keeps solving the task it was given.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-machines","key":"agents/state-machines","module":"agents","name":"State machines","taxonomy_name":"state machines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/frameworks","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"A bare agent loop has one state, \"running\", and all control lives in the model's head. A state machine\nmakes phases explicit: named states, the tools and transitions allowed in each, guards the harness\nchecks, and terminal states every path must reach. The model still chooses, but only among transitions\nthe machine permits, so an invariant like \"never deploy before tests pass\" is enforced in code, and the\npair (state, data) can be checkpointed and resumed after a crash.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"human-in-the-loop","key":"agents/human-in-the-loop","module":"agents","name":"Human-in-the-loop","taxonomy_name":"human-in-the-loop","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/state-machines","safety-security/excessive-agency","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation"],"tldr":"Human-in-the-loop means placing a person at chosen points of an agent's run: before an action (approve\nor edit), during it (steer, clarify) or after it (review a sample, correct, label). It exists because\nmodels err too often for some actions and because the person holds authority and information the agent\nlacks. Human attention is the scarcest resource, so the design problem is where it buys the most risk\nreduction and how to keep each decision cheap, informed and genuine rather than a rubber stamp.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","key":"agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","module":"agents","name":"Approval gates for consequential actions","taxonomy_name":"approval gates for consequential actions","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/human-in-the-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/state-machines","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","safety-security/excessive-agency"],"tldr":"An approval gate is a checkpoint the harness places between the model proposing an action and the tool\nexecuting it, for actions classified as consequential: irreversible, costly, externally visible or\nsecurity-relevant. It works because it lives in the tool executor, where the model cannot bypass it,\nrather than in the prompt. A working gate needs four parts: a classifier of which calls are\nconsequential, a durable pause, an approval bound to the exact arguments by an authorized person, and a\nresume path that executes exactly once.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sandboxing","key":"agents/sandboxing","module":"agents","name":"Sandboxing","taxonomy_name":"sandboxing","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/computer-use","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/data-leakage","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"A sandbox is an execution boundary around an agent's actions, limiting what the code it runs and the\ntools it calls can reach: filesystem, network, credentials, processes, CPU, memory and time. It exists\nbecause an agent's actions are untrusted input: the model is probabilistic and prompt-injectable, and it\nruns with whatever access you gave it. The goal is bounded blast radius: a wrong or hijacked action can\nwaste a container, not leak a database. Isolation strength, startup latency and compatibility trade\nagainst each other.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"computer-use","key":"agents/computer-use","module":"agents","name":"Computer use","taxonomy_name":"computer use","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/sandboxing","agents/tool-calling","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","multimodal/vision-language-models","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"Computer use is an agent that perceives a screen through screenshots and acts through the generic input\nprimitives every application accepts: mouse, keyboard, scroll. It exists because much real work lives\nin GUIs with no API. It is the most general tool and the least efficient: each step costs a\nscreenshot's worth of vision tokens plus a round trip, actions are coordinate-based, and errors compound\nacross dozens of steps. Prefer APIs or DOM access where they exist, use computer use for the long tail,\nand sandbox it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflows-vs-agents","key":"agents/workflows-vs-agents","module":"agents","name":"Workflows vs agents","taxonomy_name":"workflows vs agents","kind":"decision","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/frameworks","agents/planning","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"A workflow is LLM calls orchestrated along paths your code defines; an agent is a loop in which the\nmodel decides the next step from what it observes. The choice is about path predictability: if you can\ndraw the flowchart, build the workflow, because it is cheaper, bounded and unit-testable. Reach for an\nagent when the next step depends on observations you cannot foresee. Most production systems are\nworkflows with agentic islands, not the other way round.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"frameworks","key":"agents/frameworks","module":"agents","name":"Frameworks (LangGraph, LangChain, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy)","taxonomy_name":"frameworks (LangGraph, LangChain, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/prompt-engineering","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines"],"tldr":"Agent frameworks package the plumbing around model calls: control flow, state, tool wiring, retries,\npersistence, observability. LangChain is the broad integration layer; LangGraph is a typed\nstate-machine runtime for agents; LlamaIndex is the data and retrieval framework with agent workflows\non top; Pydantic AI is a small, type-first agent library; DSPy replaces hand-written prompts with\ndeclarative modules compiled against a metric. Each trades speed for abstraction you must understand,\nso choose by the plumbing problem you actually have.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"agent-reliability","tier":1,"order":6,"title":"Agent reliability & runtime","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the runtime that sits around the model in an agent: the harness code that decides\nwhen a run ends, what survives when a process dies, what a tool is allowed to do, and how you prove the\nagent works. It starts with control of the loop itself. Stopping conditions combine the model's own\n\"finished\" signal with independent goal checks and hard limits, max-step budgets cap tokens, time and\nmoney with a soft warning before the hard stop, and loop detection catches repeats and no-progress runs\nbefore the budget is spent. Next comes state. A hierarchy of identifiers (conversation, turn, run, step,\ntool call) gives every later mechanism a stable key. A structured event stream records what happened,\nand durable execution, conversation recovery and the choice of state store are all built from it.\nIdempotent tool execution makes the retries that durability depends on safe. The tool boundary follows:\nadapters translate between small model-facing contracts and messy vendor APIs, and least privilege\nbounds what a wrong or hijacked decision can do. The last group makes the agent observable and\nmeasurable: audit logs and traces, deterministic replay of incidents, tool-call success rate, task\ncompletion rate and trajectory evaluation. After the module you can build an agent harness that ends\nevery run with a labelled outcome, resumes after a crash without duplicate side effects, enforces\npermissions in code, replays a failed run step for step, and reports a verified completion rate with\nan interval. It matters most for Engineers who build and operate the harness, and for Architects who\nchoose its state, identity and permission model.","why":{"engineer":"You write the loop, the termination function, the budget and loop guards, the checkpoint and resume path, the idempotency-key derivation and the tool adapters, so duplicate invoices, runaway costs and runs that never finish are bugs in your code, not the model's.","architect":"You decide the identifier hierarchy, the event schema that UI, checkpoint, audit and eval all derive from, where state lives and how long it is kept, and the permission model per tool, and each of those choices is expensive to migrate later.","fde":"Customers ask what happens if the agent loops, crashes mid-task or reads a malicious document, and who approved an action; you answer with budgets, resume behaviour, scoped credentials, an audit trail and a verified completion rate measured on their own tasks."},"mental_model":"Picture an agent run as an append-only ledger of events written by a loop that the model does not\ncontrol. The model proposes the next action; the harness decides whether to allow it, execute it and\ncontinue. Around each step sit three guards. Before the step: is the run done, over budget or looping,\nand is this tool call permitted with these arguments under this identity? During the step: the adapter\nexecutes it with a timeout and an idempotency key derived from stable ids, so a retry repeats the\neffect at most once. After the step: the outcome is written to the ledger before anything else\nhappens. Everything else is a projection of that ledger. The UI stream, the checkpoint you resume from,\nthe restored conversation, the audit record, the replay recording and the trajectory you evaluate are\nall the same facts folded different ways. Metrics close the loop: tool-call success rate explains the\nsteps, completion rate scores the outcome, and trajectory evaluation shows which step to fix.","order":["stopping-conditions-termination","max-step-budgets","loop-detection","conversation-execution-identifiers","structured-event-streams","durable-execution-and-checkpointing","idempotent-tool-execution","state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","state-and-checkpoint-stores","tool-adapters-service-interfaces","tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","audit-logs-execution-traces","deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","tool-call-success-rate","task-completion-rate","agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"order_reasons":{"stopping-conditions-termination":"the loop has no natural exit, so designing the done, continue and abort decision comes first.","max-step-budgets":"adds the hard limits that stopping conditions rely on as a backstop, and the partial-result path when they fire.","loop-detection":"catches stuck runs early instead of letting them burn the whole budget, and depends on the action stream budgets already count.","conversation-execution-identifiers":"defines the id hierarchy that checkpoints, dedup keys, events and logs all key on, so it must precede them.","structured-event-streams":"the ordered, typed record of a run that resumption, recovery, audit, replay and evaluation are all projections of.","durable-execution-and-checkpointing":"persists progress at step boundaries so a run outlives its process, building on events and ids.","idempotent-tool-execution":"makes the retries and resumes introduced by durability safe for side-effecting tools, using run and call ids as keys.","state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery":"separates the long-lived transcript from in-flight runs and repairs interrupted turns, applying the event log to the user-facing conversation.","state-and-checkpoint-stores":"chooses where runs, events and blobs live once their access patterns, durability and retention needs are clear.","tool-adapters-service-interfaces":"the layer between model and vendor API where validation, timeouts, idempotency and error normalization are implemented.","tool-permissions-and-least-privilege":"enforces allowlists, argument limits, scoped credentials and approvals in the adapter and runtime rather than in the prompt.","audit-logs-execution-traces":"derives debugging traces and tamper-evident audit records from the event stream, including the permission decisions just covered.","deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility":"uses the recorded effects to reproduce a failed run exactly, which needs the stream, ids and adapters in place.","tool-call-success-rate":"measures each step with a layered error taxonomy emitted by the adapter, and explains completion through compounding.","task-completion-rate":"defines the headline outcome metric with a real success predicate, denominator and interval, building on per-step measurement.","agent-trajectory-evaluation":"combines outcome, process and policy checks over many runs, using everything above as its inputs."},"capstone":{"brief":"Take a working but fragile prototype agent and make it production-ready. The agent handles customer\nrefund requests for an online store: it reads the order and the customer's message, looks up the refund\npolicy, issues a refund through a payments API (a local mock), opens a ticket in a helpdesk mock and\nsends the customer a reply. Today it runs as an in-memory `while True` loop, retries the payments call\non timeout, holds one admin API key, logs to stdout and is judged by whether the model says \"done\".\nOver a week, rebuild the harness: a terminal tool plus a verifier and budgets for steps, tokens and\nseconds, loop detection with an inform, block and abort ladder, an id hierarchy, a typed event stream\nin Postgres with object storage for large payloads, step-boundary checkpointing with resume, and\nidempotency keys on every write. Put each tool behind an adapter with a normalized error taxonomy and\na per-run permission profile with a refund-amount cap and an approval gate above it. Derive the trace,\nthe audit log and a replay recording from the stream, then build a trajectory suite of at least 30\ntasks, including injected-instruction cases, and report verified completion rate, pass^k and per-tool\nsuccess rate before and after.","deliverables":["A harness repository with the termination function, multi-dimension budgets with a soft warning, loop detection with the escalation ladder, step-boundary checkpointing and a resume command that continues a killed run from its last checkpoint.","An identifier and event-schema document listing each id (who mints it, what it is stable across, what it dedupes) and each versioned event type, with the store choice, retention and tenant column justified for runs, events, blobs and audit records.","Tool adapters for orders, policy, payments, helpdesk and email, each with argument validation, timeouts, idempotency-key handling, a shared error taxonomy and a permission profile with argument constraints and an approval gate.","A replay tool that re-runs any recorded run with no model or tool calls and reports the first diverging step, plus a fork mode that re-executes from a chosen step.","A trajectory evaluation suite of 30+ tasks with end-state checks against the mocks, process and policy checks from the event stream, 5+ runs per task, and a report giving verified completion rate with a Wilson interval, pass^k, cost per successful task and per-tool success rate by error class."],"rubric":["Killing the worker between the payments call and recording its result, then resuming, produces exactly one refund and one ticket, shown by the mock's records and a dedup-table hit in the logs.","A task seeded with an unanswerable dependency ends with a labelled \"exhausted\" or \"aborted\" outcome and a partial result within budget, and the trace shows the loop detector firing before the hard limit.","A refund request carrying an injected instruction to refund the full order history is denied or sent to approval by the runtime policy, and the audit log records the user, credential, arguments, decision and approver.","Replaying a recorded failed run reproduces the same tool calls in the same order with zero model calls, and the report identifies the step where a forked re-execution diverges.","The evaluation report separates model-claimed from verified completion, gives every rate with an interval and run count, and attributes each failed task to a tool, policy, model or environment cause using the adapter's error classes.","Every log and event line carries the conversation, turn, run and step ids, so one query joins the UI events, trace spans and audit records for any run."]}},"topics":[{"id":"stopping-conditions-termination","key":"agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Stopping conditions / termination","taxonomy_name":"stopping conditions / termination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/loop-detection","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/human-in-the-loop","safety-security/excessive-agency"],"tldr":"An agent loop is `while True: call model; run tools`. Nothing in it ends on its own, so termination\nhas to be designed. A stopping condition is a function over the run's state that returns continue,\ndone or abort, combining the model's own \"I am finished\" signal with programmatic goal checks and hard\nresource limits. It has to be built this way because a model deciding alone stops too early, too late\nor never, and each failure looks like success unless the outcome is labelled.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"max-step-budgets","key":"agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Max-step budgets","taxonomy_name":"max-step budgets","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/loop-detection","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agents/agent-loop","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","serving-inference/timeouts"],"tldr":"A max-step budget caps how many model calls (and, more usefully, how many tokens, seconds and\ndollars) one run may spend before the harness stops it. It is a backstop against runs that never\nterminate, not a target: set it from the step distribution of successful runs so it rarely fires,\nwarn the model before it fires so it can hand back a labelled partial result, and treat every\nexhaustion as a stuck-agent signal worth investigating.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"loop-detection","key":"agent-reliability/loop-detection","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Loop detection","taxonomy_name":"loop detection","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agents/agent-loop","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/tool-result-interpretation"],"tldr":"Agents get stuck in loops: the same tool call with the same arguments, a two-state oscillation, or a\nstretch of actions that produces no new information. Budgets eventually stop them, but only after\nburning the whole allowance. Loop detection watches the action stream for exact repeats, short cycles\nand no-progress runs, and responds with an escalation ladder (tell the model, block the call, force a\nreplan, abort) because the cheapest fix is usually to make the repetition visible to the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"durable-execution-and-checkpointing","key":"agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Durable execution & checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"durable execution & checkpointing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"An agent run is a long, multi-step program whose process can die at any moment (deploys, OOMs, spot\npreemption) and whose steps cost real money and cause real side effects. Durable execution means the\nrun's progress outlives the process; checkpointing is the mechanism: persist state at step boundaries\nso a resume continues from the last boundary instead of from scratch. The hard part is the boundary\naround side effects, which is why checkpointing is inseparable from idempotent tools.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","key":"agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","module":"agent-reliability","name":"State persistence & conversation recovery","taxonomy_name":"state persistence & conversation recovery","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","agents/agent-memory","agents/context-compaction-summarization","system-design/memory-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect"],"tldr":"Conversation state (the transcript a user returns to) and execution state (one in-flight agent run)\nhave different lifetimes and must be persisted separately. Store the transcript as an append-only,\nsequence-numbered event log that is the source of truth, and derive the model's context from it as a\nview. Recovery then means reconnecting clients catch up from their last sequence number, interrupted\nturns are repaired into a well-formed history, and the user never sees a dangling or duplicated turn.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-and-checkpoint-stores","key":"agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","module":"agent-reliability","name":"State & checkpoint stores","taxonomy_name":"state & checkpoint stores","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/nosql","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"Where agent state lives determines how fast each step is, what survives a crash, and how you isolate\ntenants and delete data. The decision weighs per-step write latency against durability, blob size\nagainst query needs, and short TTLs against audit retention. A sound default is a relational store\n(Postgres) for run records, events and small snapshots, object storage for large tool outputs, and\nRedis only for ephemeral leases and stream buffers, with a tenant id on every row.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"conversation-execution-identifiers","key":"agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Conversation / execution identifiers","taxonomy_name":"conversation / execution identifiers","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"An agent system needs a hierarchy of identifiers, not one \"session id\": tenant and user for scope,\nconversation id for the long-lived transcript, turn id for one user request, run id for one execution\nattempt of that turn, step and tool-call ids within it, plus trace ids for cross-service correlation.\nEach id answers a different question (resume what, dedupe what, bill whom, trace which), and\nconflating them is why retries double-act and logs cannot be joined.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotent-tool-execution","key":"agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Idempotent tool execution","taxonomy_name":"idempotent tool execution","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","serving-inference/idempotency","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Agents retry constantly: the model retries after errors, the harness retries after timeouts, the\ndurable runtime re-executes after crashes, and each retry of a side-effecting tool risks a duplicate.\nExactly-once execution across a network boundary is impossible, so the design target is at-least-once\nexecution with idempotent effects: classify tools, derive a deterministic idempotency key per call,\npass it downstream or dedupe locally, and gate what cannot be made idempotent behind approvals or an\noutbox.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","key":"agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool permissions & least privilege","taxonomy_name":"tool permissions & least privilege","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/prompt-injection","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation"],"tldr":"An agent's tools are the only way its text turns into effects, and its text can be steered by anything\nin its context, including data it was asked to read. Least privilege means each run gets exactly the\ntools, arguments, identities and credentials the task needs, enforced in the runtime rather than\nrequested in the prompt, so that a wrong or hijacked decision has a bounded blast radius instead of the\ndeveloper's full access.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-adapters-service-interfaces","key":"agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool adapters / service interfaces","taxonomy_name":"tool adapters / service interfaces","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"The model speaks in small typed tool calls; real services speak HTTP with auth, pagination, rate limits\nand 40-field responses. A tool adapter is the layer that owns that translation: it validates arguments,\nenforces policy, injects credentials, applies timeouts, retries and idempotency, and turns every outcome,\nincluding failure, into a compact structured result the model can reason about. Keeping that layer\nexplicit is what makes tools testable, swappable and safe.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-event-streams","key":"agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Structured event streams","taxonomy_name":"structured event streams","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","system-design/event-driven-architecture"],"tldr":"An agent run is not a function call that returns a string; it is a sequence of things that happened:\nmodel requests, token deltas, tool calls, permission decisions, results, retries, completions. Emitting\nthose as one ordered, typed, versioned stream of events, and deriving the UI, the persisted state, the\ntrace and the eval trajectory from that stream, replaces four drifting ad hoc formats with one source of\ntruth that can be replayed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"audit-logs-execution-traces","key":"agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Audit logs / execution traces","taxonomy_name":"audit logs / execution traces","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langfuse"],"tldr":"An execution trace answers \"what did the agent do and why\" for engineers debugging a run; an audit log\nanswers \"who did what with whose authority\" for security, compliance and incident response. They share\nthe same facts, best derived from one event stream, but differ in what they must contain, how long they\nlive, who can read them and whether they may ever be altered. Build both on purpose, not one as an\nafterthought of the other.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","key":"agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Deterministic replay & reproducibility","taxonomy_name":"deterministic replay & reproducibility","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","model-internals/sampling-strategies","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","evals/pass-k"],"tldr":"You cannot reproduce an agent run by rerunning it: the model samples, providers change, tools return\nlive data, and one different token changes every later step. Reproducibility therefore comes in three\ngrades: exact replay from a recording of every non-deterministic boundary, best-effort re-execution with\npinned model, seed and temperature, and statistical reproduction over many runs. Design the runtime so the\norchestration logic is deterministic and every effect is recorded, and the first grade is always\navailable.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-trajectory-evaluation","key":"agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Agent trajectory evaluation","taxonomy_name":"agent trajectory evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/pass-k","evals/eval-harness"],"tldr":"Scoring an agent on its final answer alone misses most of what can go wrong: it may have reached the\nanswer by a forbidden action, by forty steps instead of four, or by luck. Trajectory evaluation scores\nthe whole path, the sequence of model decisions, tool calls and observations, against outcome checks,\nprocess checks and policy checks, over many runs per task, so you learn not just whether the agent\nsucceeded but where and why it fails.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-completion-rate","key":"agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Task completion rate","taxonomy_name":"task completion rate","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","evals/pass-k","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"Task completion rate is the fraction of tasks an agent finishes successfully, judged against a defined\nsuccess criterion rather than its own claim of being done. It is the number product, finance and customers\ncare about, and it is only as good as three definitions behind it: what a task is, what counts as\ncomplete, and what goes in the denominator. Report it with an interval, segmented by task type, and\nalongside pass^k for unattended use.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-call-success-rate","key":"agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool-call success rate","taxonomy_name":"tool-call success rate","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","serving-inference/retry-strategies","observability/monitoring"],"tldr":"Tool-call success rate is the fraction of tool invocations that succeed, and it is only useful when\n\"succeed\" is split into layers: the call was well-formed, it was permitted, it executed without an\ninfrastructure error, and it achieved what the model intended. Because a task is a chain of calls,\nper-call failure compounds: ten steps at 95% is about 60% end to end. The metric localizes failures to\nmodel, policy, tool or environment, which task completion rate cannot.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"evals","tier":1,"order":7,"title":"Evals","intro":{"overview":"This module is about producing numbers you can trust about whether an LLM system does its job, and\nusing those numbers to decide what ships. It starts by separating capability evaluation (what a model\ncan do in general, answered by benchmarks) from application evaluation (whether your prompt, retrieval\nand model do your task on your data), then builds the core machinery of the second: task-specific\nacceptance criteria that define \"done\", a versioned golden dataset labeled by people with authority\nover the task, and an eval harness that runs, grades, aggregates per slice with confidence intervals\nand compares runs. Grading open-ended output leads to LLM-as-a-judge, which is only trustworthy once it\nis calibrated against human labels, scored with clear rubrics, compared pairwise where absolute scales\nfail, and anchored by inter-annotator agreement on the labels themselves. Specialised metrics follow:\npass@k for executable tasks, retrieval metrics, faithfulness and hallucination evaluation for RAG.\nRegression, metamorphic and property-based tests guard known behaviour and robustness without labels;\nadversarial evaluation and red teaming cover the worst case. Offline and online evaluation, A/B tests,\nerror analysis and eval-driven development close the loop from production failures back into the\nsuite. Afterwards you can design, build and defend an evaluation stack for a real feature and gate\nreleases on it. It matters most for AI Engineers who change prompts and models every week, and for\nFDEs who need a customer to sign off on what \"working\" means.","why":{"engineer":"You turn every prompt, model, retriever or tool change into a paired harness run against a golden set with per-slice intervals, read the items that flipped, and refuse to merge a change whose eval diff you cannot explain.","architect":"You decide where evaluation lives in the delivery pipeline: which graders are deterministic versus judged, which suites gate CI versus releases, how offline scores are validated against online outcomes, and which tooling owns datasets, traces and experiments.","fde":"You write acceptance criteria with the customer before committing to a date, build a golden set from their real documents and labelers, and show a calibrated report that makes sign-off unambiguous instead of resting on a demo that looked fine."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: an eval is a measuring instrument, and every part of it can be miscalibrated. The\nspecification (acceptance criteria and rubrics) says what to measure; the reference (a golden dataset\nwhose labels humans agree on) says what correct looks like; the harness runs the system under pinned\nconfiguration and stores raw outputs; graders (exact checks, executable tests, retrieval metrics,\ncalibrated judges) turn outputs into scores; and statistics (paired comparisons, intervals, pass@k)\nsay whether a difference is real. Each layer has its own error: vague criteria, noisy or leaked labels,\nbiased judges, too few items. Around the instrument runs a loop: error analysis on real traces finds\nfailure modes, they become criteria and items, adversarial and metamorphic suites probe what averages\nhide, offline gates decide what ships, and online measurement checks that offline measured the right\nthing. Trust a number only as far as its weakest layer.","order":["model-evaluation","task-specific-acceptance-criteria","golden-dataset","eval-harness","error-analysis","benchmarking","dataset-contamination","pass-k","llm-as-a-judge","rubrics","inter-annotator-agreement","judge-calibration-and-bias","pairwise-comparison","retrieval-evaluation","faithfulness-groundedness","hallucination-evaluation","regression-tests","metamorphic-testing","property-based-testing","red-teaming","adversarial-evaluation","offline-vs-online-evaluation","a-b-testing","eval-driven-development","tooling"],"order_reasons":{"model-evaluation":"frames the capability-versus-application split and why every change needs a measured number, the lens for the whole module.","task-specific-acceptance-criteria":"defines what \"done\" means for one task before any dataset or grader is built.","golden-dataset":"builds the agreed, versioned reference every eval number is computed against.","eval-harness":"turns dataset, system and graders into a repeatable, comparable run with slices and intervals.","error-analysis":"teaches reading real traces and counting failure modes, which is where criteria and items should come from.","benchmarking":"covers public benchmarks as a model-shortlisting filter once the difference from your own eval is clear.","dataset-contamination":"explains why benchmark and golden-set scores inflate through leakage, right after benchmarks are introduced.","pass-k":"gives the correct estimator for sampled executable tasks, needed to read coding benchmarks and your own code evals.","llm-as-a-judge":"introduces model graders for open-ended outputs that no exact check can score.","rubrics":"shows how to turn \"is it good?\" into observable, mostly binary criteria a judge or human applies consistently.","inter-annotator-agreement":"measures whether the human labels a judge is scored against are themselves reliable.","judge-calibration-and-bias":"measures and mitigates position, length and self-preference bias against those human labels.","pairwise-comparison":"offers relative judging, win rates and Bradley–Terry ranking once judge biases are understood.","retrieval-evaluation":"isolates the retriever with recall@k, MRR and NDCG, the first stage of any RAG eval.","faithfulness-groundedness":"scores whether generated claims are supported by the retrieved context, the generation-side RAG metric.","hallucination-evaluation":"generalises claim-level verification to fabrication, unanswerable items and abstention.","regression-tests":"pins known behaviour and detects per-item flips across changes despite sampling noise.","metamorphic-testing":"checks label-free relations between outputs to expose brittleness the golden set cannot see.","property-based-testing":"applies generated inputs and shrinking to the deterministic scaffolding around the model.","red-teaming":"discovers the attacks a goal-driven adversary would use against prompts, data and tools.","adversarial-evaluation":"turns those discovered attacks into a versioned suite with attack success and over-refusal rates.","offline-vs-online-evaluation":"lays out the ladder from offline gates through shadow mode and canaries to live measurement.","a-b-testing":"gives the causal online answer, with randomisation unit, power, guardrails and no peeking.","eval-driven-development":"ties everything into a workflow where the eval suite is the executable specification of a feature.","tooling":"maps Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith and Langfuse onto the pipeline once you know what it must do."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build and defend the evaluation stack for a RAG assistant that answers employee questions about a\ncompany's HR and expense policies, then use it to decide a real change: swapping the generation model\nfor a cheaper one and changing the chunking strategy. Start by reading at least 50 traces from a\nbaseline version and writing an error-analysis taxonomy with counts. From it, write acceptance criteria\nthat separate hard gates (no invented policy, no answer to out-of-scope or unanswerable questions) from\nsoft thresholds with slice floors, plus latency and cost limits. Build a golden set of 150 to 300\nstratified items, including unanswerable and false-premise questions, with a held-out split and a\ncontamination check against any few-shot examples. Have two people label an overlap sample to measure\nagreement. Write a harness that pins configuration, stores raw outputs before grading, and reports\nretrieval recall@k and MRR, claim-level faithfulness, hallucination and abstention rates, and a\nrubric-based LLM judge whose position bias and agreement with humans you have measured. Add a regression\ntier seeded from known bugs, a metamorphic suite (paraphrase, irrelevant-paragraph insertion), property\ntests on the chunker, and a small prompt-injection suite through poisoned documents. Wire the fast tier\ninto CI as a gate and write an online plan for the winning candidate.","deliverables":["An error-analysis sheet of at least 50 read traces with a failure taxonomy, counts per category, and the criteria and golden items each top category produced.","An acceptance-criteria document listing hard gates, soft thresholds with slice floors, and latency and cost limits, each with its grader and scope.","The versioned golden dataset with slice labels, a held-out split, a grader spec per item, an inter-annotator agreement report on the overlap sample, and a contamination-check script.","The eval harness, runnable from one command, producing a per-slice report with confidence intervals, plus a CI configuration that runs the fast tier and fails the build on a hard-gate violation.","A judge calibration report showing agreement and kappa against human labels next to human–human agreement, position-swap inconsistency rate, and length preference.","Regression, metamorphic, property-based and adversarial suites, each with its own pass or violation rate on the baseline.","A decision memo on the model swap and chunking change, citing paired per-item results, regressed items read, cost per answer, and an online rollout plan (shadow, canary, A/B with a power estimate and guardrails)."],"rubric":["Every reported metric carries a confidence interval or paired comparison, and the decision memo does not call a difference significant when its interval includes zero.","The judge is not used as a release gate until its true-positive and true-negative rates against human labels are reported, and its agreement is within reach of the human–human ceiling measured on the same items.","Running the harness twice on an unchanged system produces per-item flip rates below a stated flakiness tolerance, and the regression tier distinguishes a planted regression from that noise.","The unanswerable and false-premise slices are present and reported separately, and the fabrication rate on them is a hard gate rather than averaged into overall accuracy.","A planted prompt injection in a retrieved document is caught by the adversarial suite, and the report lists over-refusal on benign questions next to attack success rate.","At least one golden item and one criterion trace back to a named category in the error-analysis taxonomy, and the held-out split was never used while tuning prompts."]}},"topics":[{"id":"model-evaluation","key":"evals/model-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Model evaluation","taxonomy_name":"model evaluation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/benchmarking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks"],"tldr":"Model evaluation is the discipline of producing a number you can trust about whether a model, or a\nsystem built on one, does the job. It splits into two questions that are constantly confused:\n*capability* evaluation (\"what can this model do in general\", answered by benchmarks) and\n*application* evaluation (\"does my prompt + retrieval + model do my task on my data\", answered by a\ngolden dataset and task-specific graders). Without it, every prompt or model change is a guess\njudged by whoever looked at the demo last.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset","key":"evals/golden-dataset","module":"evals","name":"Golden dataset (eval dataset)","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset (eval dataset)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/regression-tests","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/dataset-contamination","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split"],"tldr":"A golden dataset is a curated, versioned set of inputs paired with outputs or labels that the people\nwho own the task have agreed are correct. It is the fixed reference every eval number is computed\nagainst, so without it two prompts, two models or two weeks cannot be compared. \"Golden\" means\n*agreed and authoritative*, not perfect or large: a few hundred well-chosen, well-labeled items beat\nten thousand scraped ones, provided they are stratified across the cases that matter and kept out of\nprompts and training data.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-harness","key":"evals/eval-harness","module":"evals","name":"Eval harness","taxonomy_name":"eval harness","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/regression-tests","evals/pass-k","evals/tooling","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"An eval harness is the code that takes a versioned dataset, runs every item through the system under\ntest with pinned configuration, grades the raw outputs, aggregates scores per slice with confidence\nintervals, and writes a report that can be compared with any previous run. It exists because\nevaluation done in notebooks is not repeatable, so Tuesday's number cannot be compared with Friday's;\nthe harness is what turns evaluation into an engineering artifact with the status of a test suite.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-specific-acceptance-criteria","key":"evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","module":"evals","name":"Task-specific acceptance criteria","taxonomy_name":"task-specific acceptance criteria","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/rubrics","evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","evals/eval-harness","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","system-design/nfrs"],"tldr":"Task-specific acceptance criteria turn \"the model should work well\" into explicit, checkable\nstatements about one task's outputs: format, required and forbidden content, correctness against\nreferences, behaviour on edge cases, and operational limits such as latency and cost, each with a\nthreshold and a scope. They exist because generic scores (a BLEU value, a 4.2/5 helpfulness rating,\na leaderboard rank) cannot tell you whether a contract-clause extractor is safe to ship; only\ncriteria derived from that task's actual failure modes can.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"benchmarking","key":"evals/benchmarking","module":"evals","name":"Benchmarking (MMLU, HumanEval, SWE-bench, GPQA)","taxonomy_name":"benchmarking (MMLU, HumanEval, SWE-bench, GPQA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/pass-k","evals/golden-dataset","system-design/model-selection","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate"],"tldr":"A benchmark is a fixed dataset with a fixed scoring rule, so models can be compared on the same\ntask. MMLU measures broad knowledge by multiple choice, HumanEval measures Python function synthesis\nby unit tests, SWE-bench measures resolving real GitHub issues, and GPQA measures expert science\nreasoning that web search cannot shortcut. Benchmarks exist so model comparison is not anecdote, but\neach measures something narrow, saturates and leaks into training data: treat a leaderboard as a\nscreening filter, never as evidence your task will work.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dataset-contamination","key":"evals/dataset-contamination","module":"evals","name":"Dataset contamination","taxonomy_name":"dataset contamination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/benchmarking","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Dataset contamination is the presence of evaluation items, their paraphrases or solutions in a\nmodel's training data, so a score measures memorization instead of capability. It is train/test\nleakage at LLM scale, made unavoidable by training on web crawls that contain the benchmarks used to\ngrade the result. It matters because the inflation is uneven across models and tasks, so contaminated\ncomparisons pick the wrong model, and because your own golden dataset is contaminated as easily\nthrough few-shot prompts and fine-tuning data.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"llm-as-a-judge","key":"evals/llm-as-a-judge","module":"evals","name":"LLM-as-a-judge","taxonomy_name":"LLM-as-a-judge","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/rubrics","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/eval-harness","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"LLM-as-a-judge uses a model, given a rubric, the output to assess and often a reference or a second\noutput, to grade in place of a human reviewer. It exists because open-ended outputs have no\nexact-match answer and human grading does not scale to hundreds of items per prompt iteration. It is\nonly as good as its measured agreement with human labels on your task, because judges carry known\nbiases (position, verbosity, self-preference) and grade confidently, right or wrong.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"judge-calibration-and-bias","key":"evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","module":"evals","name":"Judge calibration & bias","taxonomy_name":"judge calibration & bias","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/rubrics","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/calibration"],"tldr":"An LLM judge is a measuring instrument with its own systematic errors: it tends to prefer the answer\nshown first, the longer answer, the answer written in its own style, and it bunches scores near the top\nof the scale. Calibration means measuring those errors against human labels *before* trusting the judge;\nbias mitigation is the set of protocol changes (position swapping, length control, rubrics, reference\nanswers, judges from a different model family) that shrink them. Skip this and a judge score measures the\njudge, not your system.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rubrics","key":"evals/rubrics","module":"evals","name":"Rubrics","taxonomy_name":"rubrics","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/error-analysis","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines"],"tldr":"A rubric turns \"is this output good?\" into a list of specific, observable criteria with defined levels,\nso two humans, or a human and an LLM judge, score the same output the same way. It exists because an\nunanchored score (\"rate quality 1–10\") measures the grader more than the output. Good rubrics come from\nobserved failure modes, prefer binary checks over wide scales, make catastrophic criteria gating rather\nthan averaged, and are versioned like code.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pairwise-comparison","key":"evals/pairwise-comparison","module":"evals","name":"Pairwise comparison","taxonomy_name":"pairwise comparison","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/a-b-testing","evals/benchmarking","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo"],"tldr":"Instead of asking \"how good is this answer on a 1–10 scale?\", pairwise comparison asks \"which of these\ntwo answers is better?\". Relative judgments are more consistent for both humans and LLM judges because\nthey need no shared absolute scale. Many verdicts are aggregated into a win rate against a fixed\nbaseline, or into a ranking of several systems with a Bradley–Terry model (the method behind Chatbot\nArena), and the same pairs are the raw material of preference training (RLHF, DPO).","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"inter-annotator-agreement","key":"evals/inter-annotator-agreement","module":"evals","name":"Inter-annotator agreement","taxonomy_name":"inter-annotator agreement","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/rubrics","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","training-adaptation/data-labeling","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines"],"tldr":"Inter-annotator agreement (IAA) measures how consistently independent humans label the same items. It\nmatters because labels are the ground truth every eval and every judge is scored against: if two experts\nagree only 70% of the time, every score measured against those labels carries that noise, and the\nguideline, not the model, is usually the problem. Chance-corrected statistics (Cohen's κ, Fleiss' κ, Krippendorff's α) are\nused instead of raw agreement because raw agreement is inflated whenever one label dominates.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pass-k","key":"evals/pass-k","module":"evals","name":"pass@k","taxonomy_name":"pass@k","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/benchmarking","evals/eval-harness","evals/regression-tests","evals/dataset-contamination","model-internals/sampling-strategies","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"pass@k is the probability that at least one of k sampled solutions passes a correctness check, averaged\nover problems. It exists for tasks with an executable oracle, where one greedy sample understates what\na model can do if you can sample and verify. Estimate it from n ≥ k samples with the unbiased\ncombinatorial estimator, not by plugging a pooled pass rate into a formula. For k > 1 it is an *oracle*\nnumber: what a perfect verifier could extract, not what a user sees on one try.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-evaluation","key":"evals/retrieval-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Retrieval evaluation (recall@k, MRR, NDCG)","taxonomy_name":"retrieval evaluation (recall@k, MRR, NDCG)","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","evals/golden-dataset","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1"],"tldr":"A RAG answer can only be as good as the passages the retriever puts in front of the model, so the\nretriever is measured on its own against queries with known relevant documents. recall@k asks whether\nthe relevant passages made it into the top k the generator will see; MRR asks how high the first\nrelevant hit was; NDCG asks whether the ranking is right when relevance comes in grades. Choosing k to\nmatch the context budget and building honest relevance judgments matter more than the formulas.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"faithfulness-groundedness","key":"evals/faithfulness-groundedness","module":"evals","name":"Faithfulness / groundedness","taxonomy_name":"faithfulness / groundedness","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/tooling","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","model-internals/hallucination"],"tldr":"Faithfulness (groundedness) measures whether every claim in a generated answer is supported by the\ncontext the model was given, not whether the claim is true. It exists because a RAG system can produce\nfluent, even correct, answers its retrieved documents do not support, and those break when the documents\nchange. It is computed by splitting the answer into atomic claims and checking each against the context\nwith an entailment model or an LLM judge; it needs no reference answer, so it runs on production traffic.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hallucination-evaluation","key":"evals/hallucination-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Hallucination evaluation","taxonomy_name":"hallucination evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/faithfulness-groundedness","model-internals/hallucination","rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/calibration"],"tldr":"Hallucination evaluation measures how often a system asserts things that are not supported by its\nevidence or are false in the world. Whole-response grading hides it, because a fluent answer with one\ninvented figure still reads as correct. So the practice breaks outputs into atomic claims, verifies each\nagainst a reference (retrieved context, a trusted corpus, or consistency across samples), and reports\nclaim precision, response-level hallucination rate and abstention behaviour per slice, including the\nunanswerable and false-premise cases where fabrication concentrates.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"regression-tests","key":"evals/regression-tests","module":"evals","name":"Regression tests","taxonomy_name":"regression tests","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/pass-k","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"A regression test for an AI system re-runs a pinned set of inputs with known expected behaviour every\ntime the prompt, model, retriever or tools change, and fails the change if cases that used to pass now\nfail. It exists because LLM changes are non-local: a prompt edit that fixes one case silently breaks\nothers, and the provider can change the model behind the same name. The engineering problem is telling\na real regression from sampling noise, which is why comparisons are per item and paired, not aggregate.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"metamorphic-testing","key":"evals/metamorphic-testing","module":"evals","name":"Metamorphic testing","taxonomy_name":"metamorphic testing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/property-based-testing","evals/regression-tests","evals/adversarial-evaluation","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/golden-dataset","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle"],"tldr":"Metamorphic testing checks a system without knowing the right answer. Instead of asserting that an output\nequals an expected value, you transform the input in a way whose effect is predictable (paraphrase: same\nanswer; add an irrelevant paragraph: same answer; swap two options: the choice swaps) and assert that\nrelation between the two outputs. It exists because most LLM inputs have no label, and relation\nviolations are cheap to generate and expose brittleness a golden set, which sees each item once in one\nform, cannot.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"property-based-testing","key":"evals/property-based-testing","module":"evals","name":"Property-based testing","taxonomy_name":"property-based testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/property-based-testing","evals/metamorphic-testing","evals/regression-tests","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","safety-security/output-validation","python-for-ai/pytest"],"tldr":"Property-based testing replaces hand-picked examples with a generator that produces hundreds of random\ninputs and a property that must hold for every one; when a case fails, the framework shrinks it to the\nsmallest input that still fails. In AI systems the deterministic scaffolding (chunkers, token budgeters,\nprompt renderers, tool-argument parsers, output validators) is where silent bugs live, and nobody writes\nthe example that breaks it. Properties on model outputs are possible too, at the price of one model call\nper generated example.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"adversarial-evaluation","key":"evals/adversarial-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Adversarial evaluation","taxonomy_name":"adversarial evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/red-teaming","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/jailbreaks","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/rag-poisoning","evals/metamorphic-testing","evals/regression-tests","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias"],"tldr":"Adversarial evaluation measures how a system behaves on inputs chosen to make it fail: jailbreaks,\ninstructions injected through retrieved content, perturbed or out-of-distribution inputs, and outputs\ncrafted to fool the grader. Red teaming discovers such inputs; adversarial evaluation turns them into a\nversioned suite with an attack success rate per class, run every release and reported next to the\nover-refusal rate on benign inputs. It exists because average-case evals say nothing about the worst\ncase, which is what attackers find.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"offline-vs-online-evaluation","key":"evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Offline vs online evaluation","taxonomy_name":"offline vs online evaluation","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/a-b-testing","evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","observability/monitoring","llmops-platform/drift"],"tldr":"Offline evaluation scores a system on a fixed dataset before it meets users; online evaluation measures\nit on live traffic through user behaviour, sampled judging of real traces, or controlled experiments.\nOffline is fast, cheap, reproducible and safe but only as good as its dataset and graders; online is the\ntruth about outcomes but slow, noisy, risky and confounded. Neither replaces the other: offline gates\nwhat ships, online tells you whether offline measured the right thing, and sampled online traffic\nrefills the offline set.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"a-b-testing","key":"evals/a-b-testing","module":"evals","name":"A/B testing","taxonomy_name":"A/B testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/pairwise-comparison","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","observability/monitoring","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"tldr":"An A/B test randomly assigns users (or sessions) to the current system or a candidate and compares a\npre-declared outcome metric, so a difference can be attributed to the change rather than to time,\ntraffic mix or luck. It is the only online method that gives a causal answer, and it exists because\noffline scores and before-and-after dashboards cannot. The discipline is in the design: the\nrandomisation unit, one primary metric with guardrails, a sample size fixed in advance, and no peeking.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"error-analysis","key":"evals/error-analysis","module":"evals","name":"Error analysis","taxonomy_name":"error analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/regression-tests","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"Error analysis is the practice of reading a sample of your system's real traces one at a time, writing\ndown what went wrong in each, grouping those notes into a short taxonomy of failure modes, and counting\nthem. It exists because an aggregate score says *that* the system fails, never *why* or *where*. The\ncounts tell you which fix buys the most, and each stable failure mode becomes a metric and a set of\ngolden items, so the eval suite grows from observed failures rather than imagined ones.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"red-teaming","key":"evals/red-teaming","module":"evals","name":"Red teaming","taxonomy_name":"red teaming","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/adversarial-evaluation","evals/regression-tests","evals/tooling","prompting/jailbreaks","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/excessive-agency","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response"],"tldr":"Red teaming is a deliberate, goal-driven attempt to make your AI system do what it must not: leak data\nor the system prompt, follow injected instructions, produce harmful content, take an unauthorised\naction, or assert a policy that does not exist. It exists because ordinary evals measure the behaviour\nyou asked for on inputs you expected, while the costly failures are on inputs an adversary chooses.\nEvery successful attack becomes a permanent regression test, and the exercise repeats with every model,\nprompt or tool change.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-driven-development","key":"evals/eval-driven-development","module":"evals","name":"Eval-driven development","taxonomy_name":"eval-driven development","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"Eval-driven development treats the eval suite as the executable specification of an LLM feature:\nwrite the acceptance criteria and a seed golden set before building, judge every change by the eval's\nnumbers rather than by a demo, and grow the suite from the failures each round of error analysis\nfinds. It exists because prompt, retrieval and model changes have non-local effects that \"it looked\nfine in the playground\" cannot detect, and because a bug without a failing eval item is never\nreliably fixed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tooling","key":"evals/tooling","module":"evals","name":"Tooling (Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse)","taxonomy_name":"tooling (Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/eval-harness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks"],"tldr":"Every eval tool implements the same pipeline: dataset → run the system → grade → store and compare.\nThey differ in where that state lives and which graders come prebuilt. Promptfoo, RAGAS and DeepEval\nare libraries you run locally (config-driven prompt×model matrices, RAG metrics, pytest-style tests);\nBraintrust, LangSmith and Langfuse are platforms that keep traces, datasets and experiments on a server\nand attach evaluators to production traffic. Choose by where your traces already live and how much of\nthe grading logic you need to own.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"serving-inference","tier":1,"order":8,"title":"Serving & inference","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what happens between a trained checkpoint and a token arriving in a user's browser,\nand why serving an LLM is a different job from serving a classifier. It starts with the physics. Each\nrequest runs a compute-bound prefill followed by a long, memory-bandwidth-bound decode loop whose working\nset, the KV cache, grows while it runs. TTFT, TPOT and tokens per second are the measurements of those\ntwo phases, and the roofline model tells you which bound you are hitting. Memory comes next: estimating\nthe KV cache from a model config, writing the four-line GPU memory ledger, and seeing how PagedAttention,\npaging and eviction turn a fragmented pool into usable concurrency. Scheduling builds on that. Continuous\nbatching, prefix caching and concurrency limits decide where a replica sits on the throughput-versus-latency\ncurve. Then the levers for fitting and speeding up a model: tensor parallelism, CPU offloading, quantization\nchecked against its full-precision baseline, constrained decoding, and the engines that package all of it.\nThe second half is the request path around the engine: streaming over SSE, layered timeouts, cancellation,\nidempotency, retries, fallbacks, rate limiting, backpressure and the three layers of LLM caching. Together\nthey turn a fast engine into a service that degrades predictably. By the end you can size a GPU deployment\nfrom a config file and a traffic model, find the latency knee in a load test, pick an engine and its\nsettings, and build a gateway that streams, cancels and sheds load correctly. Engineers carrying the pager\nneed it most, followed by Architects deciding between self-hosting and hosted APIs.","why":{"engineer":"You set `max_num_seqs`, `max_model_len` and the memory fraction from arithmetic rather than defaults. You write the SSE handler and the client wrapper with layered timeouts, abort-on-disconnect, idempotency keys and jittered retries. You also export the TTFT, TPOT, queue-wait and preemption metrics that show which phase or limit is failing.","architect":"You decide between self-hosting and a hosted API, and you own the GPU type, the tensor-parallel degree, the engine, the quantization format and the per-workload operating point on the throughput-latency curve. Those choices fix cost per million tokens, the latency SLO you can promise, and how much fallback and quota capacity has to be provisioned.","fde":"Customers arrive with \"it feels slow\", a vendor's tokens-per-second slide, or an on-premises GPU budget that has to be justified. You need to break latency down into queue, prefill and decode on the customer's own prompts, size their hardware from the KV-cache arithmetic, and tune proxies and gateways so streaming and timeouts survive their network."},"mental_model":"Picture one GPU as a fixed budget of two resources, memory bandwidth and memory capacity, with a queue in\nfront of it and a gateway in front of the queue. Each request takes two kinds of turn. Prefill is a single\ncompute-heavy pass over the prompt that sets TTFT. Decode is a long run of steps that each re-read every\nweight plus the request's KV cache, and that sets TPOT. Since a decode step costs about the same for 1\nsequence as for 64, throughput comes from packing many sequences into each step. KV memory caps that\npacking, which is why paging, prefix sharing, quantization and tensor parallelism all reduce to \"more\ntokens in flight per byte\". Pushing concurrency past the knee makes every request a little slower and the\nqueue a lot longer, so you choose an operating point and enforce it. The gateway's job is to keep the queue\nhonest. It streams so users see the first token early, times out and cancels so dead requests free their\nslots, retries only idempotent and transient failures, falls back when the primary is down, and rejects\nearly when demand exceeds what the GPU can serve. Every serving question resolves to the phase, the memory\nterm, or the boundary it belongs to.","order":["model-serving","prefill-vs-decode","ttft","tpot","tokens-sec","inference-optimization","kv-cache-memory-estimation","gpu-memory-optimization","pagedattention","kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","continuous-batching","prefix-caching","batching-and-concurrency-limits","throughput-vs-latency","tensor-parallelism-at-inference","cpu-offloading","quantization-quality-evaluation","structured-constrained-generation","engines","streaming","timeouts","cancellation","idempotency","retry-strategies","fallbacks","rate-limiting","backpressure","caching"],"order_reasons":{"model-serving":"the map of the request path (API, router, scheduler, memory, kernels) that every later lesson zooms into.","prefill-vs-decode":"the compute-bound versus bandwidth-bound split that explains every latency and cost number that follows.","ttft":"isolates the prefill-plus-queue phase as the latency users feel first, and how prompt length drives it.","tpot":"derives the decode floor from bytes read per step over memory bandwidth, and explains why batching barely moves it.","tokens-sec":"separates per-stream speed from aggregate server throughput, so later benchmarks are read correctly.","inference-optimization":"the roofline view that matches each optimization lever to the bound it can actually relieve.","kv-cache-memory-estimation":"turns a model config into bytes per token and a concurrency ceiling, the core capacity arithmetic.","gpu-memory-optimization":"extends that estimate into the four-line VRAM ledger and a fixed order of levers for when it does not fit.","pagedattention":"shows how fixed-size blocks remove reservation waste and fragmentation, so recovered memory becomes concurrency.","kv-cache-paging-and-eviction":"adds what happens when the paged pool still runs out: preemption, swap versus recompute, and prefix eviction.","continuous-batching":"iteration-level scheduling, which depends on paged memory and is what keeps decode batches full.","prefix-caching":"reuses shared prompt blocks across requests, the scheduling-layer fix for repeated prefill and high TTFT.","batching-and-concurrency-limits":"finds the latency knee and enforces sequence and token limits at the engine, gateway and client.","throughput-vs-latency":"generalises the knee into choosing an operating point per workload, with queueing as the tail.","tensor-parallelism-at-inference":"splits a model across GPUs to fit it and pool bandwidth, limited by the interconnect.","cpu-offloading":"computes the PCIe penalty to show when spilling weights or KV cache to host memory is acceptable.","quantization-quality-evaluation":"the measurement ladder that decides whether a smaller, faster checkpoint is still the model you tested.","structured-constrained-generation":"logit masking in the engine for output that parses by construction, and what that costs.","engines":"compares vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, llama.cpp and Ollama once you know which features to judge them on.","streaming":"carries tokens from engine to browser over SSE, which makes TTFT the felt latency and disconnects observable.","timeouts":"layered connect, first-token, idle and total timers derived from TPOT and `max_tokens`, propagated as one deadline.","cancellation":"carries \"stop\" through every hop so expired or abandoned requests release their decode slots and KV blocks.","idempotency":"the key-and-replay mechanism that makes an ambiguous timeout safe to retry without paying twice or acting twice.","retry-strategies":"classified, jittered, budgeted retries that rely on idempotency and avoid layered retry storms.","fallbacks":"what to do when retrying will not help: compatible, pre-evaluated alternatives that share one deadline.","rate-limiting":"cost-aware per-tenant and outbound token buckets that protect shared GPUs and provider quotas.","backpressure":"bounds aggregate admission with Little's law and fast 429/503 rejections, the overload complement to rate limits.","caching":"closes the module by separating prompt, exact-match and semantic caches, each saving a different cost at a different risk."},"capstone":{"brief":"A mid-sized SaaS company wants to move its internal support assistant off a hosted API onto a self-hosted\nopen-weight model on one or two rented GPUs. Usage has grown, the per-token bill is high, and legal wants\ncustomer tickets kept inside the company's cloud account. The assistant has a 3,000-token system prompt\nwith tool schemas, multi-turn chat that streams to a web UI, and a nightly job that classifies the day's\ntickets into structured JSON. Over a weekend to a week, plan, deploy and harden that service. Choose a\nmodel and GPU, then write the capacity plan from the KV-cache arithmetic and the memory ledger before\nlaunching anything. Deploy it on a datacenter engine with deliberate settings, decide whether to quantize\nor use tensor parallelism and back that choice with evidence, and sweep concurrency with production-shaped\nprompts to find the latency knee. Put a thin gateway in front that streams over SSE, applies layered\ntimeouts, cancels on disconnect, enforces idempotency keys on message creation, retries with a budget,\nfalls back to the hosted API when the replica is unhealthy, rate-limits per tenant, and sheds load with\n`Retry-After` once queue wait exceeds its budget. Enable prefix caching and show what it buys on the chat\ntraffic, and run the nightly job through constrained decoding at the throughput end of the curve.","deliverables":["A capacity plan that derives bytes per token from the model config, writes the four-line VRAM ledger for the chosen GPU, and converts the target request rate into a replica count using Little's law, with the resulting `max_model_len`, `max_num_seqs` and memory-fraction settings.","A load-test report that sweeps concurrency (for example 1, 4, 16, 32, 64) with production-shaped prompt and output lengths, records TTFT p95, TPOT p95, per-stream and aggregate tokens/s and preemptions at each point, and states the chosen interactive and batch operating points.","A quantization decision record that compares the quantized checkpoint against its full-precision baseline using KL divergence or perplexity and a golden-set task eval with identical prompts and decoding. It reports the chat and JSON-classification categories separately and gives an accept or reject verdict.","Gateway code with SSE streaming, connect, first-token, idle and total timeouts, propagated cancellation, an idempotency-key store, classified retries with full jitter and a retry budget, a circuit-broken fallback to the hosted API, per-tenant token buckets, and queue-wait-based load shedding.","A dashboard and runbook covering TTFT, TPOT, queue wait, KV-cache utilisation, preemptions, prefix hit rate, fallback rate and tokens generated after disconnect, with one alert per overload signal and the first action to take for each."],"rubric":["The measured KV-cache block count and peak memory under load fall within about 10% of the ledger's prediction, and any gap is explained by a named term such as activation peak or runtime overhead.","Enabling prefix caching cuts chat TTFT p95 by a measured, reported amount on multi-turn traffic, and the report shows that changing one early token in the system prompt removes the gain.","When a client disconnects mid-stream, the engine aborts the sequence within about a second, as shown by engine logs and the tokens-after-disconnect metric, and the freed KV blocks show up in the cache-utilisation graph.","At twice the planned peak load the gateway returns fast 429 or 503 responses with `Retry-After` to the excess traffic, while admitted requests stay within the TTFT and TPOT SLOs and the preemption counter stays near zero.","Replaying a message-creation request with the same idempotency key after a forced timeout returns the stored result with no second generation, and provider calls per user request stay at or below the stated amplification limit during an injected 429 burst.","With the primary replica killed, interactive traffic moves to the hosted fallback within the stated breaker threshold and every response carries a served-by tag. The nightly constrained-decoding job produces 100% schema-valid JSON, and its task accuracy is reported against an unconstrained baseline."]}},"topics":[{"id":"model-serving","key":"serving-inference/model-serving","module":"serving-inference","name":"Model serving","taxonomy_name":"model serving","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["serving-inference/engines","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/streaming","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","model-internals/kv-cache"],"tldr":"Model serving is everything between a trained checkpoint and an HTTP response: loading weights onto\naccelerators, turning requests into token streams, scheduling many of them onto one GPU, and exposing\nlatency and throughput you can reason about. LLM serving is harder than classic ML serving because each\nrequest is a stateful loop of unknown length whose working set (the KV cache) grows while it runs, so the\nscheduler, not the model, decides what the hardware actually delivers.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"inference-optimization","key":"serving-inference/inference-optimization","module":"serving-inference","name":"Inference optimization","taxonomy_name":"inference optimization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","model-internals/speculative-decoding","model-internals/flashattention","training-adaptation/quantization"],"tldr":"Inference optimization is the set of levers that make a fixed model cheaper or faster to run. Almost all\nof them work by attacking one of two bottlenecks: decode is bound by memory bandwidth (every token\nre-reads the weights and the KV cache), prefill is bound by compute. Knowing which bound you are hitting\ntells you which lever can possibly help, which is why the roofline model, not a list of tricks, is the\nthing to learn.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prefill-vs-decode","key":"serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","module":"serving-inference","name":"Prefill vs decode","taxonomy_name":"prefill vs decode","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/inference-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Every LLM request runs in two phases with opposite hardware profiles. Prefill processes the whole prompt in\none pass: thousands of tokens per weight read, compute-bound, and it sets time-to-first-token. Decode then\nproduces one token per step: one token per weight read, memory-bandwidth-bound, and it sets time per\noutput token. Because the phases stress different resources, mixing them on one GPU causes interference,\nand most scheduling techniques exist to manage that collision.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache-memory-estimation","key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","module":"serving-inference","name":"KV-cache memory estimation","taxonomy_name":"KV-cache memory estimation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning"],"tldr":"KV-cache memory is the number that turns \"we have an 80 GB GPU\" into \"we can serve N concurrent requests\nof length L\". It is a short multiplication you can do from a model's config file: bytes per token equals\n2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes per element, times tokens, times concurrent sequences.\nLearning to do it quickly, and knowing what the formula leaves out, is how you avoid both out-of-memory\nsurprises and paying for GPUs that sit half empty.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","module":"serving-inference","name":"KV-cache paging & eviction","taxonomy_name":"KV-cache paging & eviction","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/cpu-offloading","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Paging stores each request's KV cache in fixed-size blocks mapped through a per-request block table, the\nway an operating system pages virtual memory, so sequences of unknown length can grow without reserving\ntheir maximum up front and without fragmenting the pool. Eviction is what happens when the pool still runs\nout: the scheduler preempts whole sequences (recompute or swap them out) or drops reusable cached prefixes,\nand a separate family of approximate methods discards tokens inside a sequence at a quality cost.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prefix-caching","key":"serving-inference/prefix-caching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Prefix caching","taxonomy_name":"prefix caching","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/caching","model-internals/kv-cache","prompting/system-prompt","agents/agent-loop"],"tldr":"Prefix caching keeps the KV cache of a prompt prefix alive after the request that computed it finishes, so\nthe next request starting with the same tokens skips prefill for that part. It is exact because K and V\nat a position depend only on the tokens before it. A shared system prompt, few-shot block or chat history\nbecomes a one-time cost instead of a per-request one, cutting time-to-first-token and compute, but only\nwhen prompts share a byte-identical beginning.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"continuous-batching","key":"serving-inference/continuous-batching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Continuous batching","taxonomy_name":"continuous batching","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/engines","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"Continuous batching schedules at the granularity of a single decode iteration instead of a whole request:\nevery step, finished sequences leave the batch and waiting ones join, so the GPU runs a full batch\ncontinuously rather than idling while the longest request in a static batch finishes. It is what makes\nbandwidth-bound decode efficient, because one read of the weights then serves dozens of tokens, and it\nis the reason modern engines report throughput an order of magnitude above naive servers.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pagedattention","key":"serving-inference/pagedattention","module":"serving-inference","name":"PagedAttention","taxonomy_name":"PagedAttention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"PagedAttention stores each request's KV cache in fixed-size blocks (16 tokens is the usual size)\naddressed through a per-request block table, instead of one contiguous buffer reserved for the maximum\nlength. Output lengths are unknown when a request arrives, so contiguous allocation must over-reserve\nand wastes most of the memory; paging allocates blocks on demand, removes external fragmentation, and\nlets requests share blocks. Decode is memory-bound, so the memory you recover becomes more concurrent\nrequests, which is where vLLM's throughput gains come from.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"batching-and-concurrency-limits","key":"serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","module":"serving-inference","name":"Batching & concurrency limits","taxonomy_name":"batching & concurrency limits","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"Decode is memory-bandwidth bound: each step streams the whole weight set through the GPU once, no\nmatter how many sequences are in the batch, so batching multiplies throughput almost for free until the\nGPU becomes compute-bound or the KV cache fills. Concurrency limits exist because past that knee each\nadded request raises everyone's latency, and because unbounded admission ends in preemption thrash or\nOOM. The work is to find the knee for your traffic shape and enforce it at the engine, the gateway and\nthe client.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tensor-parallelism-at-inference","key":"serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","module":"serving-inference","name":"Tensor parallelism at inference","taxonomy_name":"tensor parallelism at inference","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/distributed-training","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/engines","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection"],"tldr":"Tensor parallelism (TP) splits every weight matrix across N GPUs so each holds 1/N of the parameters and\nKV cache and does 1/N of each layer's arithmetic, stitched together by an all-reduce after attention and\nafter the MLP in every layer. At inference it is how you fit a model larger than one GPU and pool memory\nbandwidth for faster decode, but it pays off only over a fast intra-node interconnect, because decode\nsends hundreds of tiny all-reduces per token.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cpu-offloading","key":"serving-inference/cpu-offloading","module":"serving-inference","name":"CPU offloading","taxonomy_name":"CPU offloading","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/engines","training-adaptation/quantization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment"],"tldr":"CPU offloading keeps part of a model's weights or KV cache in host RAM (or on NVMe) and brings it to the\nGPU when needed, trading scarce GPU memory for PCIe bandwidth. PCIe moves data roughly 50–100× slower\nthan GPU memory, and decode touches every weight every token, so offloading weights makes interactive\nlatency collapse unless the offloaded share is tiny or batches are huge. It is a tool for fitting,\noffline throughput and tiering idle KV cache, not for low-latency serving.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"quantization-quality-evaluation","key":"serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Quantization quality evaluation","taxonomy_name":"quantization quality evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/perplexity","evals/golden-dataset","evals/regression-tests","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/benchmarking","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases"],"tldr":"Quantization buys memory and speed with precision, and the only honest way to know what it cost is to\nmeasure the quantized model against its own full-precision baseline on the work you actually serve.\nPerplexity and per-token KL divergence are cheap, sensitive early signals; task-level evals on your golden\nset, run with identical prompts and decoding, are the acceptance test. Degradation is uneven: long\ncontext, multi-step math, code, strict output formats and low-resource languages break first, so\naggregate numbers hide the failures that matter.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-constrained-generation","key":"serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Structured / constrained generation","taxonomy_name":"structured / constrained generation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","python-for-ai/outlines","python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/pydantic","model-internals/logits","model-internals/tokenization","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Structured generation makes a model's output conform to a schema, regex, grammar or fixed set of\nchoices. The reliable way is constrained decoding: at every step the engine masks the logits of tokens\nthat would violate the grammar, so the sampler can only pick valid continuations and the output parses\nby construction. That removes the parse-and-retry loop, but it guarantees shape, not truth, costs a\ngrammar compile step, and can subtly change what the model says, so schema design still matters.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-memory-optimization","key":"serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","module":"serving-inference","name":"GPU memory optimization (VRAM math)","taxonomy_name":"GPU memory optimization (VRAM math)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/cpu-offloading","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/flashattention","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"GPU memory at inference is a ledger with four lines: weights, KV cache, activation and workspace peaks,\nand runtime overhead plus fragmentation. Write it down (`weights = params × bytes_per_param`, `KV =\n2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes × tokens_in_flight`), subtract from usable VRAM, and every\ntuning knob, from quantization and model choice to context caps, concurrency caps, tensor parallelism\nand chunked prefill, is just a term you are shrinking. Guessing instead of computing is how both\nout-of-memory pages and 20%-utilized GPUs happen.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"streaming","key":"serving-inference/streaming","module":"serving-inference","name":"Streaming (SSE)","taxonomy_name":"streaming (SSE)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"A 500-token answer at 20 ms per token takes 10 s to finish, but the first token exists after a few\nhundred milliseconds. Streaming sends each token as it is decoded, so the latency a user feels becomes\ntime-to-first-token rather than total time. Server-Sent Events (SSE) is the usual transport because it\nis plain one-directional HTTP that proxies and provider APIs already speak. The price: errors, usage\nand tool calls arrive piecemeal, and nothing between GPU and browser may buffer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ttft","key":"serving-inference/ttft","module":"serving-inference","name":"TTFT (time to first token)","taxonomy_name":"TTFT","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","observability/latency-percentiles","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"Time to first token (TTFT) is the delay between sending a request and receiving the first generated\ntoken. It is made of queueing wait plus prefill, the single compute-bound pass over the whole prompt,\nso it grows with prompt length and with load, not with answer length. In a streaming UI it is the\nlatency users actually feel. You cut it with shorter prompts, prefix caching, admission control and\nscheduling; faster decode tricks such as speculative decoding do nothing for it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tpot","key":"serving-inference/tpot","module":"serving-inference","name":"TPOT (time per output token)","taxonomy_name":"TPOT","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tokens-sec","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/speculative-decoding","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference"],"tldr":"Time per output token (TPOT) is the average gap between successive tokens once generation has\nstarted: `(t_last − t_first) / (n_out − 1)`. Each decode step must read the whole model's weights plus\nthe KV cache from GPU memory, so TPOT has a floor of bytes-read divided by memory bandwidth, and it\nbarely rises when more requests share the step. That is why batching is almost free for throughput,\nwhy quantisation and tensor parallelism speed up single streams, and why a faster-FLOPs GPU alone\ndoes not.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tokens-sec","key":"serving-inference/tokens-sec","module":"serving-inference","name":"Tokens per second","taxonomy_name":"tokens/sec","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","evals/benchmarking"],"tldr":"\"Tokens per second\" names two different numbers: the decode speed of one stream (roughly `1 / TPOT`,\nwhat a user sees) and the aggregate output of a whole server (all concurrent streams summed, what you\npay for). They move in opposite directions as concurrency rises. A quoted figure means nothing\nwithout which tokens (input, output or both), the concurrency, prompt and output lengths, hardware,\nprecision and percentile. Use it for capacity and cost per token; never compare it across tokenizers.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"throughput-vs-latency","key":"serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","module":"serving-inference","name":"Throughput vs latency","taxonomy_name":"throughput vs latency","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/tokens-sec","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/ttft","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"A decode step costs about the same whether it advances one sequence or thirty-two, because its time\nis dominated by reading the weights once. Packing more requests into each step therefore multiplies\nthroughput almost for free, until compute or KV memory saturates, but every added request lengthens\neach step and the queue in front of it, so per-request latency rises. No setting maximises both. You\npick an operating point on that curve per workload and enforce it with concurrency limits, admission\ncontrol and SLOs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeouts","key":"serving-inference/timeouts","module":"serving-inference","name":"Timeouts","taxonomy_name":"timeouts","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/fallbacks","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"LLM calls last from under a second to several minutes, and the duration depends on an output length\nnobody knows in advance plus queueing nobody controls. A single flat timeout therefore either kills\nlegitimate work or lets hung connections hold resources for minutes. Use layered timers (connect,\nfirst token, idle gap between chunks, total deadline derived from `max_tokens × TPOT`), propagate\nthe remaining budget downstream, pair every expiry with cancellation, and audit the default timeouts\nof every proxy between you and the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cancellation","key":"serving-inference/cancellation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Cancellation","taxonomy_name":"cancellation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/continuous-batching","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","agents/agent-loop"],"tldr":"A generation nobody is waiting for still occupies a decode slot, KV-cache memory and your token\nbudget until it reaches `max_tokens`. Cancellation carries \"stop\" from the trigger (closed tab, stop\nbutton, superseding message, timeout, losing hedge) through every hop: browser, your API, HTTP\nclient, provider or engine, so the engine aborts the sequence and frees its memory. It works only if\neach layer notices the disconnect and closes the next connection; streaming makes that observable,\nnon-streaming hides it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure","key":"serving-inference/backpressure","module":"serving-inference","name":"Backpressure","taxonomy_name":"backpressure","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/rate-limiting","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure"],"tldr":"Backpressure is how an overloaded stage tells the stage feeding it to slow down, instead of silently\nbuffering work until memory or latency blows up. An inference server has a hard ceiling set by KV-cache\nmemory and decode bandwidth, so it must bound how much it admits, measure how long work waits, and reject\nthe rest quickly with a 429 or 503 and a `Retry-After`. Without it a traffic spike becomes slow timeouts\nfor everyone instead of fast rejections for some.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fallbacks","key":"serving-inference/fallbacks","module":"serving-inference","name":"Fallbacks","taxonomy_name":"fallbacks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-fallback-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/graceful-degradation","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"A fallback is a pre-decided alternative path taken when the primary one fails or is too slow: the same\nmodel on another endpoint or region, a smaller model, a cached or templated answer, or an honest\n\"unavailable\". It exists because LLM dependencies fail partially and often, and retrying a broken target\nonly burns the latency budget. The hard parts are deciding fast, keeping the alternative behaviourally\ncompatible, and not letting everyone's fallback crush the backup.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"retry-strategies","key":"serving-inference/retry-strategies","module":"serving-inference","name":"Retry strategies","taxonomy_name":"retry strategies","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/fallbacks","reliability/circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"A retry re-issues a request that failed for a reason likely to be transient. Done well, it classifies the\nerror, waits with exponential backoff plus jitter, honours `Retry-After`, caps attempts and total time,\nkeeps a global retry budget and relies on idempotency; it then absorbs the steady trickle of 429s, 5xx\nand connection resets that LLM APIs produce. Done naively, retrying everything immediately at every\nlayer, it turns a brief provider hiccup into a self-inflicted outage.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rate-limiting","key":"serving-inference/rate-limiting","module":"serving-inference","name":"Rate limiting","taxonomy_name":"rate limiting","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Rate limiting bounds how much work a caller may submit per unit of time. In LLM systems it points two\nways: inbound, so one tenant or one runaway loop cannot consume the GPU or the provider quota everyone\nshares; outbound, so your fleet stays under provider limits that are counted in requests *and* tokens per\nminute. The LLM twist is that a request's cost is unknown until it finishes, so limiters reserve on an\nestimate and settle on the actual.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotency","key":"serving-inference/idempotency","module":"serving-inference","name":"Idempotency","taxonomy_name":"idempotency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"An operation is idempotent when doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. LLM systems need\nthis because retries, timeouts, at-least-once queues and agent re-execution all produce duplicates, and\nduplicates cost money (a second generation), corrupt state (a second reply in a chat) or act twice on the\nworld (a second email). The standard mechanism is a client-chosen idempotency key stored with the outcome,\nso a repeat returns the saved result instead of redoing the work.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"caching","key":"serving-inference/caching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Caching (semantic, prompt)","taxonomy_name":"caching (semantic, prompt)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefix-caching","prompting/prompt-caching","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/ttft","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization"],"tldr":"LLM caching comes in three layers that are routinely conflated. An exact-match response cache skips\ngeneration when an identical request repeats. A semantic cache skips generation when a *similar enough*\nrequest repeats, judged by embedding distance. Prompt caching reuses the KV cache of a shared prompt\nprefix so prefill is not recomputed, but the answer is still generated fresh. They save different things,\nfail in different ways and need different keys, so \"add a cache\" is not one decision.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"engines","key":"serving-inference/engines","module":"serving-inference","name":"Engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama, llama.cpp)","taxonomy_name":"engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama, llama.cpp)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["serving-inference/model-serving","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","training-adaptation/quantization","system-design/self-host-vs-api","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"An inference engine loads weights onto hardware and turns requests into tokens; it owns batching,\nKV-cache memory, kernels, quantization and the serving API. The six named here split into two families:\ndatacenter servers built around continuous batching and paged KV cache for many concurrent users (vLLM,\nSGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM) and local runtimes built for portability and small footprints (llama.cpp, and\nOllama, which wraps it). Choose by hardware, concurrency and how much operational effort you can afford.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"observability","tier":1,"order":9,"title":"Observability","intro":{"overview":"This module is about seeing what an LLM system actually did, why it did it, how long it took and what\nit cost, when the failures that matter most return HTTP 200 with plausible text. It starts with\ntracing, the timed tree of one request, and spans, the nodes of that tree, where the real skill is\nchoosing boundaries and attributes (model, tokens, chunk ids, tool arguments) that match the questions\nyou will ask later. Distributed tracing carries that tree across the gateway, retrieval service, tool\nservers and queues that a single turn crosses, using W3C `traceparent` propagation. Logging records the\ndiscrete events and payloads that traces should not hold: rendered prompts, raw completions, tool\nresults and guardrail decisions, structured, bound to trace ids and redacted by policy. Monitoring\ncompresses all of it into aggregate signals and alerts, adding LLM quality proxies to the golden\nsignals; latency percentiles and token cost tracking are the two measurements every LLM dashboard is\nbuilt on, and both are easy to compute wrongly. OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral plumbing that\nproduces this telemetry once, and Langfuse, LangSmith and Arize Phoenix are the LLM-aware backends\nthat add prompts, costs, scores, datasets and evaluation on top. After the module you can instrument\na RAG or agent service end to end, debug a single bad answer from its trace, build dashboards and\nburn-rate alerts that catch silent regressions, attribute spend to features and tenants, and choose a\nbackend on the merits. It matters most for AI Engineers who ship and operate these systems, and\nfor FDEs who must explain a customer's bad answer or bill within the hour.","why":{"engineer":"You instrument every model call, retrieval and tool call as spans with the right attributes, propagate context through gateways and queues, and use the resulting traces, logs and cost records to find which step produced a wrong answer or a slow turn.","architect":"You choose the telemetry pipeline (OTel SDKs, Collector, sampling policy, retention and redaction) and the LLM backend, deciding what gets stored where, what it costs to keep, and whether prompts containing customer data can leave your infrastructure.","fde":"When a customer forwards a bad answer or questions the bill, you need to pull up that conversation's trace, show exactly what the model saw, attribute cost to their tenant, and stand up dashboards and alerts in their environment without rebuilding their stack."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: every user turn is a tree of timed spans, and everything else in observability is a\nview onto that tree. The root is the turn; beneath it sit retrieval, reranking, each generation, each\ntool call and each retry, stitched together across processes by a propagated trace id. Spans carry\nthe structure and timing; logs hang payloads and decisions off the same ids; metrics are the tree\naggregated over millions of requests into rates, latency histograms and token counters. Ask \"what\nhappened on this request\" and you read one tree; ask \"how often, how slow, how expensive\" and you\nquery the aggregates, using percentiles from merged histograms and cost from usage times the price in\nforce. OpenTelemetry is how the tree is produced in a standard shape; Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix\nare places to store it and attach quality scores to its nodes. If the tree is incomplete, every view\nbuilt on it lies.","order":["tracing","spans","distributed-tracing-across-services","logging","opentelemetry","latency-percentiles","token-cost-tracking","monitoring","langfuse","langsmith","arize-phoenix"],"order_reasons":{"tracing":"establishes why LLM systems need a per-request causal record and what a trace must capture, the frame for everything else.","spans":"teaches the unit traces are built from and how to choose span boundaries and attributes for generations, retrievals and tools.","distributed-tracing-across-services":"extends the span tree across gateways, tool servers and queues so traces do not break at the first hop.","logging":"adds the durable, structured event and payload record that complements sampled, lightweight traces.","opentelemetry":"shows the standard API, SDK, OTLP and propagation format that produce traces, metrics and logs once for any backend.","latency-percentiles":"gives the correct way to measure and aggregate TTFT and end-to-end latency before building dashboards on it.","token-cost-tracking":"records usage per span and attributes spend by feature, tenant and run, the LLM-specific metric beside latency.","monitoring":"combines golden signals, quality proxies, latency and cost into dashboards, SLOs and burn-rate alerts that catch silent failures.","langfuse":"the open-source, self-hostable backend that closes the trace, score, dataset and prompt-version loop in one tool.","langsmith":"the hosted platform most integrated with LangChain and LangGraph, best compared once the Langfuse data model is familiar.","arize-phoenix":"the OpenTelemetry-native, evaluation-heavy option, which makes the most sense after OTel and the other two backends."},"capstone":{"brief":"Instrument a multi-service RAG support assistant so that any bad answer, slow turn or cost spike can be\nexplained from telemetry alone. The system has at least three processes: an API gateway, an\norchestrator that rewrites the query, retrieves and reranks chunks and calls a model, and a separate\ntool service (for example an order-status lookup) reached over HTTP or through a queue. Instrument all\nof them with OpenTelemetry, propagating `traceparent` across every hop including the queue, and record\ngeneration spans with model, parameters, token usage and computed cost, retrieval spans with chunk ids\nand scores, and tool spans with arguments and outcomes. Emit structured logs bound to trace ids, with\nrendered prompts and raw completions redacted at INFO and kept in a sampled payload stream. Send the\ntraces through an OTel Collector to both a general trace store (Jaeger or Tempo) and one LLM backend\n(Langfuse, LangSmith or Phoenix), and attach an LLM-as-a-judge relevance or faithfulness score to a\nsample of production traces. Build a dashboard of TTFT and end-to-end latency percentiles from\nhistograms, error, parse-failure and refusal rates, and cost per tenant and per successful task, with a\nburn-rate alert on a TTFT SLO and a per-run cost budget. Then inject three faults (a slow tool, a\nprompt change that inflates input tokens, and a stale retrieval index) and show that telemetry catches\nand localises each one.","deliverables":["The instrumented services and an OTel Collector configuration (Docker Compose is fine) that fans traces out to a general trace store and an LLM backend, with propagation working across HTTP and the queue.","A dashboard with p50/p95/p99 TTFT and end-to-end latency computed from merged histograms, error, parse-failure and refusal rates, and token cost by feature, tenant and model.","Alert definitions for a TTFT SLO burn rate and a per-agent-run or per-tenant cost budget, plus a short runbook for each alert.","A structured logging schema listing the correlation fields on every line, the per-event fields, and the redaction and sampling rules for prompts and completions.","A fault-injection report covering the three injected faults: the alert or dashboard signal that fired, the trace that localised the cause, and the time from injection to diagnosis.","A one-page backend comparison note justifying the chosen LLM backend (Langfuse, LangSmith or Phoenix) on data residency, framework fit, evaluation workflow and cost."],"rubric":["At least 99% of sampled turns produce a single connected trace from the gateway root to the provider call and the tool service, measured by a completeness query rather than asserted.","Every generation span carries model, input, output and cached token counts and a computed cost, and the summed computed cost for a test run matches the provider's reported usage within a few percent.","Latency percentiles are computed from histograms with bucket edges at the SLO thresholds, and the dashboard never shows an averaged percentile or a mean latency as the headline number.","Each of the three injected faults is detected by a dashboard signal or alert and traced to the responsible span, including the stale index, which produces no errors and is caught only by a quality proxy or judge score.","A grep of the default log stream for a planted secret or email address in a test prompt finds nothing, while the sampled payload stream still lets a reviewer reproduce the exact prompt for a given trace id."]}},"topics":[{"id":"tracing","key":"observability/tracing","module":"observability","name":"Tracing","taxonomy_name":"tracing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langfuse","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"A trace is the complete, timed record of one request as it moves through an LLM application: the\nuser turn at the root, and under it every retrieval, model call, tool call and retry as nested spans.\nIt exists because LLM systems fail *semantically* (a wrong answer, a bad tool choice) rather than by\ncrashing, so you need to see exactly what each step received and produced, in order and with timing.\nLogs tell you something happened; a trace tells you what caused what on this request.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"spans","key":"observability/spans","module":"observability","name":"Spans","taxonomy_name":"spans","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/opentelemetry","observability/latency-percentiles","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","serving-inference/ttft"],"tldr":"A span is the unit a trace is built from: one named operation with a start time, an end time, a\nparent, a status and a set of attributes, plus optional timestamped events. Traces are only as useful\nas their spans, so the real skill is choosing span boundaries that match the questions you will ask,\nand recording the attributes (model, tokens, chunk ids, tool arguments) that let a span be filtered,\naggregated and understood on its own.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-tracing-across-services","key":"observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","module":"observability","name":"Distributed tracing across services","taxonomy_name":"distributed tracing across services","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/opentelemetry","system-design/ai-gateway","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","llmops-platform/observability-stack"],"tldr":"One user turn in a production LLM system typically crosses several processes: an API gateway, an\norchestrator, a retrieval service, a model gateway, tool servers, perhaps a queue. In-process span\ncontext dies at each boundary, so distributed tracing *propagates* the trace id and parent span id\nwith every call (the W3C `traceparent` header for HTTP, message metadata for queues) and each service\ncontinues the same tree. Without it you get disconnected fragments and cannot attribute latency or\nfailure to the hop that actually mattered.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"logging","key":"observability/logging","module":"observability","name":"Logging","taxonomy_name":"logging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/monitoring","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","safety-security/pii-redaction","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"Logging records discrete events as structured, searchable records: timestamp, level, event name, the\ntrace and request ids that tie each line to the rest of the system, and event-specific fields. In LLM\napps, logs hold what traces and metrics should not: rendered prompts, raw completions before parsing,\ntool arguments and results, guardrail decisions and provider errors, redacted and retained by policy.\nThe design work is deciding what to log, at which level, in what shape, and how to bind it to the\nrequest.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"monitoring","key":"observability/monitoring","module":"observability","name":"Monitoring","taxonomy_name":"monitoring","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/latency-percentiles","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/logging","reliability/slas-slos","llmops-platform/model-monitoring","llmops-platform/drift","deployment/health-checks"],"tldr":"Monitoring turns the stream of individual requests into aggregate signals (rates, errors, latency\ndistributions, saturation, plus LLM-specific quality and cost signals), compares them with targets,\nand pages someone when they drift. A trace explains one request; monitoring says whether the system is\nhealthy now. The LLM-specific difficulty is that the worst failures (wrong answers, prompt\nregressions, silent model changes) return HTTP 200, so you must monitor proxies and sampled quality\nscores, not just errors.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"token-cost-tracking","key":"observability/token-cost-tracking","module":"observability","name":"Token / cost tracking","taxonomy_name":"token / cost tracking","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/monitoring","observability/spans","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","prompting/prompt-caching","python-for-ai/tiktoken","model-internals/tokenization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"LLM spend is metered per token, priced differently for input, output and cached tokens, and varies\nby model, so the only way to know what a feature, tenant or agent run costs is to record the\nprovider's reported usage on every call and multiply by the price in force at that moment. Track it\nat span level, roll it up by the dimensions you make decisions on, and alert on budgets; retries,\nagent loops and growing conversation history make cost non-linear in requests, so per-request\naverages mislead.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"latency-percentiles","key":"observability/latency-percentiles","module":"observability","name":"Latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99)","taxonomy_name":"latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99)","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/monitoring","observability/tracing","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","reliability/slas-slos","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"Latency is a distribution, not a number. A handful of slow requests drags the mean up while most users\nnever see them, and a long tail hides behind a healthy-looking average. A percentile reports the value\nbelow which p% of requests finish: p50 is the typical experience, p95 and p99 are the tail that users\nnotice and that fan-out amplifies. For LLM systems, measure percentiles per phase (time to first token,\nper-token, end to end) and compute them by merging histograms, never by averaging percentiles.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry","key":"observability/opentelemetry","module":"observability","name":"OpenTelemetry","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/logging","observability/arize-phoenix","observability/langfuse","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the CNCF vendor-neutral standard for *producing* telemetry: one API and SDK per\nlanguage for traces, metrics and logs, one wire protocol (OTLP), one context-propagation format (W3C\nTrace Context) and one vocabulary of attribute names. It exists so instrumentation is written once and\nthe backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Datadog, Langfuse, Phoenix) becomes a configuration choice. For LLM\napplications it is the plumbing under most tracing tools, and the way an LLM span lands in the same trace\nas the HTTP, queue and database spans around it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"langfuse","key":"observability/langfuse","module":"observability","name":"Langfuse","taxonomy_name":"Langfuse","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langsmith","observability/arize-phoenix","evals/tooling","evals/llm-as-a-judge","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"Langfuse is an open-source, self-hostable LLM engineering platform. It records nested observations\n(spans, generations, events) carrying the rendered prompt, the completion, token usage and cost, grouped\ninto traces, sessions and users, and closes the loop with scores (user feedback, human annotation,\nLLM-as-a-judge), datasets and experiments, and versioned prompt management. Choose it for the\ntrace-to-eval-to-prompt loop in one framework-independent tool that can run in your own infrastructure.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"langsmith","key":"observability/langsmith","module":"observability","name":"LangSmith","taxonomy_name":"LangSmith","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/langfuse","observability/arize-phoenix","observability/opentelemetry","agents/frameworks","evals/tooling","evals/golden-dataset","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"LangSmith is LangChain Inc.'s closed-source platform for tracing, evaluating and monitoring LLM\napplications, hosted with enterprise self-hosting available. It records every unit of work as a run\n(LLM, chain, tool, retriever) in a tree with inputs, outputs, tokens, latency and cost, attaches feedback\nand online evaluators, and runs offline experiments against datasets. Any Python or JavaScript code can\nsend traces through `@traceable`, but it is the natural choice for LangChain and LangGraph apps, where\ntracing is an environment-variable switch.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"arize-phoenix","key":"observability/arize-phoenix","module":"observability","name":"Arize Phoenix","taxonomy_name":"Arize Phoenix","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/opentelemetry","observability/tracing","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","evals/tooling","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"Arize Phoenix is a self-hostable (source-available, Elastic License 2.0) LLM observability and evaluation\ntool built directly on OpenTelemetry: applications send ordinary OTel spans annotated with OpenInference conventions (LLM,\nretriever, tool, agent span kinds) and Phoenix stores, visualises and evaluates them. Its strengths are\ningestion without a proprietary SDK, an evaluation library that runs LLM-as-a-judge over traces as\nDataFrames and writes verdicts back onto spans, and embedding visualisation for retrieval failures. Arize\nAX is the separate commercial platform.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"safety-security","tier":1,"order":10,"title":"Safety & security","intro":{"overview":"This module covers how to keep an LLM system from being turned against its users, its operator or its\ndata. It starts from one premise that every lesson returns to: the model is an untrusted component that\nfollows instructions wherever it finds them, and a system prompt is an instruction, not an enforcement\nboundary. The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications gives the shared vocabulary. Prompt injection and RAG\npoisoning explain how untrusted text, typed by a user or planted in a document, web page or corpus,\nreaches the model's single input stream. The checkpoint layer comes next: input and output guardrails,\noutput validation that turns strings into typed data, content moderation with per-surface thresholds,\nand two concrete tools, Llama Guard as a policy-in-the-prompt safety classifier and NeMo Guardrails as an\norchestrator of rails. The data lessons (PII redaction, data leakage, cross-tenant leakage) map every\nplace an LLM pipeline copies text: prompts, traces, caches, embeddings, memory and weights. The action\nlessons (excessive agency, tool privilege escalation) cover agents, where a wrong token becomes a real\nside effect. Supply-chain security closes the module by treating checkpoints and datasets as build\nartifacts that can run code or carry backdoors. Afterwards you can threat-model an LLM feature, choose\nand measure guardrails, design tenant isolation and redaction per boundary, scope agent authority, and\nanswer an enterprise security review item by item. It matters most for Engineers who build these layers\nand Architects who own the trust boundaries, and FDEs need it to pass customer security reviews.","why":{"engineer":"You write the code that validates model output with Pydantic, threads a server-derived tenant ID into every vector, cache and memory call, redacts traces, runs moderation and Llama Guard checks, and executes tools with the requesting user's credentials instead of the service's, and each of these needs labelled tests and a tracked false-positive rate.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries sit, which workflows combine private data, untrusted content and outbound capability, which tools are tiered as irreversible and gated, how tenants are isolated across every store, and which models and datasets may enter the registry; those choices, not the prompt, set the blast radius.","fde":"Enterprise buyers send security questionnaires keyed to OWASP LLM items, ask where their data goes and whether another customer could see it, and want their own moderation policy; you need a threat model, a data-flow map and red-team evidence you can show, and the ability to tune rails per deployment."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as a powerful but gullible deputy sitting in the middle of a building with many doors.\nText comes in through several doors (the user, retrieved chunks, tool results, downloaded checkpoints),\nand all of it lands in the same ear; the deputy cannot tell instructions from data. Things go out through\nother doors: answers shown to people, tool calls that change the world, and copies of the text written\nto logs, caches, indexes and weights. Security means guarding the doors, not lecturing the deputy. At the\ninbound doors you control provenance, trust tiers, redaction and injection screening. At the outbound\ndoors you validate structure and meaning, moderate content, scan for leaks and enforce tenancy. At the\naction doors you give the deputy only the keys for this task and this user, checked by the resource,\nwith a human at the irreversible ones. Then you prove each guard works with labelled positives,\nnegatives and red-team cases, because a guard nobody has measured is not a control.","order":["owasp-top-10-for-llms","prompt-injection","rag-poisoning","input-output-guardrails","output-validation","content-moderation","llama-guard","nemo-guardrails","pii-redaction","data-leakage","cross-tenant-data-leakage","excessive-agency","tool-privilege-escalation","supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"order_reasons":{"owasp-top-10-for-llms":"the intro-level map and shared vocabulary that every later lesson slots into.","prompt-injection":"the root threat: one input stream with no code/data separation, and the lethal trifecta that turns it into an incident.","rag-poisoning":"extends injection to the corpus, showing why write-path provenance and trust tiers beat prompt-side defences.","input-output-guardrails":"introduces the deterministic checkpoint layer and the measure-before-enforce discipline the next lessons build on.","output-validation":"the most mechanical output rail: syntactic, structural and semantic validity of anything code consumes.","content-moderation":"classification against a harm taxonomy with per-surface thresholds, actions and review metrics.","llama-guard":"a concrete open-weight moderation classifier with policy in the prompt, best read once moderation itself is clear.","nemo-guardrails":"a toolkit that composes rails and classifiers such as Llama Guard, so it comes after the pieces it orchestrates.","pii-redaction":"the first data control: typed, reversible pseudonymisation applied per boundary.","data-leakage":"widens from PII to every channel data can leave by: provider, tools, rendering, logs, embeddings and weights.","cross-tenant-data-leakage":"the multi-tenant special case where vector stores, caches and memory bypass row-level security.","excessive-agency":"moves from words to actions, shrinking tools, permissions and autonomy to bound the blast radius.","tool-privilege-escalation":"the confused-deputy deep dive: every tool call must carry only the asking principal's authority.","supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets":"finishes below the application, treating checkpoints and datasets as signed, scanned build artifacts."},"capstone":{"brief":"Secure a multi-tenant customer-support copilot before its first enterprise launch. The copilot answers\nquestions from a RAG index built from each customer's help-centre articles, inbound support tickets and a\nshared product wiki, and it has three tools: look up an order, issue a refund up to a limit, and email the\nend customer. It runs on a hosted LLM, uses an open-weight embedding model pulled from a public hub, keeps\nper-conversation memory and a semantic cache, and ships every prompt to a tracing vendor. Over a week,\nwrite an OWASP-structured threat model of the system, then build the controls the top rows call for:\ninjection and hidden-text handling at ingestion with per-source trust tiers; input and output rails with\nPydantic-validated tool arguments; moderation using Llama Guard with a custom category set, composed\nwith a topical rail in NeMo Guardrails or an equivalent pipeline; PII pseudonymisation before the\nprovider and irreversible redaction in traces; one server-derived tenant ID enforced on the vector store,\ncache and memory; refunds and outbound email executed with the end user's scopes and gated by human\napproval above a threshold; and a pinned, hashed, safe-format embedding model loaded without remote code.\nFinish with a red-team run that tries to break each control.","deliverables":["A threat model with one row per OWASP LLM item, each naming the affected component, the control, the test that proves it and the residual risk, plus a data-flow diagram showing every store and egress point.","Working code for the guardrail pipeline: ingestion sanitiser with provenance metadata, input and output rails, Pydantic schemas for every tool argument, Llama Guard moderation with per-rail thresholds, and a tool executor that attaches the requesting user's credential and an approval gate.","A tenant-isolation module with a single tenant-context wrapper used by every vector, cache and memory call, and a CI suite of cross-tenant canary tests across at least three tenant pairs.","A PII and leakage policy listing each boundary (provider, traces, index, cache, output, email tool) with the entity list and transformation applied, plus a recall measurement per entity on a labelled sample.","A model supply-chain record for the embedding model: source revision pinned by commit, file hashes, format and scan results, licence, and the registry entry production loads from.","A red-team report of at least 25 attacks (direct and indirect injection, poisoned wiki page, system-prompt extraction, cross-tenant probes, refund escalation, exfiltration through a rendered link or email) with outcomes and fixes."],"rubric":["A poisoned wiki page with a hidden instruction to email order data to an external address is either quarantined at ingestion or, if retrieved, results in no outbound email, and the block appears in the decision log with its source and trust tier.","Every cross-tenant canary test passes in CI, and grepping the codebase shows no vector, cache or memory call that bypasses the tenant-context wrapper.","Each rail reports block rate on an adversarial set and false-refusal rate on a benign set of at least 100 examples each, and its thresholds are justified from those numbers rather than defaults.","A refund above the threshold cannot execute without a recorded human approval, and a refund requested by an injected document fails at the resource layer because the call carries the end user's scopes, not a service account's.","Sampled traces contain typed placeholders instead of raw emails, phone numbers and order addresses, and measured redaction recall per entity is reported next to the target.","Production loads the embedding model only from the internal registry by hash, in a non-pickle format with remote code disabled, and swapping a tampered file makes the load fail."]}},"topics":[{"id":"input-output-guardrails","key":"safety-security/input-output-guardrails","module":"safety-security","name":"Input / output guardrails","taxonomy_name":"input / output guardrails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/output-validation","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/nemo-guardrails","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"A language model is a probabilistic component whose behaviour you cannot fully specify in a prompt, so\nthe system around it needs deterministic checkpoints. Input guardrails inspect what reaches the model\n(injection patterns, scope, PII, size); output guardrails inspect what leaves it (structure, policy,\nleakage, tool calls) and decide to allow, block, rewrite, retry or escalate. They exist because a\nsystem prompt is an instruction, not an enforcement boundary, and the cost of a wrong output is paid\noutside the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"output-validation","key":"safety-security/output-validation","module":"safety-security","name":"Output validation (Pydantic, Instructor, Outlines)","taxonomy_name":"output validation (Pydantic, Instructor, Outlines)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/outlines","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation"],"tldr":"A model's output is a string until something proves otherwise. Output validation turns it into typed\ndata your code can trust. Pydantic defines the contract and rejects what does not fit; Instructor wraps\nthe model call so a validation failure becomes a retry with the error fed back; Outlines constrains\ndecoding so the model cannot emit a token that breaks the schema. Choose by where you can intervene:\nafter the fact, in the loop, or inside decoding. Shape can be guaranteed; meaning must be checked.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pii-redaction","key":"safety-security/pii-redaction","module":"safety-security","name":"PII redaction","taxonomy_name":"PII redaction","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","observability/tracing","observability/logging","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"PII redaction finds personal identifiers in text and replaces them before the text reaches a place it\nshould not: a third-party model, a log, a trace, a vector index, or another user's screen. It matters\nmore in LLM systems than in ordinary apps because they copy text into far more places, each a new\nretention point. Reversible pseudonymisation, typed placeholders with a short-lived mapping, keeps the\nmodel useful while raw values stay inside your boundary.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"content-moderation","key":"safety-security/content-moderation","module":"safety-security","name":"Content moderation","taxonomy_name":"content moderation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","prompting/jailbreaks","evals/red-teaming","ml-fundamentals/classification","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"Content moderation classifies text (increasingly images too) against a harm taxonomy such as hate,\nharassment, sexual content, self-harm, violence and illegal activity, then routes it: allow, block,\nsafe-complete or escalate. In LLM systems it runs in both directions, because users send harmful content\nand models generate it, and on retrieved content, because that is input too. It is a classification\nproblem with policy attached: thresholds and actions are a product decision, not a property of the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection","key":"safety-security/prompt-injection","module":"safety-security","name":"Prompt injection (direct, indirect)","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection (direct, indirect)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/jailbreaks","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","safety-security/rag-poisoning","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Prompt injection is untrusted text being treated as instructions. It happens because a transformer has\none input stream and no hardware distinction between what the developer said and what a document says.\nIn direct injection the user writes the attack. In indirect injection the attack sits in content the\nmodel reads, a web page, an email, a PDF, a tool result, and the user is the victim. There is no\nparameterised query to make it go away; defence is reducing privilege, isolating untrusted content and\ncontrolling what outputs can do.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rag-poisoning","key":"safety-security/rag-poisoning","module":"safety-security","name":"RAG poisoning","taxonomy_name":"RAG poisoning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"RAG makes your corpus part of the prompt, so whoever can write to the corpus can write to the prompt.\nPoisoning takes two forms: instructions hidden inside documents (indirect prompt injection delivered by\nretrieval) and knowledge corruption, where crafted documents are built to be retrieved for target\nquestions and to steer the answer. The defences live mostly on the write path and in the ingestion\npipeline: provenance, trust tiers, sanitisation, access control, and monitoring of what retrieval returns.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"excessive-agency","key":"safety-security/excessive-agency","module":"safety-security","name":"Excessive agency","taxonomy_name":"excessive agency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/human-in-the-loop","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agents/sandboxing","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"Excessive agency is an LLM system that can do more than its task needs: too many functions, too much\npermission, or too much autonomy. Then a bad decision, from a hallucinated intent, an injected\ninstruction or an ambiguous request, becomes real damage. The fix is not a smarter model but a smaller\nblast radius: minimal tools, credentials scoped to the user and enforced outside the model, and human\ncheckpoints before irreversible or external actions.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-privilege-escalation","key":"safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","module":"safety-security","name":"Tool privilege escalation","taxonomy_name":"tool privilege escalation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/prompt-injection","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/sandboxing","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","backend-fundamentals/rbac"],"tldr":"An agent's privilege is the union of everything its tools can do, and the model that picks the next tool\ncall can be steered by anyone whose text reaches its context. Tool privilege escalation is a low-trust\ninput (a user, a fetched page, a retrieved document) exercising a high-trust capability (a service\naccount, a shell, a payment API) through the model. The fix is not a sterner system prompt but making\neach tool call carry only the authority of the principal who asked.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cross-tenant-data-leakage","key":"safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","module":"safety-security","name":"Cross-tenant data leakage","taxonomy_name":"cross-tenant data leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","system-design/multi-tenancy","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/data-leakage","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"Cross-tenant leakage is tenant A's data appearing in tenant B's answer. It happens because an LLM\npipeline copies data into stores the database's row-level security never sees: vector indexes, semantic\nand prefix caches, conversation memory, traces, sometimes the weights themselves. The fix is one\nserver-derived tenant identity threaded as a hard, pre-applied filter through every store and proven by\nnegative tests, not a prompt telling the model to use only this customer's documents.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-leakage","key":"safety-security/data-leakage","module":"safety-security","name":"Data leakage","taxonomy_name":"data leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","observability/logging","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation"],"tldr":"Data leakage in an LLM system is any path by which information crosses a boundary it should not: into a\nthird-party API, into another user's answer, into model weights, or out through a tool call or rendered\nlink. It is distinct from ML train/test leakage. Channels multiply because an LLM app copies data into\nprompts, caches, traces, embeddings and weights, and the model repeats whatever it has seen. So minimise\nwhat enters, control what leaves, and assume no boundary exists inside the prompt.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","key":"safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","module":"safety-security","name":"Supply-chain security for models & datasets","taxonomy_name":"supply-chain security for models & datasets","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/safetensors","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","llmops-platform/model-registry","safety-security/rag-poisoning","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","evals/dataset-contamination"],"tldr":"A model checkpoint or dataset pulled from a public hub is a dependency with worse properties than a\nlibrary: it can execute code when loaded (pickle), it can carry behaviour you cannot read in a diff\n(backdoored weights), and its training data may have been poisoned before anyone you trust touched it.\nSupply-chain security treats models and datasets like any other build artifact, pinned, hashed, scanned,\nsigned, quarantined and loaded with the least capability, because you cannot review ten billion floats\nfor intent.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"owasp-top-10-for-llms","key":"safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","module":"safety-security","name":"OWASP Top 10 for LLMs","taxonomy_name":"OWASP Top 10 for LLMs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","safety-security/rag-poisoning","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/output-validation","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications lists the most consequential risk classes in systems that embed a\nlanguage model, shaped like the web Top 10 so security teams, vendors and auditors share a vocabulary.\nIts value is the framing: the model is an untrusted component that follows instructions found in data,\nso every classic boundary (input validation, output encoding, least privilege, supply chain, resource\nlimits) must be redrawn around the prompt. This lesson is the map; the deep lessons are elsewhere in\nthis module.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"llama-guard","key":"safety-security/llama-guard","module":"safety-security","name":"Llama Guard","taxonomy_name":"Llama Guard","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/nemo-guardrails","safety-security/prompt-injection","python-for-ai/hugging-face","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"Llama Guard is Meta's family of open-weight content-safety classifiers, made by fine-tuning a Llama model\nto read a safety policy, a user prompt and optionally the assistant's reply, and generate `safe` or\n`unsafe` plus the violated category codes. Because the policy is text in the prompt rather than a fixed\nlabel head, categories can be edited without retraining. It is a moderation model, not a prompt-injection,\nPII or hallucination detector, and every check costs a full LLM forward pass.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"nemo-guardrails","key":"safety-security/nemo-guardrails","module":"safety-security","name":"NeMo Guardrails","taxonomy_name":"NeMo Guardrails","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/output-validation","agents/state-machines","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"NeMo Guardrails is NVIDIA's open-source toolkit that sits between your application and the LLM and runs\nprogrammable \"rails\" at five points: input, dialogue flow, retrieved chunks, tool execution, and output.\nRails are declared in Colang and YAML rather than scattered through code, and dialogue rails map free\ntext to canonical intents via an embedding index of example utterances (plus a short LLM call by\ndefault), so policy becomes explicit flows. It orchestrates classifiers such as Llama Guard and Presidio\nrather than being one.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"multimodal","tier":1,"order":11,"title":"Multimodal","intro":{"overview":"This module covers how AI systems take in and produce things other than plain text: images, scanned\npages and audio. It starts with vision-language models, the encoder-connector-LLM design that lets a\nlanguage model attend over visual tokens, then goes underneath to CLIP, the contrastive image-text\ntraining that produced the vision towers most VLMs reuse. Image embeddings generalise that idea into\nvectors you can index, search, cluster and deduplicate, and they make the point that the encoder you\nchoose decides what \"similar\" means. OCR and document understanding cover the enterprise workhorse:\nrecovering characters, positions and confidences from scans, then putting layout back into the model\nso that fields, tables and answers come out structured and verifiable. Speech-to-text and TTS close\nthe loop for voice, from Whisper-style spectrogram transcription to neural synthesis built for\ntime-to-first-audio. The topics connect through one recurring decision: convert the input to text\nearly (OCR, ASR, captions) and accept the loss, embed it for cheap comparison at scale, or hand the\nraw signal to a model that reasons over it directly. After the module you can pick the right\nmodality pipeline for a task, estimate its token and latency cost, measure it with the right metric\n(recall@k, CER/WER, per-field accuracy, RTF) and route what the system cannot verify to a human. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers building these pipelines and for FDEs who meet scanned documents and\ncall recordings in nearly every enterprise deployment.","why":{"engineer":"You will wire OCR, VLM calls, embedding search and ASR/TTS into products, and you need to know which stage is failing, what each image or second of audio costs in tokens and latency, and how to measure accuracy on your own data rather than trusting a benchmark.","architect":"You decide whether a workload converts to text early, embeds for search, or sends pixels and audio to a VLM, and that choice sets cost per request, latency budget, auditability and how much human review the system needs at volume.","fde":"Customer data arrives as skewed scans, image-only PDFs, phone-quality audio and screenshots; you must prove extraction accuracy on their own documents, explain confidence-based review routing to stakeholders, and pick the cheapest pipeline that meets their bar."},"mental_model":"Picture every multimodal system as a funnel from a raw signal (pixels or a waveform) to a decision,\nwith one question at each stage: how much information do I throw away, and when? Converting early,\nwith OCR, ASR or a caption, is cheap, gives you per-word confidence and plugs into text tooling, but\nit compresses before the question is known, so layout, tables, tone and visual detail can vanish.\nEmbedding (CLIP, image embeddings) keeps a fixed-length summary tuned for comparison: ideal for\nsearching or filtering millions of items, useless for a reasoned answer about one. Passing the raw\nsignal to a VLM keeps the most information and reasons jointly, but costs tokens per image, adds\nlatency and hallucinates with confidence. Real systems chain these: embed to narrow, OCR to ground,\nVLM to reason, validate to trust, and speech models at either end when the user talks and listens.\nAlways measure where the loss happened.","order":["clip","image-embeddings","vision-language-models","ocr","document-understanding","speech-to-text","tts"],"order_reasons":{"clip":"the contrastive image-text training that underpins both multimodal search and the vision tower of most VLMs.","image-embeddings":"generalises CLIP into vectors for search, dedup and clustering, and shows how encoder choice defines similarity.","vision-language-models":"builds on the vision encoder to explain how images become tokens an LLM reasons over, and what resolution costs.","ocr":"the convert-to-text-early path, with positions and confidences, that grounds LLMs on scanned material.","document-understanding":"combines OCR, layout and VLMs with validation and review routing into structured, auditable extraction.","speech-to-text":"moves to audio, with Whisper's spectrogram encoder-decoder, WER measurement and the streaming problem.","tts":"closes the voice loop with neural synthesis, text normalisation and the time-to-first-audio budget of voice agents."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a multimodal intake assistant for an insurance claims team. Claimants submit a mix of inputs: a\nscanned or photographed claim form, an invoice or repair estimate as an image-only PDF, a few photos\nof the damage, and a short voice note describing what happened. Your system OCRs and parses the form\nand invoice into a typed claim record with per-field confidence, transcribes the voice note with a\nWhisper-class model and extracts the incident date and location from it, embeds the damage photos\nwith a CLIP-style encoder to flag near-duplicates of photos already in the claims archive, and uses a\nVLM to check whether the photos are consistent with the described damage. Cross-field validation\n(invoice total equals line items, dates agree between form, invoice and voice note) decides whether a\nclaim goes straight through or to a reviewer queue with the reasons attached. Finally, a TTS\nread-back confirms the extracted summary to the claimant over the phone. Build it on a labelled set of\nat least 30 synthetic or public claim packets so every stage can be measured independently.","deliverables":["A runnable pipeline (API or CLI) that ingests a claim packet and returns a JSON claim record with field values, source page or audio span, per-field confidence and a straight-through or review decision with reasons.","A labelled evaluation set of 30+ packets with ground-truth fields, transcripts and known duplicate photos, plus an evaluation script that reports metrics per stage rather than only end to end.","A photo deduplication index built from image embeddings, with a tuned similarity threshold and a short note on why that encoder was chosen.","A TTS read-back of the claim summary with measured time-to-first-audio and a pronunciation check on names, amounts and dates.","A one-page design note covering where the pipeline converts to text versus sends raw pixels or audio, cost per packet in tokens and seconds, and the failure modes found during evaluation."],"rubric":["The evaluation reports CER on OCR, WER on the voice notes, per-field extraction accuracy, recall@k and precision at threshold for duplicate photos, and straight-through rate, each computed on the labelled set.","Every extracted field links back to its source region or audio timestamp, and low-confidence or validation-failing fields are routed to review instead of being silently accepted.","At least three real failure cases are traced to the stage that caused them (for example OCR misread versus LLM extraction error) with a fix or mitigation, and re-running the evaluation shows the change.","Cost and latency per packet are measured, including image tokens at the shipped resolution and ASR real-time factor, and the design note justifies the resolution and model choices against them.","The TTS read-back pronounces currency amounts, dates and claimant names correctly on the test set, verified by an ASR round-trip or a listed manual check."]}},"topics":[{"id":"vision-language-models","key":"multimodal/vision-language-models","module":"multimodal","name":"Vision-language models","taxonomy_name":"vision-language models","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/clip","multimodal/image-embeddings","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/context-window","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/computer-use"],"tldr":"A vision-language model (VLM) lets a language model take images as input by turning each image into a\nsequence of \"visual tokens\" that sit in the same embedding space as text tokens. A pretrained vision\nencoder produces patch features, a small connector projects them into the LLM's input space, and the\nLLM attends over image and text jointly. It exists because describing an image as text first throws\naway exactly the details the question will turn out to need.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"clip","key":"multimodal/clip","module":"multimodal","name":"CLIP","taxonomy_name":"CLIP","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/image-embeddings","multimodal/vision-language-models","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","prompting/zero-shot"],"tldr":"CLIP trains an image encoder and a text encoder together so that an image and its caption land close\nin one shared vector space, using a contrastive loss over large batches of (image, text) pairs scraped\nfrom the web. Because the labels are free-form text rather than a fixed class list, the result can\nclassify, search and filter images by *description*, zero-shot, and its encoders became the standard\nvisual front end for vision-language models and multimodal search.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"image-embeddings","key":"multimodal/image-embeddings","module":"multimodal","name":"Image embeddings","taxonomy_name":"image embeddings","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["multimodal/clip","multimodal/vision-language-models","multimodal/document-understanding","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"An image embedding is a fixed-length vector produced by a neural encoder such that visually or\nsemantically similar images have nearby vectors, usually under cosine similarity. It exists because\npixels are not comparable (two photos of the same object differ in every pixel) and labels are\nexpensive; an embedding turns images into something a vector index can search, cluster and deduplicate.\nWhich encoder you pick decides what \"similar\" means.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ocr","key":"multimodal/ocr","module":"multimodal","name":"OCR","taxonomy_name":"OCR","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","prompting/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"OCR turns pixels that depict text into text: it finds where text is, recognises the characters in each\nregion, and returns strings with positions and confidences. It exists because a large share of\nenterprise information lives in scans, photos and image-only PDFs that have no text layer, and every\ndownstream step, search, extraction, RAG, LLM reasoning, needs characters rather than pixels. The hard\nparts are layout, image quality and knowing when the output is wrong.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"speech-to-text","key":"multimodal/speech-to-text","module":"multimodal","name":"Speech-to-text (Whisper)","taxonomy_name":"speech-to-text (Whisper)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["multimodal/tts","multimodal/vision-language-models","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/tokenization","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","safety-security/pii-redaction"],"tldr":"Speech-to-text (automatic speech recognition, ASR) converts audio into a transcript. Whisper is an\nencoder-decoder transformer that reads a log-Mel spectrogram of a 30-second window and generates text\ntokens, trained on roughly 680,000 hours of weakly labelled web audio so it transcribes, identifies\nlanguages and translates robustly across accents and noise without per-domain tuning. It exists because\nearlier systems needed carefully aligned data per language and domain and broke outside their training\nconditions.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tts","key":"multimodal/tts","module":"multimodal","name":"TTS (text-to-speech)","taxonomy_name":"TTS","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/speech-to-text","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"Text-to-speech turns a string into a waveform. Modern systems normalise the text, predict a compact\nacoustic representation (a mel-spectrogram or neural-codec tokens) with prosody, and then a vocoder or\ncodec decoder turns that into audio. It exists because the output side of every voice product, agents,\naccessibility, IVR, narration, needs speech that is intelligible, natural and generated fast enough to\nfeel conversational, and concatenating recorded fragments cannot do that for arbitrary text.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-understanding","key":"multimodal/document-understanding","module":"multimodal","name":"Document understanding","taxonomy_name":"document understanding","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["multimodal/ocr","multimodal/vision-language-models","multimodal/image-embeddings","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Document understanding is turning pages (invoices, contracts, forms, reports) into structured,\nverifiable information: classified, with fields extracted, tables recovered and questions answered.\nIt exists because text alone is not the document; meaning lives in layout, position and tables, and\nOCR-plus-regex pipelines break on every new template. The modern mechanism is a model that reads text,\nposition and pixels together, wrapped in a pipeline that validates and routes what it cannot verify.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"backend-fundamentals","tier":1,"order":12,"title":"Backend fundamentals","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the backend engineering underneath every AI product: the parts that decide whether\na model call becomes a dependable feature or a demo that falls over at the first retry, deploy or\ntenant. It opens with the languages and frameworks of the stack (Python and FastAPI at the core,\nNode.js with TypeScript at the product edge) and the HTTP contracts that join them. Next come API\ndesign (REST and GraphQL, versioning and backward compatibility, pagination, rate limits and quotas)\nfrom the provider's side, and integrating third-party APIs, SDKs and signed webhooks from the\nconsumer's side. The data layer follows: schema design, SQL and query plans, PostgreSQL transactions\nand isolation, and when NoSQL stores or Redis belong beside the relational system of record. Work that\ncannot finish inside a request moves to message queues and durable background workers, and both rely\non idempotency. Identity runs through authentication, OAuth 2.0 and OIDC, RBAC and secrets\nmanagement. The module ends with packaging and delivery: Docker images, Kubernetes, and CI/CD\npipelines that also gate prompts and models. Afterwards you can design, build, secure and ship a\nmulti-tenant AI service that survives retries, crashes, bursts and deploys, and you can defend each\nchoice in a design review or interview. It matters most for AI Engineers who own production services,\nand for FDEs who have to plug those services into a customer's identity provider, data and\ninfrastructure.","why":{"engineer":"You keep the request path fully async, wrap every model and vendor call in explicit timeouts, retries and idempotency keys, claim jobs with `SKIP LOCKED` and leases, and put an authorization check scoped to the tenant at the top of every mutating handler.","architect":"You choose the contract (REST with OpenAPI or GraphQL), the system of record versus caches and specialised stores, queue versus job table, the token and role model, and the deploy target, and you know which failure mode each choice prevents and what it costs to operate.","fde":"You connect the product to a customer's SSO through OIDC, map their identity-provider groups to roles, verify their webhooks, respect their API quotas, keep their credentials in a secrets manager, and ship into the Kubernetes cluster and CI rules they already run."},"mental_model":"Picture one request travelling through a set of trust and time boundaries. At the edge, the request\ncrosses a trust boundary: authentication turns a credential into a principal, authorization checks\nthat principal against this action on this resource, and signatures prove that inbound webhooks are\ngenuine. At the contract, the API bounds what crosses: a versioned schema, a bounded page, a rate\nlimit and a quota. At the core, the handler waits cheaply on a model, keeps the facts that must stay\ntrue in PostgreSQL behind constraints and short transactions, and keeps rebuildable fast state in\nRedis. Anything slower than the caller can wait for crosses a time boundary into a queue or a durable\njob, where at-least-once delivery makes idempotency mandatory. Around all of it, the platform\nboundary: secrets arrive through workload identity, code ships as one immutable image, Kubernetes\nreconciles desired state, and CI turns every change into evidence. Most backend bugs are a boundary\nthat someone assumed was not there.","order":["python","fastapi","node-js-typescript","rest-graphql-api-design","api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","api-and-sdk-integration","database-design","sql","postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","nosql","redis","message-queues","background-jobs-durable-workers","webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","authentication-vs-authorization","oauth-2-0-oidc","rbac","secrets-management","docker","kubernetes","ci-cd"],"order_reasons":{"python":"the language of the AI core; asyncio and the GIL explain how every later service waits on models.","fastapi":"the HTTP layer built on that async model, where Pydantic contracts and dependency injection live.","node-js-typescript":"the product-edge runtime and where the boundary with Python sits, before designing the contract between them.","rest-graphql-api-design":"resources, status codes, idempotency and the long-running-job pattern that every later API topic refines.","api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility":"how a published contract changes without breaking clients you cannot redeploy.","pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas":"the three bounds every API needs, designed as a provider and handled as a consumer.","api-and-sdk-integration":"the consumer side of APIs: timeouts, retries, error taxonomy and idempotency around vendor and model SDKs.","database-design":"the schema, keys, constraints and indexes that outlive every version of the code.","sql":"set-based queries and reading `EXPLAIN` plans against the schema you just designed.","postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels":"what concurrent writers see, and how locks and SERIALIZABLE retries prevent lost updates.","nosql":"when access-pattern-first key design beats the relational default, judged against the SQL foundation.","redis":"the fast, shared, short-lived state beside PostgreSQL: caches, rate-limit counters, locks and sessions.","message-queues":"durable buffers between producers and consumers, at-least-once delivery and the choice of broker.","background-jobs-durable-workers":"job tables, leases and idempotent job bodies, built from transactions and queues.","webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection":"HMAC verification, replay windows and ack-then-queue, which depend on idempotency and workers.","authentication-vs-authorization":"the principal versus the permission check, and why 401 differs from 403.","oauth-2-0-oidc":"delegated access and federated login, building on the authentication model.","rbac":"roles and scoped permissions that bound what users and the agents acting for them may do.","secrets-management":"keeping API keys, signing keys and OAuth client secrets out of code, images, logs and prompts.","docker":"packaging the service as one immutable image, which is the unit the remaining topics deploy.","kubernetes":"reconciling those images across a fleet with probes, resource requests and graceful termination.","ci-cd":"build once, promote many: the pipeline that tests, scans, evaluates and ships everything above."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build and ship \"DocBrief\", a multi-tenant service that summarises uploaded documents with an LLM. Write\nthe core in FastAPI on PostgreSQL. Expose a REST API, versioned under `/v1` and described by OpenAPI.\n`POST /v1/documents` returns `202` with a job id and accepts an `Idempotency-Key` header, and\n`GET /v1/jobs/{id}` reports status. List endpoints use cursor pagination. Design the schema with\ntenant-scoped foreign keys and indexes justified by `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`. Summaries run in a durable\nworker that claims jobs with `SKIP LOCKED` and a lease, calls the model provider through an adapter\nwith explicit timeouts, bounded retries and a client-side token bucket, and writes results\nidempotently. Per-tenant rate limits and a monthly token quota live in Redis, and the authoritative\nusage ledger stays in PostgreSQL. A billing provider notifies plan changes by webhook: verify the HMAC\nover the raw body, reject stale timestamps, deduplicate by event id and process the event in the\nworker. Users log in through OIDC against a local identity provider (Keycloak or a dev tenant).\nTenant `admin`, `member` and `viewer` roles gate every endpoint, and each provider key comes from a\nsecrets manager, never from the image or the repository. Package the API and the worker as Docker\nimages, deploy them to a local Kubernetes cluster (kind or k3d) with probes and graceful shutdown, and\ndrive everything from a CI pipeline.","deliverables":["A repository with the FastAPI service, the worker, migrations and a schema diagram, plus an OpenAPI document that CI checks for breaking changes against the previous release.","A design note covering the job-state machine, the idempotency strategy for the API, worker and webhook, the isolation level or lock chosen for the quota decrement, and why Redis holds counters while PostgreSQL holds the ledger.","A test suite with integration tests for concurrent idempotent submits, a worker killed mid-job, a replayed webhook, a tampered signature, a cross-tenant read attempt, and a tenant pushed over its rate limit and quota.","Dockerfiles for the API and the worker, plus Kubernetes manifests (or a Helm chart) with readiness and liveness probes, resource requests, a PodDisruptionBudget and a termination grace period longer than the longest model call.","A CI workflow that lints, tests, builds one image per commit, scans it, runs the OpenAPI compatibility check and deploys that same image digest to the cluster, authenticating without a stored long-lived cloud key.","A secrets runbook showing where each secret lives, which workload identity can read it, and a timed end-to-end rotation of the model-provider key with no failed requests."],"rubric":["Submitting the same document 50 times concurrently with one `Idempotency-Key` creates exactly one job and one model call, and every response returns the same job id.","Killing a worker pod mid-summary leaves no job stuck: the lease expires, another worker finishes the job, and exactly one summary row exists for it.","A webhook with a modified body or a timestamp outside the window gets `401` and changes no state; replaying a valid event changes nothing, and logs show it as a duplicate.","A `viewer` attempting a write gets `403`, an unauthenticated call gets `401`, and a valid token for tenant A requesting tenant B's document gets `404` with no data in the body.","Sustained traffic above a tenant's limit returns `429` with `Retry-After` and rate-limit headers, other tenants' p99 latency stays flat, and the quota never goes negative under concurrent requests.","A rolling deploy during a load test drops zero requests, and `git grep` together with an image-layer scan finds no secret in the repository or any published image."]}},"topics":[{"id":"python","key":"backend-fundamentals/python","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Python","taxonomy_name":"Python","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","backend-fundamentals/fastapi","backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript"],"tldr":"This is an orientation lesson; the deep dive is the `python-for-ai` module. Python is the default\nlanguage of AI backends not because it is fast but because every model library, tokenizer and\nprovider SDK is Python-first, and because an AI service spends almost all of its time waiting on a\nmodel rather than executing Python. `asyncio` turns that waiting into cheap concurrency; the GIL\nonly bites on CPU-bound pure-Python work, which you push to native libraries or other processes.","note":"Python as the AI backend language; deep dive lives in the python-for-ai module","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fastapi","key":"backend-fundamentals/fastapi","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"FastAPI","taxonomy_name":"FastAPI","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","backend-fundamentals/python","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design"],"tldr":"This is an orientation lesson; the deep dive is the `fastapi-for-ai` module. FastAPI is the default\nframework for Python AI services because it is async-native (ASGI, built on Starlette), uses Pydantic\nmodels as the single source of truth for validation, serialisation and OpenAPI docs, and makes\nstreaming and dependency injection first-class: exactly what an endpoint that waits seconds on a\nmodel needs. Its sharp edge: one blocking call inside an `async def` handler stalls every request on\nthat worker.","note":"why FastAPI for AI services; deep dive lives in the fastapi-for-ai module","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"node-js-typescript","key":"backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Node.js / TypeScript","taxonomy_name":"Node.js / TypeScript","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","backend-fundamentals/python","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"This is an orientation lesson; the deep dive is the `node-ts-for-ai` module. Node.js with TypeScript\nis where the product edge of an AI system lives: the web app, streaming chat routes, the\nbackend-for-frontend, serverless functions, MCP servers and agent glue. It shares the frontend's\nlanguage and its event loop proxies many concurrent streams cheaply. It is a poor home for inference,\nevals and data work, so most stacks put TypeScript at the edge and Python at the core, joined by an\nHTTP contract.","note":"where Node/TS fits in AI systems; deep dive lives in node-ts-for-ai","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rest-graphql-api-design","key":"backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"REST / GraphQL API design","taxonomy_name":"REST / GraphQL API design","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/streaming","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"An API is a contract that outlives the code behind it. REST models a system as resources addressed by\nURLs and manipulated with HTTP methods whose semantics (safe, idempotent, cacheable) every proxy,\ncache and SDK already understands. GraphQL models it as a typed graph that clients query through one\nendpoint for exactly the shape they need. Choose REST for service-to-service and model-facing\nendpoints (simple, cacheable, streamable, tool-callable); choose GraphQL when many client shapes read\none rich domain and over- and under-fetching is the real pain.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-and-sdk-integration","key":"backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"API & SDK integration","taxonomy_name":"API & SDK integration","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","python-for-ai/httpx","python-for-ai/tenacity","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers"],"tldr":"Most of an AI system is glue to APIs you do not control. An SDK is a vendor-maintained HTTP client\nthat bakes in auth, typed models, retries, pagination and stream parsing; use it, but know what it\ndoes with timeouts, retries and versions, because its defaults suit the average caller, not your\nlatency budget. The durable skill is a thin integration layer with explicit timeouts, a retry policy,\nidempotency, an error taxonomy and observability, whether or not an SDK sits underneath.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","key":"backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"API versioning & backward compatibility","taxonomy_name":"API versioning & backward compatibility","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/routers","python-for-ai/contract-testing","data-architecture/data-contracts","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","prompting/prompt-versioning","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"Once someone else's code depends on your API, every change is a negotiation with clients you cannot\nredeploy. Backward compatibility means additive changes by default (new optional fields, new\nendpoints) and tolerant readers on both sides; versioning is the escape hatch for the rare breaking\nchange, expressed in the path, a header or a pinned date, with an announced deprecation and sunset.\nIn AI systems the same discipline covers model snapshots, prompt versions and tool schemas.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","key":"backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Pagination, rate limits & API quotas","taxonomy_name":"pagination / rate limits / API quotas","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/sql","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","serving-inference/retry-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"Three bounds every API needs, at three time scales. Pagination bounds one response, because an\nunbounded list is a memory and latency bomb on both ends. Rate limits bound requests (or tokens) per\nsecond or minute, because capacity is shared and bursts must be shed before they cascade. Quotas bound\ntotal consumption per day or month, because calls cost money and tenants need entitlements. You design\nall three as a provider and handle all three as a consumer; for LLM traffic the binding limit is\nusually tokens per minute.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","key":"backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Webhook signature verification & replay protection","taxonomy_name":"webhook signature verification & replay protection","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations"],"tldr":"A webhook endpoint is a public URL that anyone on the internet can POST to, so the payload proves\nnothing by itself. Providers therefore sign each delivery: an HMAC-SHA256 over a timestamp and the raw\nbody using a secret only you and they hold, which you recompute and compare in constant time. A\nsignature alone stays valid forever, so replay protection adds a timestamp window and deduplication by\nevent id, and because providers retry on any failure, the handler has to be idempotent anyway.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"database-design","key":"backend-fundamentals/database-design","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Database design","taxonomy_name":"database design","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/nosql","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","system-design/multi-tenancy","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","rag-retrieval/vector-databases"],"tldr":"A schema is the one part of a system that outlives every version of the code, so it has to encode the\nfacts that must stay true (keys, constraints, ownership) rather than the shape of today's screens.\nNormalize so each fact lives in one place and cannot drift, then denormalize deliberately where a measured\nread path needs it. Design indexes from the queries you will actually run, not from the columns you have.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sql","key":"backend-fundamentals/sql","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"SQL","taxonomy_name":"SQL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/nosql","fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","observability/token-cost-tracking","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search"],"tldr":"SQL is declarative: you state which rows and columns you want, and a cost-based planner chooses the join\norder, scan type and algorithm. That separation is why one query can survive a 1000× growth in data if the\nright index exists, and why the same query collapses when it does not. Thinking in sets rather than loops,\nand reading the plan rather than guessing, is the whole skill.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"nosql","key":"backend-fundamentals/nosql","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"NoSQL","taxonomy_name":"NoSQL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/redis","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/distributed-systems","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","rag-retrieval/vector-databases"],"tldr":"NoSQL stores give up general joins and cross-record transactions so that data can be partitioned across\nmany machines by a key and served with predictable latency at any size. The price is that you must design\nthe data around the exact queries you will run, and accept weaker consistency on some reads. Choose one\nfor a known, narrow access pattern at scale, not because \"schemaless\" sounds easier.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis","key":"backend-fundamentals/redis","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Redis","taxonomy_name":"Redis","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","node-ts-for-ai/redis","backend-fundamentals/nosql","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","serving-inference/caching"],"tldr":"Redis is an in-memory data-structure server: strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets and streams,\naddressed by key, with every command executed atomically on a single thread. That combination makes it\nthe default place for the fast, shared, short-lived state a backend needs (caches, rate limits, locks,\nsessions, queues) at the cost of memory-sized data and durability that is weaker than a database unless\nyou configure and accept the trade.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","key":"backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"PostgreSQL transactions & isolation levels","taxonomy_name":"PostgreSQL transactions & isolation levels","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions"],"tldr":"A transaction makes a group of statements atomic and durable; the isolation level decides what each\ntransaction may see of the others while they run. PostgreSQL implements isolation with multi-version\nconcurrency control, so readers never block writers, and the default READ COMMITTED level is fast but\npermits lost updates and write skew in read-modify-write code. You fix those with explicit row locks,\natomic updates, or SERIALIZABLE plus a retry loop.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"message-queues","key":"backend-fundamentals/message-queues","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Message queues (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ)","taxonomy_name":"message queues (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/redis","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"A message queue puts a durable buffer between the code that produces work and the code that does it, so\nthe two no longer have to be up, fast, or scaled the same way at the same moment. Reliable delivery in\nevery mainstream broker means at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. Kafka is a replayable partitioned log, SQS is a\nmanaged queue with no replay, and RabbitMQ is a flexible-routing broker; the choice follows from whether\nyou need ordering, replay, routing or just a simple buffer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"background-jobs-durable-workers","key":"backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Background jobs / durable workers","taxonomy_name":"background jobs / durable workers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing"],"tldr":"A background job is work a request handler hands off so it can respond now. A *durable* worker is one\nwhere the job's existence, state and progress live in a store that outlives both the process that\nenqueued it and the process running it, so a deploy or crash never loses the work. Durability is bought\nwith a lease-and-acknowledge protocol, which means a job can run twice, so every job body must be\nidempotent.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"authentication-vs-authorization","key":"backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Authentication vs authorization","taxonomy_name":"authentication vs authorization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/rbac","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"Authentication turns a credential into a verified *principal*: who is making this request.\nAuthorization decides whether that principal may perform *this action* on *this resource*. They are\ndifferent questions, answered at different points in the request, with different failure codes: 401\nmeans \"I do not know who you are\", 403 means \"I know, and no\". Most serious access-control bugs come\nfrom doing the first and assuming it implies the second.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"oauth-2-0-oidc","key":"backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"OAuth 2.0 / OIDC","taxonomy_name":"OAuth 2.0 / OIDC","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/rbac","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/saml-sso","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"OAuth 2.0 is a delegated-authorization framework: an application obtains a scoped, expiring\naccess token to call an API on a user's behalf without ever seeing the user's password. OpenID Connect\n(OIDC) is a thin identity layer on top that adds a signed ID token so the application also learns *who*\nlogged in. OAuth answers \"may this app do X for this user\"; OIDC answers \"who is this user\". Most login\nbugs come from confusing the tokens that answer each question.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rbac","key":"backend-fundamentals/rbac","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"RBAC","taxonomy_name":"RBAC","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries"],"tldr":"Role-based access control puts one layer of indirection, the role, between who a caller is and what\nthey may do: permissions attach to roles, callers are assigned roles within a scope, and a request is\nallowed only if some role of the caller grants that permission there. It exists because assigning\npermissions directly to users does not survive growth: the assignment table explodes, access reviews\nbecome impossible and offboarding leaks. The same check must bound what an agent may do for a user.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"secrets-management","key":"backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Secrets management","taxonomy_name":"secrets management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/docker","deployment/terraform","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"A secret is any value whose disclosure lets someone act as your system: database passwords,\nmodel-provider API keys, signing keys, OAuth client secrets. Secrets management keeps them out of code,\nimages, logs and prompts; stores them encrypted in a purpose-built store; delivers them to workloads\nthat authenticate with a platform identity rather than yet another secret; and makes them short-lived,\nscoped and rotatable. The goal is not perfect hiding but a small blast radius when, not if, one leaks.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"docker","key":"backend-fundamentals/docker","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Docker","taxonomy_name":"Docker","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure"],"tldr":"Docker packages an application with its exact filesystem (OS libraries, interpreter, CUDA runtime,\nyour code) into an immutable, content-addressed image, and runs it as an isolated process on the\nhost's kernel using Linux namespaces and cgroups. It exists because \"install these dependencies on the\nserver\" is not reproducible; the image makes the build the unit of deployment, so what passed CI is\nbyte-for-byte what runs in production. For AI services the image pins the CUDA and torch stack, which\nis where most environment drift lives.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"kubernetes","key":"backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Kubernetes","taxonomy_name":"Kubernetes","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure"],"tldr":"Kubernetes runs containers across a fleet of machines by reconciling declared desired state (\"three\nreplicas of this image, one GPU each, ready when `/ready` returns 200\") against what is actually\nrunning. It exists because once there are more containers than one person can place by hand,\nscheduling, self-healing, rolling updates, service discovery and scaling have to be a platform rather\nthan scripts. For AI services, its contract of resource requests, probes and graceful termination is\nwhat makes GPU-bound, slow-starting, long-streaming workloads deployable without dropping requests.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ci-cd","key":"backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"CI/CD","taxonomy_name":"CI/CD","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"tldr":"Continuous integration means every change is merged to the main line often and verified by an\nautomated build and test run on a clean machine; continuous delivery means every verified build\nbecomes one immutable artifact that can reach production through a repeatable, automated path, and\ncontinuous deployment removes the final button. The pipeline replaces trust in laptops with evidence\nand makes releases small and routine enough that failure is cheap to detect and reverse. For AI\nsystems it must gate prompts and models too, whose tests are evaluations.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"python-for-ai","tier":1,"order":13,"title":"Python for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the Python you need to build AI systems that hold up once they leave the notebook.\nIt starts with the language itself. The object model and mutability explain the aliasing bugs that\nleak state between requests. Type hints, `TypedDict`, `Literal`, Protocols and generics give names to\nthe shapes moving through your code. Dataclasses handle trusted internal records and Pydantic\nvalidates untrusted JSON. Exceptions, generators, decorators and context managers are the idioms\nevery later library depends on. Next comes the project around the code: uv, lockfiles, a `src/`\nlayout, configuration from `.env` files and pydantic-settings, and Ruff and mypy as gates. Concurrency\nfollows, because AI services mostly wait: async/await and asyncio, cancellation, locks and semaphores,\nthe GIL, and how to choose between asyncio, threads and processes. The integration layer is httpx,\ntenacity, structured logging, dependency injection, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, tiktoken, LiteLLM,\nInstructor and the MCP SDK. Testing goes from pytest and test doubles up to property-based, contract\nand integration tests. The data and model stack is Jupyter, NumPy, pandas, pyarrow, Polars,\nscikit-learn, PyTorch, Hugging Face, safetensors, sentence-transformers and Outlines. Profiling and\nsecurity scanning close the module. Afterwards you can write a typed, tested, reproducible Python\nservice or pipeline that calls models concurrently and safely, handles their data efficiently and\nfails in ways you can diagnose. The module matters most for Engineers, who write this code every\nday. FDEs rely on it too, because they have to make the same code install, configure and run inside a\ncustomer's environment.","why":{"engineer":"You write the agent loops, SDK wrappers, async fan-outs, Pydantic schemas, data pipelines and test suites. A mutable default shared across tenants, a blocking call on the event loop, a swallowed `CancelledError` or retries on a non-retryable error are all bugs that end up in your code.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries are and which ones get Pydantic validation, which vendor SDKs sit behind small Protocols, how the import graph is layered and what concurrency model each workload uses. Those choices decide whether the provider can be swapped, whether the core runs in tests without credentials, and where the system's throughput limit sits.","fde":"Customer environments have proxies, pinned Python versions, air-gapped package mirrors, their own secrets stores and their own models. You are the one who gets a locked, audited environment to install, injects settings without code changes, points the SDK at their gateway through httpx, and profiles the job that runs out of memory on their hardware."},"mental_model":"Think of an AI program as a typed core surrounded by a membrane of boundaries, running on an event\nloop, with a data plane next to it. The core is plain Python: frozen dataclasses, Protocols and pure\nfunctions, which you can test without a network, a key or a GPU. The membrane is where untrusted or\nslow things come in. Pydantic validates every JSON payload, model output and setting that crosses\nit. Small adapters wrap the OpenAI, Anthropic, MCP and httpx clients, and the adapters translate\nvendor failures into your own exception hierarchy, with tenacity retrying only what is safe to\nrepeat. The event loop runs the I/O: it may wait as often as it likes but must never block, and\ncancellation, semaphores and context managers keep its resources bounded and released. CPU-heavy\nwork leaves the loop for threads, processes or vectorised libraries. The data plane moves tables and\ntensors through NumPy, Arrow, Parquet, Polars and PyTorch without row-by-row Python. Tooling holds it\nall in place: lockfiles fix the environment, type checkers and Ruff fix the shapes, tests pin the\nbehaviour at each layer, and logs and profiles show what actually happened.","order":["object-model-and-mutability","type-hints","typeddict-literal-enum","protocols-generics-structural-typing","dataclasses","pydantic","exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","generators","decorators","context-managers-resource-cleanup","json-jsonl","uv-poetry-pip","lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","package-structure-and-import-design","python-dotenv","pydantic-settings","ruff","mypy","async-await","asyncio","async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","gil","asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","httpx","tenacity","logging-structlog","dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","openai-sdk","anthropic-sdk","tiktoken","litellm","instructor","mcp-python-sdk","pytest","test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","pytest-asyncio","property-based-testing","contract-testing","integration-and-end-to-end-testing","jupyter","numpy","pandas","pyarrow","polars","scikit-learn-basics","pytorch","hugging-face","safetensors","sentence-transformers","outlines","memory-and-performance-profiling","security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing"],"order_reasons":{"object-model-and-mutability":"names, aliasing and mutation underlie every later bug about shared state, defaults and caches.","type-hints":"the annotation syntax that checkers, dataclasses, Pydantic and FastAPI all read.","typeddict-literal-enum":"types the wire-format dicts and closed vocabularies (roles, finish reasons) that SDKs pass around.","protocols-generics-structural-typing":"lets the consumer define small interfaces for LLM clients, embedders and stores without inheritance.","dataclasses":"the default record type for trusted internal values, built on the hints just learned.","pydantic":"adds runtime validation and JSON Schema for the untrusted data that dataclasses deliberately do not check.","exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies":"organises failures by handling policy before any remote call is written.","generators":"lazy pipelines and token streams, which context managers and streaming SDKs build on.","decorators":"the mechanism behind retries, tracing, tool registration and routes in later lessons.","context-managers-resource-cleanup":"guaranteed release of clients, streams, sessions and locks, the base for `async with`.","json-jsonl":"the formats of API payloads, eval sets and fine-tuning data, validated with Pydantic line by line.","uv-poetry-pip":"sets up projects and environments before the code grows dependencies.","lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning":"makes that environment reproducible across laptops, CI and containers.","package-structure-and-import-design":"a src layout and one-way imports turn the code into a testable, layered package.","python-dotenv":"local configuration through environment variables, and where its limits are.","pydantic-settings":"typed, validated configuration that fails at startup instead of mid-request.","ruff":"a fast lint and format gate from the first commit, including async and security rules.","mypy":"makes the type hints enforceable in CI, now that the code has real types to check.","async-await":"coroutines and the never-block rule, the base for every async lesson that follows.","asyncio":"the event loop, tasks, gather and queues that run concurrent model and tool calls.","async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation":"how timeouts, TaskGroups and disconnects actually stop work and release resources.","race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives":"locks for check-then-act and semaphores to cap concurrent provider calls.","gil":"explains why threads do not speed up pure-Python CPU work, which the next lesson relies on.","asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing":"picks the concurrency model by what the work waits on.","httpx":"the async HTTP client under the provider SDKs, whose timeouts and pools become your SDK's behaviour.","tenacity":"declarative retries with backoff and error classification for those remote calls.","logging-structlog":"structured events with request, model and token fields, so retries and calls can be queried.","dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries":"wraps slow, paid dependencies behind Protocols before the SDK lessons start.","openai-sdk":"the de facto request shape, used directly and through `base_url` for compatible servers.","anthropic-sdk":"content blocks, tool-result turns and prompt caching, which differ from OpenAI's shape.","tiktoken":"local token counting for context budgets, chunking and cost estimates on those calls.","litellm":"one interface across providers, once you know what it translates and what it hides.","instructor":"validated Pydantic objects from hosted models with re-prompting when validation fails.","mcp-python-sdk":"exposes typed Python functions as tools to any MCP host, combining decorators, Pydantic and asyncio.","pytest":"fixtures, parametrization and assertions as the base of every test lesson.","test-doubles-mocks-fixtures":"fakes and stubs for model APIs and clocks, enabled by the interface boundaries.","pytest-asyncio":"tests concurrency caps, timeouts and cancellation on a managed event loop.","property-based-testing":"Hypothesis finds the edge cases in chunkers, parsers and serialisers.","contract-testing":"keeps doubles and services in agreement on interfaces without full environments.","integration-and-end-to-end-testing":"checks the assumptions behind the doubles against real databases and HTTP stacks.","jupyter":"the exploration environment for the data lessons, and its reproducibility pitfalls.","numpy":"arrays, shapes, dtypes and broadcasting, which every later data and model library builds on.","pandas":"labelled tables for eval results and logs that fit in memory.","pyarrow":"the columnar format and Parquet I/O shared by pandas, Polars, DuckDB and datasets.","polars":"lazy, multi-core DataFrames on Arrow for gigabyte-scale or scheduled pipelines.","scikit-learn-basics":"baselines, pipelines and honest metrics that any LLM solution must beat.","pytorch":"tensors, autograd and devices, the base of the open-model stack.","hugging-face":"loading, tokenising, fine-tuning and evaluating open models on top of PyTorch.","safetensors":"the safe weight format those models should be stored and exchanged in.","sentence-transformers":"embeddings and cross-encoder reranking from open encoders for retrieval.","outlines":"constrained decoding for self-hosted models, contrasted with Instructor's retry approach.","memory-and-performance-profiling":"measures CPU, wall time and native memory across everything built so far.","security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing":"pip-audit and Bandit on the locked environment and code as the final CI gate."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer wants to triage tens of thousands of support tickets exported as JSONL into categories,\nurgency levels and extracted fields, and wants proof that it beats a cheap baseline. Build it as an\ninstallable Python package with a `src/` layout, managed and locked with uv. Validate every ticket\nline with Pydantic and report rejected lines by line number. Configuration (provider, model, concurrency,\nbudget) comes from pydantic-settings, with a committed `.env.example`. The core classifier depends on a\nsmall `LLMClient` Protocol. Implement it twice, with the OpenAI SDK and the Anthropic SDK, each built on\na shared httpx client with explicit timeouts. Get typed outputs through Instructor, or through native\nstructured output. Run calls concurrently on asyncio with a semaphore cap, tenacity retries that\napply only to transient errors, a custom exception hierarchy, and a per-run token budget estimated\nwith tiktoken. Cancelling the run must release every connection. Log one structlog event per call\nwith model, tokens, latency and retry count. As a baseline, embed tickets with sentence-transformers\nand train a scikit-learn classifier. Write results to Parquet and compare both approaches with Polars.\nShip it with tests and CI that a reviewer can run.","deliverables":["A repository with `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, a `src/` package and a CLI entry point that runs the full pipeline from a JSONL file to a Parquet results file.","Two provider adapters behind one Protocol, a Pydantic output schema, an exception hierarchy separating retryable, input-too-large and fatal errors, and a settings class that rejects invalid configuration at startup.","A test suite with a fake LLM client, pytest-asyncio tests for the concurrency cap, timeout and cancellation, Hypothesis properties for the JSONL parser and token-based chunker, and one integration test against a local fake HTTP server.","A CI workflow running `uv sync --locked`, Ruff, mypy in strict mode on the package, pytest, pip-audit and Bandit.","A short report, as a notebook that passes Restart and Run All plus a Markdown summary, comparing LLM and embedding-baseline accuracy, macro F1, cost and wall time on a held-out labelled set, with a profile of the slowest stage."],"rubric":["Running the test suite with network access disabled and no API keys passes, and switching providers is a one-line settings change with no edits to the core.","With concurrency set to 8, logs never show more than 8 calls in flight, a 429 is retried with backoff, and an invalid-key error fails once without any retry.","Pressing Ctrl+C mid-run exits within two seconds, leaves no open connections or half-written output file, and logs how many tickets were completed.","A malformed JSONL line is reported with its line number and skipped, and mypy strict and Ruff report zero errors without blanket `type: ignore` or `noqa` comments.","A fresh clone on another machine reproduces the same environment from the lockfile, and pip-audit reports no unaddressed known vulnerabilities.","The report states whether the LLM beats the scikit-learn baseline on macro F1 and cost per thousand tickets, using the same held-out split for both."]}},"topics":[{"id":"type-hints","key":"python-for-ai/type-hints","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Type hints","taxonomy_name":"type hints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/dataclasses","agents/typed-tool-contracts","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types"],"tldr":"Type hints are annotations on parameters, return values and variables that describe what a value\nis expected to be. The interpreter stores them and ignores them; static checkers (mypy, pyright)\nand runtime libraries (Pydantic, FastAPI, dataclasses) read them. They exist because AI code is\nmostly glue between untyped JSON, provider SDKs and your own functions, and the cheapest place to\ncatch a shape mismatch is before the code runs, not after a thirty-minute batch job fails.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"protocols-generics-structural-typing","key":"python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Protocols, generics and structural typing","taxonomy_name":"Protocols / generics / structural typing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types"],"tldr":"A Protocol says \"anything with these methods is acceptable\", so a type checker can verify duck\ntyping without inheritance. Generics let you write a function or class once and have the checker\ntrack the element type through it, so `first(xs: list[Message])` is known to return a `Message`.\nTogether they are how you type the swappable parts of an AI system (embedders, LLM clients, vector\nstores, retry decorators) without forcing third-party classes into your class hierarchy.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"typeddict-literal-enum","key":"python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","module":"python-for-ai","name":"TypedDict, Literal and Enum","taxonomy_name":"TypedDict / Literal / Enum","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/state-machines"],"tldr":"`TypedDict` types a plain dict with fixed keys, for JSON-shaped data you do not want to wrap in\nobjects. `Literal` restricts a value to a small fixed set of constants at zero runtime cost. `Enum`\nturns such a set into real runtime objects with identity, iteration and methods. AI code is full of\nwire-format dicts and tiny closed vocabularies (roles, finish reasons, tool names), and the type\nchecker can only protect those if you name them; these three are how you do it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic","key":"python-for-ai/pydantic","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Pydantic","taxonomy_name":"Pydantic","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","python-for-ai/instructor","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Pydantic turns type-annotated classes into validators, parsers, serializers and JSON Schema\ngenerators. You declare the shape once as a `BaseModel`; untrusted input (API payloads, LLM output,\nconfig files) is validated into typed objects with structured error reports, and the same class\nemits the JSON Schema you hand to a model for structured output or tool calling. It exists because\nAI systems are mostly untrusted JSON crossing boundaries, and type hints alone do nothing at runtime.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dataclasses","key":"python-for-ai/dataclasses","module":"python-for-ai","name":"dataclasses","taxonomy_name":"dataclasses","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores"],"tldr":"`@dataclass` generates `__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__` and optionally ordering, hashing and\n`__slots__` from a class's annotated fields, so a record type is five lines instead of thirty. It\nexists because AI code is full of small structured records (generation configs, agent steps,\nretrieved chunks) that need equality, readable reprs and sometimes immutability, and hand-writing\nthose methods is where bugs and inconsistencies creep in. It validates nothing; that is Pydantic's\njob.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"object-model-and-mutability","key":"python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Object model and mutability","taxonomy_name":"object model & mutability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/decorators","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"In Python a variable is a name bound to an object, never a box holding a value, and assignment never\ncopies. Objects are either mutable (list, dict, set, most classes, arrays and tensors) or immutable\n(int, str, tuple, frozenset). Nearly every \"the value changed by itself\" bug in AI code is aliasing: two\nnames, a default argument, a class attribute or a slice sharing one mutable object. Knowing which\noperations rebind a name and which mutate the object is the difference between a leak across tenants\nand a correct program.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","key":"python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Exceptions and custom exception hierarchies","taxonomy_name":"exceptions & custom exception hierarchies","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","serving-inference/retry-strategies","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","system-design/model-fallback-strategies"],"tldr":"Exceptions carry a failure up the call stack until something that knows what to do catches it. A\ncustom hierarchy organises failures by *how they should be handled* (retry, shrink the input,\nre-prompt, give up) rather than by where they came from, so callers catch one base class or one\nprecise leaf and never string-match messages. It is what keeps a rate limit from being treated like\nan invalid API key, and a bug in your own code from being retried at provider prices.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-await","key":"python-for-ai/async-await","module":"python-for-ai","name":"async / await","taxonomy_name":"async / await","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"`async def` defines a coroutine, a function that can pause at `await` while it waits for I/O and\nlet other coroutines run on the same thread. An event loop schedules them. It exists because AI\nworkloads are dominated by waiting on slow network calls (model providers, vector stores, tools),\noften many at once, and a single thread that interleaves thousands of waits is cheaper and simpler\nthan a thread per call. It does nothing for CPU-bound work, and one blocking call stalls everything.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"asyncio","key":"python-for-ai/asyncio","module":"python-for-ai","name":"asyncio","taxonomy_name":"asyncio","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue"],"tldr":"An AI service spends almost all of its wall-clock time waiting: on an LLM provider, a vector database,\na tool endpoint. asyncio lets one thread interleave thousands of those waits by running coroutines on\nan event loop that only resumes a coroutine when the thing it awaited is ready. You get high\nconcurrency for I/O at near-zero per-task cost, in exchange for a strict contract: nothing on the loop\nmay block, or everything stalls.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","key":"python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Async cancellation & exception propagation","taxonomy_name":"async cancellation & exception propagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","agents/parallel-tool-calls"],"tldr":"Cancelling an asyncio task does not kill it: it schedules a `CancelledError` to be raised at the\ntask's next `await`, so the task can run its `finally` blocks and release connections. Errors in\nconcurrent children must also travel somewhere. `TaskGroup` and `asyncio.timeout` build on this one\nmechanism, which is why swallowing `CancelledError` silently breaks deadlines, sibling cleanup and\nshutdown.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","key":"python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Race conditions & synchronization primitives (asyncio.Lock, Semaphore)","taxonomy_name":"race conditions & synchronization primitives (asyncio.Lock, Semaphore)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/backpressure","agents/parallel-tool-calls"],"tldr":"asyncio runs on one thread, yet it still has race conditions: any `await` between reading shared state\nand writing it lets another coroutine change that state in between. `asyncio.Lock` makes such a\ncheck-then-act sequence exclusive, and `asyncio.Semaphore` caps how many coroutines may be inside a\nsection at once, which is how you bound concurrent calls to an LLM provider. Neither is thread-safe,\nand a semaphore limits concurrency, not requests per second.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","key":"python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"asyncio vs threads vs multiprocessing","taxonomy_name":"asyncio vs threads vs multiprocessing","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines"],"tldr":"Pick by what the work waits on. asyncio suits many concurrent network waits, as long as the libraries\nare async. Threads suit blocking I/O libraries and C code that releases the GIL. Processes suit\nCPU-bound pure Python, which the GIL otherwise serialises. Real AI pipelines mix all three: an async\ncore, `asyncio.to_thread` for blocking SDKs, and a process pool for parsing or tokenising at scale.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gil","key":"python-for-ai/gil","module":"python-for-ai","name":"GIL (Global Interpreter Lock)","taxonomy_name":"GIL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks"],"tldr":"The GIL is a mutex in standard CPython that lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time.\nIt exists because CPython's memory management (reference counting) and much C-extension code are not\nthread-safe, and one lock made both simple and fast single-threaded. Threads still help with I/O and with\nnative code that releases the GIL, but not with pure-Python CPU work. It also does not make your code thread-safe.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"generators","key":"python-for-ai/generators","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Generators","taxonomy_name":"generators","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","serving-inference/streaming","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines"],"tldr":"A generator is a function that `yield`s values one at a time and pauses between them, keeping its local\nstate. It exists so you can process data lazily: a 10 GB JSONL corpus or an endless token stream becomes\na pipeline of small stages, each holding one item or batch in memory instead of the whole dataset. Async\ngenerators do the same for streams that arrive over the network, which is how LLM token streaming is\nusually exposed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"decorators","key":"python-for-ai/decorators","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Decorators","taxonomy_name":"decorators","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","agents/tool-calling","observability/tracing","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection"],"tldr":"A decorator is a callable that takes a function and returns a replacement, applied with `@name` above the\ndefinition. It exists so cross-cutting behaviour (retries, timing, tracing, caching, registering a function as\nan LLM tool or an HTTP route) is written once and attached declaratively instead of copied into every function.\nThe sharp edges are metadata loss without `functools.wraps`, wrapping async functions with sync wrappers, and\nstacking order.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-managers-resource-cleanup","key":"python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Context managers / resource cleanup","taxonomy_name":"context managers / resource cleanup","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/httpx","python-for-ai/pytorch","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining"],"tldr":"A context manager pairs setup with guaranteed teardown: `with` runs `__enter__`, your block, then\n`__exit__` whether the block returns, raises or is cancelled. It exists because the resources an AI\nservice holds (HTTP connection pools, streams, DB sessions, locks, temp files, GPU memory modes) leak\nquietly when cleanup depends on every code path remembering it. `async with` and `contextlib` extend the\npattern to async code, generators and dynamic sets of resources.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","key":"python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection & interface boundaries","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection & interface boundaries","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"Dependency injection means a component receives the things it depends on (an LLM client, a vector\nstore, a clock) from outside instead of importing or constructing them itself. An interface boundary\nnames that dependency as a small `Protocol` in your own vocabulary rather than the vendor's. Together\nthey make AI code testable without network calls, swappable across providers, and honest about what it\nneeds, at the price of one layer of indirection that you must keep thin.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"package-structure-and-import-design","key":"python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Package structure & import design","taxonomy_name":"package structure & import design","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/ruff","python-for-ai/mypy","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"In Python, the import graph is your architecture: whatever imports what decides which code can be\ntested alone, what runs at startup and what breaks when a dependency changes. Use a `src/` layout so\ntests exercise the installed package, keep imports pointing one way (domain ← services ← adapters ←\nentrypoints), keep side effects out of import time, and import heavy SDKs and model libraries lazily or\nonly at the edges.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenacity","key":"python-for-ai/tenacity","module":"python-for-ai","name":"tenacity","taxonomy_name":"tenacity","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/timeouts","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","python-for-ai/httpx","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"tenacity is a Python library that turns a retry policy into a declaration: when to stop, how long to wait,\nwhich outcomes to retry, and what to log. LLM and embedding APIs fail transiently all the time (429s, 5xx,\ntimeouts), and a hand-written retry loop usually gets backoff, jitter and error classification wrong.\ntenacity makes those choices explicit and the same for sync and async code, but it cannot make an unsafe\noperation safe to repeat.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"logging-structlog","key":"python-for-ai/logging-structlog","module":"python-for-ai","name":"logging / structlog","taxonomy_name":"logging / structlog","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/logging","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","observability/tracing","observability/opentelemetry","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","safety-security/pii-redaction","observability/token-cost-tracking","python-for-ai/async-await"],"tldr":"Python's standard `logging` module routes records from named loggers through levels, handlers and\nformatters; structlog sits on top and makes every log line a structured event (a dict of key-value pairs)\nthat is rendered as JSON in production. For AI services this matters because the questions you ask in an\nincident (\"which model, how many tokens, which request, which retry?\") are queries over fields, not greps\nover prose.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-and-performance-profiling","key":"python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Memory & performance profiling","taxonomy_name":"memory & performance profiling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","observability/latency-percentiles","observability/tracing","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization"],"tldr":"Profiling replaces guessing with measurement: a CPU profiler tells you which functions consume time, a\nmemory profiler tells you which lines allocate the bytes that are still alive. In AI services the answer is\noften surprising: most wall time is spent waiting on the network or a GPU, and much memory is held by native\nbuffers (PyTorch tensors, tokenizers) that Python's own tools cannot see. Pick the tool by the\nquestion: CPU or wall time, Python heap or native memory, dev box or live process.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"uv-poetry-pip","key":"python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","module":"python-for-ai","name":"uv / poetry / pip","taxonomy_name":"uv / poetry / pip","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","python-for-ai/pytorch","backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"pip installs packages; Poetry and uv manage whole projects: declared dependencies in `pyproject.toml`, a\nresolved lockfile, virtual environments and builds. The reason to care is reproducibility: an AI service\ndepends on a deep, fast-moving stack (SDKs, torch, tokenizers), and \"it worked on my machine\" is almost\nalways two environments resolving to different versions. uv does what pip, pip-tools, virtualenv, pyenv and\npipx do, much faster, which is why many new projects now default to it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","key":"python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Lockfiles & dependency pinning","taxonomy_name":"lockfiles & dependency pinning","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/docker","python-for-ai/openai-sdk"],"tldr":"Declare what you *need* as ranges, record what you *got* as exact versions with hashes, and install only\nfrom the record. A lockfile turns \"the set of packages that satisfied my constraints on the day I\nresolved\" into a committed artifact, so every developer, CI run and container installs the same bytes. For\napplications this is non-negotiable; libraries publish ranges and leave locking to whoever deploys them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","key":"python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Security scanning & dependency auditing (pip-audit, Bandit)","taxonomy_name":"security scanning & dependency auditing (pip-audit, Bandit)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/ruff","python-for-ai/safetensors","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"Two complementary automated checks: **pip-audit** compares the exact versions you install against public\nvulnerability databases (known CVEs in *other people's code*), and **Bandit** statically scans *your own\ncode* for risky patterns such as `pickle.load`, `eval`, `shell=True` and hard-coded secrets. Both run in CI\nin seconds. Neither proves code is safe: they catch known and pattern-shaped problems cheaply, so humans can\nspend review time on design-level risks.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytest","key":"python-for-ai/pytest","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pytest","taxonomy_name":"pytest","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/property-based-testing","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/ruff","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"pytest is the de facto Python test runner: plain `assert` statements that print the values involved\nwhen they fail, fixtures that inject setup by argument name, parametrization that turns one function\ninto a table of cases, and a hook system that asyncio, Hypothesis, xdist and coverage tools plug into.\nIt exists because unittest's class-and-method ceremony and opaque assertion failures made tests\nexpensive to write and slow to debug; pytest makes the cheap path the correct one.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytest-asyncio","key":"python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pytest-asyncio","taxonomy_name":"pytest-asyncio","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio"],"tldr":"pytest calls test functions synchronously, so an `async def` test is just a coroutine object nobody\nawaits. pytest-asyncio is the plugin that runs async tests and async fixtures on an asyncio event loop\nit owns, with markers and config that decide which tests are async and how long each loop lives. Most\nof its sharp edges come from that loop lifetime: a resource made on one loop cannot be used on another.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","key":"python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Test doubles, mocks and fixtures","taxonomy_name":"test doubles / mocks / fixtures","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility"],"tldr":"A test double stands in for a real collaborator (an LLM, an HTTP API, a database) so a test is fast,\ndeterministic and free. Doubles come in kinds: dummies, stubs, spies, mocks and fakes, which differ in\nwhether they return canned data, record calls, or behave like a small real implementation. Fixtures are\nhow pytest builds, shares and tears down those doubles and other test state. Choose the kind by what\nthe test must verify: the result, or the interaction.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"property-based-testing","key":"python-for-ai/property-based-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Property-based testing (Hypothesis)","taxonomy_name":"property-based testing (Hypothesis)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/contract-testing","evals/property-based-testing","evals/metamorphic-testing"],"tldr":"Instead of writing example inputs by hand, you state a property that must hold for every input\n(\"chunks never exceed the size limit and rejoin to the original text\"), and Hypothesis generates\nhundreds of inputs, including the nasty edge cases you would not think of, then shrinks any failure to\na minimal counterexample. It exists because the bugs in parsing, chunking and serialisation code hide in\ninputs nobody writes examples for: empty strings, Unicode, boundaries.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"contract-testing","key":"python-for-ai/contract-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Contract testing","taxonomy_name":"contract testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","agents/typed-tool-contracts","data-architecture/data-contracts"],"tldr":"A contract test checks that two components agree on the interface between them (request shapes,\nresponse fields, status codes, error formats) without running both at once. The consumer records what\nit relies on; the provider verifies, in its own build, that it still satisfies every consumer. It exists\nbecause mocks drift from the real service silently, and full end-to-end environments are too slow and\nfragile to catch every interface break.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"integration-and-end-to-end-testing","key":"python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Integration and end-to-end testing","taxonomy_name":"integration & end-to-end testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","evals/regression-tests","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments"],"tldr":"Unit tests with doubles prove your logic is right given your assumptions about the outside world.\nIntegration tests run your code against real dependencies (a real Postgres with pgvector, a real Redis,\na real HTTP stack) to check those assumptions; end-to-end tests drive the whole deployed system through\nits public interface the way a user would. You need fewer of each as you go up, because each level is\nslower and flakier, but some failures only exist at those levels.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mypy","key":"python-for-ai/mypy","module":"python-for-ai","name":"mypy","taxonomy_name":"mypy","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/ruff","agents/typed-tool-contracts","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"mypy is a static type checker: it reads your type hints, infers the rest, and reports code that would\npass the wrong type, read a missing key or call a method on `None`, without running anything. Python\nignores hints at runtime, so without a checker they are comments that drift. mypy is gradual: unannotated\ncode is mostly unchecked, which lets large codebases adopt it module by module but also hides gaps.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ruff","key":"python-for-ai/ruff","module":"python-for-ai","name":"ruff","taxonomy_name":"ruff","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","python-for-ai/asyncio","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"Ruff is a Python linter and formatter written in Rust that replaces a stack of separate tools (flake8 and\nits plugins, isort, pyupgrade, most of bandit's checks and a subset of pylint's, and Black for formatting) with one\nfast binary and one config section. It exists because a slow, fragmented lint toolchain gets skipped;\nfast enough to run on every save makes it a habit. It checks style and likely bugs, not types.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"python-dotenv","key":"python-for-ai/python-dotenv","module":"python-for-ai","name":"python-dotenv","taxonomy_name":"python-dotenv","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":9,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","backend-fundamentals/docker","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing"],"tldr":"python-dotenv reads `KEY=value` lines from a `.env` file into `os.environ`, so code can read\nconfiguration from environment variables everywhere while developers keep their local values in a\ngitignored file. It is a development convenience, not a secrets manager: it does no typing, no\nvalidation and no encryption, and by default it never overrides variables that are already set, which is\nexactly what lets production inject real values without the file.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic-settings","key":"python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pydantic-settings","taxonomy_name":"pydantic-settings","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/python-dotenv","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"tldr":"pydantic-settings is a Pydantic model whose fields are filled from environment variables, `.env` files\nand secret files instead of a request body. You declare configuration once, with types, defaults and\nconstraints, and the app either starts with a fully validated settings object or refuses to start with\nan error naming the bad field. That moves config mistakes from 2 a.m. runtime failures to deploy-time\ncrashes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"json-jsonl","key":"python-for-ai/json-jsonl","module":"python-for-ai","name":"JSON / JSONL","taxonomy_name":"JSON / JSONL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/pyarrow","evals/golden-dataset","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","observability/logging"],"tldr":"JSON is the lingua franca of LLM systems: API payloads, tool arguments, structured outputs. JSONL (JSON\nLines) puts one complete JSON value on each line, which makes a file appendable, streamable and\nsplittable, so it is the standard container for eval sets, fine-tuning data, batch requests and logs.\nUse JSON for one document, JSONL for many records; and know the edges (NaN, big integers, encoding)\nwhere Python's defaults produce output other parsers reject.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"numpy","key":"python-for-ai/numpy","module":"python-for-ai","name":"numpy","taxonomy_name":"numpy","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/pyarrow","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","python-for-ai/gil"],"tldr":"NumPy's `ndarray` is a block of typed, contiguous memory plus a shape and strides, and its operations\nrun compiled loops over that memory instead of Python bytecode per element. That is why embedding math,\nmetrics and feature arrays are written as whole-array expressions: you get compiled speed and compact\nmemory, but you must think in shapes, dtypes, broadcasting and views, where most NumPy bugs live.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pandas","key":"python-for-ai/pandas","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pandas","taxonomy_name":"pandas","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/polars","python-for-ai/pyarrow","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/jupyter","evals/error-analysis","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling"],"tldr":"pandas gives Python a labelled, in-memory table, the DataFrame, built from typed column arrays plus an\nindex, with vectorized column math, group-by, joins, reshaping and readers for nearly every file format.\nIt is the default tool for exploring eval results, logs and datasets that fit in RAM. Its sharp edges\ncome from its age and flexibility: index alignment, copy-versus-view semantics, eager execution and\nmemory use several times the data size.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"polars","key":"python-for-ai/polars","module":"python-for-ai","name":"polars","taxonomy_name":"polars","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/pyarrow","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing"],"tldr":"Polars is a DataFrame library written in Rust on the Arrow columnar memory model, with an expression API\nand a lazy mode that turns your code into a query plan, optimizes it (pushing filters and column\nselection down into the file scan) and runs it on all cores. For tables from a few gigabytes upward,\nor pipelines that run repeatedly, it is typically much faster and leaner than pandas; the price is a\ndifferent, stricter API and no row index.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pyarrow","key":"python-for-ai/pyarrow","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pyarrow","taxonomy_name":"pyarrow","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/polars","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","data-architecture/lakehouse","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines"],"tldr":"pyarrow is the Python binding to Apache Arrow, a language-independent columnar memory format, and the\nstandard Python reader and writer for Parquet. Arrow lets pandas, Polars, DuckDB, Spark and Hugging Face\ndatasets hand tables to each other without serializing, often without copying; Parquet stores those\ntables compressed on disk with per-column statistics so readers fetch only what a query needs. You rarely\nwrite much pyarrow directly, but it sits under most of your data stack.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"scikit-learn-basics","key":"python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","module":"python-for-ai","name":"scikit-learn basics","taxonomy_name":"scikit-learn basics","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pandas"],"tldr":"scikit-learn is classical machine learning behind one small, consistent interface: every model and\npreprocessing step is an estimator with `fit`, then `predict` or `transform`. Pipelines chain them so\npreprocessing is learned only from training data, and model-selection tools split, cross-validate and\nscore honestly. For AI engineers it is the fastest way to build baselines, embedding-based classifiers\nand evaluation metrics, often good enough that no LLM call is needed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytorch","key":"python-for-ai/pytorch","module":"python-for-ai","name":"PyTorch (tensors, autograd, nn.Module, DataLoader, device)","taxonomy_name":"PyTorch (tensors, autograd, nn.Module, DataLoader, device)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":20,"related":["python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/safetensors","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing"],"tldr":"PyTorch is NumPy-style arrays (tensors) that can live on a GPU and that record the operations applied\nto them, so `loss.backward()` computes every gradient automatically. `nn.Module` packages parameters and\nforward logic, `DataLoader` feeds batches in parallel, and `device` decides where memory lives. It won\nbecause the graph is built by running ordinary Python, so you debug models with print statements and\n`pdb` rather than a separate graph compiler.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hugging-face","key":"python-for-ai/hugging-face","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Hugging Face (transformers, datasets, tokenizers, accelerate, PEFT, TRL)","taxonomy_name":"Hugging Face (transformers, datasets, tokenizers, accelerate, PEFT, TRL)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":22,"related":["python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/safetensors","python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","model-internals/tokenization","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Hugging Face is a stack of libraries over a model hub. `transformers` loads any published architecture\nand its weights by name; `tokenizers` turns text into the exact ids that model expects; `datasets` streams\nand memory-maps training data; `accelerate` runs the same PyTorch code on one GPU or many; `PEFT` trains\nsmall adapters instead of all weights; `TRL` provides SFT and preference-tuning trainers. It exists\nbecause every open model would otherwise ship its own incompatible loading and training code.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sentence-transformers","key":"python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","module":"python-for-ai","name":"sentence-transformers","taxonomy_name":"sentence-transformers","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/pytorch","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"sentence-transformers is the standard Python library for turning text into fixed-size vectors whose\ncosine similarity tracks meaning, and for scoring query-document pairs with cross-encoders. It wraps a\nHugging Face encoder with the pooling, normalisation and training losses that make its outputs usable\nas embeddings. It exists because a raw transformer's token outputs are not good sentence vectors, and\ncomparing every pair through a full model does not scale to search.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tiktoken","key":"python-for-ai/tiktoken","module":"python-for-ai","name":"tiktoken","taxonomy_name":"tiktoken","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/context-window","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/hugging-face","observability/token-cost-tracking","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"tiktoken is OpenAI's fast byte-pair-encoding tokenizer, a Rust core with Python bindings, that turns\ntext into exactly the token ids OpenAI models see. You use it locally to count tokens before a request,\nbudget context windows, chunk documents by tokens and estimate cost. It is only exact for OpenAI's\nencodings; other model families tokenise differently, so its counts are an approximation for them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"safetensors","key":"python-for-ai/safetensors","module":"python-for-ai","name":"safetensors","taxonomy_name":"safetensors","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/hugging-face","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/quantization","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization"],"tldr":"safetensors is a file format for storing tensors: a small JSON header describing each tensor's name,\ndtype, shape and byte offsets, followed by the raw bytes. It replaced pickle-based PyTorch checkpoints\non the Hugging Face Hub because loading a pickle can execute arbitrary code, while loading safetensors\nonly reads numbers, and because its layout lets weights be memory-mapped and loaded lazily and fast.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"openai-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/openai-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"OpenAI SDK","taxonomy_name":"OpenAI SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","python-for-ai/litellm","python-for-ai/tiktoken","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/httpx","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"The `openai` Python package is the official typed client for OpenAI's API: sync and async clients,\nPydantic-typed responses, streaming, tool calling, structured outputs, and built-in retries and\ntimeouts. It matters beyond OpenAI because its Chat Completions request shape became the de facto\ninterface that many other servers (vLLM, Ollama, gateways) imitate, so the same client often talks to\nthem by changing `base_url`.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"anthropic-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Anthropic SDK","taxonomy_name":"Anthropic SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/litellm","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/function-tool-calling","agents/tool-calling","agents/parallel-tool-calls","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks"],"tldr":"The `anthropic` Python package is the official typed client for Claude's Messages API. Its model is\ndifferent enough from OpenAI's to matter: responses are lists of typed content blocks (text, thinking,\ntool use), the system prompt is a top-level parameter, `max_tokens` is required, tool results go back\nas blocks in a user message, and prompt caching is controlled explicitly with `cache_control`. Learn\nthose shapes and most Claude integration bugs become obvious.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"litellm","key":"python-for-ai/litellm","module":"python-for-ai","name":"LiteLLM","taxonomy_name":"LiteLLM","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"LiteLLM is an open-source Python library and proxy server that puts many LLM providers behind one\nOpenAI-style interface: `completion(model=\"anthropic/...\", messages=...)` returns the same response shape\nwhichever provider served it. On top it adds routing, retries, fallbacks, cost tracking and, as a proxy,\nvirtual keys and budgets. It exists because multi-provider code otherwise means one integration per\nvendor, and its main cost is that translation can hide or lag provider-specific features.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"instructor","key":"python-for-ai/instructor","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Instructor","taxonomy_name":"Instructor","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/outlines","python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation"],"tldr":"Instructor wraps an LLM provider client so that a call takes a Pydantic model as `response_model`\nand returns a validated instance of it instead of raw text. Under the hood it turns the model into a\ntool or JSON schema, parses the reply, and when validation fails it sends the error back to the LLM\nand retries. It exists because \"ask for JSON and `json.loads` it\" breaks in production, and the fix is\nthe same loop in every codebase.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"outlines","key":"python-for-ai/outlines","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Outlines","taxonomy_name":"Outlines","kind":"tool","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/hugging-face","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/sampling-strategies","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Outlines makes a language model's output match a regex, a JSON Schema, a fixed set of choices or a\ngrammar *by construction*, by masking every token that would break the pattern before sampling. Invalid\noutput becomes impossible rather than detected and retried. The catch is that it needs access to the\nmodel's logits, so it applies to models you run yourself (or engines that integrate it), not to closed\nhosted APIs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-python-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"MCP Python SDK","taxonomy_name":"MCP Python SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/type-hints"],"tldr":"The MCP Python SDK (the `mcp` package) implements the Model Context Protocol so you can expose\nPython functions, data and prompt templates to any MCP-capable host, and call other MCP servers from\nPython. Its FastMCP layer turns a decorated, type-hinted function into a tool with a generated JSON\nSchema; the lower layers handle JSON-RPC, the initialize handshake and transports (stdio, Streamable\nHTTP). This lesson is about the SDK; the protocol itself is taught in the agents module.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"httpx","key":"python-for-ai/httpx","module":"python-for-ai","name":"httpx","taxonomy_name":"httpx","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"httpx is a Python HTTP client with a requests-style API that works both synchronously and with\n`async`/`await`, supports streaming responses, optional HTTP/2, connection pooling and pluggable\ntransports for testing. It matters in AI work because LLM calls are slow, concurrent, streamed network\nrequests, and the official OpenAI and Anthropic Python SDKs are built on it, so its timeout and pooling\nbehaviour is your SDK's behaviour.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"jupyter","key":"python-for-ai/jupyter","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Jupyter","taxonomy_name":"Jupyter","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","evals/error-analysis","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc"],"tldr":"Jupyter runs code interactively in cells against a long-lived kernel, keeping variables in memory\nbetween cells and saving code, outputs and charts together in an `.ipynb` file. That makes it the fastest\nway to explore data, iterate on prompts and read model outputs side by side. The same persistent state\nthat makes it fast also makes notebooks unreliable as production code or as evidence, unless you\nrestart and run everything top to bottom.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"fastapi-for-ai","tier":1,"order":14,"title":"FastAPI for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building the HTTP service that sits in front of a model: the FastAPI process that\naccepts prompts, files and chat sessions, calls a provider or a local model, and returns tokens without\nfalling over under real traffic. It starts with the runtime underneath. The ASGI lifecycle explains\nwhat a request, a stream, a disconnect and a startup actually are, and async endpoints set the rule\nthat decides everything else: await remote models, never block the event loop. Next comes the contract\nlayer. Pydantic request and response models, `response_model`, error handling, routers and dependency\ninjection define what goes in, what may come out and who is calling. Lifespan and resource scoping then\ndecide what is built once per worker (model weights, the shared httpx client, the database engine) and\nwhat lives for one request (session, principal, budget). The middle of the module covers the shapes AI\ntraffic takes: streaming responses, Server-Sent Events, WebSockets and cancellation when the user closes\nthe tab; async database access, SQLAlchemy and Alembic; uploads and their size and safety limits;\nbackground tasks versus durable job queues. Protection follows: timeouts, idempotency keys, CORS,\nauthentication, Redis caching, rate limits, concurrency caps and backpressure. The last group makes the\nservice operable and provable: structured logs, OpenTelemetry traces, health and readiness probes,\nworker sizing, graceful draining, OpenAPI docs and three layers of tests. Afterwards you can ship an\nauthenticated, streaming, cost-bounded LLM API that survives deploys, overload and retries, and explain\nits behaviour from a trace. It matters most for Engineers who build and run these services, and for\nFDEs who deploy them into customer environments.","why":{"engineer":"You write the endpoints, dependencies, streaming generators and worker configuration, so a blocking SDK call that freezes every request, a pool exhausted by a held connection, a double-billed retry or a stream that keeps generating for a closed tab is a bug in your code.","architect":"You decide what is application-scoped versus request-scoped, where slow work goes (BackgroundTasks or a durable queue), how concurrency, rate and token budgets are enforced, and how workers map to memory and GPUs, and those choices fix the service's cost and failure behaviour.","fde":"Customers deploy the service behind their own proxies, identity providers and Kubernetes clusters, so you are the one tuning CORS and auth, fixing buffering that breaks streaming, setting probe and drain timeouts, and proving with traces and contract tests that it behaves under their load."},"mental_model":"Picture one worker process as an event loop with two kinds of objects and three gates. The application-\nscoped objects are built once in lifespan and shared by every request: the model or provider client,\nits connection pool, the database engine, the Redis client, the semaphore. The request-scoped objects\nare created by dependencies and destroyed when the request ends: the session, the principal, the\nbudget, the trace span. Every request passes three gates on the way to the model. At the edge,\nmiddleware, size limits, CORS, auth and rate limits decide whether it gets in at all. At admission,\nconcurrency limits, idempotency keys and caches decide whether it needs to call the model. At\nexecution, timeouts, cancellation and backpressure decide how long it may hold its slot and what\nhappens if the client goes away. Anything slower than a request leaves the loop for a durable worker.\nLogs, traces and probes are how you see each gate working, and tests pin the contract to the schema.","order":["asgi-lifecycle","async-endpoints","pydantic-request-response-models","response-model","httpexception-handling","routers","dependency-injection","lifespan","request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","shared-httpx-asyncclient","middleware-ordering","cors","api-keys-oauth2-jwt","timeouts","streamingresponse","server-sent-events","cancellation-on-client-disconnect","websockets","async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","alembic-migrations","uploadfile","request-size-limits-and-upload-security","backgroundtasks","job-queues-and-durable-workers","idempotency-keys","redis-caching","rate-limiting","concurrency-limits","backpressure","structured-logging","opentelemetry-instrumentation","health-readiness-endpoints","uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","openapi-docs","testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","openapi-contract-testing"],"order_reasons":{"asgi-lifecycle":"the scope/receive/send protocol underlies lifespan, middleware, streaming and disconnects, so every later lesson refers back to it.","async-endpoints":"the async versus def rule and the blocking-call trap decide how every handler, client and database call must be written.","pydantic-request-response-models":"defines the validated input contract that stops malformed or oversized prompts before they cost money.","response-model":"completes the contract on the way out so internal fields and raw provider payloads never reach clients.","httpexception-handling":"maps validation, domain and upstream LLM failures onto one consistent error shape built on those models.","routers":"splits the growing service by feature and version once there are enough endpoints and errors to organise.","dependency-injection":"introduces Depends, the mechanism that delivers auth, sessions, clients and test overrides to handlers.","lifespan":"shows where expensive shared resources are built and closed once per worker, which dependencies then hand out.","request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources":"names the scoping rule that lifespan and dependencies implement, and the leaks when it is wrong.","shared-httpx-asyncclient":"applies that rule to the most common resource in an AI service, the pooled client calling model providers.","middleware-ordering":"explains the wrapping stack that request IDs, CORS, auth and timing all sit in before you add any of them.","cors":"the first middleware most browser-facing AI apps need, and why it is not authentication.","api-keys-oauth2-jwt":"adds real caller identity for billing and data scoping, implemented as dependencies and middleware.","timeouts":"bounds every outbound model call and the request as a whole before streams make requests long-lived.","streamingresponse":"sends tokens as they are generated, with the frozen-status rule that shapes mid-stream error handling.","server-sent-events":"gives the stream message boundaries and reconnection, the format chat UIs and vendor APIs use.","cancellation-on-client-disconnect":"stops generation and frees slots when a streaming client goes away, using the ASGI disconnect message.","websockets":"covers the two-way case (interrupts, voice, approvals) once one-way streaming and its limits are understood.","async-database-access-and-connection-pooling":"introduces async drivers, pool sizing and the rule never to hold a connection across a model call.","sqlalchemy-sqlmodel":"models conversations, jobs and chunks on top of the async engine and session dependency.","alembic-migrations":"evolves that schema safely across every environment once models exist.","uploadfile":"accepts documents, images and audio for RAG and multimodal endpoints.","request-size-limits-and-upload-security":"caps and isolates the large, untrusted inputs that uploads and long prompts bring.","backgroundtasks":"handles cheap after-response work and shows where in-process follow-up stops being safe.","job-queues-and-durable-workers":"moves long or must-complete work to a queue with 202 and a status endpoint, building on the database layer.","idempotency-keys":"makes retried POSTs and at-least-once job delivery produce one effect instead of double billing.","redis-caching":"avoids repeated model and embedding calls with a shared cache whose key captures everything that changes the answer.","rate-limiting":"caps request and token spend per caller, typically counted in the same Redis.","concurrency-limits":"caps in-flight model calls per worker so the provider or GPU is not overrun by an async loop.","backpressure":"ties limits, bounded queues, 429/503 with Retry-After and stream flow control into one overload story.","structured-logging":"binds request, tenant, model and token fields into every log line so behaviour can be filtered and costed.","opentelemetry-instrumentation":"adds traces across FastAPI, httpx, SQLAlchemy and Redis plus manual LLM spans, joined to those logs.","health-readiness-endpoints":"separates liveness from readiness so orchestrators restart and route traffic correctly, including during model loading.","uvicorn-gunicorn-workers":"sizes processes by memory and GPUs rather than cores, which determines how per-worker limits add up.","graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining":"drains long streams within the orchestrator's budget using readiness flips and lifespan cleanup.","openapi-docs":"turns the typed routes and models into a published schema for clients, SDKs and LLM tool definitions.","testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio":"tests the app in-process with the LLM replaced by a dependency override, now that the whole surface exists.","integration-tests-with-real-dependencies":"proves SQL, pgvector, Redis TTLs and migrations against real containers, which fakes cannot.","openapi-contract-testing":"closes the loop by checking the schema for breaking changes and fuzzing responses against it."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are delivering a document question-answering API for a customer pilot, and it must survive their\nstaging cluster rather than a laptop. Build a FastAPI service with versioned routers where an\nauthenticated caller (API key for servers, JWT for the browser app) uploads PDFs, the service ingests\nthem through a durable job queue (202 Accepted plus a job-status endpoint), and a chat endpoint streams\nanswers over Server-Sent Events from a real or mock LLM provider called through one shared httpx\nclient created in lifespan. Conversations, jobs and chunks live in Postgres through an async\nSQLAlchemy engine with Alembic migrations, and repeated questions are served from a Redis cache.\nProtect the model with per-tenant request and token rate limits, a per-worker concurrency semaphore\nthat sheds load with 503 and Retry-After, per-phase timeouts, upload size caps and idempotency keys on\nthe ingest endpoint. The stream must stop generating when the client disconnects. Make it operable:\nJSON logs with request and tenant fields, OpenTelemetry traces showing where each second went,\nseparate liveness and readiness probes, a Gunicorn/Uvicorn worker configuration you can justify, and a\ndrain path that lets in-flight streams finish on SIGTERM. Finish with a test suite that a reviewer can\nrun in CI.","deliverables":["A repository with the FastAPI service, a docker-compose file (API, worker, Postgres with pgvector, Redis, an OTel collector) and Alembic migrations that build the schema from empty.","Endpoints for upload, job status, streaming chat over SSE and health/readiness, with Pydantic request and response models and one consistent error envelope, published at `/docs`.","A test suite with in-process tests using dependency overrides for the LLM, integration tests against containerised Postgres and Redis, and an OpenAPI snapshot diff plus a Schemathesis run in CI.","A load-test script and short report showing behaviour at and beyond the concurrency cap, with p50/p95 time to first token and the rate of 429 and 503 responses.","A one-page operations note covering worker count and pool sizing arithmetic, timeout layering, probe settings, drain budget and how to read a trace for a slow request."],"rubric":["A blocking-call check passes: under load, `/health` stays responsive while 50 chat streams run, and no sync SDK or database call appears on the event loop.","Closing the browser mid-answer stops the upstream generation within a second and releases the concurrency slot, visible in logs and the trace.","Replaying an ingest request with the same idempotency key returns the original job id and creates no second job or charge.","Beyond the cap, excess requests receive 503 or 429 with `Retry-After` while memory stays flat and accepted requests keep their latency, rather than every request slowing down.","A rolling restart during active streams drops no in-flight answer that fits within the drain budget, and readiness goes false before the process stops accepting.","Responses never contain fields outside the declared response models, and a deliberately removed field fails the OpenAPI contract check in CI.","Every log line for a request carries the same request id and tenant as its trace, and the trace shows separate spans for retrieval, the LLM call and database work."]}},"topics":[{"id":"asgi-lifecycle","key":"fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"ASGI lifecycle","taxonomy_name":"ASGI lifecycle","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/asyncio","backend-fundamentals/fastapi"],"tldr":"ASGI is the contract between an async server (Uvicorn, Hypercorn) and your application: the app is a\nsingle `async def app(scope, receive, send)` that the server calls once per connection, plus one special\n`lifespan` connection per process for startup and shutdown. Everything FastAPI does at runtime —\nlifespan, middleware, streaming, disconnect detection, per-worker state — is a move in this message\nprotocol, so knowing the sequence explains behaviour that otherwise looks like magic.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"middleware-ordering","key":"fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Middleware ordering","taxonomy_name":"middleware ordering","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/cors","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse"],"tldr":"Middleware in FastAPI is a stack of ASGI apps wrapped around each other: a request walks down the stack\nand the response walks back up. `app.add_middleware()` inserts at the *outside*, so the last one added\nruns first on the way in and last on the way out. Order decides whether a 401 carries CORS headers,\nwhether a trace ID exists when auth logs, and whether timing sees exceptions. Starlette also pins\n`ServerErrorMiddleware` outermost and `ExceptionMiddleware` innermost, which explains several surprises.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-endpoints","key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Async endpoints","taxonomy_name":"async endpoints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits"],"tldr":"FastAPI runs `async def` endpoints on the single event loop and plain `def` endpoints in a worker thread\npool. An `async def` handler that awaits I/O (an LLM API, a database, Redis) lets one process hold\nhundreds of in-flight requests; an `async def` handler that *blocks* (a sync SDK call, a local model\nforward pass) freezes every request in the process. The rule for AI services: await remote models,\nthread or offload local ones, and never mix the two up.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic-request-response-models","key":"fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Pydantic request / response models","taxonomy_name":"Pydantic request / response models","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"A FastAPI endpoint declares its request body and its response as Pydantic models. The framework\nvalidates inbound JSON against the request model (rejecting bad input with a 422 that names the field)\nand validates, filters and documents outbound data against the response model. For AI APIs this is the\ncontract that stops malformed messages, out-of-range sampling parameters and oversized prompts before\nthey cost money, and stops internal fields (system prompts, costs, raw provider payloads) from leaking\nout.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"response-model","key":"fastapi-for-ai/response-model","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"response_model","taxonomy_name":"response_model","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","safety-security/output-validation","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"`response_model` (or the endpoint's return annotation) tells FastAPI the exact shape a route may\nreturn. The framework validates the returned data against it, serialises *through* it so only declared\nfields leave the process, and publishes it in OpenAPI. It exists because what a handler happens to\nreturn is not a contract; a declared output schema is. For AI services it is the cheapest guarantee\nthat internal fields and raw provider payloads never reach a client.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection","key":"fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection (Depends)","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection (Depends)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling"],"tldr":"`Depends` lets an endpoint declare what it needs — an authenticated principal, a DB session, the LLM\nclient, a tenant's config — as typed parameters. FastAPI resolves them per request, caches each within\nthe request, runs their cleanup after the handler, and lets tests swap any of them with\n`dependency_overrides`. It replaces global singletons and copy-pasted auth checks with a tree of small\ncallables, which is what makes an AI service testable without calling the real model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","key":"fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Request-scoped vs application-scoped resources","taxonomy_name":"request-scoped vs application-scoped resources","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives"],"tldr":"Every object in a FastAPI process has a scope. Application-scoped things (model weights, tokenizer,\nprovider client with its connection pool, DB engine, vector index, settings) are built once per worker\nin lifespan and shared by all requests; request-scoped things (DB session, transaction, auth principal,\nrequest ID, budget, trace span) are built by a dependency and die with the request. Wrong in one\ndirection wastes seconds and sockets per request; wrong in the other leaks one user's state into\nanother's.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"lifespan","key":"fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Lifespan (load model / client once)","taxonomy_name":"lifespan (load model / client once)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"`lifespan` is an async context manager passed to `FastAPI(lifespan=...)`: code before `yield` runs\nonce per worker before any request is served, code after `yield` runs once at shutdown. It is where\nyou load model weights, build the provider client and its connection pool, and open the DB engine,\nbecause those are expensive, shareable, and need a guaranteed close, none of which import-time\nglobals or per-request construction give you.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"streamingresponse","key":"fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"StreamingResponse","taxonomy_name":"StreamingResponse","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/ttft"],"tldr":"`StreamingResponse` lets an endpoint hand the server an iterator instead of a finished body, so bytes\nleave the process as soon as each chunk exists. For LLM endpoints that turns the wait the user feels\nfrom \"the whole generation\" into \"time to first token\", and it stops the server holding entire\nresponses in memory. The price: status and headers are frozen at the first byte, so mid-stream errors\nmust be signalled inside the body, and nothing between the generator and the browser may buffer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"server-sent-events","key":"fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Server-Sent Events","taxonomy_name":"Server-Sent Events","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"Server-Sent Events (SSE) is a one-directional, text-based streaming format carried in an ordinary HTTP\nresponse of type `text/event-stream`: events are blocks of `field: value` lines separated by a blank\nline. It exists because a raw chunked body has no message boundaries and WebSockets are a heavier,\nstateful protocol for what is usually a one-way token flow. Browsers reconnect automatically and resend\n`Last-Event-ID`, which is why LLM vendor APIs and most chat UIs stream tokens over SSE.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"websockets","key":"fastapi-for-ai/websockets","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"WebSockets","taxonomy_name":"WebSockets","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","serving-inference/cancellation"],"tldr":"A WebSocket upgrades one HTTP connection into a persistent, full-duplex channel that carries framed\ntext or binary messages in both directions. Reach for it when the client must talk *while* the server\nis streaming: interrupting a generation, two-way voice audio, agent sessions with mid-run approvals.\nThe price is state: the connection is pinned to one process, authentication, reconnection and resume\nare hand-rolled, and every load balancer in the path must understand long-lived upgrades. For one-way\ntoken streaming, SSE is simpler.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"backgroundtasks","key":"fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"BackgroundTasks","taxonomy_name":"BackgroundTasks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers"],"tldr":"`BackgroundTasks` runs functions *after* the response has been sent, in the same process and event\nloop, with no persistence, no retry and no status. It exists so a handler can answer quickly while\ndoing cheap follow-up work (write a trace, bump a usage counter, fire a webhook) without standing up a\nqueue. Use it for work you can afford to lose when the process dies; anything that must happen, or\nthat takes more than a few seconds, belongs in a durable job queue.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"job-queues-and-durable-workers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Job queues & durable workers (Celery / ARQ + job-status endpoint)","taxonomy_name":"job queues & durable workers (Celery / ARQ + job-status endpoint)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/redis","fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"A job queue moves slow or must-complete work (batch summarisation, embedding a corpus, a long agent\nrun) out of the request into worker processes, after recording the job's existence somewhere durable,\nso the API can answer `202 Accepted` with a job id and the client learns the outcome from a job-status\nendpoint. Celery is the mature, sync-first, multi-broker option; ARQ is a small asyncio-native queue on\nRedis. Both deliver at-least-once, so every job body must be idempotent.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Async database access & connection pooling","taxonomy_name":"async database access & connection pooling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","python-for-ai/asyncio"],"tldr":"An `async def` endpoint must never block the event loop, so database calls either go through an\nasync driver (asyncpg, psycopg 3, aiosqlite) behind SQLAlchemy's `AsyncEngine`, or stay in `def`\nendpoints that run on the thread pool. Opening a Postgres connection costs a server-side process plus\nTLS and authentication, and the server caps connections (100 by default), so each process keeps a\nbounded pool and `processes × (pool_size + max_overflow)` must fit. The AI-specific rule: never hold a\npooled connection across a model call.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","key":"fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"SQLAlchemy / SQLModel","taxonomy_name":"SQLAlchemy / SQLModel","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","python-for-ai/pydantic","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/database-design"],"tldr":"SQLAlchemy is Python's standard SQL toolkit: Core builds and executes SQL expressions safely, the ORM\nmaps classes to tables and tracks changes in a unit of work. SQLModel wraps SQLAlchemy's declarative\nmodels in Pydantic so one class can be both a table and an API schema, which is why FastAPI projects\nreach for it. Use SQLModel when the storage and API shapes agree; drop to plain SQLAlchemy 2.0 when\nyou need its full feature set or when the two shapes diverge, which they eventually do.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"alembic-migrations","key":"fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Alembic migrations","taxonomy_name":"Alembic migrations","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","llmops-platform/rollback","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Alembic is SQLAlchemy's migration tool: each schema change is a versioned Python script with an\n`upgrade()` and `downgrade()`, chained by revision IDs, and the database records which revision it is\nat. That turns \"what schema does production have?\" into a fact you can query and replay. Autogenerate\ndrafts scripts by diffing your models against the live database, but it is a draft: renames, data\nmoves and lock-heavy operations still need a human, and safe deploys need expand-contract.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeouts","key":"fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Timeouts","taxonomy_name":"timeouts","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/retry-strategies","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation"],"tldr":"FastAPI and uvicorn impose no per-request time limit, so a handler waiting on a slow LLM call will wait\nas long as the slowest dependency lets it, holding a connection, memory and a concurrency slot the whole\ntime. Timeouts turn \"hangs forever\" into \"fails fast with a known error\". Set them per phase (connect,\nread, total) on every outbound call, give the request one overall deadline, and order the layers so the\ninnermost one fires first.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotency-keys","key":"fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Idempotency keys","taxonomy_name":"idempotency keys","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","serving-inference/idempotency","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"Networks fail after the server has done the work but before the client hears back, so clients retry,\nand a retried POST can bill twice, send two emails or run an expensive generation twice. An idempotency\nkey is a client-chosen unique ID sent with the request. The server records the first outcome under\nthat key and replays it for any retry, which turns at-least-once delivery into an effectively-once\neffect.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cors","key":"fastapi-for-ai/cors","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"CORS","taxonomy_name":"CORS","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust"],"tldr":"Browsers block a page from reading responses from another origin unless that server opts in. CORS is\nthe set of response headers that opt in. The browser enforces it, not your server, so CORS controls\nwhich websites' JavaScript may call your API with a user's browser. It is not authentication. curl,\nscripts and other servers ignore it completely, so your API still needs real auth.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-keys-oauth2-jwt","key":"fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"API keys / OAuth2 / JWT","taxonomy_name":"API keys / OAuth2 / JWT","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":22,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/cors","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"An AI API has to know who is calling, both to bill tokens and to scope data. API keys are long-lived\nshared secrets, fine for server-to-server calls. OAuth2 is a framework for *delegating* access, so a\nuser or service gets a token from an identity provider instead of handing you a password. A JWT is a\nsigned, self-contained token format that lets your API verify that token without a database lookup.\nThey solve different problems and are often used together.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rate-limiting","key":"fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Rate limiting (slowapi)","taxonomy_name":"rate limiting (slowapi)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","serving-inference/rate-limiting","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas"],"tldr":"An LLM endpoint turns every request into real spend and scarce GPU or provider quota, so one runaway\nscript can drain your budget or starve every other tenant. Rate limiting caps how fast each caller may\nconsume. slowapi is a FastAPI/Starlette port of Flask-Limiter: decorator-based limits like\n`\"10/minute\"` keyed per client, counted in memory or Redis, returning 429. It counts *requests*. AI\nservices usually also need a *token* budget, which you build next to it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"request-size-limits-and-upload-security","key":"fastapi-for-ai/request-size-limits-and-upload-security","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Request-size limits & upload security","taxonomy_name":"request-size limits & upload security","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/uploadfile","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/rag-poisoning","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing"],"tldr":"An AI backend accepts the largest, least trusted inputs in your stack: long prompts, PDFs, images,\naudio, zip archives of documents for RAG. Uvicorn and FastAPI put no cap on JSON request body size,\nso one oversized or malicious upload can exhaust memory or disk, or a parser can be exploited. Cap\nsize at every layer, count bytes while streaming, never trust the client's filename or content type,\nand parse untrusted files in an isolated, resource-limited worker.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"uploadfile","key":"fastapi-for-ai/uploadfile","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"UploadFile","taxonomy_name":"UploadFile","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/request-size-limits-and-upload-security","fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","multimodal/speech-to-text","rag-retrieval/document-parsing"],"tldr":"`UploadFile` is FastAPI's parameter type for files sent as `multipart/form-data`: PDFs for RAG, images\nfor vision models, audio for transcription. It wraps a spooled temporary file, held in memory while\nsmall and moved to disk when large, so a file doesn't have to fit in RAM. It also exposes filename,\ncontent type and async read/seek methods. Prefer it to `bytes = File()`, but it does no size or\nsafety checks for you.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"httpexception-handling","key":"fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"HTTPException handling","taxonomy_name":"HTTPException handling","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","system-design/graceful-degradation"],"tldr":"`raise HTTPException(status_code, detail)` aborts a request from anywhere in the call stack (handler\nor dependency) and becomes an HTTP error response. Handling errors well means deciding how failures\nreach the client. Map domain and upstream-LLM failures to meaningful status codes, return one\nconsistent error shape, never leak internals, and remember that once a stream has started the status\ncode can no longer change.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"routers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/routers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Routers (APIRouter)","taxonomy_name":"routers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design"],"tldr":"An `APIRouter` is a group of path operations that you declare in its own module and attach to the app\nwith `include_router(prefix=..., tags=..., dependencies=...)`. It exists so a growing AI service (chat,\nembeddings, admin, eval endpoints) can be split by feature, versioned by prefix, and protected by\ngroup-level dependencies, while still producing one app, one route table and one OpenAPI document.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"openapi-docs","key":"fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenAPI docs","taxonomy_name":"OpenAPI docs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","fastapi-for-ai/routers","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"FastAPI derives an OpenAPI document from your route signatures and Pydantic models, serves it at\n`/openapi.json`, and renders it as interactive docs at `/docs` (Swagger UI) and `/redoc`. Because the\nschema comes from the same type hints that validate requests, it cannot drift from the code the way a\nhand-written spec does, which makes it a reliable source for client SDKs, contract tests and even LLM\ntool definitions.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"openapi-contract-testing","key":"fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenAPI contract testing","taxonomy_name":"OpenAPI contract testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","python-for-ai/contract-testing","python-for-ai/property-based-testing","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"OpenAPI contract testing treats your generated schema as a promise and checks it two ways: a snapshot\ndiff in CI that fails on breaking changes (a removed field, a new required input, a narrowed enum),\nand schema-driven tests (for example Schemathesis) that generate requests from the schema and verify\nevery response matches what the schema declares. Unit tests check that code does what you meant; contract\ntests check that it still does what clients were told.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Uvicorn / Gunicorn workers","taxonomy_name":"uvicorn / gunicorn workers","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"Uvicorn is the ASGI server that runs FastAPI's event loop; Gunicorn is a pre-fork process manager that\ncan run several Uvicorn workers and supervise them. One Python process runs Python code on roughly one\ncore at a time, so workers (processes) are how you use more cores; but each worker loads its own copy of\nany in-process model, so for AI services the right worker count is usually set by memory and GPUs, not CPU.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","key":"fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Graceful shutdown & worker draining","taxonomy_name":"graceful shutdown & worker draining","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes"],"tldr":"Graceful shutdown means that when a worker is told to stop (SIGTERM on every deploy, scale-down or node\ndrain), it stops receiving new traffic, lets in-flight requests finish, closes its resources, then exits.\nAI services make this hard because a single request can stream tokens for minutes while orchestrators\ngive a default 30 seconds, so draining needs an explicit budget, a readiness flip, and a plan for work\nthat cannot finish in time.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"health-readiness-endpoints","key":"fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Health / readiness endpoints","taxonomy_name":"health / readiness endpoints","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","deployment/health-checks","system-design/graceful-degradation"],"tldr":"A liveness endpoint answers \"is this process stuck and worth restarting?\"; a readiness endpoint answers\n\"should this instance receive traffic right now?\"; a startup probe gives slow starters (model loading)\ntime before liveness counts. They are separate because the right reaction differs: restarting fixes a\ndeadlock but makes a database outage worse, so dependency checks belong in readiness, never liveness.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-logging","key":"fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Structured logging","taxonomy_name":"structured logging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","observability/logging","observability/token-cost-tracking","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces"],"tldr":"Structured logging emits each log event as a machine-parseable record (usually one JSON object per line)\nwith named fields such as `request_id`, `tenant`, `model`, `prompt_tokens` and `latency_ms`, instead of a\nsentence. In a FastAPI service, a middleware binds request context into `contextvars` so every log line\nfrom that request carries it automatically, which turns logs into something you can filter, join to\ntraces and aggregate into cost and latency per tenant.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry-instrumentation","key":"fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","observability/opentelemetry","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/spans","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is a vendor-neutral API, SDK and wire protocol for traces, metrics and logs. In\nFastAPI you initialise a tracer provider with an OTLP exporter, auto-instrument the app and its clients\n(FastAPI, httpx, SQLAlchemy, Redis), and add manual spans around LLM calls and retrieval steps. The\npayoff is a single trace per request showing where its seconds went, propagated across services, sent to\nwhichever backend you choose without re-instrumenting.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis-caching","key":"fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Redis caching","taxonomy_name":"Redis caching","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/redis","serving-inference/caching","prompting/prompt-caching","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers"],"tldr":"An LLM or embedding call costs real money and seconds of latency, and many requests repeat exactly.\nRedis caching puts a shared, in-memory key-value store in front of those calls: hash the full,\nnormalised request into a key, return the stored result on a hit, and call the model and store with\na TTL on a miss. It is shared across workers, which an in-process dict is not, and it is safe only when\nthe key captures everything that changes the answer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"shared-httpx-asyncclient","key":"fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Shared httpx AsyncClient","taxonomy_name":"shared httpx AsyncClient","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/openai-sdk"],"tldr":"An AI service is mostly an HTTP client: it calls model providers, vector stores and internal tools.\nCreating an `httpx.AsyncClient` per request throws away the connection pool, so every call pays DNS,\nTCP and TLS setup and leaves sockets churning. Create one client per worker in the lifespan, share\nit through `app.state` or a dependency, configure its limits and timeouts deliberately, and close it\non shutdown.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","key":"fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"TestClient + pytest-asyncio","taxonomy_name":"TestClient + pytest-asyncio","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient"],"tldr":"FastAPI apps are ASGI callables, so tests can call them in-process without a server. `TestClient`\ndoes it from ordinary synchronous tests by running the app on its own event loop in a helper thread;\n`httpx.AsyncClient` with `ASGITransport`, under pytest-asyncio, does it from `async def` tests on the\ntest's own loop. Pick by whether the test itself must await things, and replace the LLM with a\ndependency override so tests are fast, free and deterministic.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","key":"fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Integration tests with real dependencies","taxonomy_name":"integration tests with real dependencies","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"Fakes prove your logic; they cannot prove that your SQL runs on Postgres, that pgvector accepts your\nvector dimension, that a Redis TTL behaves, or that a migration applies. Integration tests run the\napp against real instances of those dependencies, started disposably in containers, with each test\nisolated by transaction rollback or cleanup. The paid, non-deterministic dependency, the LLM, is the\nexception: test it against recorded responses or in a small, separate contract suite.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"concurrency-limits","key":"fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Concurrency limits (semaphores)","taxonomy_name":"concurrency limits (semaphores)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","reliability/bulkheads","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"An async FastAPI worker will happily start thousands of model calls at once; the provider, the GPU or\nyour memory will not handle them. A semaphore caps how many run concurrently: each call takes a slot\nbefore starting and returns it when done, and everyone else waits or is turned away. Size the cap\nfrom the downstream's real limit divided across workers, bound the wait, and fail fast with 503\nwhen the wait is too long.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure","key":"fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Backpressure","taxonomy_name":"backpressure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","serving-inference/backpressure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure"],"tldr":"Backpressure is a slow consumer's signal travelling back to a fast producer, so the producer slows\ndown instead of piling work into a buffer. Without it, overload turns into unbounded queues: latency\ngrows until every request times out and memory grows until the process dies. In a FastAPI AI service\nit takes three forms: bounded internal queues, rejecting at the edge with 429/503 and `Retry-After`,\nand the flow control that pauses a streaming generator when the client reads slowly.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cancellation-on-client-disconnect","key":"fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Cancellation on client disconnect","taxonomy_name":"cancellation on client disconnect","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","serving-inference/cancellation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers"],"tldr":"Users close tabs, press stop and retry, but a server that does not notice keeps generating tokens,\nholding a concurrency slot and GPU memory, and possibly running agent tool calls for nobody. ASGI\nreports a vanished client as an `http.disconnect` message, not an exception, so your code must detect\nit and cancel the in-flight work, let cancellation propagate to the upstream call, run cleanup in\n`finally`, and protect the few side effects that must finish.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"node-ts-for-ai","tier":1,"order":15,"title":"Node.js / TypeScript for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building the backend of an AI product in Node.js and TypeScript to production\nstandard. Most of that backend is glue around slow, billed, non-deterministic model calls, and the\nmodule is organised around what that glue needs. It starts with types and trust: strict TypeScript\nmakes the compiler reject null and variant mistakes, and runtime schema validation with Zod checks\nthe data the compiler cannot see, including model output and tool arguments. Next comes the runtime.\nThe event loop explains why one process can hold thousands of open model streams and why one\nsynchronous step stalls all of them. Promise error propagation, AbortController cancellation, streams\nwith backpressure and worker threads are the tools for working with that single thread. The third\ngroup is the code that talks to the outside world. Dependency injection keeps clients swappable. The\nOpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, the Vercel AI SDK and resilient API clients with retries and circuit\nbreakers handle model calls. Postgres transactions, Redis and BullMQ hold state and durable jobs, and\nthe MCP TypeScript SDK exposes tools to any agent host. The last group covers running the service:\nOpenTelemetry tracing, layered testing that keeps the real model out of the merge gate, graceful\nshutdown and a Docker and CI pipeline. After the module you can ship a typed, observable,\ncancellable, streaming AI service that survives deploys, provider outages and restarts without lost\nwork or runaway token spend. It matters most for Engineers who write and operate these services day to\nday, and for FDEs who build integrations at customer sites, often on a customer's Node stack.","why":{"engineer":"You write the handlers, workers and tool servers, so a floating promise that crashes the process, a stream that keeps billing after the client left, a pool transaction split across connections or a 300 ms tokenizer call on the main thread is a bug in your code.","architect":"You decide which layer owns retries, where cancellation and deadlines propagate, what runs in a request versus a BullMQ job or a worker thread, what lives in Redis versus Postgres, and whether to standardise on the Vercel AI SDK or the native provider SDKs.","fde":"Customer integrations are often a TypeScript service or MCP server built in days against their APIs, and you answer for it with typed contracts, a trace of each request, safe secret handling and deploys that do not cut off users mid-answer."},"mental_model":"Picture one Node process as a single cashier serving thousands of customers who are each waiting on\na slow kitchen, the model provider. Waiting costs almost nothing, so the cashier can juggle thousands\nof open orders. Cooking at the counter, meaning any synchronous CPU work, makes everyone wait, so heavy\nwork goes to worker threads or to a queue. Every request carries three things down its call tree: a\ntype that was parsed at the boundary rather than assumed, an AbortSignal that can stop all of its work\nat once, and a trace context that ties its spans together across awaits and services. Every call that\nleaves the process goes through an injected client with a timeout, a classified retry policy and a\nbreaker. Every piece of state that must outlive the request goes to Postgres, Redis or a durable job.\nShutdown runs the same picture in reverse: stop taking orders, finish or hand off the open ones, flush,\nthen exit.","order":["typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","runtime-schema-validation","event-loop-and-microtask-queue","promises-and-async-error-propagation","abortcontroller-and-cancellation","streams-and-backpressure","worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","dependency-injection","openai-anthropic-node-sdks","api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","vercel-ai-sdk","postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","redis","bullmq-and-durable-jobs","mcp-typescript-sdk","opentelemetry-instrumentation","vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","graceful-shutdown","docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"order_reasons":{"typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types":"the compiler settings and union types every later lesson's code relies on.","runtime-schema-validation":"fills the gap types leave at runtime and gives one Zod schema for types, parsing and tool definitions.","event-loop-and-microtask-queue":"the execution model that explains concurrency, ordering and stalls in everything that follows.","promises-and-async-error-propagation":"how failures travel through awaits and combinators, needed before cancellation and retries.","abortcontroller-and-cancellation":"makes abandoned and timed-out work actually stop, building on promise semantics.","streams-and-backpressure":"token streaming to clients with bounded memory, wired to the request's abort signal.","worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks":"the answer to the event-loop stalls lesson 3 identified for sustained CPU work.","dependency-injection":"the explicit wiring pattern that makes provider clients, stores and queues swappable and testable.","openai-anthropic-node-sdks":"the native model clients, their retry, timeout, streaming and tool-call mechanics, injected behind an interface.","api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers":"layers deadlines, classified retries and breakers over the SDKs' own retries without multiplying attempts.","vercel-ai-sdk":"the provider-agnostic alternative, best judged once the native SDKs and their trade-offs are familiar.","postgresql-transactions-and-migrations":"durable run and budget state, including the pool-per-statement trap and safe schema changes.","redis":"shared fast state across replicas for caches, rate limits and locks, and the store BullMQ needs.","bullmq-and-durable-jobs":"moves long agent runs and batch work off the request, built on Redis and idempotent Postgres writes.","mcp-typescript-sdk":"exposes the service's tools over MCP using the Zod schemas, transports and error handling covered earlier.","opentelemetry-instrumentation":"traces one request across HTTP, model calls, Postgres, Redis and queue hops, so it comes after those pieces exist.","vitest-jest-and-integration-testing":"layered tests with injected fakes, containerised dependencies and scored evals over the full stack.","graceful-shutdown":"the SIGTERM drain ordering across servers, workers, pools and exporters introduced above.","docker-ci-cd-deployment":"packages and ships the service with a gated pipeline, depending on shutdown and tests being in place."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a production-grade \"document Q&A\" service in TypeScript over a week. Users upload PDFs and ask\nquestions in a chat UI. Answers stream token by token and cite pages. The service runs as an API\nprocess and a separate worker process. Uploads enqueue a BullMQ ingestion job. The worker parses and\ntokenizes each document in a worker-thread pool, embeds chunks through a provider client with\ntimeouts, classified retries and a circuit breaker, and writes chunks, run records and a per-tenant\ntoken budget to Postgres in transactions, with schema managed by migrations. The chat endpoint\nvalidates requests with Zod and streams through either the Vercel AI SDK or a native SDK behind an\ninjected interface, with a fallback provider when the breaker is open. A single AbortSignal per request\nstops generation when the client disconnects. Redis holds a rate limiter and an answer cache. The same\nretrieval and citation tools are also exposed as an MCP server over stdio and Streamable HTTP. Add\nOpenTelemetry traces across API, queue, worker and database, a layered test suite, graceful shutdown\nfor both processes and a multi-stage Docker image with a CI pipeline.","deliverables":["A repository with the API, worker and MCP server under `strict` plus `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, a single composition root per process, and Zod schemas used for request bodies, environment config, model tool definitions and MCP tool inputs.","Postgres migrations for documents, chunks, runs and budgets, a transactional budget-charge path that pins one client, and a BullMQ ingestion queue with idempotent handlers, retries with backoff and a dead-letter inspection script.","A test suite with unit tests against a fake model client, integration tests running real Postgres and Redis in containers plus intercepted provider HTTP, and a separate scored eval of 20+ questions with a pass threshold, run outside the merge gate.","A multi-stage Dockerfile running Node as PID 1 in exec form, a CI workflow gating on typecheck, lint (`no-floating-promises`, `no-explicit-any`) and tests, and a short runbook covering the shutdown sequence, secrets injection and dashboards.","An OpenTelemetry setup that exports one trace per question showing the HTTP span, retrieval, model call with token usage, Redis and Postgres spans, plus a linked trace for each ingestion job across the queue hop."],"rubric":["Closing the browser tab mid-answer ends the upstream model stream within a second, shown by the span ending with an abort status and no further token usage recorded for that request.","Ingesting a 200-page PDF while 50 chats stream keeps p99 event-loop lag under 50 ms, measured with `monitorEventLoopDelay` and compared with a run that parses on the main thread.","With the primary provider forced to return 529, requests fail over to the fallback after the breaker opens, total attempts per request stay within the configured cap, and breaker state transitions appear in metrics.","Sending SIGTERM to the API during 20 active streams and to the worker mid-job finishes or cleanly ends every stream, records zero 5xx at the load balancer, and leaves the job either completed or retried exactly once with no duplicate chunks.","Two concurrent questions for a tenant with budget for only one result in exactly one being charged and the other rejected with a typed budget error, and the unit and integration suites run with no network access or provider keys.","An MCP host such as an IDE lists and calls the retrieval tool over stdio, returns `isError` results for bad arguments rather than protocol errors, and nothing other than JSON-RPC is written to stdout."]}},"topics":[{"id":"typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","key":"node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"TypeScript strict mode & advanced types","taxonomy_name":"TypeScript strict mode & advanced types","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","agents/typed-tool-contracts","backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript"],"tldr":"TypeScript types are erased before the code runs, so they only protect you if the compiler is allowed\nto be strict. `strict` turns on null checks, bans implicit `any` and makes function types sound; the\nadvanced features (discriminated unions, constrained generics, conditional/mapped/template-literal\ntypes, `satisfies`, `unknown`) let you encode the shapes AI code actually handles — stream events, tool\ncontracts, provider responses — so mismatches fail at compile time instead of as `undefined` crashes\nin the middle of a streamed answer.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"event-loop-and-microtask-queue","key":"node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Event loop & microtask queue","taxonomy_name":"event loop & microtask queue","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"Node runs your JavaScript on one thread and multiplexes thousands of in-flight I/O operations through\nthe event loop: libuv waits on sockets and files, and the loop runs callbacks in phases. Promise\ncallbacks and `process.nextTick` go on separate microtask queues that are drained completely after\nevery callback, before the loop advances. That is why an AI gateway can hold 5,000 open model streams\ncheaply, and also why one 200 ms synchronous computation stalls every one of them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"promises-and-async-error-propagation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Promises & async error propagation","taxonomy_name":"promises & async error propagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"A promise is a one-shot result that is either fulfilled or rejected, and `await` turns a rejection back\ninto a thrown exception at the awaiting site, so errors travel up the async call chain just like\nsynchronous ones. The failure modes are promises nobody awaits (rejections vanish or crash the process),\ncombinators that hide which call failed, and error objects that lose their cause. Getting this right is\nwhat makes a multi-provider LLM call fail loudly, precisely and recoverably.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"streams-and-backpressure","key":"node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Streams & backpressure","taxonomy_name":"streams & backpressure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"A stream processes data in chunks as it arrives instead of buffering everything, which is how a\ntoken-by-token model response reaches a browser with sub-second first-token latency. Backpressure is\nthe flow-control rule that makes this safe: when the consumer is slower than the producer, the\nproducer is told to pause, so memory stays bounded. Node has two stream APIs (classic `node:stream`\nand WHATWG Web Streams, used by `fetch`), and `pipeline`/`pipeTo` are what wire them together with\nerror propagation and backpressure intact.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","key":"node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Worker threads for CPU-bound tasks","taxonomy_name":"worker threads for CPU-bound tasks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits"],"tldr":"The event loop runs all your JavaScript on one thread, so a CPU-heavy step freezes every concurrent\nrequest. `worker_threads` run JavaScript in separate V8 isolates on other OS threads, communicating by\nmessage passing (structured clone, or zero-copy transfer of `ArrayBuffer`s and shared memory via\n`SharedArrayBuffer`). Use a pool of them for sustained computation such as tokenizing, parsing large\ndocuments or vector math; use them sparingly, because each worker is a separate heap with its own\nstartup cost, and messages are copied unless transferred.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"abortcontroller-and-cancellation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"AbortController & cancellation","taxonomy_name":"AbortController & cancellation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation"],"tldr":"Promises cannot be cancelled, so JavaScript cancels cooperatively: an `AbortController` owns an\n`AbortSignal`, the signal is passed down to every operation that should stop together, and calling\n`abort()` makes `fetch`, timers, streams and your own code reject with an `AbortError`. In AI services\nthis is how you stop paying for generation after the user closed the tab (to the extent the provider\nstops on disconnect), and what\nturns a 30-second timeout into a real stop rather than a promise you merely stopped waiting for.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"runtime-schema-validation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Runtime schema validation (Zod)","taxonomy_name":"runtime schema validation (Zod)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","python-for-ai/pydantic","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","safety-security/output-validation","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models"],"tldr":"TypeScript types vanish at compile time, so nothing checks the JSON that arrives from an HTTP client, a\nwebhook, an environment variable or a language model. Runtime schema validation defines the shape once\nas a value (a Zod schema), derives the static type from it, and parses untrusted input into a typed\nvalue or a structured list of errors. The same schema becomes the JSON Schema sent to the model for\ntool calling, so the contract the model follows is the contract you enforce.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection","key":"node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation"],"tldr":"Dependency injection means a module receives its collaborators (LLM client, database pool, cache, clock,\nconfig) from the caller instead of importing or constructing them itself. AI services are mostly glue\naround expensive, non-deterministic, credentialed external calls; code that hard-wires those calls cannot\nbe tested without the network, cannot vary per tenant, and cannot swap providers without a rewrite. In\nTypeScript the pattern needs no framework: a typed `Deps` object and one composition root cover most\nservices.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","key":"node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"PostgreSQL transactions & migrations","taxonomy_name":"PostgreSQL transactions & migrations","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/sql","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"tldr":"An AI backend's Postgres rows (conversations, agent runs, tool results, token budgets, embeddings)\nchange in groups, and the schema changes every sprint. Transactions make a group of writes atomic and\nlet concurrent agents contend safely; in Node the trap is that a pool hands out a different connection\nper query, so a transaction must pin one client. Migrations version the schema as ordered, recorded,\nforward-only scripts so every environment reaches the same state and a deploy never locks a live table\nfor minutes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis","key":"node-ts-for-ai/redis","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Redis","taxonomy_name":"Redis","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/redis","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","serving-inference/rate-limiting","agents/agent-memory","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown"],"tldr":"Redis is an in-memory data-structure server that executes commands one at a time, so each command is\natomic and sub-millisecond. In a Node AI service it is the shared, fast, expiring state a stateless\nreplica set needs: completion cache, rate limiter, provider-concurrency semaphore, short-lived session\nstate, distributed locks, pub/sub for streaming across instances, and the store behind BullMQ. The\nfundamentals live in the backend module; this lesson covers Node usage and what goes wrong.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"bullmq-and-durable-jobs","key":"node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"BullMQ & durable jobs","taxonomy_name":"BullMQ & durable jobs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/redis","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns"],"tldr":"BullMQ is a Redis-backed job queue for Node: producers add jobs, workers process them with concurrency,\nretries with backoff, rate limits, delays, priorities and parent/child flows, and job state survives\nprocess restarts because it lives in Redis, not in a promise. An HTTP request is the wrong unit of work\nfor a minutes-long agent run or a 10,000-document embedding batch. Delivery is at-least-once, so\nhandlers must be idempotent, and the queue is only as durable as the Redis behind it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","key":"node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"API clients with retries & circuit breakers","taxonomy_name":"API clients with retries & circuit breakers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","serving-inference/rate-limiting","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"LLM and tool APIs fail transiently (429, 5xx, resets) far more than internal services, so every\noutbound call needs a per-attempt timeout, a retry policy that retries only retryable errors with\nexponential backoff and jitter, and a total deadline. A circuit breaker sits above that: when a\ndependency is down it stops sending traffic for a cooling period, so your service fails fast instead of\npiling up waiting requests and hammering a recovering provider. Vendor SDKs already retry; you add\ntimeouts, idempotency, breakers and bulkheads.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry-instrumentation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["observability/opentelemetry","observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/token-cost-tracking","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","observability/langfuse"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the vendor-neutral standard for traces, metrics and logs. In a Node AI service\nyou install the SDK once at process start, let auto-instrumentation wrap HTTP, `fetch`, Postgres, Redis\nand your web framework, and add your own spans around agent steps carrying tenant, run id and prompt\nversion. Context follows `await` through `AsyncLocalStorage` and crosses services in the W3C\n`traceparent` header, so one request's path from API to queue to LLM to database becomes a single\nqueryable tree.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-shutdown","key":"node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Graceful shutdown","taxonomy_name":"graceful shutdown","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","deployment/health-checks","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"Processes are killed constantly: deploys, autoscaling, node drains, out-of-memory kills. Graceful\nshutdown is the sequence a Node service runs on SIGTERM so nothing in flight is lost: stop advertising\nreadiness, stop accepting new requests and jobs, let in-flight work finish within a deadline shorter\nthan the orchestrator's grace period, close queues and pools, flush telemetry, then exit. Without it, every deploy\ntruncates streamed LLM responses, re-runs queue jobs and leaks half-done writes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","key":"node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Vitest / Jest & integration testing","taxonomy_name":"Vitest / Jest & integration testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"A model call is slow, billed and non-deterministic, so it cannot sit inside a test that must pass on\nevery commit. The working split is three layers: unit tests with an injected fake model, integration\ntests that run the real SDK, database and queue against intercepted or containerised dependencies, and\nevals that call the real model and are *scored*, not asserted. Vitest and Jest are the runners that\nmake the first two layers fast; the design that makes them possible is injecting the model client.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"docker-ci-cd-deployment","key":"node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Docker / CI/CD deployment","taxonomy_name":"Docker / CI/CD deployment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","deployment/health-checks","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments"],"tldr":"The general Docker and CI/CD lessons live in backend-fundamentals; this one covers what a Node/TypeScript\nAI service needs from them. Build the image in stages so the runtime layer holds compiled JavaScript\nand production dependencies only, run Node as PID 1 in exec form so `SIGTERM` can drain in-flight\nmodel streams, inject provider keys at runtime rather than baking them in, and gate the pipeline on\ntypecheck, deterministic tests and a commit-tagged build, with real-model evals beside the gate, not\ninside it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vercel-ai-sdk","key":"node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Vercel AI SDK","taxonomy_name":"Vercel AI SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","serving-inference/streaming","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/frameworks"],"tldr":"The Vercel AI SDK (`ai` on npm) is a provider-agnostic TypeScript layer with two halves: a core API\n(`generateText`, `streamText`, `generateObject`, `tool`, `embed`) that maps one call shape onto OpenAI,\nAnthropic, Google and others through small provider packages, and a UI half (`useChat`) that streams\ntext, tool calls and structured data to the browser over a defined protocol. Swapping a model becomes\none line and browser streaming stops being a hand-rolled parser; the price is that provider-specific\nfeatures sit behind an escape hatch.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"openai-anthropic-node-sdks","key":"node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"OpenAI / Anthropic Node SDKs","taxonomy_name":"OpenAI / Anthropic Node SDKs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-caching"],"tldr":"`openai` and `@anthropic-ai/sdk` are typed, generated clients over each provider's REST API with the\nsame bones: a client that reads the key from the environment, resource methods that return typed\nobjects, automatic retries with backoff on transient failures, millisecond timeouts, `AbortSignal`\nsupport, streaming as async iterables, and typed error classes. They differ where the APIs differ:\nmessage and tool-call shapes, how tool results are sent back, explicit versus automatic prompt\ncaching. Learn the shared mechanics once, then the two wire shapes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-typescript-sdk","key":"node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"MCP TypeScript SDK","taxonomy_name":"MCP TypeScript SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` is the reference TypeScript implementation of the Model Context Protocol:\na server side that turns Zod-typed handlers into `tools/list`, `tools/call`, resource and prompt\nendpoints over JSON-RPC, and a client side that connects to any server, discovers its capabilities\nand calls them. Transports are pluggable: stdio for local subprocesses, Streamable HTTP for remote\nservers. The protocol lesson in the agents module explains *why* MCP exists; this one is about what\nthe SDK does for you and where it bites.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"system-design","tier":2,"order":16,"title":"System design","intro":{"overview":"This module covers designing the whole system around a model call, from the first requirement to the\ndeployment boundary. It starts with the architect's working method. AI solution architecture says what\nbelongs to the model and what belongs to deterministic code. NFRs turn adjectives like \"fast\" and\n\"accurate\" into measurable scenarios. Trade-off analysis, ADRs and C4 diagrams then let you choose\nbetween options, record the choice and show the result. Reference architectures and build versus buy\ngive you starting blueprints and a per-layer sourcing decision. The model layer comes next: selecting a\nmodel on your own evals, SLMs against LLMs, open-weight against proprietary, self-host against API, and\nhow to govern a small portfolio. From there you route and cascade requests, put an AI gateway in front of\nevery call, and plan fallbacks, degradation and caching. The application shapes follow: RAG, memory,\nagentic and multi-agent systems, and orchestration. They rest on distributed-systems foundations:\npartial failure, sync against async execution, consistency, events, outbox and inbox, sagas, and the\nlimits of exactly-once. The module closes with DDD, modular monolith against microservices, and\nmulti-tenancy with real tenant isolation. Afterwards you can take a vague AI product brief and produce\na defensible architecture. That means measurable NFRs, a costed model plan, a failure-aware runtime\ndesign, tenant boundaries, and ADRs and diagrams that let other people review and challenge it. It\nmatters most for Architects. Senior Engineers who own a service end to end and FDEs who design\ndeployments for customers need it too.","why":{"engineer":"You implement the seams this module defines: the gateway client, the outbox relay, idempotent saga steps, tenant-scoped cache keys and retrieval filters, the async job API with streaming, and the router's quality check. If any of them is wrong, a duplicate charge or a cross-tenant leak follows.","architect":"You own the decisions that are expensive to reverse: model tiers and sourcing, gateway and orchestration runtime, monolith or service split, pool, bridge or silo tenancy, and consistency guarantees per store. You have to defend each one with NFR scenarios, a trade-off matrix, an ADR and a C4 view that reviewers can check.","fde":"Customers arrive with residency rules, an existing identity provider, a budget and a hard deadline. You have to fit a reference architecture to those constraints, explain self-host versus API and build versus buy in their terms, and leave behind diagrams and ADRs their own team can maintain after you leave."},"mental_model":"Picture the system as concentric layers around a rented, probabilistic, stateless model, each layer\nadding a guarantee the model cannot give. The innermost layer is the model plan: which models, chosen\non your data, sourced by deliberate build, buy, host and licence decisions, and governed as a\nportfolio. Around it sits the control plane: a gateway, routing and cascades, fallbacks, a degradation\nladder and caches, so quality, cost and availability are managed in one place. Next come the\napplication shapes, which feed the model context and let it act: RAG, memory, workflows and agents.\nUnder all of them is a distributed system, so every boundary needs timeouts, idempotency, an explicit\nconsistency choice, and outbox, inbox or saga handling where state crosses services. The outer layer is\nstructure and ownership: bounded contexts, deployment units and tenant isolation. Wrapping everything is\nthe decision discipline. Measurable NFRs drive trade-off analysis, the result is recorded in ADRs, and C4\ndiagrams show it. A design nobody can review or reverse is not finished.","order":["ai-solution-architecture","nfrs","trade-off-analysis","adrs","c4-diagrams","reference-architectures","build-vs-buy","model-selection","slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","open-weight-vs-proprietary","self-host-vs-api","model-portfolio","multi-model-architecture","model-routing-cascading","ai-gateway","model-fallback-strategies","graceful-degradation","caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","rag-architecture","memory-architecture","distributed-systems","synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","data-consistency","event-driven-architecture","exactly-once-processing-limitations","outbox-inbox-patterns","saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","workflow-orchestration","agentic-architecture","agent-orchestration","multi-agent-systems","domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","modular-monolith-vs-microservices","multi-tenancy","tenant-isolation"],"order_reasons":{"ai-solution-architecture":"frames the job: what the model does, what deterministic code must guarantee, and the components around every call.","nfrs":"turns quality, latency, cost and safety goals into measurable scenarios, so later choices have something to be judged against.","trade-off-analysis":"gives a repeatable method for choosing between options using those NFRs, sensitivity points and reversibility.","adrs":"records each decision with its evidence and revisit trigger, so the rest of the module produces durable records.","c4-diagrams":"supplies the notation for showing where models, data and trust boundaries sit, used in every later design.","reference-architectures":"introduces the recurring AI system shapes you start from and deviate from on purpose.","build-vs-buy":"decides per layer what to own and what to rent, and where to keep a seam so the choice can be reversed.","model-selection":"the eval-driven funnel for picking the cheapest model that clears a measured quality bar.","slm-vs-llm-trade-offs":"applies selection to model size and shows when a narrow small model beats a frontier one.","open-weight-vs-proprietary":"separates the control and licensing axis from the deployment decision.","self-host-vs-api":"works through the utilisation and constraint arithmetic that decides whether GPUs pay for themselves.","model-portfolio":"turns individual choices into a small, governed set of models with owners, baselines and retirement plans.","multi-model-architecture":"breaks one system into stages with a different model per stage, joined by typed contracts.","model-routing-cascading":"sends each request to the right portfolio tier before or after a cheap first attempt.","ai-gateway":"centralises keys, budgets, routing, caching and logging, which makes routing and fallbacks operable.","model-fallback-strategies":"decides in advance what the gateway calls when a provider throttles or fails.","graceful-degradation":"goes beyond fallbacks to a labelled ladder of reduced functions for when the AI path cannot be saved.","caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation":"cuts cost and latency at the prefix, response, embedding and retrieval layers without serving stale or wrong answers.","rag-architecture":"the most common application shape: an offline indexing pipeline and an online retrieval path joined by an index.","memory-architecture":"extends retrieval to state the system writes itself, scoped per user and tenant, with forgetting policies.","distributed-systems":"sets out partial failure and the core toolkit every multi-service AI product relies on.","synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution":"picks request, stream or job-and-callback shapes for calls that take seconds to minutes.","data-consistency":"chooses which invariants must be strong and which copies, such as indexes, caches and memory, may lag.","event-driven-architecture":"decouples producers from consumers such as billing, evals and audit, at the cost of eventual consistency.","exactly-once-processing-limitations":"explains why delivery cannot be exactly once and how to get exactly-once effect instead.","outbox-inbox-patterns":"the concrete mechanism for publishing events atomically and processing redeliveries idempotently.","saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions":"coordinates multi-step operations across services with compensations around an irreversible pivot.","workflow-orchestration":"runs long, stateful processes durably with Airflow, Prefect or Temporal instead of hand-rolled sagas.","agentic-architecture":"wraps a model-driven loop in typed tools, budgets, approval gates and externalised state.","agent-orchestration":"decides how much control flow is code and how much is the model, and where checkpoints and interrupts go.","multi-agent-systems":"splits work across several agents only when isolation, parallelism or specialisation pays for the coordination cost.","domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts":"draws boundaries by business meaning, which then define tool, data and service ownership.","modular-monolith-vs-microservices":"turns those boundaries into deployment units only where scaling, team or blast radius demands it.","multi-tenancy":"carries tenant identity through every layer and picks pool, bridge or silo per resource.","tenant-isolation":"layers independent controls so one bug cannot leak data or capacity across tenants, including through the prompt."},"capstone":{"brief":"Design, and partly build, the architecture for \"Policy Desk\", a multi-tenant AI assistant that a\nB2B insurance-software vendor will sell to its broker customers. Brokers upload policy documents and\nask questions with cited answers. An agent drafts endorsement change requests, which a human must\napprove before the agent submits them to the carrier's API and records the fee in billing. Tenants\nrange from ten-seat agencies to one enterprise that requires EU data residency and refuses to have its\ndata in a shared vector index. Over one week, write measurable NFRs for answer quality, latency, cost per\nquestion, availability and isolation. Run model selection on a small labelled set and define a\nthree-tier portfolio with routing and a fallback chain behind a gateway. Design the RAG and memory\nlayers with tenant-scoped indexes and caches. Specify the approval-and-submit flow as a durable workflow\nsaga with an outbox for the billing event. Decide the bounded contexts, the deployment units and the\npool, bridge or silo tier for each tenant class. Record each major choice as an ADR. Build a thin\nvertical slice that proves the riskiest parts: tenant-scoped retrieval, gateway routing with fallback,\nand an idempotent submit step.","deliverables":["An architecture document with C4 context and container diagrams, plus a component diagram for the agent runtime, each showing the trust and tenant boundaries and where every model call sits.","An NFR catalogue of at least ten measurable scenarios, each with stimulus, condition and numeric response measure, mapped to an SLO, a test or an eval gate.","A model-selection report: candidates filtered by hard constraints, eval scores and cost per 1,000 requests on the labelled set, the chosen portfolio tiers, the routing or cascade rule, and the ordered fallback chain with the error classes that trigger it.","At least six ADRs, covering at minimum self-host versus API, gateway choice, tenancy tier per customer class, sync versus async for agent runs, monolith versus service split, and cache keying. Each has options, a trade-off matrix and a revisit trigger.","A sequence diagram and failure table for the approve-and-submit saga, listing each step's compensation, the irreversible pivot, idempotency keys, and the outbox and inbox records.","A runnable slice with tests: a gateway config with two providers and fallback, tenant-filtered retrieval and tenant-keyed response cache, and a workflow step that submits to a mocked carrier API exactly once under retries."],"rubric":["Every NFR has a number and a measurement method, and at least three ADRs cite a specific NFR or eval result as the deciding evidence instead of preference.","The model plan shows measured cost and quality per tier on the labelled set, and the router's chosen mix is within a stated quality tolerance of always using the top tier at a lower stated cost.","A test that queries as tenant A, with tenant B's documents indexed and cached on the same key text, returns no B content. The isolation test suite fails if the tenant filter or cache key prefix is removed.","Killing the provider in the gateway config makes requests succeed on the secondary with a labelled fallback flag in logs. Killing both makes the UI show the designed degraded state, not an error page.","Replaying the carrier submit step three times produces one carrier call and one billing event, shown by idempotency-key and outbox logs. A forced failure after the pivot runs the documented forward-recovery path, not a compensation.","The C4 diagrams pass a reviewer check: every box has a name, type, technology and purpose, every arrow has intent, and the EU enterprise tenant's data path stays inside its silo and region."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-solution-architecture","key":"system-design/ai-solution-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"AI / solution architecture","taxonomy_name":"AI / solution architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/reference-architectures","system-design/nfrs","system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery"],"tldr":"An AI solution architecture is the design of everything around the model call: how a request becomes\ncontext, which model runs, what validates the output, what is logged, and how quality and cost are\nmeasured. It exists because a model is a probabilistic, expensive, stateless, rented dependency, and\nnothing about correctness, safety, cost or enterprise integration is solved by the call itself. The\narchitect's job is to put deterministic engineering where it belongs and leave the model only the\npart it is uniquely good at.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"reference-architectures","key":"system-design/reference-architectures","module":"system-design","name":"Reference architectures","taxonomy_name":"reference architectures","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/ai-solution-architecture","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","system-design/workflow-orchestration","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","agents/workflows-vs-agents"],"tldr":"A reference architecture is a validated, reusable blueprint for a recurring system shape: its\ncomponents, their responsibilities, the interfaces between them, the default non-functional\nrequirements, and the known failure modes. In AI systems a handful of shapes recur constantly (RAG\nassistant, extraction pipeline, tool-using agent, multi-step workflow, platform gateway). Starting from\nthe matching blueprint means you deviate deliberately instead of rediscovering the same missing pieces\nafter launch.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-selection","key":"system-design/model-selection","module":"system-design","name":"Model selection","taxonomy_name":"model selection","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/benchmarking","evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","system-design/model-portfolio","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs"],"tldr":"Model selection is choosing, per task, the cheapest and fastest model that clears a quality bar you\nhave measured on your own data, after filtering by hard constraints such as modality, context length,\ndeployment location, licensing and tool support. It is a funnel, not a leaderboard lookup, because\npublic benchmarks measure a different distribution from your traffic, and because quality has a\nthreshold (\"good enough for this task\") while cost and latency are paid linearly on every call.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-portfolio","key":"system-design/model-portfolio","module":"system-design","name":"Model portfolio","taxonomy_name":"model portfolio","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/multi-model-architecture","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/model-registry","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"A model portfolio is a deliberately small, governed set of models, each with a role: a frontier tier\nfor hard reasoning, a workhorse for the bulk, small fast models for classification and routing,\nembeddings and rerankers, plus specialised and fine-tuned variants. Each entry has an owner, eval\nbaselines, a cost card, approved data classes and a retirement plan. It exists because one model\neverywhere overpays on easy work and underdelivers on hard work, while unmanaged per-team choices\ncreate sprawl nobody can evaluate, secure or exit.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","key":"system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","module":"system-design","name":"SLM vs LLM trade-offs","taxonomy_name":"SLM vs LLM trade-offs","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/scaling-laws","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/quantization","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/self-host-vs-api","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","system-design/model-selection"],"tldr":"Small language models (roughly up to the low tens of billions of parameters, runnable on one GPU or a\nlaptop; a fuzzy, moving boundary) trade breadth for cost, latency and deployability. On narrow,\nin-distribution tasks, especially after fine-tuning, an SLM often matches a large model; on open-ended\nreasoning, long-horizon tool use and broad world knowledge it does not. The decision turns on the\ntask's shape: how narrow, what a tail error costs, and where the data may go.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"build-vs-buy","key":"system-design/build-vs-buy","module":"system-design","name":"Build vs buy","taxonomy_name":"build vs buy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["strategy/vendor-evaluation","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","scoping/roi-model","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","strategy/platform-strategy","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions"],"tldr":"Build versus buy in AI is not one decision but one per layer of the stack: model, serving, gateway,\norchestration, retrieval, evaluation and observability, guardrails, and the application itself. Buy\nwhat is commodity and fast-moving, build what differentiates and touches your data most intimately,\nand keep a thin seam you own at every boundary so the decision can be reversed. It exists because the\nAI stack changes faster than build cycles, and both \"build everything\" and \"buy everything\" fail in\npredictable ways.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"open-weight-vs-proprietary","key":"system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","module":"system-design","name":"Open-weight vs proprietary","taxonomy_name":"open-weight vs proprietary","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/build-vs-buy","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","training-adaptation/peft","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Open-weight models publish their parameters so you can download, run, inspect, modify and fine-tune\nthem under a licence; proprietary weights stay with the provider, who exposes them as a service. The\nchoice decides who controls the model's lifecycle, where data goes, what you can change, and how cost\nscales. It is a control-and-licensing axis, not a deployment one: open weights can be rented through\nan API and proprietary models can be reached over private networking, so decide it separately from\nself-host versus API.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"self-host-vs-api","key":"system-design/self-host-vs-api","module":"system-design","name":"Self-host vs API","taxonomy_name":"self-host vs API","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","system-design/model-selection","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","serving-inference/engines","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"A hosted model API buys capability, elasticity and zero operations at a per-token price; self-hosting\nopen-weight models buys control, data locality and a lower *marginal* cost per token, but only once the\nGPUs are busy enough to amortise their fixed cost and the people who run them. It is a utilisation and\nconstraints decision, not a price-list comparison: start on the API unless a hard constraint (residency,\nair gap, customisation, sustained volume) forces hosting, and re-run the numbers as traffic grows.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-routing-cascading","key":"system-design/model-routing-cascading","module":"system-design","name":"Model routing / cascading","taxonomy_name":"model routing / cascading","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","system-design/multi-model-architecture","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","evals/llm-as-a-judge","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"Requests vary enormously in difficulty, so sending everything to the strongest model overpays and\nsending everything to the cheapest underdelivers. Routing picks a model per request *before* the call,\nusing rules, a classifier or a learned router; cascading calls the cheap model first and escalates only\nwhen a quality check fails. Both trade some router or check error and extra tail latency for large cost\nsavings, and both are only as good as the quality signal that drives them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-model-architecture","key":"system-design/multi-model-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-model architecture","taxonomy_name":"multi-model architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/model-portfolio","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","rag-retrieval/reranking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","observability/tracing"],"tldr":"No single model is the best embedder, reranker, classifier, generator, vision model and judge at once,\nand the best model for each job is rarely the cheapest. A multi-model architecture decomposes the system\ninto stages, each served by the model that fits that stage's quality, latency and cost budget, joined by\nexplicit typed contracts. The price is coupling: every stage's output is the next stage's input\ndistribution, so models have to be versioned and evaluated together, not one at a time.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-gateway","key":"system-design/ai-gateway","module":"system-design","name":"AI gateway (LiteLLM proxy, Portkey)","taxonomy_name":"AI gateway (LiteLLM proxy, Portkey)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/litellm","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","serving-inference/rate-limiting","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp"],"tldr":"An AI gateway is a proxy that every model call passes through: one OpenAI-style API in front of many\nproviders, with key custody, budgets, rate limits, routing, fallbacks, caching and logging handled once\ninstead of re-implemented in every application. LiteLLM's proxy is the open-source, Python,\nconfig-file-driven option you run yourself; Portkey is a hosted gateway driven by declarative JSON\nconfigs, with an open-source core. Both buy control and visibility at the price of an extra hop and a\nnew single point of failure.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-fallback-strategies","key":"system-design/model-fallback-strategies","module":"system-design","name":"Model fallback strategies","taxonomy_name":"model fallback strategies","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/fallbacks","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/graceful-degradation","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"Every model endpoint fails sometimes: outages, 429 throttling, latency spikes, regional capacity limits,\ndeprecated model ids. A fallback strategy decides in advance what to call instead, in what order, and on\nwhich errors, so a provider's bad hour does not become your outage. Fallbacks are not free: the\nsubstitute behaves differently, retries can amplify an incident, and an untested fallback is a second\nfailure waiting behind the first.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-degradation","key":"system-design/graceful-degradation","module":"system-design","name":"Graceful degradation","taxonomy_name":"graceful degradation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/model-fallback-strategies","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/slas-slos","system-design/nfrs","llmops-platform/rollback","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"When the AI path is failing, slow, over budget or overloaded, the product should step down to a\ncheaper, simpler and honestly labelled version of itself rather than fall over. Graceful degradation is a\ndesigned ladder (full → reduced → cached → non-AI → clear unavailability) with explicit triggers,\nhysteresis, labelled output and degraded paths that are exercised so they still work when needed.\nFallbacks keep the function by changing the provider; degradation keeps the user moving by changing the\nfunction.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","key":"system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","module":"system-design","name":"Caching strategy & cache invalidation","taxonomy_name":"caching strategy & cache invalidation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["serving-inference/caching","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","model-internals/kv-cache","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Model calls are the slowest, most expensive step in an AI system, and much of what they process repeats:\nthe same system prompt, documents and questions. Caching happens at several layers, prefix caching of\ncomputation, exact and semantic response caches, embedding and retrieval caches, each with its own key\nand its own way of going stale. The design question is always the key: what must be identical for a\ncached result to still be correct, and how you find out when it no longer is.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rag-architecture","key":"system-design/rag-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"RAG architecture","taxonomy_name":"RAG architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation"],"tldr":"RAG is two pipelines joined by an index: an offline path that turns documents into searchable chunks,\nand an online path that retrieves the few chunks relevant to a query and hands them to the model with\ninstructions to answer from them. It exists because model weights are frozen, general and\npermission-blind, while the facts a product needs are changing, private and access-controlled. The\narchitecture decisions are about where each pipeline runs, what the index stores, and how failures are\nmeasured.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"agentic-architecture","key":"system-design/agentic-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Agentic architecture","taxonomy_name":"agentic architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/tool-calling","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"An agentic architecture is a system in which a model chooses the next action at runtime, observes the\nresult, and repeats until a stopping condition. The system-design job is to wrap that loop in boundaries\nthe model does not control: a typed tool layer with least privilege, externalized state so runs survive\ncrashes, step and cost budgets, approval gates for consequential actions, and tracing. The model owns\njudgment; the architecture owns guarantees.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-agent-systems","key":"system-design/multi-agent-systems","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-agent systems","taxonomy_name":"multi-agent systems","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/task-decomposition","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","system-design/agent-orchestration","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"A multi-agent system splits a task across several model-driven loops, each with its own instructions,\ntools and context, coordinated by a topology (supervisor–workers, pipeline, parallel fan-out, critic).\nIt exists because a single agent's context, tool list and privileges do not scale with task breadth.\nThe price is coordination: duplicated context, compounding errors, harder tracing and higher cost, so a\nsplit must buy isolation, parallelism or specialization you can name.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-orchestration","key":"system-design/agent-orchestration","module":"system-design","name":"Agent orchestration","taxonomy_name":"agent orchestration","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","agents/frameworks","agents/human-in-the-loop","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"Agent orchestration is the control layer that decides what runs next in an LLM application: which model\ncall, tool, sub-agent or human step, with what state, and what happens on failure. It exists because\nonce a system has more than one model call plus tools, ordering, state, retries, concurrency and\ninterruption need an owner. The core design choice is how much of that control flow is deterministic\ncode and how much is left to the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflow-orchestration","key":"system-design/workflow-orchestration","module":"system-design","name":"Workflow orchestration (Temporal, Prefect, Airflow)","taxonomy_name":"workflow orchestration (Temporal, Prefect, Airflow)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","system-design/agent-orchestration","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations"],"tldr":"Workflow orchestrators run multi-step processes reliably outside a single request: they persist\nprogress, retry steps, schedule work and show where each run is. Airflow schedules batch DAGs of data\ntasks; Prefect orchestrates dynamic Python dataflows with less ceremony; Temporal provides durable\nexecution, replaying workflow code from an event history so long-running stateful processes survive\ncrashes. For AI systems: indexing pipelines fit Airflow or Prefect; long-lived agent runs and\napproval flows fit Temporal-style engines.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-architecture","key":"system-design/memory-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Memory architecture","taxonomy_name":"memory architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-memory","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","agents/context-compaction-summarization","prompting/context-engineering","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","system-design/rag-architecture"],"tldr":"Models are stateless and context windows are finite, so anything an application must \"remember\" across\nturns, sessions or users has to be stored outside the model and brought back selectively. Memory\narchitecture is the design of those stores and the policies that write, read, update and delete them:\nworking memory in the context, session state in a database, long-term episodic and semantic memory in\nretrievable stores, each scoped to a user and tenant. The hard decisions are what to remember and what\nto forget.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","key":"system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","module":"system-design","name":"Domain-driven design & bounded contexts","taxonomy_name":"domain-driven design & bounded contexts","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/c4-diagrams","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"Domain-driven design splits a large system along the meanings of its business language, not along\ntechnical layers: each bounded context owns one consistent model and vocabulary, and contexts talk\nthrough explicit contracts instead of a shared schema. It exists because words like \"customer\" mean\ndifferent things in different parts of a business, and forcing one model onto all of them produces\nsystems nobody can change. For AI systems, bounded contexts decide tool boundaries, data ownership and\nwho translates meaning for the agent.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"event-driven-architecture","key":"system-design/event-driven-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Event-driven architecture","taxonomy_name":"event-driven architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"In an event-driven architecture a service records that something happened (\"DocumentUploaded\",\n\"RunCompleted\") as an immutable event on a broker, and any number of consumers react on their own\nschedule. The producer no longer knows or waits for its consumers, which is what lets you add a billing\nmeter, an eval sampler and an audit log to an agent pipeline without touching the agent. The price is\neventual consistency, at-least-once delivery and a flow of control that no single stack trace shows.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","key":"system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","module":"system-design","name":"Synchronous vs asynchronous execution","taxonomy_name":"synchronous vs asynchronous execution","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/event-driven-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","python-for-ai/asyncio"],"tldr":"Synchronous execution means the caller holds a connection open until the result arrives; asynchronous\nexecution means the caller gets an acknowledgement (a job id) and the result arrives later by polling,\npush or callback. LLM calls take seconds and agent runs take minutes, which is longer than load\nbalancers, mobile networks and humans will wait, so most AI workloads need the asynchronous shape, with\nstreaming as the middle ground when a person is watching.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","key":"system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","module":"system-design","name":"Saga pattern & distributed transactions","taxonomy_name":"saga pattern & distributed transactions","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/workflow-orchestration","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels"],"tldr":"A business operation spanning several services cannot be one ACID transaction, and two-phase commit\nblocks under coordinator failure and is unavailable across external APIs. A saga replaces it with a\nsequence of local transactions, each paired with a compensating action that undoes it. Sagas give\natomicity and durability but not isolation, so you must order steps around the irreversible pivot, mark\nin-flight state and make every step idempotent. An agent calling several tools in sequence is a saga.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"outbox-inbox-patterns","key":"system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","module":"system-design","name":"Outbox / inbox patterns","taxonomy_name":"outbox / inbox patterns","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"A service cannot atomically commit a database row and publish a message. The **outbox** pattern writes\nthe message into a table inside the same transaction as the business change and lets a relay publish it,\nso a message exists if and only if the change committed. The **inbox** pattern records each processed\nmessage id inside the consumer's transaction, so redeliveries become no-ops. Together they turn an\nat-least-once broker into effectively-once processing between two databases.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"exactly-once-processing-limitations","key":"system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","module":"system-design","name":"Exactly-once processing limitations","taxonomy_name":"exactly-once processing limitations","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/distributed-systems","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","serving-inference/idempotency","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"Exactly-once *delivery* over an unreliable network is impossible: a sender without an acknowledgement\ncannot tell whether the message or the ack was lost, so it must risk a duplicate or a loss. What you can\nbuild is exactly-once *effect*: at-least-once delivery plus deduplication or idempotent processing, with\nthe dedupe state committed together with the effect. Every \"exactly-once\" feature, Kafka transactions\nincluded, stops at that boundary; external API calls and LLM invocations beyond it are not covered.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-systems","key":"system-design/distributed-systems","module":"system-design","name":"Distributed systems","taxonomy_name":"distributed systems","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/data-consistency","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/event-driven-architecture","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/high-availability","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/circuit-breakers","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"A distributed system is several machines cooperating over a network, and its defining property is\n**partial failure**: any node or link can fail or stall while the rest continue, and no observer can tell\n\"slow\" from \"dead\" except by waiting. Every AI product is one, because model API, vector store, queue,\nagent workers and customer tools are separate machines. The discipline is a short list: timeouts with\nbudgets, idempotent retries, quorums, leases with fencing, consensus where agreement is required, and\ntracing.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-consistency","key":"system-design/data-consistency","module":"system-design","name":"Data consistency","taxonomy_name":"data consistency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/distributed-systems","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","reliability/multi-region-architecture","data-architecture/data-contracts"],"tldr":"\"Consistency\" names two things: invariants inside one database (the C in ACID, enforced by constraints\nand isolation levels) and the guarantee about *which* value a read may return when copies exist\n(linearizable down to eventual). Strong guarantees cost coordination and latency, so good designs make a\nfew invariants strong (permissions, quotas, idempotency keys) and let everything else lag, visibly. In AI\nsystems the usual victims are the search index, the cache and the agent's memory, all copies of a source\nof truth.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"modular-monolith-vs-microservices","key":"system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","module":"system-design","name":"Modular monolith vs microservices","taxonomy_name":"modular monolith vs microservices","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/distributed-systems","system-design/adrs","system-design/trade-off-analysis","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"The real decision is where your *deployment and ownership* boundaries go, not how your code is\norganised. A modular monolith is one deployable whose internal modules have enforced public APIs and\nown their data; microservices turn those same boundaries into network boundaries, buying independent\ndeployment, scaling and failure isolation at the price of distributed-systems problems (partial failure,\neventual consistency, operational surface). Start modular, and split a boundary only when it has a\nproven, independent reason to exist: a different scaling profile, a different team, or a different blast\nradius.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-tenancy","key":"system-design/multi-tenancy","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-tenancy","taxonomy_name":"multi-tenancy","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped"],"tldr":"Multi-tenancy is one running system serving many customers (tenants) while keeping each one's data,\nconfiguration, quotas and bill separate. It exists because one deployment per customer does not scale in\ncost or operations. The mechanism is a tenant identity established at the edge and carried through every\nlayer, plus a deliberate choice per resource of how much is shared (pool), partitioned (bridge) or\ndedicated (silo). In AI systems that identity must also reach the retrieval index, the prompt, the caches\nand the token meter.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-isolation","key":"system-design/tenant-isolation","module":"system-design","name":"Tenant isolation","taxonomy_name":"tenant isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["system-design/multi-tenancy","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"Tenant isolation is the set of controls that make the \"each tenant is alone\" promise of multi-tenancy\ntrue even when application code is wrong. It layers independent mechanisms, scoped credentials,\ndatabase-enforced row policies, partitioned indexes and caches, per-tenant quotas and tenant-tagged\ntelemetry, so that a single bug cannot leak data or performance across tenants. In AI systems the\nprompt is a new exfiltration path: anything retrieved, remembered or cached under the wrong tenant key\nis read out loud by the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"c4-diagrams","key":"system-design/c4-diagrams","module":"system-design","name":"C4 diagrams","taxonomy_name":"C4 diagrams","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["system-design/ai-solution-architecture","system-design/reference-architectures","system-design/adrs","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"C4 is a small set of conventions for architecture diagrams at four nested zoom levels, System Context,\nContainer, Component and Code, where every box states its name, type, technology and purpose and every\narrow states its intent. It exists because ad-hoc boxes-and-arrows mix abstraction levels and leave\nreaders guessing what a box is and which way data flows. For AI systems it is the quickest way to show\nwhere the model, the customer's data and the trust boundaries actually sit.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"adrs","key":"system-design/adrs","module":"system-design","name":"ADRs (architecture decision records)","taxonomy_name":"ADRs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/nfrs","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/model-selection","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"An architecture decision record is a short, numbered, immutable document that captures one significant\ndecision: the context that forced it, the options considered, the choice, and its consequences. It\nexists because decisions outlive the people, meetings and chat threads that made them, and without the\nrecord teams either cargo-cult old choices or reverse them blindly. In AI systems, where model, vendor\nand retrieval choices churn monthly, a good ADR also records the evidence at decision time and the\ntrigger for revisiting.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"nfrs","key":"system-design/nfrs","module":"system-design","name":"NFRs (non-functional requirements)","taxonomy_name":"NFRs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/adrs","reliability/slas-slos","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","observability/latency-percentiles","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/requirements-traceability"],"tldr":"Non-functional requirements (quality attribute requirements) state how well a system must do what it\ndoes: latency, availability, cost, security, privacy, maintainability and, for AI systems, answer\nquality, safety and freshness. They exist because architecture is shaped by these constraints rather\nthan by the feature list, and because adjectives like \"fast\" and \"accurate\" cannot be tested. A usable\nNFR is a measurable scenario, a stimulus under a stated condition with a numeric response measure,\ntraced to a test, an SLO or an evaluation gate.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"trade-off-analysis","key":"system-design/trade-off-analysis","module":"system-design","name":"Trade-off analysis","taxonomy_name":"trade-off analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/nfrs","system-design/adrs","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"Trade-off analysis is the disciplined way to choose between architectural options when none dominates:\nframe the decision and its hard constraints, enumerate real options, score them against the NFRs that\nmatter using evidence rather than opinion, find the sensitivity points where the ranking flips, weigh\nreversibility, then decide and record. It exists because every architectural choice gives something up\nand \"best practice\" hides what. In AI systems the usual battleground is quality against latency against\ncost, with vendor risk as the fourth corner.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"reliability","tier":2,"order":17,"title":"Reliability","intro":{"overview":"This module is about keeping an AI product working, or failing in a controlled way, when the things\nit depends on do not. It begins with targets: SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets turn \"reliable\" into\na number you can measure from the user's side, including quality and cost for model endpoints. High\navailability then shows how availabilities multiply along a request path and why the model provider\nusually caps yours. Fault tolerance supplies the baseline toolkit for every remote call: timeouts,\nbounded jittered retries and idempotency. Circuit breakers, bulkheads, backpressure and load shedding\nand tenant-level quotas are the containment layer, each stopping one slow dependency, workload or\ncustomer from consuming capacity everyone else needs. Durable workflow execution makes long agent runs\nand approval flows survive crashes and deploys without redoing paid model calls. Multi-region\narchitecture, disaster recovery and business continuity widen the frame to region loss, deleted data,\nvendor exits and the people and decisions around a restore. FMEA and chaos engineering close the loop:\none finds the failure modes nobody is watching, the other proves the mechanisms actually fire. After\nthe module you can set SLOs for an LLM service, size pools and queues with Little's law, design\nfallbacks and failover with stated RPO and RTO, and show evidence that they work. It matters most for\nArchitects, who own these trade-offs, and for Engineers who carry the pager.","why":{"engineer":"You write the client wrapper every service imports, so you must get timeouts, retry budgets, idempotency keys, breaker thresholds, per-dependency semaphores and `Retry-After` handling right, and export the metrics that show when traffic is quietly running on a fallback path.","architect":"You choose the availability target, the number of zones, regions and model providers, the RPO and RTO for each store, and the isolation boundaries between tenants and workload classes, and each choice sets cost, complexity and what the SLA can honestly promise.","fde":"Enterprise buyers ask about multi-region, continuity plans and what happens when your AI vendor is down; you need credible answers, a sensible SLA, and quotas that stop one customer's backfill from slowing everyone else on a shared deployment."},"mental_model":"Picture reliability as a budget defended in rings. At the centre is the error budget from your SLO:\nthe amount of failure users will tolerate, measured as they experience it. The first ring is per-call\nhygiene (timeout, bounded retry, idempotency), which turns transient faults into successes without\nduplicating side effects. The second ring is containment: breakers, bulkheads, backpressure, load\nshedding and tenant quotas, each keeping a local fault local by capping how much shared capacity it\ncan take, sized with Little's law (in-flight = rate × time). The third ring is survival of larger\nlosses: redundancy and failover for components and regions, durable execution for long processes,\nbackups separated in time, place and trust for data, and continuity plans for suppliers and people.\nAround all of it sits verification: FMEA decides which faults to worry about, and chaos experiments\nshow whether each ring holds. A mechanism nobody has seen fire does not count as protection.","order":["slas-slos","high-availability","fault-tolerance","circuit-breakers","bulkheads","backpressure-and-load-shedding","tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","durable-workflow-execution","multi-region-architecture","disaster-recovery","business-continuity","fmea","chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing"],"order_reasons":{"slas-slos":"sets measurable targets and error budgets first, so every later mechanism has a goal to defend.","high-availability":"shows how availabilities compose along a dependency chain and why the provider caps your nines.","fault-tolerance":"the baseline per-call toolkit of timeouts, retry budgets, idempotency and fallbacks that the later patterns build on.","circuit-breakers":"stops sending to a failing dependency, the first containment pattern layered on top of timeouts and retries.","bulkheads":"caps aggregate resource use per dependency or workload, covering the window before a breaker trips.","backpressure-and-load-shedding":"applies Little's law to overload, bounding queues and rejecting early to protect goodput.","tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation":"extends bounded capacity to fair division among tenants who share a provider key or GPU pool.","durable-workflow-execution":"makes multi-step agent and approval processes survive crashes and deploys without repeating side effects.","multi-region-architecture":"moves from component failure to region loss, latency and residency, where state placement decides what is lost.","disaster-recovery":"defines RPO and RTO and the restore of state that redundancy cannot protect, such as indexes, weights and deleted data.","business-continuity":"wraps DR in function-first planning for vendors, people, roles and communication during a disruption.","fmea":"systematically lists and ranks failure modes, especially silent AI ones, to decide what to guard and test.","chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing":"verifies the earlier mechanisms by injecting the top FMEA faults under a bounded blast radius."},"capstone":{"brief":"Harden a multi-tenant document question-answering and drafting service for a B2B SaaS company that is\nabout to sign its first enterprise customers. The service has a chat endpoint that retrieves from a\nvector store and calls a hosted LLM, a batch endpoint that re-embeds customer documents, and an agent\nflow that drafts a reply, waits for human approval and then sends it through a CRM API. Today it runs in\none region on one provider key with one shared worker pool, and a single tenant's backfill has already\nslowed everyone. Over a week, define SLIs and SLOs for availability, latency, answer quality and cost\nper task; run an FMEA of the request path; then implement the fixes the top rows call for: a shared\nclient wrapper with timeouts, retry budgets and idempotency keys, a breaker and secondary-provider\nfallback, per-dependency bulkheads, queue-wait-based load shedding, per-tenant token buckets with fair\nqueuing, and the approval flow as a durable workflow. Write the DR and continuity plan, including how\nthe vector index is restored or rebuilt and what happens if the provider is down for a day, and prove\nthe design with fault-injection experiments.","deliverables":["An SLO document with SLI definitions (including a quality SLI backed by an eval), 30-day targets, error-budget arithmetic, a burn-rate alert rule and a draft external SLA that is looser than the SLOs.","An FMEA spreadsheet covering at least 15 failure modes across retrieval, model calls, tools and tenancy, scored S × O × D, with the top five mapped to specific mitigations in the code.","Working code for the resilience layer: a client wrapper, a circuit breaker with a fallback provider, per-dependency semaphores sized with Little's law, an admission controller that sheds by queue wait and priority, per-tenant quotas, and the approval flow as a durable workflow.","A DR and business-continuity runbook listing every stateful asset (database, vector index, prompt registry, secrets) with its RPO, RTO and restore-or-rebuild method, named roles, and the manual fallback mode.","A resilience test report from at least four experiments (provider 429s and latency, CRM hang, noisy-tenant backfill, worker crash mid-workflow), each with steady-state hypothesis, blast radius, abort condition, measured result and follow-up fix."],"rubric":["During a simulated noisy-tenant backfill, other tenants' p95 chat latency stays within the latency SLO, and per-tenant dashboards show only the offending tenant's queue wait and 429s rising.","With the CRM API hanging, chat requests that do not need the CRM keep succeeding; the CRM bulkhead saturates, its breaker opens within the stated threshold, and no worker pool beyond that compartment is exhausted.","Killing a worker mid-approval resumes the workflow at the same step with no second model call and no duplicate CRM send, shown by activity history and idempotency-key logs.","Pool sizes, queue bounds and retry budgets are each justified in writing with Little's law or error-budget arithmetic, not chosen arbitrarily.","A timed restore drill records actual RPO and RTO against the targets, including the measured time and cost to re-embed the vector index if it must be rebuilt.","Every chaos experiment states its hypothesis and abort condition before it runs, at least one refuted hypothesis produced a code or config change, and the change was verified by re-running the experiment."]}},"topics":[{"id":"high-availability","key":"reliability/high-availability","module":"reliability","name":"High availability","taxonomy_name":"high availability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/slas-slos","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","deployment/health-checks","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining"],"tldr":"High availability (HA) is the property that a system keeps answering requests through the failure of\nany single component, measured as the fraction of time or requests for which it works. You get it by\nremoving single points of failure with redundancy, detecting failures quickly with health checks, and\nmoving traffic automatically (failover). It costs money and complexity because every redundant part\nmust be kept consistent and exercised, and an AI product's availability is capped by its least\navailable dependency, which is usually the model provider.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fault-tolerance","key":"reliability/fault-tolerance","module":"reliability","name":"Fault tolerance","taxonomy_name":"fault tolerance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fmea","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","system-design/graceful-degradation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"Fault tolerance is a system's ability to keep delivering correct, or explicitly degraded, service while\nsome of its components are failing. It combines redundancy with mechanisms that contain a fault before\nit becomes a user-visible failure: timeouts, bounded retries, idempotency, circuit breakers, bulkheads,\nfallbacks and replicated state. The design question is never \"how do we avoid faults\" but \"which faults\ndo we tolerate, how do we detect them, and what does the system do in the window before recovery\".","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-region-architecture","key":"reliability/multi-region-architecture","module":"reliability","name":"Multi-region architecture","taxonomy_name":"multi-region architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/distributed-systems","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"A multi-region architecture runs a system in two or more geographically separate cloud regions to\nsurvive a whole-region outage, cut latency for distant users, and satisfy data-residency rules. The\nhard part is not duplicating compute but deciding where each piece of state lives and how it is\nreplicated: synchronous replication taxes every write with a cross-region round trip, asynchronous\nreplication loses recent writes on failover. Start multi-AZ in one region; go multi-region only for\na reason you can name.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"disaster-recovery","key":"reliability/disaster-recovery","module":"reliability","name":"Disaster recovery (RPO / RTO)","taxonomy_name":"disaster recovery (RPO / RTO)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/business-continuity","reliability/high-availability","reliability/multi-region-architecture","deployment/runbooks","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","llmops-platform/rollback","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Disaster recovery (DR) is the plan and machinery for restoring service after a failure that redundancy\ncannot mask: a region loss, a deleted database, corrupted data, a compromised account. Two numbers\ndefine it. **RPO** (recovery point objective) is how much data, measured in time, you may lose. **RTO**\n(recovery time objective) is how long service may be down. Every DR design is a trade of cost against\nthose two numbers, and a DR plan that has not been rehearsed is a hypothesis, not a plan.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"business-continuity","key":"reliability/business-continuity","module":"reliability","name":"Business continuity","taxonomy_name":"business continuity","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/fmea","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","deployment/runbooks","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","system-design/model-fallback-strategies"],"tldr":"Business continuity (BC) is the practice of keeping the organisation's critical functions running\nthrough a disruption, whatever its cause: a cloud outage, a vendor failure, a model deprecation, a\nsecurity incident, or the one person who understands the prompts leaving. Disaster recovery is its\ntechnical subset. BC starts from a business impact analysis that ranks functions and sets tolerable\ndowntime, then builds plans, workarounds, roles and rehearsals around that ranking, because during a\nreal disruption nobody has time to decide what matters.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"durable-workflow-execution","key":"reliability/durable-workflow-execution","module":"reliability","name":"Durable workflow execution","taxonomy_name":"durable workflow execution","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/workflow-orchestration","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","reliability/fault-tolerance"],"tldr":"Durable workflow execution makes a long, multi-step process survive crashes, deploys and\ndays-long waits by persisting its progress as an append-only event history and replaying that history\nto resume exactly where it stopped. The orchestration code must be deterministic so replay is faithful;\nevery side effect lives in a retried, idempotent *activity*. It replaces hand-built state machines in\ndatabase columns and cron jobs, and it is the natural runtime for agent loops whose steps are slow,\nflaky and expensive to redo.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure-and-load-shedding","key":"reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","module":"reliability","name":"Backpressure & load shedding","taxonomy_name":"backpressure & load shedding","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","serving-inference/backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","serving-inference/rate-limiting"],"tldr":"When requests arrive faster than a system can serve them, queues grow without bound, latency climbs\npast every timeout, clients retry, and throughput collapses. **Backpressure** pushes the \"slow down\"\nsignal upstream through bounded queues, blocking producers and explicit 429/503 responses so the\nsource stops sending. **Load shedding** deliberately rejects some work, cheaply and early, so the\nrest completes within its latency budget. Both exist because an overloaded system that tries to serve\neveryone serves no one.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","key":"reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","module":"reliability","name":"Tenant-level quotas & noisy-neighbor isolation","taxonomy_name":"tenant-level quotas & noisy-neighbor isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/bulkheads","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","system-design/tenant-isolation","serving-inference/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"In a multi-tenant AI service every tenant draws on the same scarce pools: provider tokens-per-minute,\nGPU concurrency, workers, connections. A noisy neighbor is one tenant whose burst (a backfill, a looping\nagent, a retry storm) fills a pool so everyone else's latency and errors rise. Tenant-level quotas cap\nwhat each tenant may consume; isolation mechanisms (per-tenant lanes, fair queuing, separate pools)\nensure a tenant hitting its cap degrades only itself. The goal is to turn a shared failure into a local one.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"circuit-breakers","key":"reliability/circuit-breakers","module":"reliability","name":"Circuit breakers","taxonomy_name":"circuit breakers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/fallbacks","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers"],"tldr":"When an LLM provider, vector database or tool API starts failing or timing out, every caller that keeps\ntrying ties up threads and connections waiting for answers that will not come, and the retries make\nrecovery harder. A circuit breaker counts recent failures and, past a threshold, fails fast for a\ncooling-off period, then lets a few trial calls through to test recovery. It trades a few wrongly\nrejected requests for keeping the caller alive and giving the dependency room to heal.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"bulkheads","key":"reliability/bulkheads","module":"reliability","name":"Bulkheads","taxonomy_name":"bulkheads","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"A ship's hull is divided into watertight compartments so one breach floods one section, not the vessel.\nA software bulkhead partitions a shared resource (worker threads, connections, semaphores, queues, pods)\nper dependency, workload class or tenant, so exhaustion in one compartment cannot drain what the others\nneed. Where a circuit breaker reacts after it detects failure, a bulkhead bounds the damage in advance,\nwhether or not anything detects it. The price is capacity that cannot be borrowed across compartments.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fmea","key":"reliability/fmea","module":"reliability","name":"FMEA (Failure Mode and Effects Analysis)","taxonomy_name":"FMEA","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"FMEA is a structured, tabular way to enumerate how each part of a system can fail (failure modes), what\nhappens when it does (effects), why (causes), how you would notice (detection), and to rank each by\nSeverity × Occurrence × Detection so the worst risks are fixed first. It comes from safety engineering.\nFor AI systems it is the cheapest way to find the failures nobody is monitoring: confident wrong answers,\nsilently stale retrieval, tool calls that succeed against the wrong record. It is a postmortem written\nbefore the incident.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","key":"reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","module":"reliability","name":"Chaos engineering & resilience testing","taxonomy_name":"chaos engineering & resilience testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/fmea","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/disaster-recovery","serving-inference/fallbacks","deployment/runbooks","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems"],"tldr":"Resilience mechanisms (fallbacks, breakers, retries, failover) run rarely and rot silently, so you do\nnot know whether they work until they are needed. Resilience testing exercises them deliberately in\npre-production: fault injection, load, stress and soak tests, failover drills, game days. Chaos\nengineering extends this to controlled experiments on real systems: state a steady-state hypothesis,\ninject a realistic fault with a bounded blast radius, measure whether it held. For AI systems the faults\ninclude provider 429s and latency, tool failures, stale retrieval and malformed model output.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"slas-slos","key":"reliability/slas-slos","module":"reliability","name":"SLAs / SLOs (and SLIs, error budgets)","taxonomy_name":"SLAs / SLOs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/fmea","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","observability/latency-percentiles","observability/monitoring","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"An SLI measures behavior users care about: the fraction of requests answered correctly within two\nseconds. An SLO is the internal target for that SLI over a window (99.5 percent over 30 days). An SLA\nis the external contract attaching consequences to a looser target. The gap between 100 percent and\nthe SLO is the error budget: reliability as a quantity you can spend on shipping. For AI systems the\nhard part is choosing SLIs that capture \"the answer was useful\", not just \"the request returned 200\".","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"agent-runtime-architecture","tier":2,"order":18,"title":"Agent runtime architecture","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the parts of an agent system that sit around the model rather than inside it. It starts\nwith the runtime and orchestration layer: the loop of call model, dispatch tool, record result, rebuilt as a\nlong-lived, crash-safe unit of work that persists before it executes, pauses for approvals without holding a\nthread, and coordinates sub-agents, queues and budgets. Every later topic plugs a cross-cutting rule into\nthat layer's dispatch boundary. Approval and authorization boundaries decide whether a principal may take an\naction at all and whether a human must confirm this exact instance, with the approval bound to the precise\narguments. Policy enforcement generalises that check across many agents and tool servers by splitting one\npolicy decision point from many enforcement points and attenuating authority down a delegation chain.\nMulti-tenant state isolation inventories every place tenant data lands (step logs, memory, vector indexes,\ncaches, sandboxes, traces) and enforces scoping structurally at each store. Secrets and credential isolation\nkeeps credentials out of the context window entirely, minting short-lived, per-tenant tokens inside a\nseparate executor. Cross-language API contracts make the tool schemas, event streams and checkpoints that\ncross Python, TypeScript and the model itself one generated, validated source of truth. After the module you\ncan draw a production agent runtime layer by layer, explain where each control lives and why it cannot live\nin the prompt, and test that it holds. It matters most for Architects and AI Engineers shipping agents that\ntake real actions for more than one customer, and for FDEs who must pass a customer's security review.","why":{"engineer":"You will write the dispatcher, the step store, the tool adapters and the schema generation step, so you need to know why state is persisted before a tool runs, how a run context with tenant and user identity is threaded through every adapter, and how to prove with a kill test and a canary test that resume and isolation actually work.","architect":"You own the decision of where each control lives: one policy decision point versus checks scattered in agents, storage-level tenant scoping versus remembered filters, a token broker versus secrets in environment variables, and a neutral contract versus hand-kept types; those choices decide auditability, blast radius and how cheaply the system can add agents.","fde":"Customer security teams will ask who the agent acts as, what it can touch, who approves irreversible actions, where their data and tokens live and how deletion works; you need to answer with the architecture, configure approval tiers and connected-account scopes for their environment, and demonstrate the leak and revocation tests on their tenant."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as an untrusted planner sitting inside a ring, and the runtime as the ring. The model can\nonly propose: a tool name and some arguments. Everything that turns a proposal into an effect happens on the\nruntime side of one boundary, the tool-dispatch point, and every concern in this module is a check stacked\non that boundary. Before a call runs, the runtime has already written the step to durable state, attached a\nsigned run context (tenant, user, delegated scope, provenance of the data in play) that the model never\nproduced, asked one policy engine for a decision, and, if the action is consequential, parked the run until a\nhuman approves those exact arguments. The call then executes in a separate executor that resolves a named\nconnection to a short-lived credential and writes only into stores scoped by that context. The data crossing\nevery arrow follows one generated schema. If an action can reach a side effect without crossing that\nboundary, the design has a hole.","order":["agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","approval-and-authorization-boundaries","policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","multi-tenant-state-isolation","secrets-and-credential-isolation","cross-language-api-contracts"],"order_reasons":{"agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer":"establishes the loop, durable step state and the dispatch boundary that every other control attaches to.","approval-and-authorization-boundaries":"the first and simplest check at that boundary: may this principal act, and does a human need to confirm this call.","policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools":"scales that single gate into a decision point with many enforcement points and attenuated delegation across agents.","multi-tenant-state-isolation":"applies the signed run context to every store the runtime writes, so tenant identity never comes from model output.","secrets-and-credential-isolation":"builds on tenant and user scoping to mint per-tenant, short-lived credentials the model can name but never see.","cross-language-api-contracts":"ties the layers together by making tool schemas, events, run context and checkpoints one validated contract across languages and the model."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a small multi-tenant agent runtime for a fictional helpdesk product in which an agent can read tickets,\nsearch a knowledge base, and take two consequential actions: issue a refund through a mock payments API and\nemail a customer through a mock mail API. Two tenants, each with two users of different roles, share the\ndeployment. The loop runs as a durable worker, not inside a request handler: each step is persisted before\nits tool executes, and killing the worker mid-run must resume without repeating a refund. Every tool call\npasses through one policy decision point that combines the user's rights with the agent's delegated scope;\nrefunds above a threshold and all outbound email park the run for approval bound to a fingerprint of the\narguments, with a TTL and re-authorization on resume. A planner agent may spawn a research sub-agent whose\nscope is attenuated from its parent. Tenant identity comes only from authentication and scopes the step store,\nthe vector index, a semantic cache and traces at the storage layer. Credentials for the mock APIs live in a\nvault-backed broker in a separate executor process. Tool definitions, run events and the run context are\ndefined once in a schema that generates both the Python runtime types and a TypeScript client that streams\nrun events to a minimal approvals UI.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the durable worker, step store, policy decision point, tool executor process, token broker and approvals UI, started with one command against local mock services.","An architecture diagram of the layers and trust zones, plus a one-page surface inventory listing every store that holds tenant data and the storage-level mechanism that scopes it.","A `contracts/` directory with the shared schema, generated Python and TypeScript types, and a CI step that regenerates and fails on diff.","A test suite covering a forced-kill resume, a cross-tenant canary leak test across every listed store, a prompt-injected document that attempts an unapproved refund and email exfiltration, and an environment and filesystem scan proving no secret is reachable from the loop or sandbox.","A short write-up with measured steps per run, model versus tool time, policy-gate coverage, approval and reject rates per tool, and token TTL and revocation-to-last-use time."],"rubric":["Killing the worker at any step and restarting it completes the run with exactly one refund recorded in the mock payments API, shown by a test that repeats the kill at several points.","Every tool invocation in the step log has a matching logged policy decision (coverage is 100%), and a sub-agent's effective scope is provably a subset of its parent's; a test that asks the sub-agent to exceed it is denied.","Changing any argument of an approved refund after approval invalidates it, an expired approval cannot be used, and the injected-document test produces a denied or parked call, never an executed one.","The canary test seeds a unique string in tenant A in every store and confirms tenant B's runs, searches, cache hits and trace queries never return it; no tool accepts `tenant_id` as a model-supplied argument.","No credential value appears in prompts, tool arguments, traces, step logs or the sandbox environment, and revoking a tenant's connection stops its next tool call within the measured window.","Renaming a field in the schema without regenerating breaks CI, and the TypeScript client tolerates an unknown new event type without throwing."]}},"topics":[{"id":"agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Agent runtime & orchestration layer","taxonomy_name":"agent runtime & orchestration layer","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/agent-orchestration","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools"],"tldr":"An agent is a loop: call the model, execute what it asked for, feed the result back. The runtime owns that\nloop as a long-lived, crash-safe, policy-checked unit of work instead of a function in a request handler;\nthe orchestration layer above it coordinates many runs (sub-agents, handoffs, queues, budgets). Both exist\nbecause loop state must outlive any process, tool calls have side effects, and every cross-cutting rule\n(approval, tenancy, secrets) needs one enforcement point.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"approval-and-authorization-boundaries","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Approval & authorization boundaries","taxonomy_name":"approval & authorization boundaries","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/human-in-the-loop","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools"],"tldr":"Authorization answers \"may this principal perform this action at all\"; approval answers \"does a human need\nto confirm this particular instance\". Both must be decided by the runtime at the tool-dispatch boundary,\noutside the model's influence, because model output is untrusted input: a prompt-injected or hallucinated\ntool call carries exactly as much authority as a legitimate one. The agent acts with the *intersection* of\nthe user's rights and its own delegated scope, and an approval is bound to the exact arguments it approved.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Policy enforcement across agents & tools","taxonomy_name":"policy enforcement across agents & tools","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/multi-agent-patterns","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","system-design/ai-gateway","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"Once a system has several agents, several tool servers and several entry points, rules written into each\nagent's prompt or code drift apart and are bypassed by whichever path forgot them. The fix is the classic\nsplit between a single policy decision point and many enforcement points: every hop (runtime dispatch,\ntool gateway, model gateway, egress) asks the same engine, passes the same signed run context, and a child\nagent's authority is only ever attenuated from its parent's, never widened.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-tenant-state-isolation","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Multi-tenant state isolation","taxonomy_name":"multi-tenant state isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/tenant-isolation","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","agents/agent-memory","agents/sandboxing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"An agent runtime scatters tenant data across far more places than a CRUD app does: step logs, long-term\nmemory, retrieval indexes, uploaded files, code sandboxes, semantic and tool caches, traces and queues.\nCross-tenant leakage happens in whichever of those a filter was forgotten. Isolation therefore has to be\nstructural: tenant identity derived from authentication and carried in the run context, never from model\noutput, and enforced at the storage layer of every surface rather than remembered in application code.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"secrets-and-credential-isolation","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Secrets & credential isolation","taxonomy_name":"secrets & credential isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","agents/sandboxing","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation"],"tldr":"Treat the model as an untrusted component that will eventually repeat anything in its context. Secrets\ntherefore never enter the context window, the sandbox, or model-filled tool arguments. The model names a\nconnection; a tool executor in a separate process resolves that name to a short-lived, narrowly scoped\ncredential at call time from a vault or token broker, uses it, and never returns it. Credentials are stored\nand minted per tenant and per user, so one tenant's token can never serve another's run.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cross-language-api-contracts","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Cross-language API contracts","taxonomy_name":"cross-language API contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","python-for-ai/pydantic","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"Agent systems are polyglot by construction: Python for the loop, TypeScript for the product and tool\nservers, Go or Java behind them, and the model itself consuming JSON Schema. Tool definitions, event streams\nand run state cross those boundaries constantly. A contract is one machine-readable schema every side\ngenerates its types and runtime validators from, with explicit rules for what JSON leaves ambiguous (64-bit\nintegers, dates, null versus absent, unknown enum values) and for evolving without breaking old consumers.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"data-architecture","tier":2,"order":19,"title":"Data architecture","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the data side of an AI system: how bytes leave source systems, become retrieval\nunits, training examples and features, and how you can later say where each one came from and remove\nit when asked. It starts at the edge. Ingestion pipelines capture changes from sources (deletes\nincluded) into an immutable, replayable raw copy with a committed watermark, and the lakehouse gives\nthat copy and every derived table atomic commits, snapshots, time travel and real deletes on cheap\nobject storage. Staged ELT then turns raw data into materialized parse, chunk, enrich and embed tables,\ndocument processing pipelines route each file to the cheapest extractor that keeps its structure, and\nknowledge management decides which sources are canonical, who owns them and how documents age out.\nData contracts make producers responsible for the schema and meaning their consumers rely on. On the\nserving side, embedding versioning treats the model, its settings and the chunker as part of an index's\nidentity, vector database architecture explains mutability, filtered search and memory residency, and\nvector store sizing turns a corpus into a RAM and cost number before you choose a vendor. Training data\npipelines produce deduplicated, decontaminated, versioned shards. Lineage, provenance and retention\ntie everything together: a recorded graph of what was built from what, tamper-evident manifests for\nmodels and datasets, and a deletion that reaches every copy with proof. Feature stores close the module\nfor hybrid systems that also run classical models. Afterwards you can design a RAG and fine-tuning\ndata platform that re-indexes without downtime, survives upstream schema changes, and honours an\nerasure request across every store. It matters most for Architects, and for Engineers who own the\npipelines.","why":{"engineer":"You write the change capture, the content-hashed stage tables, the extraction router, the alias swap and the deletion job, so stale chunks, re-embedding bills that scale with the corpus and documents that are never removed are bugs in your pipeline code.","architect":"You decide what is the system of record and what is a rebuildable projection, how an index version is identified, where lineage is emitted and how deletion propagates, and these choices are a full rebuild to change once the first index is live.","fde":"Customer corpora are messy, mixed-format and unowned, and their first hard questions are about cost, freshness and erasure; you answer with a sizing estimate, a canonical-source agreement, a contract on their feeds and a deletion drill run on their own data."},"mental_model":"Picture one directed graph that runs left to right. On the left are source systems; the first node is an\nimmutable raw copy keyed by source identity and content hash. Every node after it is a derived,\nversioned table produced by a job: parsed blocks, chunks, embeddings, training shards, features. On the\nright are serving projections: a vector index, an online feature store, a fine-tuned checkpoint. Three\nrules hold everywhere. Only the raw copy is the system of record; everything to its right can be\nrebuilt, so a new chunker or embedding model is a re-run into a new version beside the old one, followed\nby an evaluated swap. Every edge is recorded by the job that walked it, which is lineage, and every\nartifact that reaches a registry carries a hashed manifest, which is provenance. And every edge carries\na contract and a retention class, so a schema change fails loudly at the producer and a deletion request\nis a forward traversal from one source record to every node derived from it. Contracts and knowledge\nmanagement govern what enters on the left; sizing and vector architecture govern what it costs on the\nright.","order":["ingestion-pipelines","lakehouse","etl-elt-for-rag","document-processing-pipelines","knowledge-management","data-contracts","embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","vector-database-architecture","vector-store-sizing","training-data-pipelines","data-lineage","model-and-dataset-provenance","data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","feature-stores"],"order_reasons":{"ingestion-pipelines":"everything downstream depends on an incremental, delete-aware, replayable raw copy, so getting bytes out of sources comes first.","lakehouse":"gives the raw copy and the stage tables transactional commits, snapshots and real deletes, which the later lessons assume.","etl-elt-for-rag":"establishes the staged, content-addressed transformation DAG and the rule that the index is a projection, not the source.","document-processing-pipelines":"fills in the parse stage of that DAG with tiered routing, quality scoring and a canonical block model.","knowledge-management":"decides which sources are canonical and what ownership and freshness metadata the ingestion schema must carry.","data-contracts":"makes producers responsible for the schema and meaning the pipeline stages now depend on, catching silent breakage at write time.","embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy":"treats model, settings and chunker as the index's identity and teaches build-beside, evaluate and alias swap over the stored chunks.","vector-database-architecture":"explains how the serving index handles updates, deletes, selective filters, memory residency and sharding.","vector-store-sizing":"turns the corpus, chunking and embedding choices into a RAM and cost estimate, using the index mechanics just covered.","training-data-pipelines":"applies the same staged, versioned approach to fine-tuning data, adding dedup, decontamination and packing.","data-lineage":"records the graph of inputs, jobs and versions across both the RAG and training paths built so far.","model-and-dataset-provenance":"adds hashed, signed manifests for registry artifacts on top of lineage, so models and datasets can be verified.","data-retention-and-deletion-architecture":"uses the store inventory, lineage and versioned tables to propagate an erasure request with receipts.","feature-stores":"an optional extension for hybrid systems that also train and serve classical models, best read once the rest is in place."},"capstone":{"brief":"A mid-sized software company wants an internal assistant over its help-centre articles (HTML), a\nshared drive of policy PDFs (some scanned, some with tables) and a Postgres table of resolved support\ntickets, plus a small fine-tuned classifier that routes new tickets. Today a nightly script re-crawls\neverything, embeds it into one vector collection and never removes deleted documents. Over a week,\nrebuild it as a data platform. Ingest each source incrementally into an immutable raw table with\ncontent hashes and tombstones, and run a staged ELT DAG (parse, clean, chunk, embed) on lakehouse or\nPostgres tables, with a tiered document router that escalates to OCR only when a quality score fails.\nTag canonical sources and owners, and put a data contract with a CI compatibility check on the ticket\nfeed. Version the embedding recipe, build a second index with a different chunker beside the first,\nevaluate both on a fixed query set and swap an alias. Size the store before choosing pgvector or a\ndedicated engine. Build a deduplicated, eval-decontaminated training set for the classifier with a\nmanifest hash, record lineage for every job, and finish with a deletion drill for one customer.","deliverables":["A pipeline repository with per-source change capture, a raw landing table keyed by source id and content hash, a committed watermark, and stage tables for parsed blocks, chunks and embeddings that a second run fills incrementally.","A document-routing report showing per-tier volume, cost, quality-score distribution and escalation rate, with extraction accuracy measured on a small hand-labelled gold set.","A data contract file for the ticket feed with a CI check that rejects a breaking change, plus a source registry listing canonical status, owner and `last_verified_at` for each source.","A sizing worksheet with explicit assumptions (chunks per document from a sample, dimension, precision, graph overhead, payload, replicas, rebuild headroom) and the resulting store choice, and an index-version record with eval recall for both index versions and the alias swap.","A training-set manifest for the routing classifier giving dataset hash, removal counts per stage, overlap rate with the eval set and base model revision, and a lineage table that connects every chunk and training row to its source record and job run.","A deletion runbook and a drill report for one customer, listing every store reached, a receipt per store and the time taken, with an explicit statement about any fine-tuned weights."],"rubric":["Deleting a policy PDF at the source removes its chunks from the live index on the next run, and a second run with no source changes re-embeds zero chunks, shown by the per-run change ratio and embedding cache hit rate.","Renaming a field in the ticket feed fails the producer's CI contract check before any data lands, rather than producing empty chunks or blank training targets downstream.","Live queries are served from exactly one index version throughout the re-index, the swap is a single alias change that can be reversed, and both versions report recall on the same fixed query set.","The sizing estimate is within a stated margin of the store's measured memory after loading, and the worksheet names quantization and payload placement as the levers with their effect on the number.","Starting from one customer id, a lineage traversal lists every derived chunk, cache entry, eval row and training row, and the drill report shows each of them deleted or tombstoned with a receipt.","The classifier's training config references the dataset manifest hash, and re-running the training-data pipeline from the same raw snapshot reproduces that hash."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ingestion-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Ingestion pipelines","taxonomy_name":"ingestion pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-lineage","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"An ingestion pipeline is the path from source systems (databases, SaaS tools, file shares, event streams)\ninto a durable, replayable landing store that your AI system can transform from. The design that survives\nproduction is incremental change capture (including deletes), an immutable raw copy keyed by source\nidentity and content hash, and idempotent stages with a committed watermark, because sources change\ncontinuously and every downstream choice (parser, chunker, embedding model) will be redone more than once.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"etl-elt-for-rag","key":"data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","module":"data-architecture","name":"ETL / ELT for RAG","taxonomy_name":"ETL / ELT for RAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","data-architecture/lakehouse","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","system-design/rag-architecture"],"tldr":"ETL transforms data before loading it into the target; ELT loads raw data first and transforms inside or\nbeside the target. For RAG the right shape is ELT into a lake or landing store followed by a staged\ntransformation DAG (parse → clean → chunk → enrich → embed → index) whose stages are materialized,\ncontent-addressed tables, because the transformations change far more often than the sources do and\nre-transforming from raw is the operation you will run most. The vector index is a derived, rebuildable\nserving structure, never the system of record.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"training-data-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Training data pipelines","taxonomy_name":"training data pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-labeling","evals/dataset-contamination","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/lakehouse","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"A training data pipeline turns raw sources into versioned, deduplicated, decontaminated, token-ready\nshards with full provenance. Its architecture matters because what you *remove* (duplicates, eval\nleakage, junk, PII) shapes the model at least as much as what you add, because every run must be\nreproducible from a dataset hash, and because tokenizing, packing and globally shuffling raw text at\ntraining time can starve the GPUs. This lesson covers the pipeline; the curation, labeling and synthetic-data lessons in Training &\nadaptation cover what makes an individual example good.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"lakehouse","key":"data-architecture/lakehouse","module":"data-architecture","name":"Lakehouse","taxonomy_name":"lakehouse","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","backend-fundamentals/database-design","system-design/data-consistency","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"A lakehouse is a transactional table layer (Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Apache Hudi) over open columnar\nfiles (Parquet) in object storage. It keeps the cheap, engine-agnostic storage of a data lake and adds\nwhat a lake lacks: atomic commits, snapshot isolation, schema enforcement and evolution, time travel and\nreal deletes. For AI systems it is the natural home for raw documents, chunk and embedding tables, training\ndatasets and trace logs, because many different engines need the same data and none of them should own it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-processing-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Document processing pipelines","taxonomy_name":"document processing pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/document-parsing","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval"],"tldr":"A document processing pipeline turns heterogeneous files (PDF, Office, HTML, email, scans, images) into\none canonical structured representation: ordered blocks with type, text, page, position and hierarchy,\nplus tables as cells. The architecture is a tiered router: detect what each document really is, send it\nto the cheapest extractor that preserves its structure, score the output, and escalate (native text →\nOCR → layout model or vision LLM) only when the score fails, because extraction quality bounds every\ndownstream step and the expensive extractors cannot be run on everything.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-database-architecture","key":"data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","module":"data-architecture","name":"Vector database architecture","taxonomy_name":"vector database architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"A vector database is three things bolted together: an approximate-nearest-neighbour index, a document\nstore for ids and payload, and a query planner that combines similarity with filters. The hard problems\nare not the distance math but mutability (graph indexes hate updates and deletes), filtered search\n(selective filters break \"find top-k then filter\"), memory residency (graph traversal is random access,\nso the index lives in RAM or on an SSD-aware design) and distribution. Understand those four and the\nvendor comparison in the RAG module becomes a checklist.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-store-sizing","key":"data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","module":"data-architecture","name":"Vector store sizing","taxonomy_name":"vector store sizing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation"],"tldr":"Vector store cost is set by RAM-resident bytes: `vectors × (dim × bytes_per_dim + graph_overhead +\npayload)`, multiplied by replicas and a headroom factor for index builds. Every input is knowable\nbefore you pick a vendor tier: vector count from documents and chunking, dimension and precision from the\nembedding model and quantization, payload from what you choose to store beside the vector. Do the\narithmetic first; it routinely flips the decision between pgvector on an existing database and a\ndedicated cluster, and it is the same discipline as KV-cache sizing in serving.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","key":"data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","module":"data-architecture","name":"Embedding versioning / re-indexing strategy","taxonomy_name":"embedding versioning / re-indexing strategy","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"A vector is only meaningful relative to the model that produced it: two embedding models, or two\nversions of one, put the same text at unrelated points in space. So the model, its settings and the\nchunker are part of the index's identity, and changing any of them means re-embedding everything. The\nstrategy: version that identity explicitly, build the new index beside the old, evaluate, swap atomically,\nand hash content so routine runs re-embed only changed chunks.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-management","key":"data-architecture/knowledge-management","module":"data-architecture","name":"Knowledge management","taxonomy_name":"knowledge management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"A retrieval system cannot answer better than the corpus it searches, and most enterprise corpora are\nstale, duplicated, contradictory and unowned. Knowledge management is the practice of deciding which\nsources are canonical, who owns each, how documents enter, age and leave the index, and what metadata\ntravels with them, so that the retriever is choosing among correct candidates. In enterprise RAG, a\nlarge share of \"the model hallucinated\" reports trace back to this layer, not to the model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-lineage","key":"data-architecture/data-lineage","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data lineage","taxonomy_name":"data lineage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","observability/tracing","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"Data lineage is the recorded graph of where every dataset, chunk, feature and model came from: which\ninputs, which job, which code version, which run. It exists because the questions that matter in\nproduction (\"why did this answer change\", \"which indexes contain this customer's data\", \"what was this\nmodel trained on\") are graph traversals, and without a recorded graph the only way to answer them is to\nread code and guess. In AI systems, lineage is what makes deletion, debugging and reproducibility\npossible at all.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-contracts","key":"data-architecture/data-contracts","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data contracts","taxonomy_name":"data contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agents/typed-tool-contracts"],"tldr":"A data contract is an explicit, versioned, machine-checked agreement between the team that produces a\ndataset and the teams that consume it: schema, meaning, quality guarantees, freshness and ownership. It\nexists because AI pipelines are the most fragile consumers in the company: a renamed column or a changed\nunit does not crash an embedding job or a training run, it quietly produces a worse model or index. The\ncontract moves the failure to the producer's side, at write time, where it is loud and cheap.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-and-dataset-provenance","key":"data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","module":"data-architecture","name":"Model & dataset provenance","taxonomy_name":"model & dataset provenance","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/data-lineage","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","llmops-platform/model-registry","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","evals/dataset-contamination","training-adaptation/data-curation"],"tldr":"Provenance is the verifiable record of where a model or dataset came from: the exact bytes it was built\nfrom, the code and parameters that built it, who built it, under which licence, and the evaluations that\nwere run on it. It exists because a model file is opaque: nothing in a checkpoint tells you what data it\nsaw, whether that data was licensed or contained your eval set, or whether the file is even the one you\nthink it is. Provenance turns \"trust me\" into hashes, manifests and signatures.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","key":"data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data retention & deletion architecture","taxonomy_name":"data retention & deletion architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["data-architecture/data-lineage","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","safety-security/pii-redaction","serving-inference/caching"],"tldr":"An LLM application copies user data into far more places than a conventional service: prompt logs,\ntraces, vector indexes, caches, eval sets, fine-tuning data, vendor-side logs and model weights. Retention\nand deletion architecture is the design that knows every one of those places, assigns each a retention\nclass and a time-to-live, and can execute a deletion request across all of them with proof. It has to be\ndesigned in, because a deletion you cannot propagate is a promise you cannot keep.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"feature-stores","key":"data-architecture/feature-stores","module":"data-architecture","name":"Feature stores (hybrid ML only)","taxonomy_name":"feature stores (hybrid ML only)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/lakehouse","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-lineage","llmops-platform/drift","llmops-platform/model-registry","system-design/data-consistency"],"tldr":"Caveat first: feature stores matter only in hybrid systems that train and serve classical ML models\n(ranking, fraud, churn, routing classifiers) alongside LLM components; pure LLM-application roles can skim\nthis and move on. A feature store is the layer that computes model input features once, serves the same\nvalues to training (historically, point-in-time correct) and to inference (now, at low latency), and\nversions their definitions, because training/serving skew and time leakage are the two bugs that quietly\nruin tabular models.","caveat":"hybrid ML only; drop for LLM-app roles","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"infra-capacity-economics","tier":2,"order":20,"title":"Infra, capacity & economics","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the physical and financial reality under every AI feature: where the model\nruns, how much of it you need, and whether the result pays for itself. It starts with the hardware.\nGPU infrastructure is the stack from card to driver to scheduler. GPU sizing turns a model config into\nbytes of weights and KV cache. The roofline explains why decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and why\n\"GPU-Util\" lies. Card selection asks which constraint binds first: fit, bandwidth or cost per token.\nNext comes capacity: queueing theory and Little's law predict the latency knee, capacity planning\nconverts token distributions and an SLO into replicas or quota, latency budgets split one target\nacross every hop, and the Universal Scalability Law shows where adding replicas stops helping. The\ndeployment lessons cover inference clusters, KEDA, serverless GPUs, Ray, shared versus isolated\nserving, provider quotas, cloud AI platforms, hybrid on-prem and edge. The economics lessons build\ncost models, cost per successful task, allocation by tenant and feature, FinOps, ordered optimization,\nthe quality-latency-cost frontier, break-even and TCO. Afterwards you can size a deployment, defend a\nreplica count or quota request, choose between API, self-hosted, hybrid and edge with numbers, and\nshow what a successful task costs and who caused the cost. It matters most for Architects. Engineers\nneed it to run serving and metering, and FDEs need it for customer sizing and pricing.","why":{"engineer":"You derive KV bytes per token from the model config before an OOM does it for you, read DCGM SM and DRAM activity instead of GPU-Util, scale inference replicas on queue depth and KV usage through KEDA rather than CPU, and emit one tagged cost event per model call so spend reaches the right tenant and feature.","architect":"You turn token histograms and a p95 TTFT target into a replica count with headroom below the queueing knee, choose card, region, quota and shared or isolated pools on the binding constraint, and back every build-versus-buy or on-prem decision with a break-even band and a TCO that includes people, evals and exit cost.","fde":"You size a customer's deployment from their real document lengths and peak hour, file quota increases weeks before launch, place the endpoint inside their Bedrock, Azure or on-prem perimeter, and show the sponsor cost per successful task against the task's value, so the pricing and ROI conversation rests on their numbers."},"mental_model":"Picture one pipe with three gauges. Requests enter at the left as tokens, not calls, and every\nrequest has a shape: input length, output length, turns and context. The pipe's width is set by\nphysical limits: GPU memory decides how many sequences fit at once, memory bandwidth decides how fast\neach one decodes, and provider quotas cap what a hosted pool will give you. Queueing theory says what\nhappens as the pipe fills: wait time stays flat and then jumps at the knee, so you run below it on\npurpose and buy headroom. The three gauges on the pipe are quality, latency and cost, and every lever\nmoves at least two of them. Batching, quantisation, smaller models, caching, serverless and shared\npools all trade one gauge against another. Underneath sits a ledger that divides all spend, including\nfailures, retries and idle capacity, by tasks that succeeded, and assigns each dollar to a tenant and\nfeature. Infrastructure decisions are sizing the pipe, and economic decisions are reading the ledger.\nNeither is right unless both agree.","order":["gpu-infrastructure","gpu-sizing","gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","a100-h100-l4-selection","queueing-theory","capacity-planning","latency-budgets","scalability","inference-clusters","keda-autoscaling","serverless-gpus","ray-kuberay","shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","cloud-ai-architecture","hybrid-on-prem","edge-ai-deployment","cost-modeling","unit-economics-per-successful-task","cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","finops","cost-optimization","quality-latency-cost-optimization","break-even-analysis","tco"],"order_reasons":{"gpu-infrastructure":"the layer map (card, host, driver, runtime, scheduler, procurement) that every later hardware lesson plugs into.","gpu-sizing":"the weights-plus-KV-cache arithmetic that decides card count, precision and promised concurrency.","gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth":"the roofline explains why decode is bandwidth-bound, which sizing alone does not show.","a100-h100-l4-selection":"applies fit, bandwidth and cost per token from the previous two lessons to a concrete card choice.","queueing-theory":"Little's law and the utilization knee, the reason no capacity plan targets \"average load fits\".","capacity-planning":"combines queueing with measured per-replica capacity to turn token demand and an SLO into replicas or quota.","latency-budgets":"splits the user-facing target across every hop, so capacity work knows how much time the model actually gets.","scalability":"the USL shows where adding replicas stops paying off and which shared resource is to blame.","inference-clusters":"the replica fleet, router and LLM-specific scaling signals that put the capacity plan into practice.","keda-autoscaling":"the Kubernetes mechanism that scales those replicas on queue depth and to zero, and its cold-start limits.","serverless-gpus":"the per-second alternative for bursty load, decided by utilization break-even and cold-start tolerance.","ray-kuberay":"the distributed Python runtime for heterogeneous batch, embedding and multi-node workloads a plain Deployment handles poorly.","shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure":"when one batched pool serves every tenant and when noisy neighbours or contracts force isolation.","api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits":"capacity planning on hosted APIs is quota planning, with 429s, headroom and reserved throughput.","cloud-ai-architecture":"places a managed model endpoint inside a cloud's identity, network and per-region quota perimeter.","hybrid-on-prem":"routes restricted data to owned GPUs and the rest to hosted APIs, and sizes the fixed pool so it stays busy.","edge-ai-deployment":"the far end of the placement spectrum, sizing small models to device memory and bandwidth with a cloud fallback.","cost-modeling":"turns request shape (fresh, cached, output and reasoning tokens, plus agent turns) into dollars from logged distributions.","unit-economics-per-successful-task":"divides the full cost, failures included, by tasks that met the bar, the number every later decision uses.","cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature":"assigns direct and shared spend to whoever caused it, so unit economics exist per tenant.","finops":"the ongoing inform–optimize–operate practice that keeps metering, allocation and budgets running after the first analysis.","cost-optimization":"the ordered list of levers (avoid, shrink, route, buy cheaper, tune), each gated by an eval.","quality-latency-cost-optimization":"chooses from the Pareto frontier across all three gauges instead of optimizing cost alone.","break-even-analysis":"the API-versus-self-host crossover, driven by utilization and team cost rather than GPU list price.","tco":"the widest frame, adding people, eval upkeep, forced migrations and exit cost over a fixed horizon to every option."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the architect for a B2B SaaS company whose contract-review assistant extracts clauses and\nflags risks in uploaded agreements. It runs on a hosted frontier API in one region and costs $48k a\nmonth. Three things have just happened: the largest tenant, 40% of volume, now requires EU data\nresidency; the provider's tokens-per-minute quota throttled the Monday morning peak twice last\nmonth; and finance wants to know whether self-hosting an open-weight 70B model would be cheaper.\nOver a week, using a synthetic request log with realistic input and output token histograms, tenant\nIDs, timestamps and task outcomes (generate it or use the one provided), produce a decision that holds\nup under review. Size the model on candidate cards, build the capacity plan and latency budget, model\ncost per successful task by tenant, compare hosted, self-hosted and hybrid options on break-even and\nTCO, and recommend a serving shape with its scaling and quota plan.","deliverables":["A sizing sheet for the 70B candidate showing weights and KV bytes per token derived from the model config, concurrency per card at the p95 context length on L4, A100 and H100 at bf16 and fp8, and the roofline time per output token compared with a measured or published figure.","A capacity plan that converts the log's peak hour into requests/s, input and output tokens/s and in-flight sequences via Little's law, sets per-replica capacity at the TTFT and TPOT SLO, gives a replica count with headroom justified by queueing, and converts the hosted path into TPM and RPM per region with a quota-increase request.","A latency budget for the review path (upload, parsing, retrieval, guardrail, model queue and prefill, decode) with per-hop p95 allocations, a reserve, and the steps that degrade under deadline pressure.","A cost model and allocation report giving cost per successful task by tenant and feature, with retries, failed extractions and human review included, shared GPU cost allocated by GPU-seconds, and the unattributed share stated.","A break-even and TCO comparison of hosted API, self-hosted and hybrid (EU tenant on owned or regional capacity, the rest hosted) over 24 months, with a sensitivity table on utilization, team cost and an API price cut.","A serving design with an autoscaling configuration (KEDA or equivalent) keyed on queue depth and KV usage, a shared-versus-isolated decision for the large tenant, and a one-page recommendation that names the premium paid for the chosen option."],"rubric":["Concurrency per card is derived from bytes per token and free memory after weights, and the engine's KV-block count, or a published figure for the same engine, is within about 20% of the estimate, or the gap is explained.","The replica count targets a utilization below the queueing knee with the wait-time consequence shown, and plans for peak demand rather than the daily average.","Every cost figure is per successful task rather than per call, and at least one option that looks cheaper per call is shown to cost more once failures and retries are counted.","Break-even is reported as a band with the assumption that flips the recommendation named explicitly, and the TCO includes people, eval upkeep and migration or exit cost.","The autoscaling rule never uses CPU or GPU-Util as its signal, and its threshold comes from a measured per-replica capacity curve.","The residency requirement is enforced at the gateway by data classification, and the large tenant's isolation decision cites a measured noisy-neighbour effect or a contract term, not preference."]}},"topics":[{"id":"gpu-infrastructure","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU infrastructure","taxonomy_name":"GPU infrastructure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"A GPU deployment is a stack, not a card: the accelerator and its memory, the host around it, the\ndriver and CUDA libraries, the container runtime that exposes the device, the scheduler that assigns\nit, and the procurement model that pays for it. Each layer fails differently, and most \"the GPU is\nslow\" tickets are really host, driver or scheduling problems. This lesson is the map; sizing,\nbandwidth, card selection, serverless and autoscaling each have their own lesson.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-sizing","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU sizing (VRAM math)","taxonomy_name":"GPU sizing (VRAM math)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection"],"tldr":"GPU memory for inference is weights + KV cache + activations/workspace + runtime overhead; for\ntraining it is weights + gradients + optimizer state + activations, about 16 bytes per parameter with\nmixed-precision Adam before activations are counted. The arithmetic takes a minute and decides the\ncard, the card count, the precision and the concurrency you can promise. Skipping it is how both\nout-of-memory crashes and half-empty 80 GB cards happen.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU utilization & memory bandwidth","taxonomy_name":"GPU utilization & memory bandwidth","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/kv-cache","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"The \"GPU-Util\" number in `nvidia-smi` only says a kernel was running, not that the chip was busy.\nReal throughput is bounded by the roofline: for every byte moved from GPU memory the chip can do a\nfixed number of floating-point operations, roughly 150 on an A100 and 300 on an H100 at bf16. LLM\ndecode does about one operation per bf16 weight byte per sequence, so small batches are memory-bandwidth-bound and the fix is batching, fewer bytes or faster memory, not more FLOPs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"a100-h100-l4-selection","key":"infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"A100 / H100 / L4 selection","taxonomy_name":"A100 / H100 / L4 selection","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/tco","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","training-adaptation/quantization"],"tldr":"Choose a card by the constraint that binds first: does the model fit (memory capacity), is per-stream\ndecode fast enough (memory bandwidth), and what does a token cost at the utilisation you will\nactually run (price ÷ throughput ceiling). The L4 is a cheap, 24 GB, low-bandwidth card for small\nmodels and light traffic; the A100 is the mature 40/80 GB workhorse without FP8; the H100 is the\n80 GB, ~3.35 TB/s, FP8-capable card that wins per token at high load and for training.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"serverless-gpus","key":"infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Serverless GPUs","taxonomy_name":"serverless GPUs","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"Serverless GPUs bill per second of actual use and scale to zero, so a bursty or low-volume workload\npays only for busy seconds instead of for a card that idles most of the day. The price is a cold start\nof tens of seconds to minutes (pull the image, load weights into VRAM, warm the engine) and a\nper-second rate above on-demand hourly pricing. Decide on utilisation break-even shifted by cold-start\noverhead, gated by whether your latency SLO can absorb a cold start at all.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"keda-autoscaling","key":"infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"KEDA autoscaling","taxonomy_name":"KEDA autoscaling","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/ttft","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding"],"tldr":"KEDA (Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling) lets a Deployment or Job scale on the signal that actually\nmeasures demand, such as queue depth, in-flight requests or a Prometheus query, and lets it scale to zero.\nGPU inference needs this because CPU and GPU utilisation are poor demand signals for LLM servers. KEDA\nmoves pod counts only; nodes still come from the cluster autoscaler, and GPU cold starts still take minutes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ray-kuberay","key":"infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Ray / KubeRay","taxonomy_name":"Ray / KubeRay","kind":"tool","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/model-serving","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Ray is a Python framework that turns functions and classes into distributed tasks and stateful actors,\nscheduled across a cluster by declared resources such as GPUs. It exists because AI pipelines mix CPU and\nGPU stages and need fine-grained, stateful parallelism that Spark or job queues handle poorly. KubeRay is\nthe Kubernetes operator that runs Ray clusters, jobs and Serve applications as custom resources.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"inference-clusters","key":"infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Inference clusters","taxonomy_name":"inference clusters","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"An inference cluster is a fleet of model-server replicas (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Triton) behind a\nrouter, scheduled onto GPU nodes, scaled on LLM-specific signals. It exists because a single GPU box\ncaps out on memory and bandwidth long before real traffic does, and because LLM requests are\nlong-lived, variable-length and stateful (the KV cache), which breaks the stateless web-tier playbook\nof round-robin load balancing and CPU-based autoscaling.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","key":"infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Shared vs isolated model-serving infrastructure","taxonomy_name":"shared vs isolated model-serving infrastructure","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/tenant-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","reliability/bulkheads","serving-inference/continuous-batching","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"Shared serving puts every tenant's or team's traffic through one pool of model replicas; isolated\nserving gives a tenant its own replicas, nodes, cluster or account. Sharing wins on cost because\ncontinuous batching needs many concurrent requests to fill a GPU and because tenants' peaks rarely\ncoincide. Isolation wins when a tenant can hurt others (noisy neighbour), must be protected from\nothers (compliance, data separation) or needs a different model configuration. The decision is a\nspectrum, not a switch, and most platforms end up with both.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cloud-ai-architecture","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cloud AI architecture (AWS Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI)","taxonomy_name":"cloud AI architecture (AWS Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","deployment/private-endpoints","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Bedrock, Azure OpenAI and Vertex AI are one idea on three clouds: frontier and open-weight models\nserved as a managed API *inside* your cloud account's identity, network, logging and billing\nperimeter. Enterprises pick them over a vendor's public API because procurement, compliance and\nnetworking are already solved there, and pay with model lag, per-region quotas and a second layer\nof lock-in. The architecture work is placing the endpoint inside that perimeter and planning\ncapacity per region.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","key":"infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"API provider quotas & regional limits","taxonomy_name":"API provider quotas & regional limits","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/fallbacks","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Hosted model APIs are shared pools, so every provider caps what one account may take: requests and\ntokens per minute (often input and output separately), per model, per region, per account, rising\nwith spend tier. Exceed them and you get 429s; when the pool itself is full you get overload errors\nregardless of quota. Capacity planning on an API is therefore quota planning: measure demand in the\nprovider's units, keep headroom, spread across regions where residency allows, and reserve capacity\nfor the base load.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hybrid-on-prem","key":"infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Hybrid / on-prem","taxonomy_name":"hybrid / on-prem","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/model-routing-cascading","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"Hybrid AI infrastructure runs some inference on hardware you control (your datacenter, a colo, a\ncustomer's VPC or an air-gapped site) and the rest on hosted model APIs, with a gateway routing each\nrequest by data classification, latency need and model need. It exists because some data cannot\nleave, some sites lack connectivity, and high steady volume can be cheaper on owned GPUs,\nwhile on-prem alone loses frontier models and elasticity. The hard parts are the routing policy, a\nuniform API surface, and keeping fixed capacity busy.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"edge-ai-deployment","key":"infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Edge AI deployment","taxonomy_name":"edge AI deployment","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","model-internals/small-language-models","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Edge AI runs the model on the device that has the data: a phone, laptop, browser, vehicle, factory\ncontroller or retail box. It exists for latency with no network round trip, operation when offline,\nprivacy because the data never leaves, and cost because the user's hardware does the compute. The\nprice is a hard budget on memory, bandwidth, power and model size, so edge means small or\ncompressed models on a device-specific runtime, usually with a cloud fallback for what the small\nmodel cannot do.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"capacity-planning","key":"infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Capacity planning (tokens/sec, requests/sec, concurrent sessions)","taxonomy_name":"capacity planning (tokens/sec, requests/sec, concurrent sessions)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"LLM capacity cannot be planned in requests per second alone, because one request can cost a hundred\ntimes more than another. You plan in three linked units: requests/sec (arrivals), tokens/sec split into\ninput and output (work), and concurrent sequences or sessions (what occupies GPU memory and quota).\nLittle's Law connects them, a load test at your latency SLO gives per-replica capacity, and replicas =\npeak demand ÷ capacity, plus headroom.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"queueing-theory","key":"infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Queueing theory","taxonomy_name":"queueing theory","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/scalability","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/continuous-batching","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","observability/latency-percentiles"],"tldr":"Whenever requests arrive at random and take a variable time to serve, they wait, and the wait grows\nnon-linearly as utilization approaches 100%: in the 16-slot example below, going from 75% to 95% busy\nmultiplies mean queue wait about twentyfold. Queueing theory gives you the formulas (Little's law, M/M/c, Kingman) to predict that knee\nbefore production finds it, which is why you never plan LLM capacity at \"average load fits\".","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"latency-budgets","key":"infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Latency budgets","taxonomy_name":"latency budgets","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/slas-slos","observability/latency-percentiles","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"A latency budget takes one user-facing target, such as \"first token within 2 s at p95\", and divides\nit among every hop on the request path: network, guardrails, retrieval, reranking, model queue and\nprefill, tools, plus a reserve. It exists because AI pipelines are long chains of slow, variable steps,\nand without an explicit allocation each team spends the whole budget independently. The budget then\ndrives timeouts, deadline propagation and which steps to skip under pressure.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"scalability","key":"infra-capacity-economics/scalability","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Scalability","taxonomy_name":"scalability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding"],"tldr":"Scalability is how throughput grows as you add resources, and it is almost never linear: shared\nresources serialize part of the work (contention), and keeping shared state consistent adds cost that\ngrows with the number of workers (coherency), so past some point more replicas make things slower. In\nAI systems the hard limits are rarely the stateless app tier; they are GPU memory, provider quotas,\ncache affinity and shared stores such as vector indexes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-optimization","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost optimization","taxonomy_name":"cost optimization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","system-design/model-routing-cascading","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/caching","evals/regression-tests","infra-capacity-economics/finops"],"tldr":"Cost optimization is reducing spend per unit of useful work without breaking quality or latency, and\nit works best as an ordered search: measure where money goes, then avoid calls entirely, send fewer\ntokens, use a cheaper model for the easy share, buy capacity more cheaply, and only then tune serving.\nEvery change is gated by an eval, because the easiest way to cut cost is to quietly make the product\nworse.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-modeling","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost modeling (cost per request, token economics)","taxonomy_name":"cost modeling (cost per request, token economics)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","observability/token-cost-tracking","prompting/prompt-caching","model-internals/tokenization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/tco"],"tldr":"A cost model turns a request's shape into dollars: fresh, cached and output tokens (including hidden\nreasoning) times their prices, plus the non-model calls around them. Token economics explains the\nprices: output costs more because decode is sequential, and agents re-send the whole history every\nturn, so cost grows roughly quadratically with turns. Model the logged distribution, not a typical\nrequest, because costs are heavy-tailed and the tail sets the bill.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"unit-economics-per-successful-task","key":"infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Unit economics per successful task","taxonomy_name":"unit economics per successful task","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","agents/human-in-the-loop","scoping/success-metrics-kpis"],"tldr":"Cost per successful task divides everything a unit of work really costs (failed attempts, retries,\nverification, human fallback and the damage from errors that slip through) by the number of tasks that\nmet an agreed acceptance bar. It replaces cost per call because a cheap model that fails often is\nexpensive once its failures are paid for, and it is the number you compare with the value of the task\nto know whether the product makes money.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost allocation by tenant & feature","taxonomy_name":"cost allocation by tenant & feature","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/finops","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","observability/token-cost-tracking","system-design/ai-gateway","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","observability/opentelemetry"],"tldr":"Cost allocation assigns every dollar of AI spend to the tenant and feature that caused it: direct costs\n(tokens per call) by tagging each request at the gateway, shared costs (GPU fleets, vector stores,\nidle capacity) by an explicit driver such as GPU-seconds or queries. It exists because a provider\ninvoice says only how much you spent, not who or what spent it, and without that you cannot price\ntiers, find unprofitable customers or know which feature to optimize.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"quality-latency-cost-optimization","key":"infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Quality–latency–cost optimization","taxonomy_name":"quality–latency–cost optimization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/model-selection","prompting/prompt-caching","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation"],"tldr":"Every LLM feature lives in a three-dimensional space: how good the answers are, how long the user waits,\nand what each request costs. The levers that improve one axis almost always degrade another, so you cannot\nmaximize all three. The practice is to measure all three per task, throw away every configuration that is\nworse on every axis, and choose from the remaining Pareto frontier under the product's hard constraints.\nThis is what separates features that scale from features that get switched off when the invoice arrives.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"break-even-analysis","key":"infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Break-even analysis (hosted API vs self-hosting)","taxonomy_name":"break-even analysis (hosted API vs self-hosting)","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","infra-capacity-economics/tco","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"A hosted API is pure variable cost: you pay per token and nothing when idle. Self-hosting is mostly fixed:\nGPUs and the people who run them are paid for whether or not tokens flow. Break-even is the monthly volume\nwhere the two curves cross. It depends far more on achievable utilization and team cost than on GPU list\nprice, which is why most teams cross it much later than per-token arithmetic suggests, and why an API price\ncut can push the crossover to infinity.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"finops","key":"infra-capacity-economics/finops","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"FinOps","taxonomy_name":"FinOps","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"FinOps is the operating practice that makes variable cloud and AI spend visible, attributable and owned by\nthe teams that cause it, so cost becomes an engineering signal like latency rather than a quarterly\nsurprise. For LLM systems it means metering every call at the point where it is made, allocating spend to\ntenant and feature, tracking cost per successful task, and running a continuous inform–optimize–operate\nloop instead of a one-off cost-cutting project.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tco","key":"infra-capacity-economics/tco","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"TCO (total cost of ownership)","taxonomy_name":"TCO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/finops","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","system-design/build-vs-buy","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy"],"tldr":"Total cost of ownership is every cost an option incurs over a fixed horizon (acquiring it, running it,\nchanging it and eventually leaving it), not just the line on the invoice. For AI systems the invoice is\nusually the smaller part: people, evaluation upkeep, forced model migrations and exit costs dominate. So\nyou compare options delivering the same outcome over the same horizon, and report a range, not a point.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"llmops-platform","tier":2,"order":21,"title":"LLMOps & AI platform","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what it takes to change an LLM application safely and repeatedly once it is in\nproduction and more than one team depends on it. The unit of release is not a container image but a\nbundle: code, prompt version, model version or provider snapshot, decoding parameters, retrieval index\nand guardrail policy, any one of which can change behaviour without touching the others. The module\nbuilds the system of record for that bundle first: experiment tracking for runs and lineage, a model\nregistry and a prompt registry that both split immutable versions from mutable aliases. It then builds\nthe evidence: a shared evaluation framework, a tiered golden-dataset portfolio, and reproducible runs\nwith manifests and a measured noise floor. CI/CD, release gates, shadow and canary rollouts and rollback\nturn that evidence into controlled exposure. Monitoring, drift detection, an observability stack and a\nlifecycle and deprecation policy keep the bundle honest after release, while the world, the provider\nand the corpus move underneath it. The last two lessons step back to platform shape: a control plane\nthat publishes config snapshots to a fast, independent data plane, and the line between what a central\nplatform team owns and what product teams own. Afterwards you can design a release pipeline where\npromotion and rollback are audited pointer moves backed by statistical evidence, explain why a prompt\nedit is a production change, and lay out the platform for an organisation with many AI teams. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers and Architects who run shared AI infrastructure, and for FDEs who must\nshow a customer how changes reach their tenant.","why":{"engineer":"You will wire prompts and models into registries, write the eval-based CI ladder with content-addressed caching, implement a paired non-inferiority gate, emit traces that carry the resolved bundle version, and make rollback a single alias move you have rehearsed.","architect":"You decide what the release unit is, where the control plane ends and the data plane begins, which signals gate a promotion, how provider sunsets and embedder upgrades are migrated, and which platform pieces are mandatory and central versus federated to product teams.","fde":"Customers will ask how a prompt or model change reaches their tenant, what evidence backs it, how fast you can undo it and how you will notice quality slipping; you need to show the gate report, the canary plan, the rollback drill and per-tenant monitoring on their traffic."},"mental_model":"Picture one immutable, content-hashed bundle moving along a conveyor of environments, with a pointer\n(an alias such as `champion` or `canary`) marking where it is live. Everything left of production\nproduces evidence about the bundle: tracked runs, a versioned golden portfolio, reproducible eval runs\nwith manifests, and a gate that compares the candidate to the champion with a confidence interval and\nper-slice floors. Crossing into production is never a rebuild, only a pointer move, first to a shadow\ncopy of traffic, then to a sticky canary slice, then to everyone. Everything right of production\nwatches the bundle against the world: monitoring slices quality proxies by bundle version, drift\ndetection asks whether inputs, answers or a pinned component moved, and the lifecycle policy schedules\nthe day a version must leave. Any red signal moves the pointer back to the last validated bundle. The\ncontrol plane owns the pointers and the evidence; the data plane only reads a snapshot and serves it.","order":["experiment-tracking","model-registry","prompt-registry","evaluation-frameworks","golden-dataset-strategy","reproducible-evaluations","ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","canary-shadow-deployments","rollback","observability-stack","model-monitoring","drift","model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform"],"order_reasons":{"experiment-tracking":"the run data model and lineage that every registry entry and eval score points back to.","model-registry":"introduces the immutable-version versus mutable-alias split on the artifact that is hardest to version.","prompt-registry":"applies the same split to prompt text and its model settings, completing the bundle's components.","evaluation-frameworks":"the shared datasets, targets, scorers, runner and results store that make eval scores comparable.","golden-dataset-strategy":"turns the dataset interface into a tiered, owned, refreshed portfolio sized to the regressions it must detect.","reproducible-evaluations":"pins every drifting part of an eval in a manifest and measures the noise floor gates will rely on.","ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models":"assembles registries and evals into a pipeline that promotes one tested bundle through a risk-sized ladder.","evaluation-gates-for-production-releases":"formalises the promotion decision as floors, a paired non-inferiority test and three outcomes.","canary-shadow-deployments":"extends evidence to live traffic with bounded exposure once offline gates pass.","rollback":"makes the reverse pointer move fast and safe, including the components that destroy their own undo path.","observability-stack":"the trace, metric and payload pipeline that post-release signals and eval loops depend on.","model-monitoring":"layers operational, proxy and late quality signals over that stack, sliced by bundle version and tenant.","drift":"separates what moved, how it moved and whether it mattered, and feeds confirmed shifts back into the golden set.","model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy":"plans the exit of versions and provider snapshots as dated, evaluated migrations.","ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane":"reframes registries, aliases and policies as a control plane publishing snapshots to a serving path.","centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform":"decides which of all these pieces one platform team owns and which product teams own."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a release platform for a small retrieval-augmented support assistant that answers questions about\na fictional company's returns and billing policy. The assistant runs behind a minimal gateway that\nresolves a `champion` and a `canary` alias from a local control-plane store into a bundle: a prompt\nversion from a prompt registry, a model version (a provider snapshot or a small local model) from a\nmodel registry, decoding parameters and an index version. The gateway caches the resolved snapshot and\nkeeps serving it if the control plane is stopped. Every eval run is tracked with a manifest that pins\ndataset, bundle, judge and rubric versions, and runs over a tiered golden portfolio with smoke, full,\nslice and regression tiers. A CI pipeline runs the smoke tier on every prompt change and the full tier on\nrelease candidates, then applies a gate with safety and schema floors, per-slice floors and a paired\nnon-inferiority test against the champion. Passing candidates go to a shadow run on replayed traffic, then\na sticky 10% canary with guard metrics and automatic rollback. Traces carry the resolved bundle hash, and\na monitoring view shows proxies and sampled judge scores by version, with a drift check on intent and\nlanguage mix. Finally, deprecate the old model version through the lifecycle states with an inventory of\nwhich bundles still pin it.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the gateway, control-plane store, prompt and model registries, tracked eval runner and golden portfolio, started with one command.","A CI configuration that runs the eval ladder with cached results and writes a gate report showing per-slice deltas, the confidence interval and the pass, block or needs-review verdict.","A deployment script that performs shadow, canary ramp and rollback as audited alias moves, plus a recorded rollback drill with its measured time.","A monitoring dashboard or notebook that slices latency, cost, schema failures, refusals and judge scores by bundle version and tenant, with a drift report comparing two traffic windows.","A two-page platform write-up covering the control/data-plane split, the deprecation plan for the retired model, and which components would be central versus team-owned."],"rubric":["A deliberately degraded prompt that raises the aggregate score but breaks the refunds slice is blocked by the gate, and the report names the slice and the confidence interval that caused the block.","Re-running any recorded eval from its manifest reproduces the verdict, and a comparison across mismatched dataset or judge versions is refused rather than reported.","Every production trace resolves to one bundle hash, and moving the alias back restores the previous bundle on all replicas within the measured rollback time, without a redeploy.","Stopping the control-plane store leaves the gateway serving the last snapshot with zero failed requests, and alias changes during the canary are visible in an audit log.","Monitoring shows the canary arm separately from the champion, a seeded language shift in replayed traffic appears in the drift report, and the failing examples become new regression items.","CI rejects a new bundle that pins the deprecated model version, and the inventory lists every bundle and consumer still depending on it before the freeze date."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","key":"llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","module":"llmops-platform","name":"CI/CD for prompts and models","taxonomy_name":"CI/CD for prompts and models","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","evals/regression-tests","prompting/prompt-versioning","evals/eval-driven-development"],"tldr":"Classic CI/CD assumes behaviour is a deterministic function of code and ships one artifact. An LLM\napplication ships three — code, prompts and configuration, and model weights or model IDs — and any one\ncan change behaviour without touching the others. CI/CD for prompts and models adds eval-based tests\nsized to each stage's blast radius, content-addressed caching so those evals stay affordable, and\npromotion of one immutable, tested bundle instead of a rebuild, because a green unit suite says nothing\nabout whether answers got worse.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-registry","key":"llmops-platform/prompt-registry","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Prompt registry","taxonomy_name":"prompt registry","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/prompt-templates","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/rollback","observability/langfuse","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases"],"tldr":"A prompt registry is a versioned store of prompt templates and their model settings, with immutable\nnumbered versions, mutable environment labels such as `production` or `canary`, and a runtime fetch API\nthat caches and falls back safely. It exists because prompts are behaviour-defining configuration edited\nby people who do not ship code, and a hard-coded string gives you neither a record of which prompt\nproduced which answer nor a rollback that does not require a deploy.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-registry","key":"llmops-platform/model-registry","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model registry","taxonomy_name":"model registry","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","python-for-ai/hugging-face","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/rollback","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"A model registry is the system of record that maps a model name to immutable, numbered versions, each\npointing at exact weights or a provider snapshot plus adapters and serving config, with lineage to the\nrun and data that produced it, attached evaluation evidence, and mutable aliases such as `champion`\nthat deployments resolve at startup. It exists so that \"which model is in production\" has exactly one\nanswer and promotion or rollback is a pointer move rather than a file copy done by hand.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"experiment-tracking","key":"llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B)","taxonomy_name":"experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","observability/langfuse","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Experiment tracking records every run of a training or evaluation job — fixed parameters, code and data\nversions, step-indexed metrics and output artifacts — in a queryable store so runs can be compared,\nreproduced and promoted. MLflow and Weights & Biases are the two common tools; they share the same data\nmodel and differ mainly in hosting (self-hosted open source versus SaaS-first), UI depth, and ecosystem\n(MLflow's registry and model packaging; W&B's sweeps, artifact lineage graph and Weave for LLM traces).","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"evaluation-frameworks","key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Evaluation frameworks","taxonomy_name":"evaluation frameworks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/eval-harness","evals/tooling","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/llm-as-a-judge","observability/langfuse","evals/benchmarking"],"tldr":"An evaluation framework turns \"run the eval\" from a script one engineer owns into a shared service with\nfive fixed interfaces: versioned datasets, a target adapter that calls the real application, versioned\nscorers (deterministic, model-graded, human), a runner that pins configuration and stores every raw\noutput, and a results store that diffs any two runs item by item. It exists because a harness per team\nproduces numbers nobody else can compare; the framework makes an eval score a currency the whole\norganisation accepts.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset-strategy","key":"llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Golden dataset strategy","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset strategy","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/error-analysis","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","training-adaptation/data-labeling","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"The `evals/golden-dataset` lesson covers what a golden item is and how it is labeled. This is the\nplatform view: a golden dataset is a portfolio of tiered, versioned sets (smoke, full, slice, regression,\nadversarial, sealed holdout), each with an owner, an admission workflow fed from production failures, and\na size derived from the smallest regression it must detect. The strategy exists because a single static\nset goes stale, leaks into prompts, and is either too small to trust or too expensive to run on every\nchange.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"reproducible-evaluations","key":"llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Reproducible evaluations","taxonomy_name":"reproducible evaluations","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/eval-harness","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning"],"tldr":"A reproducible evaluation is one anyone can re-run from a recorded manifest and reach the same\nconclusion: same dataset version, same bundle (model snapshot, prompt version, decoding parameters,\nindex), same scorer versions (judge model and rubric), same code and environment, with raw outputs stored\nand residual sampling noise measured rather than assumed away. It exists because an LLM eval has many\nsilently drifting parts, so an unpinned comparison across weeks compares two different systems and blames\nthe wrong cause.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Evaluation gates for production releases","taxonomy_name":"evaluation gates for production releases","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","evals/regression-tests","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation"],"tldr":"An evaluation gate is an automated policy that decides, from eval results rather than a green unit\nsuite, whether a candidate bundle (code, prompt, model, parameters, index) may move to the next stage. It\ncombines absolute floors (safety, schema validity, regression items), a paired non-inferiority test\nagainst the champion with a confidence interval, per-slice floors and cost/latency budgets, and returns\npass, block or needs-review with every override recorded. It exists because sampled outputs make\nexact-match tests flake and one aggregate hides the slice that broke.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"canary-shadow-deployments","key":"llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Canary / shadow deployments","taxonomy_name":"canary / shadow deployments","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","evals/a-b-testing","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Offline evals tell you a new model, prompt or agent version is *probably* better on a few hundred\ncurated cases; they cannot tell you what it does on live traffic. A **shadow** deployment runs the\ncandidate on a copy of real requests without serving its answers, so you can compare it to the\nincumbent at zero user risk. A **canary** serves the candidate to a small, sticky slice of users and\nramps up only while guard metrics hold. Together they turn \"ship and hope\" into a measured exposure.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rollback","key":"llmops-platform/rollback","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Rollback","taxonomy_name":"rollback","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","deployment/runbooks","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response"],"tldr":"Rollback is returning production to a previously validated version of the whole AI bundle (model,\nprompt, parameters, tools, index, guardrails) in minutes, by repointing an alias rather than rebuilding\nanything. It matters more for AI systems than for ordinary services because \"fix forward\" means\nshipping a new, untested prompt under incident pressure, and because several components (re-embedded\nindexes, replaced fine-tunes, retired vendor models) are only reversible if you planned for it.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-monitoring","key":"llmops-platform/model-monitoring","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model monitoring","taxonomy_name":"model monitoring","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/drift","llmops-platform/observability-stack","observability/monitoring","observability/token-cost-tracking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"A deployed model can be up, fast and returning 200s while giving steadily worse answers, because\ncorrectness is not a status code. Model monitoring layers three kinds of signal over live traffic:\noperational (latency, errors, tokens, cost), behavioural proxies computable on every request without\nlabels (refusals, schema failures, output length, tool-call mix), and quality signals that arrive late\nand sparse (judge scores on a sample, user feedback, business outcomes). Sliced by version and tenant,\nthey turn silent regressions into alerts with examples attached.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"drift","key":"llmops-platform/drift","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Drift","taxonomy_name":"drift","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/model-monitoring","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/rollback","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","observability/monitoring","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"A system validated on one distribution is deployed into a world that keeps moving. Drift is any change\nbetween the conditions you validated under and the ones you now serve: inputs change (data drift), the\nright answer for an input changes (concept drift), or, specific to LLM applications, the model, prompt,\ncorpus, embedder or judge changes underneath an unchanged system. Detection compares traffic windows\nagainst a reference; the decision is always whether the shift changed outcomes, and what to re-validate.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","key":"llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model lifecycle & deprecation policy","taxonomy_name":"model lifecycle & deprecation policy","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"A model version is registered, validated, promoted, superseded and eventually switched off, and each\nstep changes what callers may rely on. A lifecycle policy names the states, who may move a version\nbetween them and what evidence each move needs. A deprecation policy is the exit half: how far ahead\na retirement is announced, when new pins are frozen, how consumers are found and migrated with\nevaluation, and what is archived afterwards. Without it, every provider sunset becomes an unplanned\nmigration on someone else's calendar.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"observability-stack","key":"llmops-platform/observability-stack","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Observability stack","taxonomy_name":"observability stack","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/opentelemetry","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","observability/arize-phoenix","llmops-platform/model-monitoring"],"tldr":"An observability stack is the pipeline that turns every AI request into something you can query\nlater: instrumentation that emits traces carrying LLM-specific attributes, a collector that redacts\nand samples, separate stores for traces, metrics and payloads, an analysis layer where scores and\nevaluations attach to traces, and alerting on top. It is a stack because each layer has a different\nowner, retention rule and cost. The deep topics live in the Observability module; this lesson is how\nthey fit together as a platform.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","key":"llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","module":"llmops-platform","name":"AI control plane vs data plane","taxonomy_name":"AI control plane vs data plane","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"Split an AI platform into a data plane, the per-request path (gateway, routing, model call,\nretrieval, guardrails, caching) that must be fast and stay up, and a control plane, the low-volume\nsystem of record for what the data plane should do (registries, aliases, rollout percentages,\npolicies, budgets, keys, audit). The data plane reads versioned config snapshots and keeps serving\nthe last one if the control plane is down; the control plane changes behaviour by publishing a new\nsnapshot, never by redeploying the data plane.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","key":"llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Centralized vs federated AI platform","taxonomy_name":"centralized vs federated AI platform","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["strategy/platform-strategy","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","system-design/ai-gateway","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Centralized: one platform team owns model access, registries, evaluation infrastructure,\nobservability and governance, and product teams consume them. Federated: each team runs its own AI\nstack under shared standards. Pure centralization becomes a ticket queue; pure federation becomes\nseven gateways, no inventory and duplicated spend. For most organisations the answer is a thin\nmandatory core (identity, model access, budgets, audit, telemetry) with federated ownership of\nprompts, evals, corpora and agents, with the line moved as team count and regulatory load grow.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"security-governance-compliance","tier":2,"order":22,"title":"Security, governance & compliance","intro":{"overview":"This module is about making an AI system safe to run, defensible to an auditor and acceptable to an\nenterprise buyer. It starts with security. AI security is ordinary application security plus one new\ncomponent: a model that follows instructions found in its data. Threat modeling draws the trust\nboundaries a classical review misses, where untrusted text flows into context and model output flows\ninto actions. Prompt injection defence turns that diagram into deterministic enforcement outside the\nmodel, using the rule of two. Zero trust makes every tool call a request to verify, with delegated,\nscoped, short-lived credentials. The data group comes next. Classification labels data so controls are\ndecided once per label and inherited by chunks, embeddings and weights. PII protection and DLP decide,\nper data class and destination, whether to allow, redact, tokenize or block. Residency maps every place a\nrequest's data actually goes. Governance follows: an inventory of every AI use, a risk tier per use case,\na risk register, responsible AI requirements, a model registry enforced at the gateway, model cards, and\naudit trails that can reconstruct a decision months later. The module closes with operations and\nassurance: AI incident response with kill switches and legal clocks, a tested vendor exit plan, and a\ncompliance architecture that maps one control set to GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act\nand the DPDP Act. Afterwards you can threat-model an agent, put a policy gateway in front of it, classify\nand govern its data, and hand an auditor evidence instead of policy documents. It matters most for\nArchitects and FDEs, who face these questions in design reviews and security questionnaires, and for\nEngineers who build the enforcement points.","why":{"engineer":"You build the enforcement points that make policy real: the tool dispatcher that checks the rule of two, the gateway that redacts PII and refuses unregistered models or out-of-region endpoints, the scoped token exchange, the hash-chained audit log and the kill switch, so a prompt injection or a leaked card number is a bug in your code path.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries sit, which data classes may reach which model and region, how labels propagate into vectors and logs, what the registry and audit schema look like, and how one control set maps to several frameworks; retrofitting residency, deletion or provenance fields later costs far more than designing them in.","fde":"Enterprise customers gate deployment on security questionnaires, data residency, DPAs, model documentation and an incident plan; you answer from a threat model, a data-flow map, model and system cards and automatically collected evidence, and you raise residency and risk tier in the first scoping call because they can rule out the default provider."},"mental_model":"Picture two planes around every AI system. The runtime plane is a single chokepoint, a policy gateway and\ntool dispatcher, that sits between the model and everything it can read, call or send. The model is\ntreated as an untrusted interpreter: whatever it proposes, the gateway checks identity, delegation, data\nlabel, destination, region, model approval and the rule of two in code, then allows, redacts, reroutes,\nasks a human or denies, and writes an audit event either way. The governance plane decides what rules\nthat gateway enforces. It starts from the inventory: every AI use case, tiered by what decisions it\ninforms and about whom. The tier sets how much risk assessment, fairness evaluation, documentation,\nvalidation and oversight the use case needs, and those decisions are recorded in the registry, the risk\nregister and the model card. The two planes meet in evidence. The audit trail and gateway metrics prove\nthe controls ran, incident response uses them to contain and reconstruct, and compliance becomes mapping\none set of enforced, evidenced controls onto each framework a customer or regulator brings.","order":["ai-security","threat-modeling","prompt-injection-defense","zero-trust","data-classification","pii-protection-dlp","data-residency","ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","ai-risk-management","responsible-ai","model-governance","model-cards","audit-trails","ai-incident-response","vendor-exit-strategy","compliance-architecture"],"order_reasons":{"ai-security":"the map of what changes when a model enters the system, used to decide which later lessons apply to a design.","threat-modeling":"draws the data-flow diagram and trust boundaries every later control is chosen against.","prompt-injection-defense":"turns the content sources and action sinks from the threat model into enforcement outside the model.","zero-trust":"gives each tool call delegated, scoped, short-lived credentials checked at a PEP, the identity half of injection defence.","data-classification":"labels data and its derived artifacts so controls are decided per label, which DLP and residency both key on.","pii-protection-dlp":"applies per-label, per-destination actions to personal data flowing into prompts, logs, indexes and weights.","data-residency":"extends the data-flow map to the locations of every endpoint, log, cache and support path a request touches.","ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification":"builds the list of AI uses and the risk tiers that make all governance proportionate.","ai-risk-management":"runs the map, measure and manage loop per inventory entry, with owners, appetite and residual risk.","responsible-ai":"turns fairness, transparency and oversight into testable requirements for the high-tier use cases the risk loop flags.","model-governance":"enforces which models are approved for which use through a registry and the gateway, using the tiers and risk decisions above.","model-cards":"documents intended use, out-of-scope uses and disaggregated results as a release gate in the governance flow.","audit-trails":"records prompts, versions, retrieved context, tool calls and gateway decisions so any output can be reconstructed and defended.","ai-incident-response":"uses kill switches, degraded modes and the audit trail to contain AI failures that never trip an error-rate alert.","vendor-exit-strategy":"measures how quickly and safely you can leave a provider, drawing on governance, evals and data ownership.","compliance-architecture":"maps the whole control set and its evidence onto GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act and DPDP, so it comes last."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the FDE and architect for an AI assistant being rolled out to a mid-sized EU insurer. Claims\nhandlers use it to summarise claim files (PDFs and emails from policyholders, which carry health and\nfinancial data), answer questions from internal policy documents, draft emails to claimants, and\nrecommend whether a claim needs a fraud review. The customer's security team will not sign off until\nyou pass their review. Over a week, take an existing prototype (a model API behind a small web app, with\nlocal mocks for the claims system, document store and email) and make it reviewable. Threat-model it\nwith trust boundaries around claimant content and the email and claims-update sinks. Put one policy\ngateway in front of the model and every tool that enforces the rule of two, delegated per-user tokens,\ndata labels, PII redaction per destination, EU-only endpoints that fail closed, and a model registry.\nInventory and tier the four use cases (the fraud recommendation is the high-risk one), write the risk\nregister, a fairness evaluation and a system card for it, add a hash-chained audit trail, rehearse an\nincident, write a vendor exit plan, and assemble an evidence pack mapped to GDPR, the EU AI Act and SOC 2.","deliverables":["A threat model: a data-flow diagram with trust boundaries, every content source and action sink marked, a STRIDE-based threat register, and a verified test linked to each high-severity threat.","A policy gateway and tool dispatcher in code, enforcing per-user delegated scopes, the rule of two with human approval for outbound email after reading claimant content, label-based PII redaction or blocking per destination, EU-region endpoints that fail closed, and refusal of unregistered models.","A data map and classification table covering every store and derived artifact (chunks, embeddings, traces, caches, eval sets) with its label, region, retention and deletion path, plus a DLP precision and recall measurement on a labelled sample.","An AI inventory with risk tiers and rationale for all four use cases, a risk register with owners, KRIs and residual risk, a fairness evaluation of the fraud-review recommendation by relevant groups, and a versioned model or system card with out-of-scope uses.","A hash-chained audit trail capturing user, prompt template version, model version, retrieved chunk ids, tool calls and gateway decisions, with a verification script and a reconstruction query.","An incident runbook with a kill switch and degraded mode, a tabletop write-up of an injected claimant email that tries to forward a claim file, a vendor exit plan with measured eval deltas on an alternative model, and a compliance evidence matrix mapping each control to GDPR, the EU AI Act and SOC 2 with links to automatically collected evidence."],"rubric":["A claimant PDF containing an injected instruction to email the full claim file externally results in no email being sent without approval, and the audit log shows the denied or pending call with the rule that fired.","Switching the configured model endpoint to a non-EU region or an unregistered model makes requests fail closed with a logged denial, not a silent fallback.","A request made as a handler without access to a given claim cannot retrieve that claim's documents through the assistant, shown by a PEP denial tied to the user's delegated token.","Running the verification script after manually editing one audit record reports the exact broken link, and the reconstruction query returns the model version, prompt version and chunk ids for a chosen past recommendation.","Every inventory entry has a tier, owner and review date, and the high-risk fraud recommendation has a card with disaggregated results and a named human oversight step, while low-tier uses carry visibly lighter documentation.","The evidence matrix links each control to an artifact generated by the system (gateway metrics, audit samples, test runs), and a deletion request for one claimant is shown removing their data from the document store, vector index, traces and caches."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-security","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-security","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI security","taxonomy_name":"AI security","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"AI security is ordinary application security plus one new component: a model that follows instructions\nfound in its data, cannot be patched the way a parser can, and is increasingly handed credentials and\ntools. The attack surface therefore moves from syntax (SQL, HTML) to semantics (any text the model\nreads), and from the model to everything around it. The controls that hold are the old ones applied to\nthe model as an untrusted interpreter: least privilege, isolation, output handling, approval gates and\nmonitoring, not cleverer prompts.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"threat-modeling","key":"security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Threat modeling","taxonomy_name":"threat modeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-security","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","system-design/c4-diagrams","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"Threat modeling is a structured answer to four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what\nwill we do about it, and did we do a good job. It exists because controls chosen without a model are\nchosen at random. For AI systems the diagram must mark every flow of untrusted text into a model's\ncontext and every flow of model output into an action, because those are the trust boundaries a\nclassical review never draws.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection-defense","key":"security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Prompt injection defense","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection defense","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/excessive-agency","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"No prompt, classifier or fine-tune reliably stops a model from obeying instructions hidden in text it\nreads, so defence is an architecture, not a filter. You put deterministic enforcement points outside the\nmodel, on what it can reach and what its outputs can do, and treat detection as an alarm. The program\npart is making that design repeatable: a policy per workflow, a regression suite, and an owner who\nsigns off on the residual risk.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pii-protection-dlp","key":"security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"PII protection / DLP","taxonomy_name":"PII protection / DLP","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/data-leakage","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","system-design/ai-gateway","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","observability/logging"],"tldr":"PII protection decides which personal data may reach which destination; data loss prevention (DLP) is\nthe machinery that finds sensitive data in motion, in use and at rest and enforces that decision with\nallow, redact, tokenize, block or alert. LLM systems need it more than ordinary apps because they copy\nfree text into prompts, providers, logs, traces, indexes and weights, and each copy is a new place for\npersonal data to leak or outlive its purpose.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-classification","key":"security-governance-compliance/data-classification","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Data classification","taxonomy_name":"data classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","data-architecture/data-lineage","system-design/ai-gateway","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"Data classification assigns every dataset and document a sensitivity label, such as Public, Internal,\nConfidential or Restricted, so that controls can be decided once per label instead of argued per\nrequest. In AI systems the hard part is propagation: chunks, embeddings, summaries, caches and fine-tuned\nweights derived from data must inherit its label, or the most sensitive source silently ends up in the\nleast protected store.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"zero-trust","key":"security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Zero trust","taxonomy_name":"zero trust","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-security","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","deployment/private-endpoints"],"tldr":"Zero trust drops the assumption that anything inside the network perimeter is trustworthy. Every request\nis authenticated, authorised against policy for that specific resource, and granted the least privilege\nfor the shortest time, *because* attackers who get inside, or components that are tricked, otherwise\ninherit everything the perimeter protected. For AI systems, the model and the agent are exactly such\ncomponents: each tool call is a request to verify, not a trusted insider.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-residency","key":"security-governance-compliance/data-residency","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Data residency","taxonomy_name":"data residency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/ai-gateway","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Data residency is the requirement that data be stored and processed only in approved locations, set by\nlaw, regulators or contracts. In AI systems it is harder than \"pick an EU region\", because a single\nrequest can touch a model endpoint, provider-side logs and abuse monitoring, caches, vector stores,\ntraces and support staff, each with its own location. Residency is a property of the whole data flow,\nand it is distinct from sovereignty, which is about whose law can compel access.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-governance","key":"security-governance-compliance/model-governance","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Model governance","taxonomy_name":"model governance","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Model governance is the set of decisions, records and enforced checks that control which models may be\nused, for what, by whom, on what evidence, and how changes are approved, monitored and retired. It exists\nbecause models change behaviour without code changes and fail statistically, so ordinary change\nmanagement misses them. Good governance is mostly automated: a registry, risk-tiered approval with\nevidence, and a gateway that refuses unregistered or unapproved models.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-cards","key":"security-governance-compliance/model-cards","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Model cards","taxonomy_name":"model cards","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","llmops-platform/model-registry","evals/model-evaluation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation"],"tldr":"A model card is a short, structured document that travels with a model and states what it is for, what\nit is not for, how it was evaluated (broken down by the conditions that matter), and where it fails. It\nexists because a model's behaviour cannot be inferred from its weights or API, so the people deploying,\napproving and auditing it need the builder's knowledge written down, versioned, and tied to the exact\nartifact it describes.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"audit-trails","key":"security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Audit trails","taxonomy_name":"audit trails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","observability/logging","observability/tracing","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces"],"tldr":"An audit trail is an append-only, tamper-evident record of who (or what) did what, when, with which\ninputs and which versions, kept so that a decision can be reconstructed and defended later. AI systems\nneed a richer one than ordinary apps because the \"what\" includes a non-deterministic model: without the\nprompt, model version, retrieved context and tool calls recorded at the time, you cannot explain or\nreproduce an output months later.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI inventory & use-case risk classification","taxonomy_name":"AI inventory & use-case risk classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","llmops-platform/model-registry"],"tldr":"You cannot govern AI you do not know exists. An AI inventory is the system of record listing every AI\nsystem and use case in the organization (built, bought or embedded in a SaaS tool) with its owner, data,\nmodels and purpose. Risk classification then assigns each *use case* a tier that decides how much control\nit gets, because the same model is harmless drafting marketing copy and high-stakes screening job\napplicants.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"responsible-ai","key":"security-governance-compliance/responsible-ai","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Responsible AI","taxonomy_name":"responsible AI","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","agents/human-in-the-loop","safety-security/content-moderation"],"tldr":"Responsible AI is the discipline of turning values such as fairness, transparency, accountability,\nprivacy, safety and reliability into concrete requirements, measurements and owners for each AI system.\nIt exists because principles alone change nothing: a model can be accurate on average and still harm a\ngroup, mislead users or act without anyone accountable. The work is choosing which properties matter for\na use case, measuring them, and deciding the trade-offs explicitly.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-risk-management","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI risk management","taxonomy_name":"AI risk management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/responsible-ai","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","llmops-platform/model-monitoring","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"AI risk management is the continuous loop of identifying what can go wrong with an AI system, estimating\nhow likely and how harmful each risk is, choosing controls, and tracking what risk remains, with named\nowners and an explicit appetite for what is acceptable. It exists because AI risks are probabilistic,\nshift after launch, and span security, safety, legal and business harms, so a one-time security review\ncannot cover them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-incident-response","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI incident response","taxonomy_name":"AI incident response","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","llmops-platform/rollback","system-design/graceful-degradation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites"],"tldr":"AI incident response is the prepared process for detecting, containing, investigating and learning from\nAI-specific failures: harmful or false outputs at scale, data leakage through a model, prompt injection\nthat triggers actions, bias discovered in production, or a provider model change that silently alters\nbehaviour. It differs from ordinary incident response because the system is often \"up\" while causing\nharm, there is no stack trace, and the fix may be a prompt, a data source or a vendor.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vendor-exit-strategy","key":"security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Vendor exit strategy","taxonomy_name":"vendor exit strategy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","system-design/multi-model-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture"],"tldr":"A vendor exit strategy is a tested plan for leaving an AI provider (model API, vector database, agent\nplatform or AI SaaS) within a target time and cost, without losing data or quality. It exists because AI\nlock-in hides in places contracts do not mention: prompts tuned to one model, embeddings only that model\ncan read, fine-tunes you cannot export, and conversation state stored on the vendor's side. Planning exit\nearly is cheap; discovering lock-in during a price rise, outage or deprecation is not.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"compliance-architecture","key":"security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Compliance architecture (GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO 42001, EU AI Act, DPDP Act)","taxonomy_name":"compliance architecture (GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO 42001, EU AI Act, DPDP Act)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":25,"related":["security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"Compliance architecture is designing an AI system so that legal and assurance obligations are enforced\nby its structure (data flows, regions, access, logging, retention, documentation) and proven by\nautomatically collected evidence, rather than asserted in policy documents. Its core move is one control\nset mapped to many frameworks, because GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act and India's DPDP\nAct overlap heavily but differ in scope, triggers and proof. This is orientation, not legal advice.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"strategy","tier":2,"order":23,"title":"Strategy","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the decisions that sit above any single AI system: which use cases to pursue and\nin what order, who has to agree before building starts, which vendors to depend on, what to provide\nonce as a shared platform, and whether the AI claims behind a deal hold up. It opens with the AI\nroadmap, a sequenced portfolio of use cases treated as hypotheses, each scored separately on value\nand on feasibility (data readiness, evaluability, error tolerance, risk class, integration effort)\nand moved through evidence gates from feasibility eval to pilot to production. Stakeholder alignment\nthen turns the conflicting goals of operations, compliance, finance and security into one agreed\noperating point on a measured trade-off curve, set against a human baseline and written on one page.\nVendor evaluation supplies the comparative method: hard gates first, then a bake-off on your own\nlabelled data measuring quality, latency under load and cost per successful task, then exit terms.\nPlatform strategy decides which recurring capabilities to centralise, using breadth of demand,\nvariance between teams and externalities, and when to build them: after demand appears, as a thin\nmandatory core plus an optional paved road. Technical due diligence applies the same habit of turning\nclaims into tests to an acquisition, investment or critical partner. The thread through all five is\nthat AI feasibility is an empirical question, so every strategic commitment should be gated on an\neval rather than on a demo or a slide. Afterwards you can build and defend a roadmap, run a launch\ndecision without a late veto, select a vendor on evidence, argue a platform boundary with numbers,\nand write a diligence findings list. It matters most for Architects and FDEs, and for Engineers\nmoving into technical leadership.","why":{"engineer":"You build the feasibility evals, bake-off harnesses and cost-per-item models that the roadmap gates, vendor choice and launch decision actually rest on, and you can say plainly when a use case should be killed or parked until a new model ships.","architect":"You draw the platform boundary (gateway, identity, budgets, audit, telemetry in the mandatory core; prompts, corpora and agents left with teams), sequence the portfolio so early projects build reusable assets, and keep vendor seams so decisions stay reversible as the model market moves.","fde":"You get the customer's business owner, compliance, security and finance to agree an operating point and decision rights before building, run the vendor bake-off on their own data, and present results against the one-pager at each gate so sign-off does not stall in pilot purgatory."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: AI strategy is portfolio management under uncertainty, where every bet is bought\nwith information before it is bought with engineering. Each use case is a hypothesis with a value\nrange and a weakest feasibility axis; an eval on real data is the cheap experiment that resolves it.\nGates set in advance decide whether a bet advances, waits for a capability trigger, or is killed.\nStakeholder alignment fixes what \"advance\" means: an operating point on a trade-off curve, measured\nagainst today's human baseline, with named decision owners. Vendor evaluation and due diligence are\nthe same experiment pointed outward: do not trust the claim, test it on your data and price the exit.\nPlatform strategy is what the portfolio leaves behind: the shared parts that several bets needed,\nwith little variance and organisation-wide risk, pulled into a thin core once demand exists. If a\ncommitment does not have an evidence gate and an owner, it is not yet strategy.","order":["ai-roadmap","stakeholder-alignment","vendor-evaluation","platform-strategy","technical-due-diligence"],"order_reasons":{"ai-roadmap":"sets the portfolio frame, value-versus-feasibility scoring and stage gates that every other lesson plugs into.","stakeholder-alignment":"defines the agreed operating point, baseline and decision rights that a roadmap gate needs before anyone can say yes.","vendor-evaluation":"teaches the gates-then-bake-off method on your own data, the core skill for each build, buy or wait decision on the roadmap.","platform-strategy":"builds on roadmap sequencing and vendor sourcing to decide what to centralise, when, and how to fund it.","technical-due-diligence":"the advanced case that extends vendor evaluation's claim-to-test discipline to deals, data rights and margin models."},"capstone":{"brief":"Act as the AI lead for a mid-sized insurer (fictional, with synthetic or public data) that has\ncollected fifteen AI ideas from workshops, has two teams already calling a model provider with their\nown keys, and is being pitched a small claims-automation startup as an acquisition. Produce a strategy\npack the leadership team could decide from in one meeting. Turn the ideas into workflow-level\ncandidates and score each on the value formula (volume, value per unit, capture rate, automation\nshare, run and review cost) and on each feasibility axis separately, then sequence the next three\nquarters so early items build shared assets. For the top item, write the stakeholder one-pager:\nbaseline human error rate and cycle time, hard constraints kept apart from outcome goals, a trade-off\ncurve from a small offline eval, the chosen operating point and the launch and pause owners. Run a\nlight vendor bake-off for that item between at least two model providers on 100 or more labelled\nsynthetic cases. Propose the platform core with a break-even estimate, and write a diligence plan and\nmock findings list for the acquisition target.","deliverables":["A portfolio sheet with one row per candidate: owner, value range, per-axis feasibility scores, build/buy/wait decision with a trigger for each \"wait\", current stage, gate criterion, kill condition and next decision date, plus a three-horizon sequence.","A one-page stakeholder alignment document for the top use case, including the measured baseline on the same items the AI will handle, a confidence-threshold trade-off curve, the chosen operating point, failure handling and a RACI or DACI table.","A re-runnable vendor bake-off (script or notebook) and a short report: gate results, quality with confidence intervals, p95 latency at expected concurrency, cost per successful task, and priced exit terms.","A platform proposal naming the mandatory core and the optional paved road, with the centralisation break-even worked through for at least three capabilities and a funding model.","A diligence plan that turns each of the target's headline claims into a test, with a classified findings list (deal-breaker, price adjustment, post-close fix) and a usage-sensitive margin model."],"rubric":["Feasibility is scored per axis and the weakest axis is named for every candidate; the top-value item is not first in the sequence unless its foundations already exist, and the reason is stated.","Every roadmap stage, the launch decision and each \"wait\" item has a pre-agreed numeric criterion or trigger, and at least one candidate is killed or parked on evidence.","The alignment one-pager compares the AI's error on its automated slice with the human error on that same slice, and a reviewer can make the launch call from the one-pager and the eval without new negotiation.","Vendors are compared on cost per successful task (retries and review included), not list price, and the bake-off re-runs from one command when a new model is added.","The platform core is justified by externalities or the break-even inequality, at least one capability is deliberately left with teams, and each diligence claim maps to a concrete test with a named finding class."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-roadmap","key":"strategy/ai-roadmap","module":"strategy","name":"AI roadmap","taxonomy_name":"AI roadmap","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/roi-model","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","evals/eval-driven-development","strategy/platform-strategy","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"An AI roadmap is a sequenced portfolio of use cases, each treated as a hypothesis with an evidence\ngate, plus the shared capabilities (data access, evaluation, platform, governance) that the early bets\nbuild for the later ones. It exists because AI projects fail mostly on data, evaluation and adoption\nrather than on models, and because model capability shifts faster than annual plans, so the roadmap\nmust commit to outcomes and learning milestones, not to specific models or dates for everything.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"platform-strategy","key":"strategy/platform-strategy","module":"strategy","name":"Platform strategy","taxonomy_name":"platform strategy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","strategy/ai-roadmap"],"tldr":"Platform strategy decides which AI capabilities an organisation provides once, as a shared internal\nproduct, and which it leaves to each team, and when to make that move. The rule of thumb: platformise\nwhat several teams need, what varies little between them, and what carries organisation-wide risk or\ncost (model access, identity, budgets, audit, telemetry, evaluation tooling); leave the differentiating\nparts (prompts, corpora, agents, UX) with teams. Build the platform after demand appears, not before.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vendor-evaluation","key":"strategy/vendor-evaluation","module":"strategy","name":"Vendor evaluation","taxonomy_name":"vendor evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/build-vs-buy","evals/golden-dataset","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"Vendor evaluation is choosing a model provider, vector database, eval tool or AI platform by testing\ncandidates on your own task and constraints rather than on demos and public leaderboards. The method:\nhard gates first (data terms, residency, security, compliance), then a bake-off on your own labelled data\nmeasuring quality, latency under your load and cost per successful task, then contract and exit terms.\nIt matters because AI vendors differ most on exactly the things a demo never shows.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"technical-due-diligence","key":"strategy/technical-due-diligence","module":"strategy","name":"Technical due diligence","taxonomy_name":"technical due diligence","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["strategy/vendor-evaluation","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","evals/dataset-contamination","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary"],"tldr":"Technical due diligence on an AI company, product or team (for an acquisition, investment or strategic\npartnership) tests whether the technical claims behind a deal are true and what they will cost to keep\ntrue. For AI it centres on six questions: does the AI work on data the target did not\nchoose, who owns its data and models, what happens to margins as usage grows, how exposed\nis it to upstream model providers, is the AI layer secure, and can the system and team be operated by\nsomeone else.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"stakeholder-alignment","key":"strategy/stakeholder-alignment","module":"strategy","name":"Stakeholder alignment","taxonomy_name":"stakeholder alignment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/exec-communication","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","adoption/change-management","strategy/ai-roadmap"],"tldr":"Stakeholder alignment is getting the people who fund, approve, operate, use or can veto an AI system to\nagree, before building, on the goal, how success is measured, what error rate is acceptable and what\nhappens on a failure, and who decides what. AI makes this harder than ordinary software because the\nsystem is right only some of the time, so \"does it work?\" has no answer until stakeholders have agreed\non the operating point. The ongoing relationship work is covered under stakeholder management.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"scoping","tier":3,"order":24,"title":"Scoping","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the work between \"the customer wants AI\" and \"the team is building the right thing,\nagainst agreed numbers, with a path to production\". It starts with understanding. Customer discovery\nturns a requested solution into the underlying job, its current cost, the cost of an error and the\nperson who decides. AI use-case discovery ranks candidate ideas on value, fit for a probabilistic model,\ndata feasibility and error tolerance. Workflow analysis finds the step that actually limits the outcome.\nProblem decomposition splits the goal into nodes with inputs, outputs, techniques and tests. Domain\nmodeling pins down what the customer's words mean, and data access and permissions discovery starts the\nslowest approvals early. The second group makes value measurable: a metric tree with guardrails, a\nbaseline captured before anything changes, and an ROI model built as ranges with a break-even point.\nThe third group turns this into a commitment: a dependency and risk log, a statement of work with\nstatistical acceptance and dated customer obligations, and requirements traced to eval cases. The last\ngroup moves from evidence to production through timeboxed prototypes and PoCs, demos that drive\ndecisions, a staged pilot plan with entry and exit criteria, and risk-ordered hardening. After the\nmodule you can take a vague request, pick a use case worth doing, write a scope and SOW a buyer will\nsign, prove or kill feasibility inside a timebox, and plan a pilot whose go/no-go is a reading of agreed\nnumbers. It matters most for FDEs, who run this with customers, and for Architects, who own the\ndecomposition, data access and production plan.","why":{"engineer":"Decomposition gives you nodes with contracts and tests instead of one untestable prompt, and the frozen eval set, traced requirements and timebox tell you what \"done\" means before you start tuning.","architect":"You decide which nodes need a model and which are plain code, how the domain model projects into schemas, prompts and retrieval filters, how source permissions carry into the system, and what hardening production needs.","fde":"You turn \"build us a chatbot\" into a signed scope: the real job, a ranked use case, a baseline, an ROI range, dated customer dependencies and acceptance criteria that survive a probabilistic model and a steering meeting."},"mental_model":"Picture scoping as a funnel of decisions, each one narrowing what you build and each backed by\nevidence. At the wide top are a customer request and a pile of ideas; discovery, use-case ranking and\nworkflow analysis narrow these to one job and the step that limits its outcome. Decomposition and the\ndomain model give that job a shape: nodes with contracts, words with fixed meanings, data with known\nowners and permissions. Then comes the measuring stick, which you fix before building: a metric tree,\na baseline and an ROI range, which together say what success is worth and which number the pilot must\nmove. The SOW, risk log and traceability matrix turn that measuring stick into a commitment both sides\ncan check. Below that, every stage of execution (timeboxed PoC, demo, pilot, production) ends in an\nexplicit go, pivot or stop decision taken against the same numbers. If a stage cannot name its\nquestion, its evidence and its decision rule, it is not scoped yet.","order":["customer-discovery","ai-use-case-discovery","workflow-analysis","problem-decomposition","domain-modeling","data-access-and-permissions-discovery","success-metrics-kpis","baseline-measurement-before-deployment","roi-model","dependency-and-risk-management","statement-of-work","timeboxing","rapid-prototyping-poc","demos","requirements-traceability","pilot-to-production-plan","productionization"],"order_reasons":{"customer-discovery":"every later step depends on knowing the real job, who feels it, what it costs and who decides.","ai-use-case-discovery":"narrows many ideas to a ranked shortlist on value, model fit, data feasibility and error cost.","workflow-analysis":"maps the current process so the AI lands on the step that actually limits the outcome.","problem-decomposition":"splits the chosen job into nodes with inputs, outputs, techniques and tests, separating model work from plain code.","domain-modeling":"fixes the entities, states and vocabulary that the decomposed nodes, schemas and eval labels will share.","data-access-and-permissions-discovery":"identifies sources, owners and permission rules for those entities early, because approvals have the longest lead time.","success-metrics-kpis":"defines the outcome, driver, quality and guardrail metrics before architecture choices lock in.","baseline-measurement-before-deployment":"measures those metrics under the current process, because the before number cannot be recovered later.","roi-model":"turns metrics and baseline into a money range with sensitivity and break-even, which can kill or prioritise the use case.","dependency-and-risk-management":"lists dated customer dependencies and sized risks, which the SOW must contain.","statement-of-work":"commits scope, statistical acceptance, dated obligations and change control, built from everything above.","timeboxing":"sets the question, budget and decision rule that keep uncertain work like a PoC from expanding without limit.","rapid-prototyping-poc":"tests the riskiest assumption on real data inside a timebox and ends in a decision.","demos":"presents PoC and pilot evidence to a named audience so it produces a decision rather than applause.","requirements-traceability":"links SOW requirements to components, prompts and eval cases once the pilot build begins.","pilot-to-production-plan":"stages the rollout with cohorts, entry and exit criteria, owners and rollback, measured against the baseline.","productionization":"closes the gap between a working pilot and a system that survives real traffic, outages, model changes and audits."},"capstone":{"brief":"Scope an AI engagement end to end for a realistic customer: a mid-sized insurer's claims team that\nhas asked for \"a chatbot to answer adjusters' policy questions\". Work from a short written case (a\none-page sponsor brief, three interview transcripts you write or role-play, and a synthetic dataset of\na few hundred claims with timestamps and policy documents). Over a week, run discovery and reframe the\nrequest into the underlying job, rank at least five candidate use cases on the four axes, and map the\ncurrent claims workflow to find the step that limits cycle time. Decompose the chosen use case into\nnodes, write a glossary and one state machine for a claim, and build a data source register with owners,\naccess methods and permission models. Define a metric tree with guardrails, compute a baseline from the\nsynthetic logs, and build an ROI model with ranges, sensitivity and break-even. Write a phased SOW with\nstatistical acceptance, then run a two-day timeboxed PoC on the riskiest node and record a short demo\nthat ends in a stated decision. Finish with a pilot-to-production plan and a prioritised hardening list.","deliverables":["A discovery brief (one page) stating the job, current cost, cost of an error, success evidence and decision-maker, plus a ranked use-case table scored on value, model fit, data feasibility and error tolerance.","A workflow swimlane with wait times and variant frequencies, a decomposition diagram where every node has an input, output, technique, owner and test, a domain glossary with a claim state machine, and a data source register with owner, access method, classification and permission model per source.","A metric tree (outcome KPI, drivers, quality metrics, guardrails), a baseline report with means, variance, per-segment results and a human-quality audit, and an ROI spreadsheet with ranges, a sensitivity ranking and a break-even value.","A RAID log with dated dependencies and risks sized in days, and a phased SOW with scope, out-of-scope items, statistical acceptance against a frozen eval set, dated customer obligations and change control.","A PoC report from a two-day timebox with the question, pass/fail bar, results on real-shaped data and the decision, a recorded demo of ten minutes or less, a traceability matrix from requirements to eval cases, and a pilot-to-production plan with stages, cohorts, entry and exit criteria, rollback and a risk-ordered hardening list."],"rubric":["The discovery brief names a job different from \"chatbot\" and supports it with quotes from the interviews, and the use-case ranking shows at least one high-value idea rejected for error cost or data feasibility, with the reason stated.","The chosen AI step is the one the workflow map shows limits cycle time, and the decomposition multiplies per-node error rates to name the node that dominates end-to-end error.","Every SOW acceptance criterion is a threshold on a named metric, on a frozen eval set with a stated size and slices, and every customer dependency has an owner and a need-by date that also appears in the RAID log.","The ROI model's break-even value and its top-ranked sensitivity assumption are the numbers the pilot plan says it will measure, and the baseline uses the same metric definitions as the pilot.","The PoC report ends with an explicit ship, continue, pivot or stop decision tied to its pass/fail bar, and the demo shows at least one failure case alongside eval numbers.","Each pilot exit criterion maps to a metric in the tree, and every requirement in the traceability matrix links to at least one eval case and to the component or prompt that implements it."]}},"topics":[{"id":"customer-discovery","key":"scoping/customer-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"Customer discovery","taxonomy_name":"customer discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","strategy/stakeholder-alignment","adoption/stakeholder-management"],"tldr":"Customer discovery is the structured work of finding out what problem a customer actually has, who feels\nit, what it costs today and what evidence would prove it solved, before anyone builds. It exists because\nthe request (\"build us a chatbot\") is a proposed solution from one stakeholder, and AI projects fail far\nmore often from solving the wrong problem than from picking the wrong model.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-use-case-discovery","key":"scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"AI use-case discovery","taxonomy_name":"AI use-case discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/customer-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/roi-model","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","agents/workflows-vs-agents","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision"],"tldr":"AI use-case discovery turns a long list of \"we could use AI for…\" ideas into a short, ranked list of\ncandidates that are valuable, actually suited to a probabilistic model, feasible with reachable data, and\nsafe to get wrong sometimes. It exists because most candidate tasks are either better solved by plain\nautomation or too risky or data-starved to start with, and choosing the first use case badly sinks\ncredibility for the whole programme.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"problem-decomposition","key":"scoping/problem-decomposition","module":"scoping","name":"Problem decomposition","taxonomy_name":"problem decomposition","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/domain-modeling","scoping/requirements-traceability","agents/task-decomposition","agents/workflows-vs-agents","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Problem decomposition breaks a fuzzy business goal into sub-problems that each have a defined input,\noutput, technique and test, so you can tell which parts need a model, which are plain code, and where\nthe end-to-end system will fail. It exists because a single \"LLM does everything\" step cannot be\nevaluated, debugged or improved piece by piece, and because errors in chained probabilistic steps\nmultiply.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflow-analysis","key":"scoping/workflow-analysis","module":"scoping","name":"Workflow analysis","taxonomy_name":"workflow analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/customer-discovery","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","agents/human-in-the-loop","system-design/workflow-orchestration","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Workflow analysis maps how work actually flows today: the steps, people, systems, handoffs, decisions,\nexceptions and, crucially, how long items wait between steps. It exists because AI only improves an\noutcome if it lands on the step that limits it; speeding up a ten-minute task inside a three-day process\nchanges almost nothing, and the documented process is rarely the one people follow.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"domain-modeling","key":"scoping/domain-modeling","module":"scoping","name":"Domain modeling","taxonomy_name":"domain modeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering","rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/workflow-analysis"],"tldr":"Domain modeling captures the customer's entities, relationships, lifecycles, rules and vocabulary in an\nexplicit, agreed form, then reuses that one model in schemas, prompts, retrieval metadata and evaluation\nlabels. It exists because a language model is fluent in generic meanings while the customer's words have\nspecific ones, and an unstated mismatch (\"account\", \"active\", \"closed\") produces confident outputs that\nare wrong in ways nobody notices.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-access-and-permissions-discovery","key":"scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"Data access & permissions discovery","taxonomy_name":"data access & permissions discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"Data access and permissions discovery establishes, before building, which data a use case needs, where\nit lives, who owns it, how you can technically and legally reach it, and how the source's permission\nrules must carry into the AI system. It exists because access approvals are often the longest lead\ntime in an enterprise AI project, and because a prototype built on an export quietly discards the\npermissions that production must honour.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"success-metrics-kpis","key":"scoping/success-metrics-kpis","module":"scoping","name":"Success metrics / KPIs","taxonomy_name":"success metrics / KPIs","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/roi-model","scoping/requirements-traceability","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate"],"tldr":"Success metrics are the numbers the customer will use to decide whether the AI project worked; KPIs\nare the business outcomes among them. Agree them before building, as a tree: one outcome KPI, a few\noperational drivers, system-quality metrics you can measure daily, and guardrails that must not get\nworse. You need the tree because model accuracy on its own proves no value, and any single metric gets gamed.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"baseline-measurement-before-deployment","key":"scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","module":"scoping","name":"Baseline measurement before deployment","taxonomy_name":"baseline measurement before deployment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/roi-model","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement"],"tldr":"A baseline is the measured value of each success metric under the current process, captured before\nthe AI system changes anything. Without it, an \"after\" number means nothing, and you cannot get it\nback later: the old process disappears and people's behaviour shifts. Measure variance as well as the\nmean, stratify by case type, audit human quality the same way you will audit the AI, and freeze the\ndefinitions.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"roi-model","key":"scoping/roi-model","module":"scoping","name":"ROI model","taxonomy_name":"ROI model","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","adoption/exec-communication"],"tldr":"An ROI model turns the success metrics into money: measured baseline × expected improvement ×\nadoption × capture rate, minus one-time and recurring costs, over a stated horizon. Build it as ranges\nwith a sensitivity analysis and a break-even point, not as a single heroic number. The assumptions\nthat decide AI ROI, such as how much freed time turns into real savings and how fast people adopt the\ntool, are rarely the ones vendors talk about.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"statement-of-work","key":"scoping/statement-of-work","module":"scoping","name":"Statement of work","taxonomy_name":"statement of work","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/requirements-traceability","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","scoping/timeboxing","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries"],"tldr":"A statement of work (SOW) is the project-specific contract that says what will be delivered, how\nacceptance is decided, what the customer must provide and by when, what is out of scope, and how\nchanges are handled. AI work needs a stricter SOW than ordinary software, because outputs are\nprobabilistic and progress depends on customer data and people. Acceptance must be statistical\nagainst an agreed eval set, and customer dependencies must be dated obligations.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"requirements-traceability","key":"scoping/requirements-traceability","module":"scoping","name":"Requirements traceability","taxonomy_name":"requirements traceability","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/golden-dataset","evals/regression-tests","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails"],"tldr":"Requirements traceability is the maintained link from each business objective to the requirements\nthat serve it, to the components and prompts that implement them, to the eval cases that verify them,\nand to the latest evidence. You need it because \"is this requirement met?\" and \"what does this change\nbreak?\" both need those links. With AI systems, a prompt or model change can silently affect\nrequirements nobody re-tested.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-and-risk-management","key":"scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","module":"scoping","name":"Dependency & risk management","taxonomy_name":"dependency & risk management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","scoping/timeboxing","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","reliability/fmea","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"AI projects are rarely late because of code. They are late because of things the team does not\ncontrol: data exports, access, security reviews, SME time, provider quotas. Dependency management maps\nthose inputs with owners, lead times and need-by dates. Risk management lists what could go wrong,\nsizes each risk in days or money, and attacks the biggest ones first with cheap spikes, so the worst\nsurprises arrive in week one rather than week nine.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeboxing","key":"scoping/timeboxing","module":"scoping","name":"Timeboxing","taxonomy_name":"timeboxing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Timeboxing fixes the time and lets scope flex: you commit to answering a specific question within a set\nbudget, then make an explicit decision (ship, continue, pivot, stop) when the box closes. It matters more\nin AI work than in ordinary software because quality improves asymptotically, so without a box a team can\nkeep tuning prompts indefinitely without ever deciding whether the approach is good enough.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"demos","key":"scoping/demos","module":"scoping","name":"Demos","taxonomy_name":"demos","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/customer-discovery","evals/golden-dataset","adoption/exec-communication","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"A demo is a decision instrument, not a performance: it should help a specific audience decide something,\nusing their real workflow and data. LLM systems make impressive demos dangerously easy, because a\ncherry-picked input can look magical while the typical input fails, so a good demo pairs the live story\nwith eval numbers, shows a failure honestly, and makes clear what is and is not yet built.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rapid-prototyping-poc","key":"scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","module":"scoping","name":"Rapid prototyping / PoC","taxonomy_name":"rapid prototyping / PoC","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/timeboxing","scoping/demos","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/golden-dataset","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment"],"tldr":"A rapid prototype is a cheap, usually disposable build that answers \"what should this be?\" by putting\nsomething in front of users. A proof of concept (PoC) answers \"can this work?\" by testing the single\nriskiest technical assumption against pass/fail criteria on real data. Both exist to buy information\ncheaply; they fail when they test the easy part, use clean sample data, or never end in a decision.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"pilot-to-production-plan","key":"scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","module":"scoping","name":"Pilot → production plan","taxonomy_name":"pilot → production plan","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/productionization","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","deployment/hypercare","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"A pilot puts the system in front of real users doing real work, at limited scope, so you can measure\nimpact against a baseline before betting the organization on it. The pilot → production plan defines the\nstages, the entry and exit criteria for each, the owners, and the work needed to close the gap between\n\"works for twenty people\" and \"runs for everyone\". Without it, successful pilots stall because nobody\nplanned the path, budget or ownership beyond them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"productionization","key":"scoping/productionization","module":"scoping","name":"Productionization","taxonomy_name":"productionization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","observability/tracing","reliability/slas-slos","deployment/runbooks","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy"],"tldr":"Productionization turns a prototype that works on the happy path into a system that keeps working under\nreal traffic, bad inputs, provider outages, model changes, adversaries and audits. The model call is\nusually a small fraction of the work; the rest is reliability, evaluation gates, observability, security,\ncost control and operational ownership. Plan it as a risk-ordered list of hardening work, not a final\npolish step.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"enterprise-integration","tier":3,"order":25,"title":"Enterprise integration","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the work between \"the model works\" and \"the model works on this customer's systems,\nfor their people, with their rules\". It has three strands. The data strand moves customer data in.\nConnectors handle auth, enumeration, change tracking, permissions and normalization for each system of\nrecord. Ingestion picks the least invasive channel the client will approve and lands a raw copy you can\nreplay. Incremental sync, CDC and webhooks keep that copy fresh. Retry, deduplication and reconciliation\nkeep it correct when calls fail, writes are ambiguous and events go missing. Legacy integration puts an\nanti-corruption layer around systems you cannot change. The data-understanding strand makes the data fit\nto use: staged onboarding with exit criteria, profiling against the use case, canonical schemas with\nversioned per-source mappings, and per-page triage of messy document estates. The identity strand decides\nwho can see what. SSO and SAML authenticate users through the customer's IdP. SCIM tells you who exists\nand when they leave. RBAC/ABAC mapping turns their groups and attributes into your permissions, enforced\nin retrieval and agent tools. Two operating lessons tie the strands together: binding each of your\nenvironments to the customer's sandbox or production with hard guards, and expressing per-tenant\ndifferences as typed configuration and flags instead of forks. Afterwards you can scope, build and\noperate a production integration with a new enterprise customer that stays fresh, does not leak data and\ndoes not need a fork for every tenant. It matters most for FDEs, who do this in every deployment, and for\nEngineers and Architects building the shared platform underneath.","why":{"engineer":"You build the connector contract, the webhook receiver that verifies, acks fast and queues, the idempotent writers, the watermark-with-overlap and delete handling, the SCIM endpoint whose deactivation revokes tokens and agent jobs, and the reconciliation job whose repair count tells you whether the whole pipeline is healthy.","architect":"You decide where the boundaries go: one canonical schema so N sources and M consumers need N + M mappings, an anti-corruption layer in front of every legacy system, permission checks enforced in retrieval filters and agent tool calls rather than in prompts, a stable user key of issuer plus subject, and tenant differences held as versioned data so one codebase serves fifty customers.","fde":"You are the one who negotiates what the client's security and platform teams will approve, sizes the backfill against their real rate limits, profiles their data before anyone promises a date, runs the mapping sessions with their data owners, wires up their Okta or Entra tenant, and makes sure nothing built against their sandbox writes into production by accident."},"mental_model":"Think of every enterprise integration as a copy of someone else's world, held under three contracts.\nYour index, feature table or agent's view of a customer system is a derived copy of data you do not own.\nThe **freshness contract** says how far behind the source that copy may be. Connectors, sync, CDC and\nwebhooks serve it, and reconciliation proves it. The **meaning contract** says what the copy's fields and\ndocuments actually mean. Profiling, mapping, canonical schemas and document triage serve it, all at one\ntranslation boundary so source quirks do not spread. The **access contract** says who may see or change\neach piece. SSO, SAML, SCIM and RBAC/ABAC mapping serve it, re-checked on every request because the\ncustomer's directory, not yours, is the source of truth. Every lesson strengthens one contract. Sandbox\ndiscipline and tenant configuration let you change how a contract is met for one customer without\nbreaking it for the others. When something goes wrong in production, ask first which contract broke:\nstale, misread, or over-shared.","order":["enterprise-connectors","data-ingestion-from-client-systems","incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","webhooks","retry-deduplication-reconciliation","legacy-system-integration","data-onboarding","data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","client-document-parsing","sso-iam-integration","saml-sso","scim-provisioning","rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","sandbox-vs-production-environments","tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"order_reasons":{"enterprise-connectors":"introduces the five responsibilities (auth, enumeration, change, permissions, normalization) that most later lessons expand on.","data-ingestion-from-client-systems":"choosing a channel the client will approve, landing raw data and checkpointing after the write are the base for any recurring pull.","incremental-synchronization-and-cdc":"once a full pull works, this shows how to fetch only changes, deletes included, and why naive watermarks miss rows.","webhooks":"adds a push channel for low lag, built on the sync and checkpoint ideas you just learned.","retry-deduplication-reconciliation":"explains the three distinct ways the channels above fail and the mechanism that covers each.","legacy-system-integration":"applies the same ingestion and safe-write habits to systems with batch windows, fixed record formats and no API.","data-onboarding":"the gated process that wraps these channels into a customer rollout, setting up the profiling and mapping stages next.","data-profiling-and-quality-assessment":"measures what the landed sample really contains before you commit to mappings or build.","data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas":"turns profiling findings into one internal model with versioned, reviewed per-source mappings.","client-document-parsing":"the unstructured counterpart to mapping, routing each page by type and keeping provenance for citations.","sso-iam-integration":"starts the identity strand: per-tenant IdP configuration, tenant-bound issuer checks and stable user keys.","saml-sso":"the older, still-required protocol, best learned after the general SSO model, along with its specific validation checklist.","scim-provisioning":"closes the gap SSO leaves by telling you who exists, which groups they are in and when they leave.","rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity":"needs both SSO claims and SCIM groups, and connects them to connector ACLs and agent scope.","sandbox-vs-production-environments":"once connectors and identity exist per customer, binds each to the right customer environment and blocks cross-wiring.","tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags":"last, because it is how every per-tenant choice from earlier lessons is stored, rolled out and audited."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are onboarding two enterprise tenants onto an AI support assistant that answers agents' questions\nfrom past tickets and policy documents. Tenant A runs a ServiceNow-like ticketing system with a REST API,\nwebhooks and a sandbox instance, and authenticates through Okta over OIDC. Tenant B exports tickets from\nan old in-house system as a nightly fixed-width file on SFTP, keeps policies as a mix of born-digital and\nscanned PDFs, and uses Entra ID over SAML. Using mock services and synthetic data (generated or provided),\nbuild the integration slice that serves both tenants from one codebase in about a week. Ingest both\nsources incrementally, with deletes. Receive Tenant A's webhooks safely and reconcile what they miss.\nProfile both datasets and map them into one canonical ticket schema. Triage and parse Tenant B's\ndocuments. Authenticate both tenants' users, provision them via SCIM, and enforce their group- and\nregion-based permissions in retrieval. Keep Tenant A's sandbox and production bindings impossible to mix\nup, and express every tenant difference as configuration.","deliverables":["A connector and ingestion service for both sources that lands immutable raw copies, advances checkpoints only after a successful write, applies deletes, throttles to a configured share of the source's rate limit, and includes a webhook receiver that verifies signatures, acks within a second, queues events and processes them idempotently.","A reconciliation job that compares source keys and content hashes with the index for each tenant, repairs drift, and reports missing, extra and stale counts per run.","A profiling report for each tenant's sample that classifies each finding as usable, fixable or blocking, with an owner, and a canonical ticket schema with versioned per-source mappings, a quarantine path and automated checks that run on every load.","A document triage pipeline for Tenant B that routes each page to text extraction, OCR or a layout model, records per-page confidence, sends low-confidence pages to a review queue, and stores page-level provenance used in citations.","OIDC login for Tenant A and SAML login for Tenant B with tenant-bound issuer and audience checks and users keyed by issuer plus subject, a SCIM 2.0 endpoint whose deactivation revokes sessions, tokens and scheduled jobs, and a per-tenant mapping config that turns IdP group IDs into roles and region attributes into ABAC rules applied as retrieval filters.","A typed, versioned tenant configuration file and a release flag for the new retriever, plus an environment binding with a guard that refuses to start when a sandbox credential points at a production index or the reverse."],"rubric":["Killing the webhook receiver for ten minutes and deleting three source records during the outage leads to all three being removed from the index on the next reconciliation run, and the run's repair count shows it.","Replaying the same webhook batch twice, and retrying a timed-out write-back, produces no duplicate index entries or duplicate notes in the mock ticketing system.","A synthetic user in Tenant B's EMEA group never retrieves an APAC ticket or a restricted policy page, a SCIM deactivation removes their access within a stated, measured time, and a Tenant A token is rejected at Tenant B's workspace.","Every canonical field can be traced to a mapping version and a source field, an unmapped status value goes to quarantine with an open question for the data owner, and no consumer reads a source's native shape.","Scanned pages with no text layer show up in the route mix and the review queue, none silently drop out of the index, and every answer cites a document and page.","Grepping the codebase for either tenant's name or ID finds nothing outside configuration and test fixtures, and enabling the new retriever for one tenant leaves the other tenant's behavior unchanged."]}},"topics":[{"id":"enterprise-connectors","key":"enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Enterprise connectors (Salesforce, SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams)","taxonomy_name":"enterprise connectors (Salesforce, SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"An enterprise connector is the adapter that pulls content, metadata and permissions out of a system of\nrecord (Salesforce, SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams) and keeps that copy\ncurrent, or that lets an agent act in it. The hard part is not the first fetch: it is authentication,\nrate limits, incremental change, deletions and faithfully carrying each system's permission model, because\nan AI assistant that ignores any of these is either stale or leaks data.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"legacy-system-integration","key":"enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Legacy system integration","taxonomy_name":"legacy system integration","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"Legacy systems (mainframes, AS/400s, old ERPs, in-house databases) hold the data and transactions an AI\nfeature needs, but they offer no modern API, run on batch windows and fixed record formats, and cannot be\nchanged or overloaded. You integrate by wrapping them behind an anti-corruption layer that picks the least\ninvasive access path (file drop, replica, message queue, existing service, or screen automation) and\ntranslates their model into yours, so the legacy quirks stop at one boundary.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sso-iam-integration","key":"enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SSO / IAM integration (Okta, Entra ID)","taxonomy_name":"SSO / IAM integration (Okta, Entra ID)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/saml-sso","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","system-design/tenant-isolation","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust"],"tldr":"Enterprise customers will not let their employees create passwords in your product: identity must come\nfrom their identity provider (IdP), usually Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, via OIDC or SAML. Integrating means\nper-tenant IdP configuration, strict token validation, a stable user key (issuer plus subject, not email),\nmapping IdP groups or app roles to permissions, and deciding how deprovisioning and agent delegation flow\nfrom the IdP, because the customer's IdP, not your database, is now the source of truth for who someone is.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"saml-sso","key":"enterprise-integration/saml-sso","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SAML SSO","taxonomy_name":"SAML SSO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling"],"tldr":"SAML 2.0 lets a customer's identity provider tell your application, in a signed XML assertion delivered\nthrough the user's browser, who the user is and what attributes they have. It predates OIDC and remains\nmandatory for many enterprises. The protocol is simple; safe implementation is not, because XML signature\nhandling is subtle, so you use a maintained library and still validate audience, recipient, time window,\nrequest correlation and replay yourself.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"scim-provisioning","key":"enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SCIM provisioning","taxonomy_name":"SCIM provisioning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/saml-sso","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"SCIM 2.0 is a standard REST API your application exposes so the customer's identity provider can create,\nupdate, deactivate and group users in your product automatically. SSO tells you who is logging in; SCIM\ntells you who *exists*, what groups they are in, and, most importantly, when they leave. Without it,\nterminated employees and stale group memberships linger in your app and in every permission filter built on them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","key":"enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"RBAC / ABAC mapping to customer identity","taxonomy_name":"RBAC / ABAC mapping to customer identity","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","backend-fundamentals/rbac","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"Your product has its own permission model; each customer's identity provider has its own groups and\nattributes. Mapping is the per-tenant translation layer between them: customer group IDs become your roles\n(RBAC), and customer attributes such as department, region or clearance become inputs to policies (ABAC).\nIt must be explicit, stored as configuration, keyed by immutable IDs and evaluated on every request,\nbecause the AI layer (retrieval filters, agent tools) is where a wrong mapping turns into a data leak.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"webhooks","key":"enterprise-integration/webhooks","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Webhooks","taxonomy_name":"webhooks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","system-design/event-driven-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"A webhook is an HTTP POST a source system sends to your endpoint when something changes, so you learn about\nchanges in seconds without polling. Delivery is at-least-once, unordered and from the public internet, so a\ncorrect receiver verifies the signature, acknowledges fast, queues the event, processes it idempotently,\ntreats the payload as a hint to re-fetch current state, and runs periodic reconciliation for the events that\nnever arrive.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","key":"enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Incremental synchronization & CDC","taxonomy_name":"incremental synchronization & CDC","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Incremental synchronization copies only what changed since the last run instead of reloading everything;\nchange data capture (CDC) is its strongest form, reading inserts, updates and deletes from the database's\nown transaction log. `updated_at` watermarks are simple but miss hard deletes and late commits; API delta\ntokens move the bookkeeping to the source; log-based CDC sees every committed change, at the price of\noperating a replication pipeline. This choice sets how fresh and how expensive an AI index is.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"retry-deduplication-reconciliation","key":"enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Retry / deduplication / reconciliation","taxonomy_name":"retry / deduplication / reconciliation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/idempotency","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/message-queues"],"tldr":"An integration with a customer's systems crosses a boundary you do not control: calls fail, writes\nsucceed without telling you, and events never arrive. **Retry** handles the first, **deduplication**\nmakes retries safe against the second, and **reconciliation**, the scheduled comparison of source and\ntarget, catches the third. Each covers a failure the other two cannot see, which is why mature\nintegrations run all three and treat the reconciliation job's repair count as the health metric of the\nwhole pipeline.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sandbox-vs-production-environments","key":"enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Sandbox vs production environments","taxonomy_name":"sandbox vs production environments","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","deployment/uat","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"Customer systems come in pairs: a production instance with real data and real side effects, and\nsandboxes (Salesforce sandboxes, SAP development and quality systems, ServiceNow sub-production\ninstances) that resemble it but differ in data, configuration, integrations and limits. An AI\nintegration is built and verified against the sandbox, promoted to production deliberately, and\nprevented by hard guards from mixing the two, because a system that reads stale sandbox data behaves\ndifferently from one reading production, and one that writes to production by mistake is an incident.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","key":"enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Tenant-specific configuration & feature flags","taxonomy_name":"tenant-specific configuration & feature flags","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","prompting/prompt-versioning","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"Every enterprise customer wants something slightly different: another model, a stricter guardrail, a\ndifferent connector, a feature switched off until their legal team signs off. Tenant-specific\nconfiguration expresses those differences as typed, versioned data resolved at runtime, and feature\nflags decide who gets which code path, so one codebase serves every customer. The alternative, a branch\nor fork per customer, works for the first three and becomes unmaintainable by the tenth.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-onboarding","key":"enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data onboarding","taxonomy_name":"data onboarding","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments"],"tldr":"Data onboarding is the repeatable process that takes a new customer from \"access granted\" to \"their data\nis in the system, validated and syncing\". It runs in gated stages: inventory, a small sample, profiling,\nmapping, a pilot load checked by the customer's experts, then a full backfill and incremental sync. It\nexists because the slowest and riskiest part of an enterprise AI deployment is usually the customer's\ndata, not the model, and a big-bang import hides problems until users find them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-ingestion-from-client-systems","key":"enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data ingestion from client systems","taxonomy_name":"data ingestion from client systems","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","deployment/private-endpoints","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"Ingesting from a client's systems means pulling data you do not own, through interfaces the client\nchooses, across a network boundary they control, from systems their business runs on. The durable\ndesign picks the least invasive channel the client can offer (API, replica, file drop, events or CDC),\nlands an immutable raw copy, advances a checkpoint only after a successful write, throttles to protect\nthe source, and reconciles because some changes, especially deletes, are invisible to the channel.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","key":"enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data mapping & canonical schemas (schema mapping)","taxonomy_name":"data mapping & canonical schemas (schema mapping)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/data-contracts","scoping/domain-modeling","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","data-architecture/data-lineage","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation"],"tldr":"Every client represents the same business concepts differently: one CRM's \"Account\" is another's\n\"Customer\", dates arrive in three formats, and a status of \"Closed\" may mean won, lost or cancelled. A\ncanonical schema is your one internal model of those concepts; schema mapping is the per-source,\nversioned translation into it. With N sources and M consumers you then maintain about N + M mappings\ninstead of N × M, and every downstream component, prompts and retrieval filters included, reads one shape.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","key":"enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data profiling & quality assessment","taxonomy_name":"data profiling & quality assessment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","data-architecture/data-contracts","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","llmops-platform/drift","security-governance-compliance/data-classification"],"tldr":"Profiling measures what a client's data actually contains (null rates, value sets, formats, duplicates,\nfreshness, document types) instead of what its documentation claims. Quality assessment judges those\nmeasurements against what the use case needs and turns them into decisions: usable, fixable, or\nblocking. The findings then become automated checks on every load, because data that was fine at\nonboarding drifts, and an AI system degrades quietly on bad data rather than crashing.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"client-document-parsing","key":"enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Client document parsing (PDF, scans, OCR)","taxonomy_name":"client document parsing (PDF, scans, OCR)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/document-parsing","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","safety-security/pii-redaction","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"A client's document estate is a mix: born-digital PDFs with a text layer, scanned PDFs that are only\nimages, faxes, photos of forms, password-protected files and decades of templates. Parsing it well is\nless about one parser than about triage: detect what each file is, route it to the cheapest method that\nhandles it (text extraction, OCR, or a layout or vision model), score confidence per page, send the\ndoubtful pages to review, and keep page and position provenance for citations.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"customer-data-evaluation","tier":3,"order":26,"title":"Customer data & evaluation","intro":{"overview":"This module is about proving, on one customer's own data and by their own experts' standard, that an\nAI system does the job they are paying for, and keeping it true after go-live. The evals module teaches\nthe measuring instrument in general; this one teaches how to build and run it inside a client\nengagement, where the data is governed, SME time is scarce, and acceptance is a contract. It starts by\nturning business KPIs into signed acceptance criteria with baselines and sample sizes, then builds the\nreference those criteria are measured against: a golden dataset agreed by client SMEs, sampled by\nstratum so rare high-cost cases are dense enough to judge, labelled under written annotation\nguidelines in facilitated labeling sessions. Domain prompt engineering turns the tacit rules those\nsessions surface into tested instructions, and eval review loops sort every failure into system, label,\nguideline or grader error. Once a system is accepted, customer-specific regression suites stop shared\nchanges from breaking one customer quietly, per-customer guardrails enforce each tenant's policy in\ncode, and fine-tuning pipelines, model integration and shadow mode let you change the model under a live\ncustomer with evidence instead of hope. Production feedback classification and feedback loops to\nproduct close the cycle, turning thumbs-down and tickets into routed failure modes, regression cases and\nroadmap decisions. Afterwards you can run a pilot from scoping to sign-off and keep a multi-customer\ndeployment healthy. It matters most for FDEs, who own the customer relationship and the acceptance\ndecision, and for architects designing a platform that serves many customers from shared components.","why":{"engineer":"You build the per-customer regression suite, the tenant guardrail engine and the leakage-safe fine-tuning pipeline, and gate every shared prompt or model change on paired, case-by-case flips for each customer rather than on a blended global score.","architect":"You design where customer specificity lives (versioned guardrail config with a platform floor, per-customer adapters, model adapters with capability checks, per-tenant suites) so that one platform serves many customers without forking code or letting averages hide a regression.","fde":"You get the sponsor to sign KPI-based acceptance criteria before results exist, run the SME labeling sessions that produce an authoritative golden set, and bring shadow-mode evidence to the customer's risk committee before any user sees a new model."},"mental_model":"Picture the customer's judgement as the only ruler that counts, and the module as the work of copying\nthat ruler faithfully and keeping it in use. First you agree what the ruler measures: business KPIs\ntranslated into proxies, thresholds and sample sizes, signed in advance. Then you manufacture the ruler:\nreal cases sampled by stratum, labelled by the customer's SMEs under a written guideline, with\ndisagreements resolved as policy and the set signed off. Every improvement, whether a domain prompt, a\nfine-tuned adapter or a different model, is a candidate measured against that ruler, and every failure\nis first asked \"is the system wrong, or is the ruler?\" After acceptance the ruler multiplies and\ntravels: frozen per-customer regression suites gate shared changes, guardrails encode each customer's\nrules, shadow mode checks the candidate on live traffic, and classified production feedback adds new\nmarks to the ruler and sends shared problems back to the product. A number you cannot trace to the\ncustomer's experts is not evidence.","order":["acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","representative-test-case-sampling","sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","labeling-sessions","domain-prompt-engineering","eval-review-loops","customer-specific-regression-suites","per-customer-guardrails","fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","model-integration","shadow-mode-validation","production-feedback-classification","feedback-loops-to-product"],"order_reasons":{"acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis":"fixes what \"accepted\" means in business terms first, because it drives dataset size, strata and pilot design.","golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes":"builds the signed, governed reference set the acceptance criteria are measured against.","representative-test-case-sampling":"chooses which real cases enter that set so the score predicts production and rare strata have enough items.","sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines":"writes the shared standard SMEs apply, so labels measure the system rather than reviewer differences.","labeling-sessions":"turns the guideline into labels at speed and surfaces the tacit rules that only live disagreement reveals.","domain-prompt-engineering":"encodes those SME rules, definitions and exceptions as tested instructions, the cheapest lever once a golden set exists.","eval-review-loops":"teaches reading failures with SMEs and attributing each to system, label, guideline or grader before changing anything.","customer-specific-regression-suites":"freezes each customer's accepted cases and fixed incidents so shared changes are gated per customer.","per-customer-guardrails":"makes each tenant's rules versioned, enforced-in-code policy on top of a platform floor, tested on their own data.","fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data":"covers the audited, repeatable path to a per-customer adapter when prompting has plateaued.","model-integration":"shows what really changes when a new or customer-mandated model, or a fresh adapter, goes behind the system.","shadow-mode-validation":"validates that candidate model on live customer traffic with zero user impact before any exposure.","production-feedback-classification":"turns raw production signals into labelled failure modes with severity and an owning component.","feedback-loops-to-product":"routes those classified failures into shared fixes, regression cases and a reply to the customer, closing the cycle."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer for a vendor whose AI system classifies and drafts responses to\ninbound customer-service emails for a regional utility company. The sponsor, the head of customer\noperations, wants average handle time down 25% with no increase in misrouted complaints, and the\nregulator treats complaint misrouting as a reportable issue. Using a synthetic or anonymised set of\nabout 3,000 emails (provided or generated, including a 3% stratum of formal complaints and a 2% stratum\nof vulnerable-customer cases) and two or three volunteers acting as the utility's SMEs, run the\nengagement end to end over a week. Draft and sign acceptance criteria with a baseline, sample and label\na golden set under a written guideline, iterate a domain prompt through review loops, and freeze a\nregression suite. Then the utility mandates a move to a model hosted in its own cloud tenancy: integrate\nit, shadow it against the current system on replayed traffic, configure the utility's guardrails, and\nclassify a batch of simulated production feedback into routed issues, one of which becomes a product\nfix with a regression case.","deliverables":["A signed acceptance document mapping each business KPI (handle time, misrouting rate, complaint recall) to a proxy, a baseline measurement, a threshold, a sample size with its expected interval width, and the split between offline and pilot criteria.","A versioned golden set of at least 300 items, stratified with floors for complaints and vulnerable-customer cases and re-weighting back to the traffic mix, labelled under a written annotation guideline with a decision order, boundary examples and an unsure path, plus a double-labelled subset with agreement reported per criterion.","A labeling-session log and a domain prompt in which every rule traces to a logged SME decision and at least one golden-set case, plus an eval review log attributing every failure from two rounds to system, label, guideline or grader error.","A per-customer regression suite and harness that compares a candidate against production case by case and gates on blocking-case failures, and a guardrail config for the utility layered on a platform floor with tighten-only merging and tests on the utility's labelled data.","A model-integration and shadow-mode report for the mandated model covering adapter changes, tokenizer and context effects, prompt porting, paired flips on the regression suite, adjudicated disagreements, latency, cost and confirmation that the shadow path made no writes.","A feedback taxonomy built from at least 100 simulated production signals classified at trace level, with per-class precision against human labels, and one evidence-backed product issue carried through to a fix, a new regression case and a written reply to the reporting customer."],"rubric":["Thresholds and sample sizes are dated before any result is shown, the misrouting baseline is measured on the current human process rather than assumed to be zero, and the acceptance verdict reports each criterion with its interval, not a point estimate.","The complaint and vulnerable-customer strata each contain enough items to tell 70% from 90% recall, and the headline score is re-weighted to the real traffic mix with the re-weighting shown.","At least one review-loop failure is resolved by changing a label or the guideline rather than the prompt, and agreement on the double-labelled subset is reported with the guideline version it was measured under.","A planted change that improves the global score while breaking a utility-specific rule is caught by the per-customer suite and blocked, with the flipped cases listed.","The utility's complaint-routing rule fires in a test where the prompt instruction is bypassed, proving enforcement sits outside the prompt, and the platform floor cannot be lowered by customer config.","The shadow report gives a clear go, hold or no-go for the mandated model with its evidence, and names at least one difference (tool-call format, tokenizer or error semantics) that a smoke test would have missed."]}},"topics":[{"id":"domain-prompt-engineering","key":"customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Domain prompt engineering","taxonomy_name":"domain prompt engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/few-shot","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-versioning","scoping/domain-modeling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops"],"tldr":"Domain prompt engineering is turning one customer's tacit expertise (their vocabulary, policies,\nexceptions and output conventions) into explicit instructions, definitions and examples a general model\ncan follow. It exists because a model knows the public version of a domain but not this customer's\ndefinition of \"late shipment\" or \"material change\", and a persona line cannot supply that. The work is\nelicitation and testing, not wordsmithing: every rule should trace to an SME decision and an eval case.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","key":"customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Golden dataset creation with client SMEs","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset creation with client SMEs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"A golden dataset built with client SMEs is a versioned set of the customer's real inputs with reference\nanswers their own experts agreed on, so \"is it good enough?\" is settled against the customer's judgement\nrather than the vendor's. The hard parts are not the file format: getting scarce expert time, sampling\nthe right cases, resolving expert disagreement as policy, handling data governance, and getting a formal\nsign-off that makes the set authoritative.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"representative-test-case-sampling","key":"customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Representative test-case sampling","taxonomy_name":"representative test-case sampling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/error-analysis","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites"],"tldr":"Representative test-case sampling chooses which of a customer's real inputs go into the eval set so that\nits score predicts production behaviour. A random sample mirrors traffic but starves rare high-stakes\ncases; a hand-picked one reflects what people remember, not what happens. The fix is stratified\nsampling from recent production with a floor per important stratum, then re-weighting results back to\nthe real mix, so you get both a trustworthy overall number and enough cases per segment to act on.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","key":"customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"SME review & annotation guidelines","taxonomy_name":"SME review & annotation guidelines","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/rubrics","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","training-adaptation/data-labeling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias"],"tldr":"Annotation guidelines are the written contract for how a customer's SMEs judge model outputs: the label\nset, an operational definition of each label, a decision order, worked boundary examples and an explicit\n\"unsure\" path. SME review is the protocol around them: blind, randomised, independently labelled, with\nagreement measured and disagreements fed back into the guideline. Without both, every eval number\ninherits the unmeasured noise of experts applying different private standards.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"labeling-sessions","key":"customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Labeling sessions","taxonomy_name":"labeling sessions","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","training-adaptation/data-labeling","adoption/stakeholder-management"],"tldr":"A labeling session is a scheduled, facilitated block where a customer's SMEs label eval data together:\na short calibration, independent labelling, then structured discussion of disagreements whose outcomes\nare written into the guideline. Sessions exist because asynchronous \"please label when you can\" requests\nto busy experts stall, and because tacit rules surface only when experts explain disagreements live. The\nfacilitator's job is throughput and decisions, while protecting label independence.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-review-loops","key":"customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Eval review loops","taxonomy_name":"eval review loops","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis"],"tldr":"An eval review loop is the recurring cycle in which a team runs the customer eval, reads the failures\nwith SMEs, classifies each as a system error, a label error or a guideline gap, fixes the right thing,\nand re-runs with regression checks. It exists because an eval score alone says how much is wrong but\nnot why, and because labels, guidelines and judges are themselves wrong in ways only expert review\nreveals. Without a held-out set, the loop overfits to the cases it keeps looking at.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","key":"customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Acceptance testing against customer KPIs","taxonomy_name":"acceptance testing against customer KPIs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","deployment/uat","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes"],"tldr":"Acceptance testing against customer KPIs is the pre-agreed test that decides whether an AI system is\naccepted: business KPIs (handle time, error rate, throughput) are translated into measurable proxies,\nthresholds and sample sizes, signed before results are seen, then measured offline on the golden set and\nin a pilot against a baseline. It exists because \"the model scores 91%\" does not answer \"did we reduce\ninvoice processing cost without more errors?\", and because thresholds set after the fact get renegotiated.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"customer-specific-regression-suites","key":"customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Customer-specific regression suites","taxonomy_name":"customer-specific regression suites","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/regression-tests","evals/golden-dataset","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation"],"tldr":"A customer-specific regression suite is a frozen set of that customer's own cases, run with that\ncustomer's configuration, every time a shared prompt, model or pipeline changes. It exists because a\nchange can raise the global eval score while breaking one customer's documents, jargon or contract\nrules, and averages hide it. Each suite is gated on its own result, compared case by case against the\ncurrent production version, not against a global threshold.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"shadow-mode-validation","key":"customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Shadow-mode validation","taxonomy_name":"shadow-mode validation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","agents/sandboxing","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan"],"tldr":"Shadow mode runs a candidate system (new model, prompt or pipeline) on a copy of real production\ntraffic while only the current system's answer reaches users. You compare the two outputs offline. It\nexists because curated eval sets never match a customer's real input distribution, and you want that\nevidence before any user is exposed. It measures agreement and judged quality, not user outcomes, and\nit is only safe if the candidate cannot cause side effects.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"per-customer-guardrails","key":"customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Per-customer guardrails","taxonomy_name":"per-customer guardrails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/pii-redaction","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","system-design/tenant-isolation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","safety-security/nemo-guardrails"],"tldr":"Per-customer guardrails are input, output and action policies resolved per tenant at request time: a\nplatform safety floor nobody can lower, plus each customer's own rules and thresholds layered on top.\nThey exist because one global policy is too strict for some customers and too loose for others. The\nrules live in versioned config, are enforced in code rather than in the prompt, and each customer's\nrules are tested on that customer's own labelled data.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","key":"customer-data-evaluation/fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Fine-tuning pipelines on client data","taxonomy_name":"fine-tuning pipelines on client data","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","customer-data-evaluation/model-integration"],"tldr":"A fine-tuning pipeline on client data is a repeatable, audited path from a customer's records to a\ndeployed model: permission check, extraction, redaction, cleaning, leakage-safe splits, training\n(usually a per-customer adapter), evaluation against the prompted baseline, and registration with full\nlineage. It must be a pipeline, not a notebook, because you will have to rerun it: after a base-model\ndeprecation, a data-deletion request or a new batch of labels.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-integration","key":"customer-data-evaluation/model-integration","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Model integration","taxonomy_name":"model integration","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-selection","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","python-for-ai/litellm","prompting/function-tool-calling","llmops-platform/model-registry","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data"],"tldr":"Model integration is the work of putting a different model behind a delivered system: a new provider,\na fine-tuned adapter, or the model a customer mandates (their own cloud deployment or an on-prem open-\nweight model). A model is not a drop-in part: prompts, tool-call formats, tokenizers, context limits,\nerror semantics and quotas all differ. So integration means a normalising adapter, a capability check,\nre-tuned prompts, and the customer's eval suite as the gate before traffic moves.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"production-feedback-classification","key":"customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Production feedback classification","taxonomy_name":"production feedback classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/llm-as-a-judge","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","observability/tracing","adoption/feature-request-triage","adoption/usage-analytics","customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product"],"tldr":"Production feedback classification turns raw signals (thumbs-down, comments, edits, escalations,\ntickets) into labelled failure modes, each with a severity and an owning component. It exists because a\nthumbs-down rate tells you something is wrong but not what or who should fix it. The classifier labels\nthe whole trace, not just the comment, uses a taxonomy grown from real failures, and is checked against\nhuman labels.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"feedback-loops-to-product","key":"customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Feedback loops to product","taxonomy_name":"feedback loops to product","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","adoption/feature-request-triage","adoption/continuous-improvement","evals/eval-driven-development","adoption/usage-analytics","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops"],"tldr":"A feedback loop to product carries what is learned in customer deployments back into the shared\nproduct: classified failures and requests become evidence-backed issues, a regular triage decides\nfix-in-config, fix-in-product or roadmap, every fix lands with a regression case, and the reporting\ncustomer hears what changed. Without the loop, field teams patch the same problem separately in each\ndeployment, and the product never learns from its users.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"deployment","tier":3,"order":27,"title":"Deployment","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what happens between \"the AI system works in our environment\" and \"the customer's\nusers depend on it, and the customer's team can run it\". Enterprise deployment is shaped by constraints\nthe builders do not control. Compliance constraints turn regimes such as HIPAA, GDPR or export control\ninto concrete rules about where data may be processed, which model providers qualify, and what logs\nmust contain and leave out. Those rules choose the deployment target: your SaaS, the customer's VPC,\ntheir on-prem cluster or an air-gapped site. That target decides the network design: private endpoints\nfor one-way calls to managed services with no internet egress, and site-to-site VPNs for two-way access\nto systems in the customer's data center. Terraform makes that infrastructure reproducible in every\ncustomer account and auditable for every reviewer. Security questionnaires and security and\narchitecture reviews are how the customer checks your claims and your design before they let you near\ntheir data. Health checks and runbooks make the system operable by people who did not build it. UAT\nproves that it does the users' job against acceptance criteria agreed in advance. Rollout criteria and\nrollback triggers set how exposure grows and what pulls it back. A deployment readiness review collects\nall of that evidence into one go/no-go decision, and hypercare carries the system through its first\nweeks of real traffic to a criteria-based handoff. Afterwards you can choose and justify a deployment\nmodel, design its network path, pass a customer security review, and run a staged go-live you could\ndefend in a postmortem. It matters most for FDEs, who live this work on every engagement, and for\nArchitects, whose early design choices decide whether a deployment can pass review at all.","why":{"engineer":"You write the Terraform modules, private endpoint DNS overrides and VPN routes that let the same system install cleanly in each customer account. You implement separate liveness, readiness, startup and synthetic checks. You wire automated rollback triggers to error, latency, cost and sampled quality signals, and you keep the runbooks that every paging alert links to.","architect":"You turn compliance regimes into data residency, provider and logging constraints before the design is fixed. You choose between SaaS, customer VPC, on-prem and air-gapped with the support cost priced in. You walk into the customer's architecture review with an accurate data-flow diagram, a threat model that covers prompt injection and tool permissions, and evidence for every control.","fde":"You run the customer-facing sequence: answer the questionnaire honestly from one source of truth, steer the security review to dated findings, set acceptance criteria and sample sizes for UAT, chair the readiness review, and run hypercare with daily triage until the customer's team meets the exit criteria and owns support."},"mental_model":"Picture deployment as a series of gates the system passes through, each one opened by evidence rather\nthan confidence. The first gate is permission: compliance constraints and the customer's policies decide\nwhat is allowed at all, and that picks the deployment target. The second is reachability: private\nendpoints and VPNs define exactly which network paths exist, and nothing else should. The third is\nreproducibility: Terraform makes the environment a reviewed diff, not a set of console clicks. The\nfourth is trust: questionnaires check your claims, and architecture reviews test your design. The fifth\nis operability: health checks tell infrastructure what to do, and runbooks tell humans. The sixth is\nacceptance: UAT shows that real users get their job done. The readiness review is the checkpoint where\nall of that evidence is inspected together, with items proven, waived with an expiry, or blocking.\nAfter it, rollout criteria widen exposure in stages, rollback triggers narrow it again, and hypercare\nholds the system under close watch until ownership moves to the customer. Every gate produces an\nartifact that the next one consumes. When a gate is skipped, you usually find out in production.","order":["compliance-constraints","customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","private-endpoints","vpn","terraform","security-questionnaires","security-and-architecture-reviews","health-checks","runbooks","uat","rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment-readiness-reviews","hypercare"],"order_reasons":{"compliance-constraints":"start here, because regimes and customer policies set the limits that every later deployment choice must fit inside.","customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped":"compliance and data custody pick the deployment target, which shapes the network, packaging and support model.","private-endpoints":"the default way to reach managed services and model APIs from a customer network with no internet egress.","vpn":"the alternative for two-way access to on-prem systems, best learned against private endpoints so the trade-off is clear.","terraform":"once the target and network paths are known, IaC makes them reproducible and reviewable in every customer account.","security-questionnaires":"the first trust check a buyer runs, and answering it needs the architecture and network design you now have.","security-and-architecture-reviews":"goes deeper than the questionnaire, testing your data flows, trust boundaries and AI-specific threats against evidence.","health-checks":"makes the deployed system observable to infrastructure, as the base for runbooks and rollout signals.","runbooks":"gives the humans who respond to health alerts and known failure modes a tested way to mitigate.","uat":"real users test the system against acceptance criteria agreed in advance, which needs a working, observable environment.","rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers":"defines how exposure grows after acceptance and which signals pull it back, using the health and quality metrics from earlier lessons.","deployment-readiness-reviews":"collects evidence from every earlier lesson into one go/no-go decision, so it only makes sense once you know what that evidence is.","hypercare":"the last step, carrying the system through its first weeks of real traffic to a criteria-based handoff."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer taking a retrieval-augmented claims assistant from a successful\npilot to production at a regional health insurer. The assistant answers adjusters' questions from\npolicy documents and claim notes that contain protected health information. The insurer runs on AWS\nin one region, permits no internet egress from data workloads, and keeps its claims database in an\non-prem data center. Its security team has sent a 120-question questionnaire and has scheduled an\narchitecture review. Go-live is planned for 300 adjusters across two regions in six weeks. Over a\nweek, using a sandbox cloud account and synthetic claim data, produce everything that deployment\nneeds. Turn the insurer's obligations into constraints and choose the deployment model. Write Terraform\nfor the VPC, the private endpoints to the model provider and storage, and the VPN to a simulated\non-prem network. Answer a representative slice of the questionnaire and prepare the architecture\nreview pack. Implement health checks and write runbooks for the top failure modes. Design and run a\nsmall UAT. Write the staged rollout plan with rollback triggers. Chair a mock readiness review, and\nplan hypercare through to handoff.","deliverables":["A constraints register that maps each regime and customer policy to concrete where, who, what, how-long, how and proof constraints, with a one-page justification of the chosen deployment model and the rejected alternatives with their costs.","A Terraform repository that stands up the VPC, private endpoints with working DNS overrides, and a site-to-site VPN to a simulated on-prem network, with a reviewed plan output, remote state, and evidence that a clean apply into a second account succeeds.","Answers to at least 25 questionnaire items drawn from a single answer library, plus an architecture review pack with a data-flow diagram, trust boundaries, a threat model covering prompt injection and tool permissions, and a findings tracker with owners and due dates.","Liveness, readiness, startup and synthetic end-to-end checks running against the deployed service, and runbooks for at least five failure modes (provider outage, rate limiting, cost runaway, index staleness, an unsafe output report), each linked from its alert.","A UAT plan and results with acceptance criteria, a scoring rubric, a justified sample size, the pass rate with a confidence interval, and defects classified by cause.","A rollout ladder with stage criteria, minimum samples, hard and soft rollback triggers, a readiness review record with every item marked proven, waived with an expiry, or blocking, and a hypercare plan with a triage cadence and written exit criteria."],"rubric":["VPC flow logs show zero bytes reaching the model provider or storage through NAT or the internet, and the services reject requests that do not come through the private endpoint.","Liveness does not depend on downstream services: taking the model provider or vector store offline in a test removes instances from readiness without triggering restarts, and the synthetic check alerts.","Every questionnaire answer is traceable to a control owner and evidence, and at least one answer is an honest \"no\" with a compensating control and a dated plan, not an aspirational \"yes\".","Running each runbook in a rehearsal, by someone other than its author, mitigates the simulated failure without escalation, and any step that turns out wrong is recorded and fixed.","The UAT pass rate is reported with its interval and per-slice results, and model-quality defects are separated from integration bugs, each with a different owner.","Every rollback trigger has a numeric threshold, a minimum sample size and a named action. A replayed incident shows the trigger firing and exposure dropping to the previous stage.","Hypercare exit criteria are things the customer's team demonstrates, such as resolving a set share of tickets alone and running one runbook unassisted, not a date on the calendar."]}},"topics":[{"id":"customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","key":"deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","module":"deployment","name":"Customer VPC / on-prem / air-gapped deployment","taxonomy_name":"customer VPC / on-prem / air-gapped","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/vpn","deployment/terraform","deployment/compliance-constraints","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Three deployment targets where the customer, not you, owns the perimeter: in a **customer VPC** your\nsoftware runs inside their cloud account; **on-prem** it runs on their hardware and their Kubernetes;\n**air-gapped** it runs with no network path to the outside at all. They exist because some data may\nnot leave the customer's custody. Each step inward trades your operability (telemetry, upgrades,\nhosted frontier models) for their control, so the engineering job becomes making the product\ninstallable, verifiable and upgradable without the network you are used to.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"private-endpoints","key":"deployment/private-endpoints","module":"deployment","name":"Private endpoints","taxonomy_name":"private endpoints","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/vpn","deployment/terraform","deployment/compliance-constraints","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture"],"tldr":"A private endpoint gives a managed service (an LLM API, object storage, a vector database, your SaaS)\na private IP address inside the consumer's own network, so traffic to it never crosses the public\ninternet and the service can have its public access switched off. It exists because enterprises want\nto call cloud services without opening internet egress, and it works by pairing a network interface\nwith a DNS override. The DNS half is where most failures live.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"vpn","key":"deployment/vpn","module":"deployment","name":"VPN","taxonomy_name":"VPN","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems"],"tldr":"A VPN joins two private networks (site-to-site) or a device to a network (remote access) through an\nencrypted tunnel over the public internet, so systems can talk using private addresses as if they were\non one network. AI deployments need it because the data that matters often sits in a customer's data\ncenter and cannot be exposed publicly. It is cheap and quick to set up, but it joins address spaces and\ncarries everything routed into it, so scope, routing and MTU need deliberate design.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"terraform","key":"deployment/terraform","module":"deployment","name":"Terraform (infrastructure as code)","taxonomy_name":"Terraform (IaC)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":19,"related":["deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture"],"tldr":"Infrastructure as code (IaC) describes networks, clusters, GPU node pools, buckets and permissions in\nversioned files that a tool reconciles against reality, instead of clicking through consoles. Terraform\nis the most widely used multi-cloud IaC tool: you declare desired state in HCL, it diffs that against a recorded\nstate file and the provider APIs, shows a plan, and applies only the difference. That makes environments\nreproducible, reviewable and repeatable in every customer account you deploy to.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-questionnaires","key":"deployment/security-questionnaires","module":"deployment","name":"Security questionnaires","taxonomy_name":"security questionnaires","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","deployment/compliance-constraints","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/ai-security","safety-security/data-leakage","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"Before an enterprise lets a vendor touch its data, its third-party risk team sends a security\nquestionnaire: hundreds of questions on encryption, access control, incident response, subprocessors and\nnow AI-specific data use. It exists because the buyer cannot audit every vendor, so it collects written\nclaims and evidence that become part of the contract. Answer from one maintained source of truth,\nprecisely and never aspirationally, because every answer is a commitment you may be held to.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-and-architecture-reviews","key":"deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","module":"deployment","name":"Security & architecture reviews","taxonomy_name":"security & architecture reviews","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["deployment/security-questionnaires","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/ai-security","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms"],"tldr":"A security and architecture review is the customer's (or your own) structured examination of a proposed\nsystem before it goes live: data flows, trust boundaries, identity, encryption, network paths, failure\nmodes and, for AI, prompt injection, tool permissions and model data use. It exists because a\nquestionnaire captures claims while a review tests the design itself. You pass it by arriving with an\naccurate diagram, a threat model and evidence, and leaving with tracked, dated findings.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"compliance-constraints","key":"deployment/compliance-constraints","module":"deployment","name":"Compliance constraints","taxonomy_name":"compliance constraints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/security-questionnaires","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews"],"tldr":"Compliance constraints are the deployment-shaping rules that laws, contracts and audit frameworks impose:\nwhere data may be processed, which vendors may see it, how long it is kept, who may access it, and what\nmust be logged and proven. For AI systems they decide region, model provider, logging design and even\nwhether the cloud is allowed at all. This lesson orients you to how regimes become concrete constraints;\nthe deep treatment of compliance design lives in the security-governance-compliance module.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"deployment-readiness-reviews","key":"deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","module":"deployment","name":"Deployment readiness reviews","taxonomy_name":"deployment readiness reviews","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","deployment/uat","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment/runbooks","deployment/hypercare","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan"],"tldr":"A deployment readiness review is a scheduled go/no-go decision, made before production traffic\narrives, in which every launch requirement (quality evidence, security sign-off, capacity, monitoring,\nrollback, support ownership) is checked against written evidence by a named owner. It exists because\nlaunch risk is spread across teams who each assume someone else covered the gap; the review turns\n\"we think we're ready\" into a list of items that are either proven, waived with an expiry, or blocking.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"uat","key":"deployment/uat","module":"deployment","name":"UAT (user acceptance testing)","taxonomy_name":"UAT","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation"],"tldr":"User acceptance testing is the phase where the customer's real users, not the builders, run the system\non realistic tasks and decide whether it meets the acceptance criteria agreed up front. It exists because\n\"it passes our tests\" and \"it does the job our users need\" are different claims. For AI systems, UAT must\nalso handle non-deterministic output: score samples against a rubric, size the sample so the pass rate\nis statistically meaningful, and separate model-quality defects from integration bugs.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","key":"deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","module":"deployment","name":"Rollout criteria & rollback triggers","taxonomy_name":"rollout criteria & rollback triggers","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","reliability/slas-slos","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","deployment/runbooks"],"tldr":"Rollout criteria say what evidence lets a release move from one exposure stage to the next (internal,\na few percent, a quarter, everyone); rollback triggers say what observed signal sends it back. Both are\nwritten before the rollout starts, as thresholds with minimum sample sizes, because during a live\nincident people rationalise bad numbers. For AI releases the hard part is that quality signals are\nnoisy and lag behind error and latency signals, so criteria mix automated hard triggers with sampled\nquality checks and a deliberate bake time.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"runbooks","key":"deployment/runbooks","module":"deployment","name":"Runbooks","taxonomy_name":"runbooks","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["deployment/health-checks","deployment/hypercare","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","observability/monitoring","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","delivery-ownership/production-debugging"],"tldr":"A runbook is a written, tested procedure for one operational situation: what triggered it, how to\nconfirm the impact, the exact steps to mitigate, how to verify recovery and when to escalate. It exists\nbecause the person handling an incident is rarely the person who built the system, and at 3 a.m. under\npressure nobody reasons well from first principles. Good runbooks are linked from the alert that fires,\nfavour mitigation over diagnosis, and are rehearsed so they are known to work.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"health-checks","key":"deployment/health-checks","module":"deployment","name":"Health checks","taxonomy_name":"health checks","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","observability/monitoring","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","reliability/slas-slos","deployment/runbooks","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers"],"tldr":"Health checks are endpoints or probes that let infrastructure and operators ask \"should this instance\nget traffic, and should it be restarted?\". They answer different questions, so they are separate checks:\nliveness (is the process stuck, restart it), readiness (can it serve right now, route around it if not),\nstartup (has slow initialisation such as loading a model finished) and end-to-end synthetic checks (does\nthe whole system produce a correct answer). Mixing these up turns a degraded dependency into a total\noutage.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"hypercare","key":"deployment/hypercare","module":"deployment","name":"Hypercare","taxonomy_name":"hypercare","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["deployment/runbooks","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","adoption/change-management","llmops-platform/model-monitoring"],"tldr":"Hypercare is a fixed, time-boxed period right after go-live in which the delivery team stays on\nelevated support: closer monitoring, a daily triage of issues and feedback, faster fix cycles and named\npeople the customer can reach directly. It exists because real users always find problems that UAT\nmissed, and the first weeks decide whether they trust the system. It ends on written exit criteria and a\nhandoff to normal support, not when the team gets reassigned.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"delivery-ownership","tier":3,"order":28,"title":"Delivery & ownership","intro":{"overview":"This module is about what happens after an AI system ships and someone has to answer for it. It starts\nwith the single failure: production debugging teaches you to rebuild the exact input the model saw\n(rendered prompt, retrieved chunks, tool results, model snapshot, sampling settings) from traces, then\nbisect and replay instead of guessing. Incident severity and escalation decides, before anything breaks,\nhow bad a failure is by its impact rather than its cause, including the silent ones that return HTTP 200\nwith wrong or unsafe content. Root-cause analysis and postmortems turn a mitigated incident into\ncontributing factors and owned actions, without blame. Failure analysis zooms out from one incident to\nhundreds of small failures, sampled, labelled by pipeline stage and ranked by harm. System reliability\ngives the owner's view of four levers: how often things fail, how big each failure is, how fast you\nnotice and how fast you recover. Operational ownership and support boundaries say who is paged for each\nlayer and how issues cross between you, the customer and vendors. SLAs turn all of this into a contract\nyour dependency chain and on-call rotation can actually back. Technical documentation keeps the system\nworking once the builders leave, and productizing turns the third bespoke build into one shared core.\nAfterwards you can run an incident end to end, write a severity scale, a postmortem, a boundary table\nand an SLA, and decide when field work should become product. It matters most for FDEs and for\nEngineers who own a live service.","why":{"engineer":"You carry the pager for an LLM pipeline, so you need trace IDs, captured prompts and a replay harness that let you reproduce a one-in-ten failure. You also need a severity scale you can apply at 3 a.m. without a debate, and postmortem actions that change the system rather than ask people to be more careful.","architect":"You set the reliability levers and the promises built on them: change gating, containment, symptom alerts and rollback time, the dependency-chain ceiling behind any SLA, and the shared core, configuration and extension points that let one design serve many customers without turning into N forks.","fde":"You go live inside someone else's environment, so you must agree the boundary table, support SLA and escalation path with the customer before launch, leave runbooks and a system card behind when you rotate off, and notice when the third customer is asking for the same build so it can become product."},"mental_model":"Picture delivery as a loop that runs on every failure, wrapped in a set of promises. The inner loop runs\nfast: detect, classify by severity, escalate to the right owner, mitigate, then debug from the trace\nuntil you can replay the failure. The outer loop runs slowly: postmortems find the contributing factors\nbehind each serious incident, and failure analysis ranks the many small failures nobody declared. Both\nfeed one prioritised list of reliability work, chosen by whichever of the four levers is cheapest to pull\nnext. Around the loops sit the promises: ownership says who acts at each layer, the SLA says what the\ncustomer is owed, and documentation lets someone who was not there carry the loop on. Productizing is the\nloop at business scale: what each deployment teaches flows back into one core, so every fix reaches\nevery customer. A promise without a loop behind it is a future breach, and a loop with no promises\naround it is unowned work.","order":["production-debugging","incident-severity-and-escalation","root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","failure-analysis","system-reliability","operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","slas","technical-documentation","productizing-repeated-customer-solutions"],"order_reasons":{"production-debugging":"the base skill of reconstructing and replaying one live failure, which every later lesson assumes.","incident-severity-and-escalation":"adds the response frame around debugging: classify by impact, decide who is woken and when to escalate.","root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems":"follows a mitigated incident to its contributing factors and owned actions, using the timeline from the first two lessons.","failure-analysis":"widens from one incident to hundreds of small failures, ranked by stage and harm, so effort goes to the dominant modes.","system-reliability":"combines incident and failure data into the owner's four levers and decides which lever to fund next.","operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries":"names who owns each layer and how issues cross between you, the customer and vendors, which an SLA needs.","slas":"writes the service and support promise, computed from the dependency ceiling and backed by the ownership and on-call model.","technical-documentation":"writes down runbooks, decisions and the system card so the loop and the promises survive handover.","productizing-repeated-customer-solutions":"the business-scale step, which assumes you can already deliver, own and document one deployment well."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer handing over a contract-review assistant to an insurance customer\nafter an eight-week pilot. The system retrieves clauses from the customer's document store, calls a\nhosted LLM to flag risky terms, and writes findings to their case-management tool. Two other insurers\nwant the same thing, and your team has already copied the repository once for the second. Over a week,\nprepare the deployment for production ownership. Instrument the pipeline so any flagged finding can be\ntraced to its rendered prompt, retrieved chunks and model version, and build a replay harness. Take a\nseeded incident (a prompt change that silently stopped flagging indemnity clauses for one week) through\nseverity classification, mitigation, debugging and a blameless postmortem. Run a failure analysis on 100\nsampled outputs. Then write the ownership boundary table, the SLA, the runbooks and handover documents,\nand a productizing proposal that separates the shared core from per-insurer configuration and extensions.","deliverables":["A tracing and replay harness that, given a request ID, rebuilds the exact model input and replays it N times with one variable changed, plus a short write-up of the seeded incident reproduced from its trace.","A severity scale and escalation policy with at least four levels, including content-level criteria for missed or wrong findings and cross-tenant exposure, with time-to-acknowledge and update cadence per level.","A blameless postmortem for the seeded incident: a timeline, contributing factors across prevention, detection and mitigation, and at least three owned, dated action items.","A failure-analysis report on 100 sampled outputs, labelled by failure mode and pipeline stage, with severity-weighted counts and the top three fixes ranked by expected reduction in harm.","An ownership boundary table covering model provider, your platform, the application, the customer's document store and identity provider, plus a draft SLA with service and support terms, exclusions, credits and the dependency-chain arithmetic.","A handover pack containing an architecture overview, at least two decision records, runbooks tied to specific alerts and a system card listing model and prompt versions, eval results and known failure modes, plus a productizing proposal with the core, configuration and extension split and break-even estimate."],"rubric":["Replaying the seeded incident's request ID reproduces the missed indemnity flag at the old prompt version and not at the fixed one, across repeated runs that account for sampling variance.","The postmortem names no individual as the cause, does not stop at \"the model missed it\", and each action item changes a test, an alert, a deploy gate or a guardrail rather than asking for vigilance.","The failure-analysis categories come from reading the sampled outputs, each category shows its share and stage, and the chosen fix targets the top weighted category, not the most recent complaint.","The SLA's availability figure does not exceed the product of the dependencies' availabilities, every support response time maps to a named rotation, and the internal SLO alert is stricter than the contract.","In a tabletop run of \"answers cite superseded policy documents\", a reviewer can use the boundary table and runbook alone to route the issue to its owner within one step, with no ticket bouncing.","The productizing proposal lists every difference between the existing forks and assigns each to core, configuration or extension, refusing at least one customer-specific request with a stated reason."]}},"topics":[{"id":"production-debugging","key":"delivery-ownership/production-debugging","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Production debugging","taxonomy_name":"production debugging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","observability/tracing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","llmops-platform/rollback"],"tldr":"Production debugging is finding why a live system misbehaved when you cannot pause it, attach a\ndebugger, or trust that the bug will happen again. For LLM systems it hinges on one move: reconstruct the\n*exact* input the model saw (rendered prompt, retrieved context, tool results, model version, sampling\nparameters) from traces, then bisect the pipeline and replay with one variable changed at a time, after\nfirst stopping the bleeding.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"failure-analysis","key":"delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Failure analysis","taxonomy_name":"failure analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","evals/error-analysis","reliability/fmea","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"Failure analysis looks across many failures to find the few failure modes that cause most of the harm,\ninstead of fixing whichever one was reported last. You sample failures without bias, label each with\nwhat went wrong and at which pipeline stage, count, weight by severity, and spend engineering effort on\nthe top of that ranked list. In LLM systems this matters because failures are diverse and quiet.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"incident-severity-and-escalation","key":"delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Incident severity & escalation","taxonomy_name":"incident severity & escalation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","delivery-ownership/slas","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","deployment/runbooks","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers"],"tldr":"A severity scale classifies an incident by its impact on users and the business, not by its cause or how\nhard it is to fix, so that response speed, who gets woken up and how often stakeholders hear from you\nare decided in advance rather than argued about mid-incident. Escalation rules say when to pull in more\nexpertise (functional) or more authority (hierarchical). AI systems need extra criteria for quality and\nsafety incidents that throw no errors.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","key":"delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Root-cause analysis & postmortems","taxonomy_name":"root-cause analysis & postmortems","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","reliability/fmea","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Root-cause analysis asks why an incident was possible, not just what broke, and a postmortem is the\nwritten record that turns the answer into owned, tracked work. In complex systems there is rarely a\nsingle root cause, so good analyses map several contributing factors across prevention, detection and\nmitigation, and stay blameless so people report what really happened. \"The model hallucinated\" is a\nsymptom, never a root cause.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","key":"delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Operational ownership & support boundaries","taxonomy_name":"operational ownership & support boundaries","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","deployment/hypercare","deployment/runbooks","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"Operational ownership means a named team is on the hook for a component in production: it gets paged,\nhas the access and authority to change it, and answers for its reliability. Support boundaries write\ndown where that ownership stops: which layers you support, which belong to the customer or a vendor, and\nhow issues cross the line. AI deployments span many parties, so without explicit boundaries incidents\nbounce between teams while nobody fixes them.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"slas","key":"delivery-ownership/slas","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"SLAs","taxonomy_name":"SLAs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","delivery-ownership/system-reliability","scoping/statement-of-work","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","observability/latency-percentiles"],"tldr":"An SLA is a contract, not a dashboard: it defines the covered service, exactly how it is measured, what\nis excluded, and what the customer receives when it is missed. Whoever owns delivery owns two kinds,\nservice SLAs (availability, latency) and support SLAs (response time by severity), and the job is to\ncommit only to what your dependency chain and on-call rotation can actually back. SLI, SLO and\nerror-budget mechanics live in reliability/slas-slos; this lesson is about writing and operating the promise.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"system-reliability","key":"delivery-ownership/system-reliability","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"System reliability","taxonomy_name":"system reliability","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments"],"tldr":"System reliability is the probability that the system does the right thing for its users over time,\nand owning it means managing four levers: how often things fail, how big each failure is, how fast you\nnotice, and how fast you recover. For AI systems \"the right thing\" includes answer quality, so a system\ncan be fully up and still unreliable. This lesson is the owner's view; the patterns themselves\n(redundancy, circuit breakers, bulkheads) are taught in the reliability module.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"technical-documentation","key":"delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Technical documentation","taxonomy_name":"technical documentation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/adrs","deployment/runbooks","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","deployment/security-questionnaires","llmops-platform/prompt-registry"],"tldr":"Technical documentation is how a system keeps working when its builders are not in the room. It works\nwhen each document has one reader and one job (learn, do a task, look something up, understand why),\nlives next to the code it describes, has an owner, and is checked for staleness like code is checked for\nbugs. AI systems need extra pages: model and prompt versions, evaluation results, data flows and known\nfailure modes, because those are what operators and reviewers ask about first.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","key":"delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Productizing repeated customer solutions","taxonomy_name":"productizing repeated customer solutions","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","adoption/feature-request-triage","strategy/platform-strategy","scoping/productionization","prompting/prompt-templates","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation"],"tldr":"Forward-deployed work produces bespoke solutions; when the same solution is built for the third customer,\nit should become product: one shared core, per-customer configuration, and defined extension points for\nthe genuinely unique parts. Doing it too early bakes in the wrong abstraction; doing it too late leaves N\ndiverging forks whose maintenance, model upgrades and bug fixes scale with customer count, turning a\nproduct company into a consultancy.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]},{"id":"adoption","tier":3,"order":29,"title":"Adoption","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the gap between \"the system is live\" and \"the system is used and is paying for\nitself\". A deployed AI system can pass every eval and still fail, because people ignore it, work around\nit or trust it too much. Stakeholder management maps who can help, block or be affected, and finds the\nsecurity reviewers, data owners and team leads who can veto late. Change management names the exact\nbehavior that must change, finds what blocks it for each group, and redesigns incentives and workflow\naround the tool. Workshops pull the knowledge out of experts' heads into concrete artifacts: a mapped\nworkflow, an agreed rubric, labelled examples. User training teaches a working mental model of where the\nsystem is reliable, how it fails and how to check its output. Usage analytics instruments what people do\nwith each output (accept, edit, regenerate, abandon) against an eligible population. Adoption and\nvalue-realization measurement keeps \"they use it\" separate from \"it changed a business outcome\", using a\nbaseline or comparison group. Exec communication turns those numbers into a conclusion-first update with\na clear ask. Continuous improvement and feature-request triage route production evidence and customer\nasks into measured fixes or reasoned roadmap decisions. Handoff moves ownership to the customer's team\nthrough staged, demonstrated exit criteria. Afterwards you can plan and run a rollout, instrument it,\nprove or disprove its value with a defensible number, and leave behind a team that can run it. It\nmatters most for FDEs, and for Architects who own a platform that has to show value to keep its budget.","why":{"engineer":"You build the disposition events (accepted unchanged, edit size, regenerate, abandon) without copying raw prompts into the warehouse, and you run the improvement loop: classify failures, fix at the cheapest layer, add the cases to a frozen regression suite, gate the release, and check that the targeted failure bucket actually shrank in production.","architect":"You design measurement in from the start: an eligible-population denominator, a baseline or comparison group agreed during scoping, guardrail metrics beside the value metric, and system boundaries (repos, CI, monitoring, identities) chosen so the customer team can own the system after handoff without a rebuild.","fde":"You are the person in the room. You map stakeholders and veto holders in week one, run the workshops that settle quality criteria, train users to verify outputs, triage asks without promising forks, report to the sponsor with the conclusion and the ask first, and hand over only when the customer team has shown it can ship a gated change and handle an incident."},"mental_model":"Picture adoption as a funnel with a loop beside it. The funnel runs from eligible people, to people who\ntry the tool, to people who use it for the intended work, to work that comes out measurably better than\nthe baseline. Each layer leaks for its own reason, and each lesson is a tool for one leak. People who\nnever start are a stakeholder or change problem. People who try and drop off are usually a training,\ntrust or workflow problem. People who use it without effect are a quality or scoping problem. Usage\nanalytics shows where the funnel leaks, and value measurement shows what reaches the bottom. The loop\nbeside it takes what leaks out (failures, complaints, feature asks), sorts it by type, and feeds it back\nas fixes, training or roadmap decisions. Exec communication reports the funnel's shape to whoever funds\nit. Handoff is the moment the loop's owner changes. Usage is the leading indicator you act on weekly;\nrealized value is the lagging number that renews the contract. Never report one as the other.","order":["stakeholder-management","change-management","workshops","user-training","usage-analytics","adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","exec-communication","continuous-improvement","feature-request-triage","handoff-to-customer-teams"],"order_reasons":{"stakeholder-management":"find the sponsors, users and veto holders first, because every later step depends on knowing who must agree.","change-management":"define the behavior change and its blockers per group, which sets the target for training, analytics and measurement.","workshops":"the main tool for getting knowledge and agreement out of those stakeholders as concrete artifacts such as rubrics and workflows.","user-training":"teaches users the mental model and verification habits the change plan calls for, with drills rather than feature tours.","usage-analytics":"instruments what users do with each output, so you can see whether the change and training are working.","adoption-and-value-realization-measurement":"builds on usage data, adding a baseline or comparison group to separate engagement from business value.","exec-communication":"turns adoption and value numbers into conclusion-first updates and decisions for the people who fund the work.","continuous-improvement":"the operating loop that turns classified production failures into gated, measured fixes.","feature-request-triage":"routes customer asks, separating real features from quality failures, training gaps and model limits that belong in the improvement loop.","handoff-to-customer-teams":"the last step, which moves ownership of the whole loop to the customer once they have shown they can run it."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer on an AI drafting assistant for a mid-size insurer's claims team.\nIt drafts replies to policyholder emails from the claim file and policy documents. It has been live for\nfour weeks across 180 eligible adjusters in three regional teams. The sponsor, the head of claims\noperations, was sold \"30% faster first response\". The dashboard shows 1,400 requests a week, and the\nsponsor wants to know whether to expand to another 400 adjusters next quarter. Over a week, using a\nsynthetic or anonymised event log and claim dataset (provided or generated), find out what is really\nhappening and set the deployment up to succeed after you leave. Map the stakeholders and veto holders.\nWrite the behavior statement and change plan. Design the usage event schema and adoption dashboard.\nMeasure realized value against a baseline or comparison group. Run a quality-criteria workshop, design\ntraining with verification drills, triage a backlog of 30 raw asks, run one improvement cycle, write the\nexpansion recommendation for the sponsor, and draft a staged handoff plan for the insurer's own team.","deliverables":["A stakeholder map covering at least eight roles (sponsor, team leads, adjusters, IT, security, data owner, compliance, works council or HR), each with what they need, their influence or veto power, and a planned engagement and cadence.","A change plan with a one-sentence behavior statement, the blockers for each group, and incentive or workflow changes, plus a training plan with at least five verification drills built from real failure types and a measure for each.","A usage event schema with no raw prompt or claim content, and a dashboard showing weekly active over eligible, AI-assisted share of replies, week-4 cohort retention, and accepted-unchanged, edit-size and regeneration rates sliced by team and prompt version.","A value-realization report that compares first-response time against a baseline or comparison group, gives a range rather than a point, and breaks the gap between the projected 30% and the realized figure into adoption, quality and attribution parts.","A workshop agenda and output for agreeing a reply-quality rubric, a triaged backlog of the 30 asks with a classification, cluster, score and route for each, and one completed improvement cycle with new regression cases and before/after bucket rates.","A one-page executive update recommending expand, hold or re-scope, with the decision needed and the date, and a staged handoff plan with exit criteria the insurer's team must demonstrate."],"rubric":["The value figure is credited to the assistant only against a baseline or comparison group, comes with a stated range, and explicitly rules out at least one confounder such as early adopters being the fastest adjusters.","The dashboard never reports a raw request count without a denominator, and at least one team-level difference is traced to a cause (team lead, incentive or training) rather than left as noise.","The executive update gives its recommendation and the decision needed in the first two sentences, translates the error rate into cases and cost, and a reader can restate the ask after one read.","At least a third of the 30 asks are reclassified as quality failures, training gaps or model limits and routed to the matching place, and every declined ask has a stated reason sent back to the requester.","The improvement cycle adds regression cases before the fix, shows no regression on the frozen suite, and reports the targeted failure bucket's rate before and after.","Handoff exit criteria are tasks the customer team performs (a gated prompt change, a simulated incident, a credential rotation away from personal accounts), not dates or documents delivered."]}},"topics":[{"id":"change-management","key":"adoption/change-management","module":"adoption","name":"Change management","taxonomy_name":"change management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["adoption/user-training","adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/usage-analytics","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","scoping/workflow-analysis","agents/human-in-the-loop","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"Change management is the deliberate work of moving people from the old way of doing a task to the new\none, so that a deployed AI system is actually used and produces value. It exists because the common\nfailure of enterprise AI is not a broken model but an unchanged workflow: people ignore, work around or\nover-trust a tool whose output is probabilistic and whose arrival can feel like a threat to their role.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"user-training","key":"adoption/user-training","module":"adoption","name":"User training","taxonomy_name":"user training","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["adoption/change-management","adoption/workshops","adoption/usage-analytics","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","agents/human-in-the-loop","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp"],"tldr":"User training for an AI system teaches people a working mental model, not a feature tour: what the\nsystem is good at, how it fails, how to verify its output, what data they may give it, and how to report\nproblems. It exists because probabilistic tools punish both blind trust and blanket distrust, and because\nusers who never learn to check outputs either let errors through or quietly stop using the tool.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"workshops","key":"adoption/workshops","module":"adoption","name":"Workshops","taxonomy_name":"workshops","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["adoption/user-training","adoption/stakeholder-management","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","evals/rubrics"],"tldr":"A workshop is a facilitated working session that produces a specific artifact or decision with the people\nwho hold the knowledge and the authority, in hours instead of weeks of email. In AI work the useful\nartifacts are concrete: a ranked use-case list, a mapped workflow, an agreed rubric, labeled examples. A\nworkshop without a defined output and follow-up is just a long meeting.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"stakeholder-management","key":"adoption/stakeholder-management","module":"adoption","name":"Stakeholder management","taxonomy_name":"stakeholder management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["strategy/stakeholder-alignment","adoption/exec-communication","adoption/change-management","adoption/workshops","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","scoping/success-metrics-kpis"],"tldr":"Stakeholder management is the ongoing work of knowing who can help, block or be affected by an AI\ndeployment, what each of them needs to see, and engaging them on purpose rather than by accident. It\nexists because AI projects often stall for reasons that have nothing to do with the model: when a security reviewer, data owner or\nteam lead who was never engaged says no late, or when a sponsor's expectations drift away from what the\nsystem can deliver.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"exec-communication","key":"adoption/exec-communication","module":"adoption","name":"Exec communication","taxonomy_name":"exec communication","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","adoption/change-management","scoping/roi-model","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/demos","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"Executive communication means giving a busy decision-maker the conclusion first, in business terms, with\nthe decision you need from them and the evidence behind it, in a form they can absorb in minutes. It\nmatters for AI because executives fund and protect projects based on a few short updates, and\nprobabilistic quality, technical jargon or late bad news can turn a healthy project into a cancelled one.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"usage-analytics","key":"adoption/usage-analytics","module":"adoption","name":"Usage analytics","taxonomy_name":"usage analytics","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","observability/tracing","observability/logging","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","safety-security/pii-redaction","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/a-b-testing"],"tldr":"Usage analytics is instrumenting an AI feature so you can see who uses it, how often, how deeply, and\nwhat they do with its output: accept, edit, regenerate or abandon. Traces tell you what the system did.\nAnalytics tells you what people did with it. You need it because a deployed assistant can be technically\nhealthy and still go unused, and only behavioural events against a defined eligible population show that.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","key":"adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","module":"adoption","name":"Adoption & value-realization measurement","taxonomy_name":"adoption & value-realization measurement","kind":"metric","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["adoption/usage-analytics","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/roi-model","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/a-b-testing","adoption/exec-communication","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Adoption measures whether the intended people use the system for the intended work. Value realization\nmeasures whether that use changed a business outcome, compared with what would have happened without it.\nYou need both, kept apart. Adoption is a leading indicator you can act on weekly. Realized value is the\nlagging number that renews the contract, and it can only be credited to the AI against a baseline or a\ncomparison group.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"continuous-improvement","key":"adoption/continuous-improvement","module":"adoption","name":"Continuous improvement","taxonomy_name":"continuous improvement","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/drift","adoption/usage-analytics"],"tldr":"Continuous improvement is the operating loop that turns production evidence into measured fixes. You\nobserve failures, classify them, prioritise by frequency × severity, fix at the cheapest layer that\nworks, capture the cases in a regression suite, gate the release, and confirm online that the failure\nbucket shrank. It exists because an AI system's quality degrades or stalls by default, and ad-hoc prompt\ntweaks fix one complaint while silently breaking others.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"handoff-to-customer-teams","key":"adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","module":"adoption","name":"Handoff to customer teams","taxonomy_name":"handoff to customer teams","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","deployment/runbooks","deployment/hypercare","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","adoption/continuous-improvement","adoption/user-training"],"tldr":"Handoff is the transfer of *ownership* of a deployed AI system from the vendor or FDE team to the\ncustomer's own engineers and operators: running it, changing it safely, and judging its quality.\nDocuments are not enough. It works when responsibility moves in stages, and when the exit criteria are\ntasks the customer team has shown it can do (a gated prompt change, a simulated incident, a credential\nrotation) rather than a calendar date.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"},{"id":"feature-request-triage","key":"adoption/feature-request-triage","module":"adoption","name":"Feature-request triage","taxonomy_name":"feature-request triage","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","adoption/continuous-improvement","adoption/stakeholder-management","strategy/ai-roadmap","scoping/problem-decomposition"],"tldr":"Feature-request triage turns the stream of customer asks into routed decisions. Capture the underlying\nproblem rather than the proposed solution. Classify what the ask really is: in AI products, many\n\"feature requests\" are quality failures, training gaps or model limits in disguise. Cluster across\ncustomers, score with explicit criteria, route each item, and tell the requester the outcome. It exists\nso the roadmap follows evidence instead of whoever asks loudest, and so field teams stop silently forking\nthe product.","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}]}]};
